{"record_id":"D16-B92-R03","batch_id":"D16-B92","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Query Design","concept":"Join cardinality correctness","knowledge_type":"ERROR","question":"Why can an incorrect join predicate multiply rows?","answer":"A join returns combinations satisfying its join condition, so a one-to-many or many-to-many relationship can multiply rows when the query expects one result per parent.","explanation":"Unexpected row multiplication is often a logical query error rather than a database performance problem.","principle":"Expected result cardinality must be part of query design and verification.","rule":"Define the intended cardinality before adding joins and verify it against representative duplicate relationships.","correct_example":"Use aggregation, EXISTS, or an explicitly constrained relationship when the required result is one row per parent.","invalid_example":"Join a parent to multiple child rows and then assume the result still contains exactly one row per parent.","problem":"Downstream calculations or pagination operate on duplicated logical entities.","failure_mode":"Incorrect totals, duplicate API objects, or inflated aggregates.","cause":"Relationship cardinality was ignored.","solution":"Choose a query structure that matches the intended cardinality.","alternative":"Pre-aggregate child data before joining when the parent result must remain unique.","tradeoff":"Pre-aggregation can increase query complexity but makes cardinality explicit.","constraint":"Correctness depends on actual data constraints as well as query structure.","edge_case":"Historical data may violate assumptions even when current data appears one-to-one.","security_implication":"Duplicated rows can cause inconsistent authorization or data-export behavior.","performance_implication":"Unexpected many-to-many expansion can dramatically increase intermediate result size.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate child relationships and aggregation boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Compare expected entity counts before and after each join.","language":"SQL","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Joins","Cardinality"],"related_records":["D16-B92-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Relational algebra and official SQL query documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B93-R01","batch_id":"D16-B93","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Query Design","concept":"Aggregation correctness","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should be verified when using GROUP BY and aggregate functions?","answer":"The query must aggregate at the intended grain, and every selected non-aggregated expression must satisfy the database's grouping rules.","explanation":"Aggregation changes result grain. Joining additional tables before aggregation can also multiply inputs and produce incorrect totals.","principle":"Every aggregate query must have an explicit logical grain.","rule":"Identify the intended grouping key before constructing joins and aggregate expressions.","correct_example":"Aggregate order-line amounts by order_id when the required result is one row per order.","invalid_example":"Sum a measure after joining multiple independent one-to-many relationships without controlling row multiplication.","problem":"Totals or counts become inflated.","failure_mode":"Silent analytical corruption.","cause":"Input cardinality changed before aggregation.","solution":"Pre-aggregate independent relationships or redesign the query to preserve intended grain.","alternative":"Use correlated existence checks or separate aggregates when relationships are independent.","tradeoff":"More explicit query structure can require additional subqueries or CTEs.","constraint":"Grouping syntax and functional-dependency inference vary by database.","edge_case":"COUNT(*) and COUNT(column) have different NULL behavior.","security_implication":"Aggregated results can still disclose sensitive information through small groups or inference.","performance_implication":"Large grouping operations may require sorting, hashing, memory, or temporary storage.","testing_guidance":"Test duplicate relationships, NULLs, empty groups, and boundary group sizes.","verification_guidance":"Reconcile aggregate results against independently computed known cases.","language":"SQL","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B92-R03"],"prerequisites":["Aggregation","Joins"],"related_records":["D16-B93-R02","D16-B93-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official SQL aggregation documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B93-R02","batch_id":"D16-B93","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Query Design","concept":"COUNT semantics","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why does COUNT(*) differ from COUNT(column)?","answer":"COUNT(*) counts qualifying rows, while COUNT(column) counts qualifying rows for which the specified expression is non-null.","explanation":"The distinction matters whenever nullable columns or outer joins are involved.","principle":"Aggregate functions must be selected according to the intended population being counted.","rule":"Specify whether the query needs row count or non-null expression count.","correct_example":"Use COUNT(*) when counting rows in a relation after filtering.","invalid_example":"Use COUNT(child.id) in a LEFT JOIN and interpret it as the number of parent rows without considering NULL child values.","problem":"Reported counts do not match intended entity counts.","failure_mode":"Incorrect metrics or business calculations.","cause":"Aggregate NULL semantics were ignored.","solution":"Choose the appropriate COUNT expression and test outer-join cases.","alternative":"Use COUNT(DISTINCT key) when duplicate join paths must be collapsed and that matches the intended metric.","tradeoff":"DISTINCT can improve correctness for duplicate paths but may increase computational cost.","constraint":"Exact optimizer behavior is engine-specific.","edge_case":"COUNT(DISTINCT ...) has different semantics and resource requirements from ordinary COUNT.","security_implication":"Incorrect counts can affect security monitoring or quota enforcement.","performance_implication":"Distinct aggregation can require additional memory or sorting/hashing.","testing_guidance":"Test NULL, duplicate, empty, and outer-join cases.","verification_guidance":"Compare each metric to a manually verified dataset.","language":"SQL","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B93-R01"],"prerequisites":["Aggregation","NULL semantics"],"related_records":["D16-B93-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official SQL aggregate-function documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B93-R03","batch_id":"D16-B93","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Query Design","concept":"Distinct aggregation cost","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can COUNT DISTINCT and other DISTINCT aggregates be expensive?","answer":"Distinct aggregation requires identifying unique values, which can require additional memory, sorting, hashing, or other database work depending on the execution strategy.","explanation":"DISTINCT is sometimes necessary for correctness but should not be added merely to hide unintended row multiplication.","principle":"Correctness should come from correct relational structure rather than indiscriminate duplicate elimination.","rule":"Use DISTINCT only when duplicate elimination is part of the intended semantics.","correct_example":"Count distinct customer_id when the business metric explicitly means unique customers.","invalid_example":"Add DISTINCT to every query because a join unexpectedly creates duplicates.","problem":"A logical join error is hidden while expensive duplicate elimination is added.","failure_mode":"Incorrect or unnecessarily expensive queries.","cause":"DISTINCT was used as a repair mechanism rather than a semantic requirement.","solution":"Fix relationship/cardinality logic first, then apply DISTINCT only when required by the result definition.","alternative":"Pre-aggregate or use EXISTS where the intended semantics are existence.","tradeoff":"Correct DISTINCT can provide accurate metrics but may increase execution cost.","constraint":"Execution strategy depends on database engine and available indexes.","edge_case":"Data growth can change a previously inexpensive distinct aggregation into a resource-intensive operation.","security_implication":"Distinct results can still reveal sensitive population information.","performance_implication":"Large distinct sets can consume significant memory and temporary storage.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark at expected and worst-case cardinalities.","verification_guidance":"Inspect execution plans and confirm that DISTINCT represents intended semantics.","language":"SQL","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B93-R01"],"prerequisites":["Aggregation","Query planning"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official database query-planner and aggregate documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B94-R01","batch_id":"D16-B94","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Document Databases","concept":"Document schema validation","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should schema validation still be used in schema-flexible document databases?","answer":"Schema flexibility does not imply absence of invariants. Validation rules can enforce required fields, types, ranges, and structural expectations that protect downstream consumers.","explanation":"Flexible schemas are useful when data evolves, but uncontrolled variation can become an interoperability and correctness problem.","principle":"Schema flexibility should be bounded by explicit invariants.","rule":"Enforce the minimum structural contract required for reliable consumers while allowing intentional evolution.","correct_example":"Require a document type discriminator and validate required fields for each supported document version.","invalid_example":"Accept arbitrary document structures and assume every consumer will correctly handle every possible shape.","problem":"Consumers receive incompatible or malformed documents.","failure_mode":"Runtime parsing errors and inconsistent application behavior.","cause":"Schema flexibility was confused with schema absence.","solution":"Define and enforce appropriate document validation rules.","alternative":"Validate entirely at the application boundary when the database cannot express the required contract.","tradeoff":"Validation improves integrity but can make rapid schema experimentation more constrained.","constraint":"Validation language and enforcement modes vary by document database.","edge_case":"Legacy documents may require migration before stricter validation can be enabled.","security_implication":"Validation can reduce malformed structures reaching security-sensitive processing paths.","performance_implication":"Validation adds write-time processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test old, new, malformed, partial, and boundary document versions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that invalid documents are rejected under the deployed validation mode.","language":"","framework":"Document database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Document modeling","Schema evolution"],"related_records":["D16-B94-R02","D16-B94-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official document-database schema-validation documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B94-R02","batch_id":"D16-B94","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Document Databases","concept":"Document version discriminator","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can an explicit document version field help schema evolution?","answer":"A version discriminator allows readers and migration logic to distinguish structural representations and apply version-specific interpretation or transformation.","explanation":"Schema evolution becomes safer when historical and current representations can be identified explicitly.","principle":"Evolving data structures require an observable representation of structural version when multiple versions may coexist.","rule":"Use an explicit version marker when readers may encounter more than one valid document structure.","correct_example":"Store schema_version=2 and route parsing according to the supported schema version.","invalid_example":"Infer document version from the accidental presence or absence of arbitrary fields when those fields can legitimately be optional.","problem":"Readers cannot reliably determine the document structure.","failure_mode":"Incorrect parsing or destructive migration.","cause":"Schema version was implicit and ambiguous.","solution":"Add an explicit version discriminator and define supported transitions.","alternative":"Perform an immediate controlled migration when the database and operational model guarantee a single representation.","tradeoff":"Version markers add metadata but improve compatibility and migration safety.","constraint":"Versioning does not automatically migrate or validate documents.","edge_case":"Multiple historical versions may require long-term compatibility support.","security_implication":"Version-specific parsers must enforce the same authorization and validation rules.","performance_implication":"Supporting multiple versions can increase parsing and query complexity.","testing_guidance":"Test every supported version and all migration boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify that readers reject unsupported or malformed versions safely.","language":"","framework":"Document database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B94-R01"],"prerequisites":["Document schema evolution"],"related_records":["D16-B94-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Document schema-evolution principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B94-R03","batch_id":"D16-B94","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Document Databases","concept":"Partial document migration","knowledge_type":"MIGRATION_PATTERN","question":"When can lazy document migration be useful?","answer":"Lazy migration updates documents when they are naturally read or written, which can avoid a large one-time migration when the application can safely support multiple versions during transition.","explanation":"Lazy migration is appropriate only when old representations remain readable and the migration process is idempotent and operationally bounded.","principle":"Migration timing should match data volume, availability requirements, and compatibility guarantees.","rule":"Use lazy migration only when compatibility with legacy documents is explicitly implemented and stale documents are controlled.","correct_example":"Read version 1 documents, transform them to version 2, validate them, and persist the new representation safely.","invalid_example":"Deploy a new reader that assumes version 2 while old version 1 documents remain unreadable.","problem":"Legacy records fail immediately after deployment.","failure_mode":"Runtime compatibility failure.","cause":"Migration and reader compatibility were not coordinated.","solution":"Implement dual-read or staged compatibility before lazy migration.","alternative":"Perform a controlled bulk migration before removing old-reader support.","tradeoff":"Lazy migration reduces deployment-time load but prolongs multi-version complexity.","constraint":"Migration may never reach rarely accessed documents without an explicit completion mechanism.","edge_case":"A stale document may be read only during an unusual operational path.","security_implication":"Every version parser must enforce equivalent security semantics.","performance_implication":"First access may incur additional migration latency.","testing_guidance":"Test old documents, concurrent reads/writes, retries, partial migration, and failures.","verification_guidance":"Track migration coverage and verify that unsupported versions eventually reach a controlled terminal state.","language":"","framework":"Document database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B79-R03","D16-B94-R02"],"prerequisites":["Document versioning","Migration safety"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Document database migration principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B95-R01","batch_id":"D16-B95","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Key-Value Databases","concept":"Hot key","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What is a hot key in a key-value database?","answer":"A hot key is a key that receives disproportionately high traffic, potentially creating a bottleneck in a partition, shard, cache node, or storage path.","explanation":"Key-value systems often distribute workload based on keys, so skewed access can concentrate load even when total traffic is manageable.","principle":"Workload distribution matters as much as total workload volume.","rule":"Identify and mitigate disproportionately accessed keys when the storage architecture makes them a bottleneck.","correct_example":"Partition or cache high-frequency aggregate data using a design that distributes requests where the underlying system supports such distribution.","invalid_example":"Assume horizontal scaling automatically eliminates a single-key hotspot.","problem":"A single logical key concentrates requests on one resource.","failure_mode":"Latency spikes or throttling despite available aggregate capacity.","cause":"Access distribution was highly skewed.","solution":"Redesign access patterns, shard logical state where semantically valid, cache appropriately, or use precomputed partitions.","alternative":"Use local caching when freshness and invalidation requirements permit it.","tradeoff":"Distribution can improve throughput but complicate consistency and aggregation.","constraint":"A single logical value may not be safely split without changing semantics.","edge_case":"One key may become hot only during incidents or traffic spikes.","security_implication":"Hot keys can become denial-of-service targets.","performance_implication":"Hotspot mitigation can materially improve tail latency and throughput.","testing_guidance":"Generate skewed access distributions rather than uniform synthetic traffic only.","verification_guidance":"Inspect per-key or per-partition metrics where available.","language":"","framework":"Key-value database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Key-value partitioning","Workload analysis"],"related_records":["D16-B95-R02","D16-B95-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official key-value database scaling documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B95-R02","batch_id":"D16-B95","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Key-Value Databases","concept":"Atomic operation selection","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should key-value updates use database-supported atomic operations when available?","answer":"Atomic database operations can prevent lost updates between concurrent readers and writers without requiring the application to reproduce synchronization logic incorrectly.","explanation":"Read-modify-write sequences performed as separate operations can race under concurrency.","principle":"Concurrency-sensitive state transitions should use the strongest appropriate atomic primitive provided by the storage system.","rule":"Prefer native atomic increments, compare-and-set, conditional writes, or transactions when the operation semantics require them.","correct_example":"Use an atomic conditional update when changing a counter or versioned value under concurrent writers.","invalid_example":"Read a value, increment it locally, and write it back as separate operations while assuming no concurrent writer exists.","problem":"Concurrent updates overwrite one another.","failure_mode":"Lost updates or inconsistent state.","cause":"The read-modify-write sequence was not atomic.","solution":"Use an atomic storage operation or explicit transaction mechanism.","alternative":"Application-level distributed locking when storage primitives cannot express the required invariant.","tradeoff":"Native atomic operations are usually simpler but may have narrower semantics than full transactions.","constraint":"Atomicity scope and supported operations vary by key-value engine.","edge_case":"A logically multi-key invariant may exceed the atomic scope of a single-key operation.","security_implication":"Atomic authorization-related state transitions must not be bypassed by weaker client-side logic.","performance_implication":"Native atomic operations can avoid extra round trips.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent writers and verify invariant preservation.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the storage operation semantics and execute contention tests.","language":"","framework":"Key-value database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B32-R02"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency","Atomic operations"],"related_records":["D16-B95-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official key-value atomic-operation documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B95-R03","batch_id":"D16-B95","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Key-Value Databases","concept":"Value-size constraint","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must value size be considered in key-value schema design?","answer":"Large values can increase memory usage, network transfer, serialization cost, replication cost, and latency, and many key-value systems impose explicit value-size limits.","explanation":"A key-value model does not make arbitrarily large blobs operationally cheap.","principle":"Value size is part of the access-pattern and capacity model.","rule":"Define expected value size and growth bounds before choosing a key-value representation.","correct_example":"Store a small frequently accessed state object directly while placing large immutable payloads in a storage system designed for large objects.","invalid_example":"Store arbitrarily growing binary or historical datasets as one key-value value.","problem":"Large values become expensive or exceed engine limits.","failure_mode":"Write failures, high latency, memory pressure, or inefficient replication.","cause":"Value growth was not modeled.","solution":"Bound values and separate large payloads when appropriate.","alternative":"Chunking where the storage engine and application semantics support it safely.","tradeoff":"Externalizing large payloads adds coordination but improves storage efficiency.","constraint":"Maximum value size is implementation-specific.","edge_case":"A value may remain small initially but grow over time.","security_implication":"Large sensitive values increase the amount of data exposed by a single read.","performance_implication":"Large values amplify network and serialization costs.","testing_guidance":"Test near-limit and worst-case value sizes.","verification_guidance":"Check engine-specific size limits and measure actual read/write behavior.","language":"","framework":"Key-value database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Key-value modeling","Capacity planning"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official key-value database limits and performance documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B96-R01","batch_id":"D16-B96","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Graph Databases","concept":"Graph indexing","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why are indexes still important in graph databases?","answer":"Indexes can accelerate locating starting nodes or relationships based on selective properties before traversal begins, reducing the amount of graph that must be searched.","explanation":"Graph traversal and graph indexing solve different problems. An index often finds the traversal starting point while graph topology determines subsequent expansion cost.","principle":"Efficient graph queries combine selective entry-point lookup with controlled traversal.","rule":"Index properties used frequently to locate traversal starting points and verify that query plans use those indexes.","correct_example":"Index a unique account identifier used to locate the starting account before traversing relationships.","invalid_example":"Assume graph traversal automatically makes property lookup efficient without any supporting index.","problem":"The database scans excessive graph elements before traversal.","failure_mode":"High query latency.","cause":"Starting-point lookup was not optimized.","solution":"Add appropriate indexes and verify traversal plans.","alternative":"Use identifiers or storage-native lookup mechanisms when they already provide efficient access.","tradeoff":"Indexes consume storage and maintenance resources but can sharply reduce lookup cost.","constraint":"Supported index types and optimizer behavior differ by graph engine.","edge_case":"An index may be selective for current data but become ineffective as cardinality distributions change.","security_implication":"Indexes can contain sensitive property values and require appropriate protection.","performance_implication":"Good starting-point indexes reduce unnecessary graph expansion.","testing_guidance":"Compare indexed and non-indexed traversal plans under realistic graph sizes.","verification_guidance":"Inspect the graph query plan for actual index use.","language":"","framework":"Graph database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B88-R01"],"prerequisites":["Graph indexes","Traversal"],"related_records":["D16-B96-R02","D16-B96-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official graph database indexing documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B96-R02","batch_id":"D16-B96","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Graph Databases","concept":"Bounded graph traversal","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should graph traversals have explicit bounds when unrestricted traversal is unnecessary?","answer":"Traversal bounds limit the amount of graph the database must explore and reduce the risk of accidental explosive result sets.","explanation":"Variable-depth traversals can expand exponentially or otherwise become extremely large depending on graph topology.","principle":"Traversal depth and result size should be constrained according to the business question.","rule":"Specify maximum depth, relationship types, predicates, or result limits whenever unrestricted traversal is not semantically required.","correct_example":"Traverse dependency relationships up to a documented maximum depth when computing affected components.","invalid_example":"Traverse all reachable nodes indefinitely from a user-controlled starting node.","problem":"A legitimate query can expand into most of the graph.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion or extreme latency.","cause":"Traversal scope was unbounded.","solution":"Apply explicit depth and predicate constraints.","alternative":"Precompute bounded relationship summaries for repeated analytical queries.","tradeoff":"Bounds improve safety and performance but may omit relationships beyond the defined analytical horizon.","constraint":"Some business questions genuinely require unbounded reachability and need specialized strategies.","edge_case":"Cycles can cause repeated traversal unless the engine or query semantics prevent revisiting.","security_implication":"Unbounded traversal can become a data-exfiltration or denial-of-service vector.","performance_implication":"Bounds directly constrain worst-case traversal work.","testing_guidance":"Test cycles, dense graphs, supernodes, and maliciously chosen start nodes.","verification_guidance":"Confirm query plans and actual result-size limits.","language":"","framework":"Graph database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B88-R03"],"prerequisites":["Graph traversal","Resource controls"],"related_records":["D16-B96-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official graph query and traversal documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B96-R03","batch_id":"D16-B96","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Graph Databases","concept":"Graph query result limiting","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should graph query result size be bounded when user-controlled input affects traversal?","answer":"Result limits reduce accidental or malicious requests that attempt to materialize an excessively large graph region.","explanation":"Depth limits alone may not control result size in dense graphs. Query authorization, predicates, depth, and result limits should be considered together.","principle":"Resource boundaries should be enforced at the query interface, not inferred from expected user behavior.","rule":"Apply appropriate result-size, traversal, timeout, and authorization controls to externally influenced graph queries.","correct_example":"Restrict both traversal depth and maximum returned relationships for an interactive graph exploration endpoint.","invalid_example":"Allow clients to choose arbitrary traversal depth and unlimited result size.","problem":"A single request can consume disproportionate database resources.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion or service degradation.","cause":"Query resource bounds were left to client behavior.","solution":"Enforce server-side query limits and authorization.","alternative":"Use asynchronous bounded jobs for large analytical traversals.","tradeoff":"Limits may prevent legitimate large queries, requiring a separate controlled analytical path.","constraint":"Exact query controls depend on graph engine capabilities.","edge_case":"Privileged analysts may legitimately need larger limits than ordinary users.","security_implication":"Server-side limits reduce abuse and accidental graph-wide disclosure.","performance_implication":"Limits improve predictable tail latency and resource utilization.","testing_guidance":"Test maximum depth, maximum results, timeout, and unauthorized traversal cases.","verification_guidance":"Verify limits cannot be bypassed through alternate query syntax or endpoints.","language":"","framework":"Graph database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B96-R02"],"prerequisites":["Graph security","Resource governance"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Graph database security and query-governance principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B97-R01","batch_id":"D16-B97","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Sensitive-data minimization","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why should databases minimize unnecessary storage of sensitive data?","answer":"Reducing collection and retention of sensitive data reduces the impact and operational burden of compromise, unauthorized access, backups, exports, and logging.","explanation":"Security is improved when data that does not need to exist is not persistently stored.","principle":"Data minimization is a database security and lifecycle-design control.","rule":"Store only the sensitive data required for documented functionality, legal obligations, and operational needs.","correct_example":"Store a reference or derived token rather than retaining an unnecessary copy of sensitive source data.","invalid_example":"Persist every input field indefinitely because storage is inexpensive.","problem":"Unnecessary sensitive data accumulates across primary databases, replicas, backups, and exports.","failure_mode":"Expanded breach and retention exposure.","cause":"Collection and retention requirements were not defined.","solution":"Classify data, minimize collection, define retention, and remove unnecessary fields.","alternative":"Tokenization or controlled external storage when the original value is operationally required elsewhere.","tradeoff":"Minimization can require additional integration or data-retrieval mechanisms.","constraint":"Retention requirements may require specific records to remain available.","edge_case":"Derived fields can themselves become sensitive even when source values are removed.","security_implication":"Every additional copy expands the attack and governance surface.","performance_implication":"Smaller records can reduce storage, indexing, backup, and transfer costs.","testing_guidance":"Inventory sensitive fields and verify retention/deletion behavior.","verification_guidance":"Compare actual stored fields against documented data requirements.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Security design"],"related_records":["D16-B97-R02","D16-B97-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Data-protection and secure-database design principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B97-R02","batch_id":"D16-B97","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Field-level encryption tradeoff","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"When might field-level encryption be appropriate?","answer":"Field-level encryption can be appropriate when particularly sensitive values require protection beyond database-wide storage encryption or when different data-access boundaries must be enforced.","explanation":"Field encryption changes queryability, indexing, key management, rotation, and application complexity.","principle":"Use fine-grained encryption when the threat model requires it and the resulting operational constraints are acceptable.","rule":"Define searchable, sortable, rotation, recovery, and key-management requirements before encrypting individual fields.","correct_example":"Encrypt a highly sensitive field that does not need ordinary plaintext filtering and protect its encryption key separately from the database.","invalid_example":"Encrypt every field indiscriminately without considering query, indexing, rotation, and recovery requirements.","problem":"Required queries or migrations become impossible or unreliable.","failure_mode":"Broken functionality or insecure key-handling workarounds.","cause":"Cryptographic controls were selected without workload analysis.","solution":"Classify fields and select encryption granularity according to threat model and access requirements.","alternative":"Database-level encryption at rest when the primary threat is storage compromise.","tradeoff":"Field encryption improves isolation but increases application and key-management complexity.","constraint":"Encrypted data may not support ordinary equality/range indexes without specialized techniques.","edge_case":"Key rotation can require re-encryption of large datasets.","security_implication":"Security depends on key isolation, rotation, access control, and cryptographic implementation.","performance_implication":"Encryption and decryption add CPU and potentially storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test encryption, decryption, key rotation, backup recovery, and failure handling.","verification_guidance":"Verify that keys are not stored with the protected ciphertext in an equivalent trust boundary.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Cryptography","Key management","Data classification"],"related_records":["D16-B90-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Cryptographic security guidance and official database encryption documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B97-R03","batch_id":"D16-B97","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Backup data security","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must database backups receive security controls equivalent to production data?","answer":"Backups can contain complete or historical copies of sensitive database contents and therefore can become an alternative path to the same data.","explanation":"A secure primary database does not protect an unencrypted or broadly accessible backup.","principle":"Copies of sensitive data inherit appropriate confidentiality and integrity requirements.","rule":"Protect backups with access control, encryption, retention, monitoring, and recovery procedures appropriate to their data classification.","correct_example":"Encrypt database backups, restrict backup access, and test controlled restoration.","invalid_example":"Store production database dumps in a broadly accessible location because the live database itself is protected.","problem":"Attackers bypass production controls through backup storage.","failure_mode":"Large-scale data disclosure or destructive backup manipulation.","cause":"Backup copies were excluded from the security boundary.","solution":"Treat backups as security-sensitive database assets.","alternative":"Use managed backup systems with documented security controls and independent access policies.","tradeoff":"Strong backup protection adds key management and operational complexity.","constraint":"Backup mechanisms differ by database and deployment environment.","edge_case":"Temporary exports and developer copies can become forgotten backup-like datasets.","security_implication":"Backup compromise can expose historical records no longer present in production.","performance_implication":"Encryption and backup processing consume compute and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Test backup encryption, access controls, restoration, and retention deletion.","verification_guidance":"Inspect actual backup artifacts and access permissions, not only backup policy documents.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B90-R01"],"prerequisites":["Backup security","Data classification"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official database backup and security documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B98-R01","batch_id":"D16-B98","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Selection","concept":"Relational versus document model","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should relational and document database models be compared?","answer":"Relational models emphasize explicit relations, constraints, joins, and structured transactional semantics, while document models emphasize aggregate-oriented storage, flexible structures, and access patterns centered on documents.","explanation":"The correct choice depends on workload, consistency requirements, relationship complexity, schema evolution, operational ecosystem, and access patterns.","principle":"Database-model selection should follow workload and correctness requirements rather than technology popularity.","rule":"Evaluate data relationships, transaction boundaries, query patterns, consistency needs, and operational constraints before selecting the model.","correct_example":"Prefer a relational model when many entities have complex relationships and strong cross-entity constraints are central.","invalid_example":"Choose a document database solely because the application uses JSON objects in its API.","problem":"Storage model diverges from workload and integrity requirements.","failure_mode":"Excessive application-side joins or integrity complexity.","cause":"API representation was mistaken for persistence model.","solution":"Model actual persistence relationships and access patterns.","alternative":"Use multiple specialized stores only when the added operational complexity is justified.","tradeoff":"Relational systems provide strong structural constraints; document systems can simplify aggregate-oriented evolution.","constraint":"Capabilities vary substantially between products in each category.","edge_case":"A hybrid architecture may require consistent ownership of authoritative data.","security_implication":"Authorization boundaries must remain explicit regardless of database model.","performance_implication":"Performance depends on workload shape, indexing, data distribution, and implementation rather than database category alone.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark representative workloads rather than synthetic single-operation tests.","verification_guidance":"Validate the chosen model against functional, consistency, security, and operational requirements.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Database model selection","Workload analysis"],"related_records":["D16-B98-R02","D16-B98-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database data-modeling principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B98-R02","batch_id":"D16-B98","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Selection","concept":"Key-value versus relational model","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can a key-value database be preferable to a relational database?","answer":"A key-value database can be appropriate when the dominant access pattern is direct key-based retrieval or atomic key-oriented state manipulation and relational query capabilities are not central requirements.","explanation":"Key-value systems can simplify high-throughput direct access but generally provide a narrower query model than relational systems.","principle":"Use the simplest data model that fully satisfies required correctness and access patterns.","rule":"Select key-value storage when direct-key access and its consistency/atomicity capabilities cover the workload without requiring complex relational queries.","correct_example":"Store short-lived session state accessed primarily by a deterministic session key.","invalid_example":"Store highly relational financial data in a key-value store solely because point reads are fast.","problem":"Application code becomes responsible for relationships and integrity that the storage model does not naturally enforce.","failure_mode":"Data inconsistency and increasingly complex application logic.","cause":"Storage model did not match domain relationships.","solution":"Use relational storage or explicitly introduce another authoritative relational component.","alternative":"Use a hybrid architecture with key-value caching around an authoritative relational database.","tradeoff":"Key-value simplicity can provide efficient direct access but shifts more integrity responsibility to application design.","constraint":"Query and transaction capabilities vary among key-value products.","edge_case":"A workload can evolve from simple key lookup to complex querying, requiring model migration.","security_implication":"Authorization scope must be represented and enforced independently of key lookup.","performance_implication":"Direct lookup can be efficient, but poor key distribution can create hotspots.","testing_guidance":"Test both expected direct access and future query requirements.","verification_guidance":"Map every required query and invariant to an explicitly supported storage capability.","language":"","framework":"Key-value database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Key-value modeling","Relational modeling"],"related_records":["D16-B95-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database architecture and data-modeling principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B98-R03","batch_id":"D16-B98","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Selection","concept":"Graph versus relational model","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How should graph and relational models be compared for connected data?","answer":"Graph models make relationship traversal a first-class operation, while relational models represent relationships through tables and can support joins and recursive queries depending on the engine.","explanation":"Graph-shaped data alone does not prove that a graph database is necessary. Query frequency, traversal complexity, relationship algorithms, constraints, transactions, and operational ecosystem matter.","principle":"Select the model according to dominant operations and required guarantees.","rule":"Evaluate representative connected queries rather than selecting a graph database merely because entities have relationships.","correct_example":"Evaluate a graph model when variable-depth traversal and relationship-centric queries dominate the workload.","invalid_example":"Migrate every relational schema containing foreign keys to a graph database without workload analysis.","problem":"Operational complexity increases without solving the actual bottleneck.","failure_mode":"Technology mismatch.","cause":"Presence of relationships was treated as sufficient evidence for graph storage.","solution":"Benchmark representative relational and graph formulations against actual requirements.","alternative":"Retain relational storage when joins and constraints adequately satisfy the workload.","tradeoff":"Graph models can simplify traversal-centric reasoning while relational models provide mature tabular constraints and tooling.","constraint":"Product capabilities and query languages vary across graph databases.","edge_case":"Some systems support graph capabilities on top of relational storage, blurring category boundaries.","security_implication":"Graph traversal authorization can be more complex because relationships themselves can reveal sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Traversal topology rather than raw row count becomes a major performance variable.","testing_guidance":"Test representative multi-hop queries, relationship filters, updates, and worst-case graph topology.","verification_guidance":"Compare measured plans and operational requirements across candidate models.","language":"","framework":"Graph database","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Graph modeling","Relational modeling"],"related_records":["D16-B88-R01","D16-B96-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database architecture and graph-data modeling principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B99-R01","batch_id":"D16-B99","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Operational Schema Design","concept":"Database ownership boundary","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"Why should each database table, collection, key namespace, or graph domain have an explicit ownership boundary?","answer":"Explicit ownership identifies which component is responsible for schema changes, invariants, lifecycle, access control, and compatibility.","explanation":"Ambiguous ownership causes conflicting migrations, undocumented dependencies, and unauthorized direct writes.","principle":"A data structure without an accountable owner becomes difficult to evolve safely.","rule":"Define authoritative ownership and allowed access paths for every important persistent data domain.","correct_example":"Declare one service authoritative for customer identity while exposing controlled read or API interfaces to other components.","invalid_example":"Allow multiple independent services to modify the same tables directly without a shared schema contract.","problem":"Independent deployments change shared state incompatibly.","failure_mode":"Schema drift and cross-service corruption.","cause":"Data ownership was undefined.","solution":"Assign authoritative ownership and formalize access contracts.","alternative":"Shared database ownership with centralized governance when organizational architecture requires it.","tradeoff":"Ownership boundaries improve autonomy but may require APIs or synchronization mechanisms.","constraint":"Legacy systems may have unavoidable shared databases during transition.","edge_case":"Reporting and migration tools need carefully controlled exceptional access.","security_implication":"Ownership clarifies who may mutate sensitive data and who may administer it.","performance_implication":"Ownership boundaries can reduce uncontrolled query contention and workload interference.","testing_guidance":"Test unauthorized direct writes and schema changes from non-owner components.","verification_guidance":"Inspect actual credentials, grants, deployment pipelines, and migration ownership.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Database architecture","Access control"],"related_records":["D16-B89-R02","D16-B99-R02","D16-B99-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database architecture and service data-ownership principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B99-R02","batch_id":"D16-B99","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Operational Schema Design","concept":"Schema contract","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What should a persistent schema contract define?","answer":"A schema contract should define structure, types, required fields, constraints, compatibility expectations, ownership, migration rules, and the supported consumers or access interfaces.","explanation":"A schema is an operational interface when multiple components depend on its structure or semantics.","principle":"Persistent data structures should be treated as explicit contracts when they cross component boundaries.","rule":"Document compatibility guarantees before allowing independent consumers to depend on persistent structures.","correct_example":"Define which columns may be added, changed, or removed without breaking deployed consumers.","invalid_example":"Change a shared column's meaning because existing consumers are assumed to update simultaneously.","problem":"Independent consumers interpret the same stored value differently.","failure_mode":"Silent semantic corruption.","cause":"Schema compatibility was treated as only a syntactic concern.","solution":"Define semantic and structural compatibility rules.","alternative":"Expose a stable API/read model rather than sharing physical tables directly.","tradeoff":"Contracts require governance but reduce hidden coupling.","constraint":"Some internal schemas can remain private and need less compatibility overhead.","edge_case":"A column can remain type-compatible while its meaning changes incompatibly.","security_implication":"Schema contracts should include access classification and sensitive-field handling.","performance_implication":"Compatibility requirements can constrain indexing and storage evolution.","testing_guidance":"Run contract and migration tests against old and new consumers.","verification_guidance":"Validate both structural compatibility and semantic interpretation.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Schema evolution","Service contracts"],"related_records":["D16-B79-R03","D16-B99-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database schema-evolution and compatibility principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B99-R03","batch_id":"D16-B99","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Operational Schema Design","concept":"Online schema-change risk","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can schema changes become availability risks even when logically correct?","answer":"DDL and schema-management operations can acquire locks, rebuild indexes, rewrite data, consume resources, or interact with concurrent transactions in engine-specific ways.","explanation":"Schema correctness does not imply operational safety under production workload.","principle":"Database migrations must be evaluated as live workload operations, not only as static schema transformations.","rule":"Assess lock behavior, rewrite behavior, duration, resource consumption, rollback characteristics, and concurrent-traffic impact before production schema changes.","correct_example":"Test a large index or column migration against production-like data volume and concurrency before deployment.","invalid_example":"Run a potentially table-rewriting schema operation in peak traffic solely because it succeeds instantly on a small development database.","problem":"A correct migration causes unacceptable production contention or outage.","failure_mode":"Lock contention, latency spike, failed deployment, or availability degradation.","cause":"Migration operational behavior was not tested at realistic scale.","solution":"Use staged migrations, online mechanisms where supported, controlled maintenance windows, and production-scale rehearsal.","alternative":"Shadow or asynchronous migration techniques when supported by the architecture.","tradeoff":"Safer migration strategies increase planning and implementation complexity.","constraint":"Lock and rewrite behavior is highly database- and version-specific.","edge_case":"A migration that was safe on one version can behave differently after an engine upgrade.","security_implication":"Emergency migration privileges should be tightly controlled and audited.","performance_implication":"Schema operations can compete directly with application workload for CPU, I/O, and locks.","testing_guidance":"Test migrations with representative table size, indexes, concurrent writes, failures, and cancellation.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual lock and resource behavior on the target database/version before production rollout.","language":"SQL","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B80-R02","D16-B80-R03"],"prerequisites":["Migrations","Concurrency","Database operations"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Official database DDL and migration documentation"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B100-R01","batch_id":"D16-B100","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Design Synthesis","concept":"Workload-driven database design","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the central principle for selecting and designing database structures?","answer":"Database design should derive from domain invariants, access patterns, consistency requirements, lifecycle behavior, security boundaries, expected scale, and operational constraints rather than from isolated feature preferences.","explanation":"Relational, document, key-value, and graph models each expose different strengths and constraints. A sound design maps requirements to enforceable storage semantics and measurable workload behavior.","principle":"Database design is an evidence-driven mapping from requirements and workload to data model, constraints, access paths, lifecycle, and operational guarantees.","rule":"Do not finalize a database design until required queries, invariants, transaction boundaries, growth assumptions, security controls, migration paths, and failure behavior are explicitly represented.","correct_example":"Document the domain model, critical queries, consistency guarantees, constraints, indexes, migration strategy, security boundary, and expected workload before productionizing the schema.","invalid_example":"Select a database because it is popular or fast in general without mapping its capabilities to the actual workload and correctness requirements.","problem":"The storage design satisfies isolated use cases but fails under realistic workload or lifecycle conditions.","failure_mode":"Performance degradation, integrity failures, security exposure, or costly redesign.","cause":"Database design was optimized around technology preference instead of requirements and evidence.","solution":"Use workload-driven modeling, explicit constraints, measured query plans, controlled migrations, security-by-design, and continuous validation.","alternative":"Re-evaluate the model when requirements or workload materially change.","tradeoff":"Comprehensive upfront modeling requires more design effort but reduces hidden coupling and late-stage migration risk.","constraint":"No database model can optimize every workload simultaneously; trade-offs must remain explicit.","edge_case":"A system may require different models for different bounded workloads while preserving clear ownership and consistency boundaries.","security_implication":"Security properties must be designed into identity, authorization, storage, query construction, encryption, auditing, and lifecycle controls.","performance_implication":"Performance claims require workload-specific evidence; schema and index design should be validated using realistic data distributions and concurrency.","testing_guidance":"Maintain unit, integration, migration, negative, concurrency, security, performance, and regression tests for critical database behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify database guarantees from actual deployed configuration, schema metadata, query plans, migration results, and representative workload tests.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B98-R01","D16-B98-R02","D16-B98-R03","D16-B99-R01","D16-B99-R02","D16-B99-R03"],"prerequisites":["Complete D16 scope"],"related_records":["D16-B100-R02","D16-B100-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database architecture and design principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B100-R02","batch_id":"D16-B100","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Design Synthesis","concept":"Database design decision record","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What evidence should support a production database design decision?","answer":"A production database decision should be supported by requirements, workload characteristics, consistency and integrity requirements, security analysis, compatibility constraints, measured or explicitly bounded performance evidence, migration strategy, and operational considerations.","explanation":"A database technology or schema choice is an engineering decision whose correctness depends on context rather than generic claims.","principle":"Database decisions should be traceable from requirements to evidence and constraints.","rule":"Record the reason for the model, key assumptions, rejected alternatives, measurable acceptance criteria, and conditions that would trigger reevaluation.","correct_example":"Record expected query patterns, transaction requirements, data growth, index strategy, security boundary, migration approach, and benchmark methodology for a database decision.","invalid_example":"Record only that a database was selected because it is scalable or commonly used.","problem":"Future engineers cannot determine whether the original assumptions remain valid.","failure_mode":"Architecture drift and repeated design mistakes.","cause":"Decision rationale and assumptions were not preserved.","solution":"Create evidence-backed database architecture decision records.","alternative":"Use an architecture repository or schema registry when it preserves equivalent traceability.","tradeoff":"Decision records require maintenance but improve institutional memory.","constraint":"Evidence quality depends on realistic workload measurements and authoritative documentation.","edge_case":"Early-stage decisions may rely partly on assumptions that must be explicitly marked as unverified.","security_implication":"Security assumptions and trust boundaries must be included in the decision evidence.","performance_implication":"Measured performance should identify dataset size, workload, concurrency, hardware/environment, and methodology.","testing_guidance":"Attach reproducible tests and acceptance criteria to critical database decisions.","verification_guidance":"Periodically compare current production behavior with the assumptions recorded in the decision.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B100-R01"],"prerequisites":["Architecture decision records","Database evaluation"],"related_records":["D16-B100-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database architecture decision and engineering verification principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D16-B100-R03","batch_id":"D16-B100","dataset_id":"D16","topic":"Database Design","subtopic":"Database Design Synthesis","concept":"Database lifecycle completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must be covered before considering a database design complete?","answer":"A complete design addresses data modeling, integrity, indexes, queries, transactions, consistency, concurrency, migrations, security, performance, scalability, failure behavior, testing, observability, ownership, and version compatibility relevant to the system.","explanation":"A schema is only one part of database engineering. Production correctness depends on the full lifecycle from creation through change, operation, recovery, and eventual retirement.","principle":"Database completeness is lifecycle completeness, not merely schema completeness.","rule":"Mark a database design complete only after functional, operational, security, performance, migration, and verification requirements have explicit coverage or documented non-applicability.","correct_example":"A database design review contains traceable decisions for schema, constraints, access paths, transactions, migration, security, workload performance, operational ownership, and verification.","invalid_example":"Declare the database complete because all tables and collections have been created.","problem":"Critical operational requirements remain implicit or untested.","failure_mode":"Production defects appearing outside the original schema review.","cause":"Completion criteria focused only on structure.","solution":"Use a lifecycle-oriented database completeness checklist and evidence map.","alternative":"Use a formal architecture review process with database-specific acceptance criteria.","tradeoff":"Broader review requires more engineering effort but exposes hidden risks before deployment.","constraint":"Not every system requires every database capability; non-applicable areas should be explicitly marked rather than silently omitted.","edge_case":"A database may initially be complete for a bounded workload but require redesign as scale or requirements change.","security_implication":"Security must cover data, credentials, queries, backups, replicas, exports, logs, and administrative access.","performance_implication":"Performance completeness requires workload-specific evidence rather than generic benchmark claims.","testing_guidance":"Require representative functional, negative, concurrency, migration, security, and performance tests before production acceptance.","verification_guidance":"Map each critical requirement to concrete evidence and record unknown or unverified areas explicitly.","language":"","framework":"","version":"","version_range":"","dependencies":["D16-B100-R01","D16-B100-R02"],"prerequisites":["Complete database design lifecycle"],"related_records":[],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"Database engineering lifecycle principles"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B01-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Fundamentals","subtopic":"System Definition","concept":"Distributed system","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a distributed system?","answer":"A distributed system is a collection of independent computing components that communicate over a network and cooperate to provide behavior perceived as a unified system or service.","explanation":"The components execute independently and communicate through mechanisms that can delay, reorder, duplicate, or lose information. This separates distributed systems from single-process systems where coordination occurs through local execution state.","principle":"Distributed computation must reason explicitly about communication, independent execution, and incomplete knowledge.","rule":"Do not assume that state observed by one component is simultaneously known or identical at every other component.","correct_example":"Multiple service instances communicate over a network while jointly serving requests for a logical application.","invalid_example":"Treating all remote components as if they shared instantaneous memory and a perfectly synchronized clock.","problem":"Designs become incorrect when local observations are treated as global truth.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent decisions caused by incomplete or stale distributed state.","cause":"Independent execution and non-instantaneous communication.","solution":"Model communication delay, failures, concurrency, and state propagation explicitly.","alternative":"A centralized single-process design avoids many distributed coordination problems but may have different scalability and availability characteristics.","tradeoff":"Distribution can improve scalability, fault isolation, and geographic reach while introducing coordination and failure complexity.","constraint":"Network communication cannot generally be assumed instantaneous or perfectly reliable.","edge_case":"A component remains operational while all communication paths to another component fail.","security_implication":"Each communication boundary must be treated as a trust boundary unless authenticated and authorized otherwise.","performance_implication":"Network latency and serialization become part of end-to-end latency.","testing_guidance":"Test behavior under delayed, lost, duplicated, and reordered messages.","verification_guidance":"Verify that correctness arguments do not depend on impossible assumptions such as zero latency or universal shared state.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B01-R02","D17-B02-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B01-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Fundamentals","subtopic":"State","concept":"Distributed state","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is distributed state fundamentally harder than local state?","answer":"Distributed state is harder because no component has instantaneous, complete, and necessarily current knowledge of all other components.","explanation":"A node observes local state and messages received so far. Other nodes may have progressed, failed, or observed different events that have not yet propagated.","principle":"A local view is not automatically a global view.","rule":"Separate locally observed facts from globally established facts.","correct_example":"A service marks a remote node as suspected failed because heartbeats stopped, while recognizing that the remote node may still be running.","invalid_example":"Concluding that a remote node has definitely crashed solely because a message or heartbeat was not observed within an arbitrary interval.","problem":"False global conclusions based on local observations.","failure_mode":"Incorrect failover, duplicate execution, or conflicting decisions.","cause":"Incomplete information and timing uncertainty.","solution":"Use explicit failure semantics, coordination protocols, leases, acknowledgements, quorum rules, or other mechanisms appropriate to the required guarantee.","alternative":"Centralized coordination can reduce distributed uncertainty at the cost of introducing a coordination dependency.","tradeoff":"Stronger global knowledge usually requires additional communication, coordination, or availability constraints.","constraint":"Observational evidence must not be confused with certainty unless the protocol establishes that certainty.","edge_case":"A network partition makes two healthy groups unable to observe one another.","security_implication":"Stale or incomplete state can cause incorrect authorization or policy decisions if security state is distributed.","performance_implication":"Increasing coordination frequency can reduce uncertainty but increases network and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject stale observations and delayed state propagation.","verification_guidance":"Identify the protocol evidence required before treating a distributed state transition as authoritative.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B01-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B01-R01","D17-B04-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B01-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B01-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Fundamentals","subtopic":"Coordination","concept":"Coordination cost","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why does coordination create cost in distributed systems?","answer":"Coordination requires communication, waiting, state exchange, conflict resolution, or agreement among independently executing components.","explanation":"Coordination can add latency and reduce availability when participants are unreachable. Stronger coordination guarantees generally require more protocol work or restrictions on independent progress.","principle":"Distributed coordination is a correctness mechanism with measurable latency, availability, and resource costs.","rule":"Choose the weakest coordination guarantee that safely satisfies the requirement.","correct_example":"A system uses quorum coordination only for operations that require strong agreement while allowing independent reads where weaker consistency is acceptable.","invalid_example":"Adding synchronous cross-region coordination to every operation without establishing that the application requires the resulting guarantee.","problem":"Unnecessary coordination increases latency and failure coupling.","failure_mode":"Cascading delays or unavailable operations during network impairment.","cause":"Overly strong coordination requirements.","solution":"Classify operations by correctness requirement and apply appropriate coordination selectively.","alternative":"Eventual convergence can reduce coordination for workloads that tolerate temporary divergence.","tradeoff":"Less coordination can improve availability and latency while weakening immediate consistency guarantees.","constraint":"Relaxing coordination is safe only when the application invariant tolerates the resulting uncertainty.","edge_case":"A critical operation spans components in geographically distant regions.","security_implication":"Authorization or policy decisions requiring global agreement may need stronger coordination than ordinary reads.","performance_implication":"Coordination adds network round trips, serialization, contention, and waiting.","testing_guidance":"Measure behavior under normal and degraded inter-node latency.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every coordination mechanism corresponds to a stated correctness requirement.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B01-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B01-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B01-R01","D17-B08-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B01-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_SYSTEMS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B02-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Models","subtopic":"Execution Model","concept":"Independent process execution","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does independent execution mean in a distributed system?","answer":"Each process or node advances according to its own local execution and receives information about other components through communication rather than shared instantaneous execution state.","explanation":"Independent progress means components can execute at different speeds and may temporarily have different views of system state.","principle":"Correctness must tolerate independent progress and relative execution speed differences.","rule":"Never rely on accidental timing equality between distributed components.","correct_example":"Two services process requests at different rates while exchanging explicit messages.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a message sent immediately before another local operation will necessarily be processed first by a remote component.","problem":"Timing assumptions create race conditions.","failure_mode":"Order-dependent behavior that changes under load or network delay.","cause":"Implicit dependence on relative execution speed.","solution":"Make ordering or coordination requirements explicit in the protocol.","alternative":"A single-threaded centralized component provides stronger local ordering semantics.","tradeoff":"Centralization simplifies ordering but can create bottlenecks or failure concentration.","constraint":"Network communication does not preserve arbitrary application-level execution ordering unless the protocol establishes it.","edge_case":"One participant is paused while another continues processing.","security_implication":"Security decisions must not rely on a remote participant having already processed an earlier event unless acknowledged by protocol.","performance_implication":"Unequal processing speeds can create queues and backpressure.","testing_guidance":"Run participants at deliberately different processing speeds.","verification_guidance":"Check protocol correctness when any participant is arbitrarily delayed within the assumed model.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B01-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B03-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B01-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_EXECUTION_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B02-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Models","subtopic":"Failure Model","concept":"Crash failure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a crash failure?","answer":"A crash failure occurs when a process stops taking steps or becomes unavailable to perform its expected computation.","explanation":"Crash failure is a model of failure behavior; it does not imply that the process sends malicious or arbitrary messages before or after failure.","principle":"Failure models define what incorrect behavior a correctness argument must tolerate.","rule":"Do not treat a crash-failure assumption as protection against arbitrary or malicious behavior.","correct_example":"A server stops responding and performs no further protocol actions after crashing.","invalid_example":"Assuming a crash-failure protocol can automatically tolerate a compromised server that sends contradictory messages to different peers.","problem":"Using a weaker failure model than the real environment requires.","failure_mode":"Protocol safety failure under behavior outside its assumed model.","cause":"Failure-model mismatch.","solution":"State the failure model explicitly and use a protocol designed for the required model.","alternative":"Byzantine-fault-tolerant protocols address stronger classes of arbitrary behavior.","tradeoff":"Stronger failure tolerance generally increases protocol complexity and resource requirements.","constraint":"A correctness proof is valid only within its stated failure assumptions.","edge_case":"A process crashes after sending a message to only some recipients.","security_implication":"Compromise or malicious behavior requires security mechanisms beyond ordinary crash detection.","performance_implication":"Failure detection often requires heartbeats, timeouts, or repeated communication.","testing_guidance":"Terminate participants at protocol-critical points and observe recovery.","verification_guidance":"Record the assumed failure model in every distributed protocol design.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B01-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B01-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B02-R03","D17-B04-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B01-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B02-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Models","subtopic":"Failure Model","concept":"Byzantine failure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a Byzantine failure?","answer":"A Byzantine failure is a failure model in which a participant may behave arbitrarily, including sending conflicting or misleading information to different participants.","explanation":"Byzantine behavior is substantially stronger than crash failure because the faulty participant can continue participating while violating protocol expectations.","principle":"Protocol assumptions must match the strongest relevant failure behavior.","rule":"Do not claim Byzantine fault tolerance for a protocol that only handles crash or omission failures.","correct_example":"A protocol explicitly tolerates a bounded number of malicious or arbitrarily faulty participants under its stated assumptions.","invalid_example":"Calling a timeout-and-retry mechanism Byzantine fault tolerant merely because it detects unresponsive nodes.","problem":"Confusing availability failure handling with arbitrary-fault tolerance.","failure_mode":"Conflicting state or decisions caused by malicious protocol participation.","cause":"Failure model is stronger than protocol assumptions.","solution":"Use an explicitly Byzantine-resilient protocol and authenticated communication where required.","alternative":"Crash-fault-tolerant coordination is simpler when the environment genuinely excludes arbitrary faults.","tradeoff":"Byzantine tolerance increases communication, computation, and protocol complexity.","constraint":"Exact fault thresholds depend on the chosen consensus protocol and network/authentication assumptions.","edge_case":"A compromised node behaves correctly toward some peers and incorrectly toward others.","security_implication":"Byzantine behavior directly intersects with authentication, integrity, key management, and adversarial threat models.","performance_implication":"Additional validation, signatures, quorum communication, or rounds may increase overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject contradictory messages and invalid protocol states within the authorized test environment.","verification_guidance":"Verify the stated fault threshold and network assumptions against the actual protocol.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B02-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B10-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B02-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B03-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Communication","subtopic":"Network Semantics","concept":"Message delay","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why must distributed protocols account for message delay?","answer":"A message may take a nonzero and variable amount of time to reach its destination, so a sender cannot generally infer remote state from elapsed local time alone.","explanation":"Variable latency can make a healthy remote component appear unavailable and can cause responses to arrive after newer messages.","principle":"Elapsed time is an observation, not automatic proof of remote state.","rule":"Design timeout behavior around explicit protocol assumptions rather than treating every timeout as proof of failure.","correct_example":"A service marks a request attempt as timed out and uses a safe retry policy without assuming the original request was never processed.","invalid_example":"Retrying a non-idempotent operation solely because the response did not arrive before a fixed timeout.","problem":"Timeouts are mistaken for operation failure.","failure_mode":"Duplicate effects or incorrect failure handling.","cause":"Request completion and response delivery are separate events.","solution":"Use operation identifiers, idempotency mechanisms, acknowledgements, and explicit outcome reconciliation where necessary.","alternative":"Synchronous local execution can avoid network-delay uncertainty for operations contained within one process.","tradeoff":"Longer timeouts reduce premature suspicion but increase waiting; shorter timeouts improve responsiveness but can increase false suspicions and retries.","constraint":"Timeout values cannot by themselves prove remote failure in an asynchronous environment without stronger timing assumptions.","edge_case":"The operation completes remotely but the response is lost.","security_implication":"Timeout-triggered fallback actions must not bypass authorization or duplicate privileged operations.","performance_implication":"Latency variance directly affects tail latency and timeout/retry load.","testing_guidance":"Inject latency, jitter, delayed responses, and response loss.","verification_guidance":"Verify that timeout paths remain correct when the original operation may already have succeeded.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B02-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B02-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R02","D17-B05-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B02-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_COMMUNICATION_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B03-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Communication","subtopic":"Network Semantics","concept":"Message loss","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is the consequence of message loss?","answer":"Message loss can prevent a recipient from observing an event, acknowledgement, request, or state update that another component believes it transmitted.","explanation":"Transmission and observation are distinct events. A sender may need protocol-level acknowledgements or retransmission to establish delivery-related guarantees.","principle":"Sending a message does not automatically establish that its intended recipient processed it.","rule":"Define delivery, processing, and acknowledgement semantics separately.","correct_example":"A request protocol uses a request identifier and acknowledgement so the sender can reconcile uncertain completion.","invalid_example":"Assuming absence of an acknowledgement means the recipient definitely did not process the request.","problem":"Uncertain message outcome causes duplicate or missing effects.","failure_mode":"At-least-once retries duplicate operations or lost events produce state divergence.","cause":"Communication outcome is uncertain.","solution":"Use explicit delivery semantics, deduplication, durable messaging, or idempotent operations as appropriate.","alternative":"Best-effort messaging may be acceptable for non-critical telemetry where loss is explicitly tolerated.","tradeoff":"Stronger delivery guarantees require additional storage, acknowledgements, retransmission, or coordination.","constraint":"Exactly-once application effects generally require more than a network transport claim.","edge_case":"The message is processed but the acknowledgement is lost.","security_implication":"Security events must not silently disappear when audit completeness is required.","performance_implication":"Retransmission and acknowledgement traffic increase bandwidth and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Drop messages and acknowledgements independently.","verification_guidance":"Trace message lifecycle from send through receipt, processing, persistence, and acknowledgement.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R03","D17-B07-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_COMMUNICATION_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B03-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Communication","subtopic":"Network Semantics","concept":"Message reordering and duplication","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must distributed systems handle reordered or duplicated messages?","answer":"Network and messaging mechanisms may deliver messages in an order different from application creation order or may cause retransmitted messages to be observed more than once.","explanation":"Correctness should derive required ordering and uniqueness from protocol mechanisms rather than assuming transport behavior provides stronger guarantees than documented.","principle":"Ordering and deduplication are application or protocol properties unless explicitly guaranteed by the communication layer.","rule":"Assign stable message or operation identifiers when duplicate detection is required.","correct_example":"A consumer records processed event identifiers so retransmitted events do not create duplicate effects.","invalid_example":"Applying every received event blindly when the producer may retry delivery.","problem":"Duplicate or out-of-order events corrupt state.","failure_mode":"Repeated side effects or invalid state transitions.","cause":"Missing ordering or deduplication semantics.","solution":"Use sequence numbers, logical clocks, idempotency keys, consumer offsets, or protocol-specific ordering mechanisms.","alternative":"A communication channel with documented ordering guarantees can simplify some cases but does not automatically solve cross-channel ordering.","tradeoff":"Stronger ordering and deduplication require metadata, storage, coordination, or reduced concurrency.","constraint":"Ordering guarantees are scoped to the mechanism that establishes them.","edge_case":"Messages from different producers have no shared sequence.","security_implication":"Replay protection may be required when duplicated messages could repeat privileged actions.","performance_implication":"Deduplication state consumes memory or durable storage.","testing_guidance":"Deliver duplicates and permutations of valid event sequences.","verification_guidance":"Verify state-machine invariants under all permitted delivery orders.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B10-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_MESSAGING_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B04-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Failures","subtopic":"Partial Failure","concept":"Partial failure","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is partial failure?","answer":"Partial failure occurs when some components or communication paths fail while other components continue operating.","explanation":"A distributed system may remain partially functional even when part of its dependency graph is unavailable or unreachable.","principle":"Failure can be local to part of the system rather than a single global up-or-down state.","rule":"Design degraded behavior explicitly for subsets of unavailable components.","correct_example":"One service instance fails while other instances continue serving requests.","invalid_example":"Treating the entire application as either completely healthy or completely failed.","problem":"Partial failure creates inconsistent local observations and degraded capabilities.","failure_mode":"Cascading failure or incorrect recovery decisions.","cause":"Independent components fail independently.","solution":"Use isolation, timeouts, health detection, graceful degradation, and recovery protocols.","alternative":"A centralized architecture can reduce the number of independent failure domains but may create a larger common failure domain.","tradeoff":"Failure isolation can preserve availability but may produce temporarily incomplete functionality.","constraint":"A component being reachable does not prove every dependency it requires is healthy.","edge_case":"A dependency is reachable but returns stale, incomplete, or degraded data.","security_implication":"Fail-open fallback behavior can unintentionally bypass security controls.","performance_implication":"Retries and failover can multiply load during partial failure.","testing_guidance":"Disable individual nodes, links, and dependencies independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify that failure of each declared dependency produces the documented degraded state.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B03-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B03-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B04-R02","D17-B06-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B03-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B04-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Failures","subtopic":"Failure Detection","concept":"Failure suspicion","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why is failure detection often based on suspicion rather than certainty?","answer":"In distributed environments, a missing response can result from process failure, network delay, congestion, partition, or other causes, so a detector may only establish that a component is suspected to be unavailable.","explanation":"The distinction between suspected failure and proven failure is important when decisions can cause irreversible effects.","principle":"Failure detectors produce observations with assumptions and uncertainty, not universal certainty.","rule":"Treat suspicion according to the guarantees and false-positive behavior of the failure-detection mechanism.","correct_example":"A node is suspected after missed heartbeats and the system initiates a controlled failover procedure.","invalid_example":"Destroying or permanently deleting a remote state merely because a heartbeat was missed.","problem":"False failure detection triggers destructive actions.","failure_mode":"Split-brain, duplicate ownership, or unnecessary failover.","cause":"Network delay or partition mistaken for crash.","solution":"Use leases, fencing, quorum mechanisms, or other safeguards where false positives could cause conflicting ownership.","alternative":"Longer detection windows reduce false suspicion at the cost of slower reaction.","tradeoff":"Faster detection improves responsiveness but can increase false positives.","constraint":"The detector's semantics must be understood before using it for correctness-critical decisions.","edge_case":"The monitored process is healthy but temporarily overloaded.","security_implication":"Attackers may induce connectivity failures to trigger failover or degraded security behavior.","performance_implication":"Aggressive heartbeats and probes consume network and CPU resources.","testing_guidance":"Inject packet loss, delay, and process pauses separately.","verification_guidance":"Verify that suspected-failure transitions cannot violate ownership or safety invariants.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B04-R03","D17-B05-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_DETECTION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B04-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Failures","subtopic":"Failure Domains","concept":"Failure domain","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What is a failure domain?","answer":"A failure domain is a set of components that can be affected together by a common failure cause.","explanation":"Failure domains can be defined by host, rack, zone, region, network segment, dependency, control plane, or other shared infrastructure.","principle":"Independent redundancy requires independence from relevant common failure causes, not merely multiple copies.","rule":"Place redundant components across failure domains appropriate to the threat and availability requirement.","correct_example":"Replicas are distributed across independent infrastructure zones for protection against a zone-level outage.","invalid_example":"Calling two replicas independent when both depend on the same failed network switch or control plane.","problem":"Correlated failures defeat apparent redundancy.","failure_mode":"Multiple replicas fail simultaneously despite nominal replication.","cause":"Shared failure dependency.","solution":"Map dependency and infrastructure topology before selecting replica placement.","alternative":"Co-located replicas may reduce latency but provide weaker fault isolation.","tradeoff":"Greater failure-domain separation can increase latency and operational complexity.","constraint":"True independence depends on the actual infrastructure and failure causes.","edge_case":"Two regions share a common identity or DNS control plane.","security_implication":"A common administrative domain may become a shared security failure domain.","performance_implication":"Cross-domain replication can increase latency and bandwidth consumption.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled failure-domain isolation tests.","verification_guidance":"Document and verify the independence assumptions behind redundancy claims.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B07-R01","D17-B06-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_AVAILABILITY_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B05-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Timing","subtopic":"Synchrony","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous assumptions","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous distributed-system assumptions?","answer":"A synchronous model assumes bounded timing properties such as message delivery or process execution within known limits, while an asynchronous model does not provide such known global timing bounds.","explanation":"The distinction affects what can be inferred from elapsed time and which coordination guarantees are achievable under the model.","principle":"Timing assumptions are part of the correctness model, not merely performance configuration.","rule":"Do not use a timeout as a correctness proof unless the system model establishes a suitable timing bound.","correct_example":"A protocol explicitly states bounded-delay assumptions and derives its timeout behavior from those assumptions.","invalid_example":"Assuming a fixed timeout proves remote failure in a model with unbounded message delay.","problem":"Incorrect timing assumptions invalidate distributed reasoning.","failure_mode":"False failure detection or unsafe coordination.","cause":"Model mismatch.","solution":"State timing assumptions explicitly and select algorithms compatible with them.","alternative":"Partial synchrony models can represent systems where useful bounds eventually hold without assuming they always hold.","tradeoff":"Stronger timing assumptions can simplify algorithms but reduce applicability under unpredictable environments.","constraint":"Real systems must be evaluated against actual timing behavior and the guarantees of the chosen model.","edge_case":"The system operates normally for long periods but experiences temporary extreme latency.","security_implication":"Attackers may exploit timing assumptions by deliberately inducing delay.","performance_implication":"Timing assumptions influence timeout tuning and tail-latency behavior.","testing_guidance":"Test beyond normal latency and under sustained delay.","verification_guidance":"Check every timing-dependent proof against the stated model.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B05-R02","D17-B05-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_TIMING_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B05-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Timing","subtopic":"Synchrony","concept":"Partial synchrony","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is partial synchrony?","answer":"Partial synchrony is a distributed-system model in which timing bounds may not always be known or hold immediately, but bounded behavior can eventually hold under the model's assumptions.","explanation":"Partial synchrony is useful for reasoning about systems where temporary periods of unpredictable delay occur before the system reaches a sufficiently stable timing regime.","principle":"A protocol can rely on eventual timing properties without assuming permanent immediate timing bounds.","rule":"State precisely which eventual timing assumptions a protocol requires.","correct_example":"A consensus protocol uses increasing timeouts and can eventually make progress once communication becomes sufficiently timely.","invalid_example":"Assuming partial synchrony means messages always arrive within the configured timeout.","problem":"Confusing eventual bounds with permanent bounds.","failure_mode":"Premature failure decisions or incorrect protocol reasoning.","cause":"Timing-model misunderstanding.","solution":"Separate safety properties from liveness assumptions and document the timing model.","alternative":"A fully asynchronous model makes weaker timing assumptions but can impose stronger limitations on deterministic progress.","tradeoff":"Partial synchrony can enable practical progress while preserving robustness to temporary instability.","constraint":"Exact guarantees depend on the specific protocol and partial-synchrony definition used.","edge_case":"The system remains indefinitely unstable, preventing liveness even though safety remains preserved.","security_implication":"Adversarial delay can intentionally keep the system outside its favorable timing regime.","performance_implication":"Adaptive timeouts may trade faster recovery for fewer false suspicions.","testing_guidance":"Test both stable and unstable timing periods.","verification_guidance":"Verify which properties remain safe when timing assumptions temporarily fail.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B05-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B05-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B10-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B05-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_TIMING_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B05-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Timing","subtopic":"Timeouts","concept":"Adaptive timeout","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can adaptive timeouts be preferable to one fixed timeout?","answer":"Adaptive timeouts can account for observed latency and changing system conditions while maintaining bounded operational behavior.","explanation":"A single fixed timeout may be too aggressive during normal latency spikes or unnecessarily slow under healthy low-latency conditions.","principle":"Timeouts should reflect the protocol's failure-detection and workload requirements rather than arbitrary constants.","rule":"Tune timeouts using measured latency distributions and explicit safety margins, not only average latency.","correct_example":"A service derives timeout thresholds from observed tail latency while enforcing an upper operational bound.","invalid_example":"Setting every distributed timeout to the average observed request latency.","problem":"Poor timeout selection causes either premature retries or excessive waiting.","failure_mode":"Retry storms, false failure detection, or unnecessarily high latency.","cause":"Ignoring latency variance and tail behavior.","solution":"Use measured distributions, bounded retry policies, and load-aware timeout strategies.","alternative":"Fixed conservative timeouts may be simpler where workload and latency are stable.","tradeoff":"Adaptive policies can improve responsiveness but add tuning and state complexity.","constraint":"Timeout adaptation must not violate protocol safety requirements.","edge_case":"Latency distribution changes sharply during an outage.","security_implication":"Attackers can induce latency to manipulate adaptive mechanisms if inputs are not robustly handled.","performance_implication":"Better timeout selection can reduce unnecessary retries and wasted work.","testing_guidance":"Evaluate timeout behavior across normal, congested, and failure latency distributions.","verification_guidance":"Verify timeout changes cannot cause unsafe duplicate effects or premature ownership changes.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B05-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B05-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R01","D17-B04-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B05-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_TIMEOUT_DESIGN"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.94,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B06-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Availability Model","concept":"Availability versus consistency tradeoff","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why can availability and strong consistency conflict during network partition?","answer":"When communicating components cannot establish the coordination required for a strong global decision, continuing to serve writes may require accepting divergent or temporarily unconfirmed state, while refusing operations preserves stronger consistency at the cost of availability.","explanation":"The exact tradeoff depends on the system model and consistency definition. The important design step is to specify which operations may proceed without coordination.","principle":"Availability is not equivalent to returning any response; the response must satisfy the required correctness contract.","rule":"Define partition behavior explicitly for each operation class.","correct_example":"A system rejects writes that require a quorum when the required quorum cannot be reached.","invalid_example":"Claiming that every operation can remain strongly consistent and fully available during every possible network partition without additional assumptions.","problem":"Unspecified partition behavior produces ambiguous correctness guarantees.","failure_mode":"Divergent state or unexpected unavailability.","cause":"No explicit consistency/availability contract.","solution":"Specify quorum, consistency, conflict, and failure behavior before implementation.","alternative":"Some workloads can safely continue with eventual consistency and later reconciliation.","tradeoff":"More availability during partition can imply weaker immediate consistency.","constraint":"The allowed tradeoff depends on application invariants and protocol guarantees.","edge_case":"A read can reach one replica while another replica has newer committed information.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive state may require stronger consistency than ordinary cached or analytical data.","performance_implication":"Strong coordination can increase latency and reduce throughput during contention or partition.","testing_guidance":"Partition replicas and test each operation class independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify returned results against the documented consistency contract during partition.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R01","D17-B05-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R01","D17-B05-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B06-R02","D17-B08-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSISTENCY_AVAILABILITY_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B06-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Degraded Operation","concept":"Graceful degradation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is graceful degradation in a distributed system?","answer":"Graceful degradation is the deliberate reduction of functionality or freshness while preserving explicitly acceptable service behavior during dependency failure or resource stress.","explanation":"Degradation must be designed rather than emerging accidentally from timeout and exception handling.","principle":"A degraded state should be a valid system state with explicit guarantees.","rule":"Define which functionality can be reduced, what data may become stale, and which operations must fail closed.","correct_example":"A recommendation feature becomes unavailable while core transaction processing remains operational.","invalid_example":"Disabling authentication because an authorization dependency is unavailable.","problem":"Fallback behavior accidentally bypasses critical invariants.","failure_mode":"Unsafe fail-open behavior.","cause":"No distinction between optional and mandatory dependencies.","solution":"Classify dependencies and define fail-open/fail-closed behavior explicitly.","alternative":"Full failover may preserve functionality if an independent dependency is available.","tradeoff":"Degradation can preserve core availability but reduces functionality.","constraint":"Security and correctness-critical controls should not be bypassed merely to preserve availability.","edge_case":"The optional dependency recovers while the system remains in degraded mode.","security_implication":"Fallback paths must preserve authentication, authorization, validation, and auditing requirements.","performance_implication":"Degraded paths may reduce load or may increase it if fallback computation is expensive.","testing_guidance":"Exercise every declared degraded mode under dependency failure.","verification_guidance":"Verify that degraded behavior satisfies its documented minimum invariants.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B06-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_AVAILABILITY_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B06-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Availability","subtopic":"Load Behavior","concept":"Backpressure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is backpressure important in distributed systems?","answer":"Backpressure limits or controls incoming work when downstream capacity is insufficient, preventing unbounded queues and resource exhaustion.","explanation":"Without backpressure, overload can propagate through retries and queues until multiple components fail.","principle":"A distributed pipeline must explicitly manage mismatches between production and consumption rates.","rule":"Bound queues or define admission and shedding behavior when downstream capacity is exhausted.","correct_example":"A service rejects or delays excess work when its bounded queue reaches a defined capacity.","invalid_example":"Allowing unbounded request queues because the downstream service will eventually recover.","problem":"Overload accumulates faster than it can be processed.","failure_mode":"Memory exhaustion, latency explosion, cascading failure.","cause":"No capacity-aware flow control.","solution":"Use bounded queues, rate limiting, admission control, load shedding, or producer throttling.","alternative":"Elastic scaling can absorb some load increases but does not remove the need for finite-resource protection.","tradeoff":"Rejecting work protects system stability but reduces immediate acceptance rate.","constraint":"Backpressure policy must preserve critical work according to explicit priority rules.","edge_case":"A retrying client ignores rejection and rapidly retries.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit unbounded work acceptance for denial-of-service.","performance_implication":"Proper backpressure stabilizes latency and resource utilization under overload.","testing_guidance":"Drive producers above sustainable consumer capacity and observe queue and retry behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify bounded resource usage under sustained overload.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FLOW_CONTROL_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B07-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replication","subtopic":"Replication Fundamentals","concept":"Replication","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is replication in a distributed system?","answer":"Replication maintains multiple copies or representations of data or service state across distributed components.","explanation":"Replication can improve availability, fault tolerance, read capacity, geographic reach, or durability, but requires a strategy for maintaining or reconciling replica state.","principle":"Replication creates multiple state holders and therefore introduces consistency and coordination requirements.","rule":"Define what constitutes an authoritative update and how replicas converge or agree.","correct_example":"Multiple service nodes maintain replicated state according to a documented replication protocol.","invalid_example":"Creating several copies and assuming they are automatically consistent.","problem":"Replica divergence is not explicitly managed.","failure_mode":"Stale, conflicting, or permanently divergent state.","cause":"Missing replication semantics.","solution":"Define replication direction, acknowledgement, conflict, recovery, and consistency behavior.","alternative":"Partitioning distributes different data rather than maintaining copies of the same state.","tradeoff":"Replication improves resilience or read scale but consumes additional storage, network bandwidth, and coordination.","constraint":"Replication does not automatically guarantee strong consistency or independent failure tolerance.","edge_case":"A replica remains offline while writes continue elsewhere.","security_implication":"Every replica becomes another location requiring equivalent access control and data protection.","performance_implication":"Replication adds write traffic and may increase write latency depending on acknowledgement policy.","testing_guidance":"Delay, disconnect, restart, and recover replicas during active updates.","verification_guidance":"Verify replica state against the protocol's declared consistency and recovery guarantees.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B07-R02","D17-B07-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_REPLICATION_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B07-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replication","subtopic":"Replication Modes","concept":"Synchronous and asynchronous replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous replication?","answer":"Synchronous replication requires specified replicas or acknowledgers to participate before an operation is considered committed under the protocol, while asynchronous replication allows the primary operation to complete before all replicas have necessarily received the update.","explanation":"The exact acknowledgement semantics are protocol-specific; neither term alone establishes a universal durability or consistency guarantee.","principle":"Replication acknowledgement policy determines when the system considers an update sufficiently propagated.","rule":"Specify exactly which replicas must acknowledge and what an acknowledgement proves.","correct_example":"A write is committed only after the protocol receives required acknowledgements from a defined replica set.","invalid_example":"Claiming that any write sent to two replicas is synchronously replicated without defining acknowledgement and failure semantics.","problem":"Replication terminology is used without precise commit semantics.","failure_mode":"Unexpected data loss or stale reads after failure.","cause":"Ambiguous replication contract.","solution":"Document acknowledgement, durability, ordering, and recovery guarantees explicitly.","alternative":"Asynchronous replication can reduce write latency when temporary replica lag is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Stronger synchronous acknowledgement can improve durability/consistency guarantees while increasing latency and reducing availability under replica failure.","constraint":"The guarantee depends on the actual quorum and persistence semantics.","edge_case":"An acknowledged replica loses volatile state before durable persistence.","security_implication":"Sensitive data replication must preserve security controls and encryption requirements at every replica.","performance_implication":"Cross-region synchronous acknowledgement can materially increase write latency.","testing_guidance":"Fail replicas before and after acknowledgement boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Verify the exact state represented by a successful acknowledgement.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B07-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B07-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B06-R01","D17-B07-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B07-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_REPLICATION_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B07-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replication","subtopic":"Replica Lag","concept":"Replica lag","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is replica lag?","answer":"Replica lag is the difference in applied or durable state between a replica and the state it is expected to follow.","explanation":"Lag can arise from network delay, processing bottlenecks, storage latency, flow-control limits, or protocol behavior.","principle":"A replica can be operationally healthy while temporarily behind the source of updates.","rule":"Do not assume a successful replica response represents the latest global state unless the consistency contract guarantees it.","correct_example":"A read path identifies a replica as potentially stale and routes freshness-sensitive reads appropriately.","invalid_example":"Using any healthy replica interchangeably for a read that requires the newest committed state.","problem":"Stale data violates application freshness requirements.","failure_mode":"Incorrect decisions based on old state.","cause":"Unbounded or unrecognized replication lag.","solution":"Track lag, define freshness guarantees, and route or reject reads according to requirements.","alternative":"Read-your-writes mechanisms can provide session-level freshness without globally synchronizing every read.","tradeoff":"Freshness guarantees may reduce read scalability or increase coordination.","constraint":"Lag measurement itself may have delay or uncertainty.","edge_case":"Replica lag grows during a temporary load spike and later recovers.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or revocation state can create security exposure.","performance_implication":"Aggressive lag monitoring and catch-up can increase network and I/O load.","testing_guidance":"Inject replica delay and measure application behavior at multiple lag levels.","verification_guidance":"Verify that freshness-sensitive operations enforce their documented lag bound or consistency mechanism.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B07-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B07-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B08-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B07-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_REPLICATION_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B08-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consistency","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Strong consistency","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does a strong consistency guarantee mean in a distributed system?","answer":"A strong consistency guarantee specifies that operations observe state according to a defined ordering or freshness property that is stronger than merely eventual convergence.","explanation":"The term strong consistency is not a single universal formal guarantee. A production system must identify the exact consistency model and scope.","principle":"Consistency must be specified as a precise observable behavior rather than a marketing label.","rule":"Name the concrete consistency guarantee required by the application.","correct_example":"An API explicitly specifies that successful reads after a committed write in the same defined scope observe that write.","invalid_example":"Claiming strong consistency merely because data is replicated.","problem":"Ambiguous consistency terminology causes incorrect application assumptions.","failure_mode":"Stale or conflicting reads violate business invariants.","cause":"Undefined consistency contract.","solution":"Define operation ordering, visibility, session guarantees, or linearizability-like semantics as applicable.","alternative":"Weaker consistency can improve availability, latency, and scalability for workloads that tolerate staleness.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency generally requires more coordination or restricts available execution during failures.","constraint":"Consistency guarantees are scoped to specific operations, replicas, and failure conditions.","edge_case":"A read crosses regions after a recent write.","security_implication":"Revocation and authorization data may require stronger guarantees than ordinary content.","performance_implication":"Strong coordination can increase latency and reduce throughput under contention.","testing_guidance":"Test reads immediately after writes across replicas and failure states.","verification_guidance":"Verify observed behavior against the explicitly named consistency model.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B06-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B06-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B08-R02","D17-B08-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B06-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSISTENCY_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B08-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consistency","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Eventual consistency","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is eventual consistency?","answer":"Eventual consistency is a consistency model in which, if updates stop and the system continues operating according to its assumptions, replicas may eventually converge to a common state.","explanation":"Eventual consistency does not by itself specify how stale reads behave, how conflicts are resolved, or how long convergence takes.","principle":"Convergence is weaker than immediate agreement.","rule":"Specify freshness, conflict-resolution, and convergence expectations separately from the label eventual consistency.","correct_example":"A replicated catalog permits temporary stale reads and defines how replicas converge after updates propagate.","invalid_example":"Assuming eventual consistency guarantees that every read sees the newest write.","problem":"Application logic assumes stronger guarantees than the system provides.","failure_mode":"Temporary stale or conflicting observations cause incorrect behavior.","cause":"Missing explicit freshness contract.","solution":"Define acceptable staleness and conflict behavior for each workload.","alternative":"Stronger consistency can be used for operations that cannot tolerate divergence.","tradeoff":"Eventual consistency can improve availability and geographic scalability at the cost of temporary divergence.","constraint":"Convergence depends on communication, conflict handling, and continued operation under the protocol's assumptions.","edge_case":"Concurrent conflicting writes occur before replicas converge.","security_implication":"Eventual propagation of revocations or security policy changes can create temporary exposure.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous propagation can reduce write latency and coordination overhead.","testing_guidance":"Delay replication and perform concurrent updates from multiple replicas.","verification_guidance":"Verify eventual convergence and explicitly test the permitted stale states.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B08-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B08-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B08-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B08-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSISTENCY_MODELS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B08-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consistency","subtopic":"Session Guarantees","concept":"Read-your-writes consistency","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is read-your-writes consistency?","answer":"Read-your-writes consistency ensures that a client or session does not observe a version older than a write it has already successfully observed as completed within the defined consistency scope.","explanation":"This session-level guarantee can be useful even when global strong consistency is unnecessary.","principle":"Consistency requirements can be defined per session or operation rather than applied uniformly to the entire system.","rule":"Track the required session version or routing information needed to prevent reads from moving behind acknowledged writes.","correct_example":"After a client writes an object successfully, subsequent reads in the same session are routed or constrained so they cannot return an older version.","invalid_example":"Sending every post-write read to an arbitrary lagging replica.","problem":"Users observe their own completed update disappearing temporarily.","failure_mode":"Confusing or incorrect user-visible state.","cause":"Read routing ignores session freshness.","solution":"Use session tokens, version tracking, sticky routing, or an equivalent consistency mechanism.","alternative":"Global strong consistency provides a stronger guarantee but may require more coordination.","tradeoff":"Session guarantees can provide useful semantics with less global coordination.","constraint":"The mechanism must define what happens when the required freshness cannot be obtained.","edge_case":"The client changes regions immediately after the write.","security_implication":"Security changes may require stronger propagation than ordinary session freshness.","performance_implication":"Routing constraints can reduce replica utilization.","testing_guidance":"Write and immediately read through different replicas and regions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that acknowledged session writes remain visible within the declared session scope.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B08-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B08-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B08-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B08-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_SESSION_CONSISTENCY"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B09-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Partitioning","subtopic":"Data Distribution","concept":"Partitioning versus replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between partitioning and replication?","answer":"Partitioning divides a logical dataset or workload across components, while replication maintains multiple copies or representations of the same data or service state.","explanation":"Partitioning primarily distributes ownership or workload; replication primarily provides redundancy or additional copies. Systems can use both simultaneously.","principle":"Distribution of different data and duplication of the same data solve different problems.","rule":"Choose partitioning and replication independently according to scalability and fault-tolerance requirements.","correct_example":"A service partitions customers across shards and maintains multiple replicas for each shard.","invalid_example":"Assuming three partitions automatically provide three redundant copies of every record.","problem":"Confusing distribution with redundancy.","failure_mode":"Unexpected data loss or unavailable partitions.","cause":"Incorrect mental model of topology.","solution":"Model ownership, replica placement, and failure domains separately.","alternative":"A centralized dataset avoids partitioning but may face capacity and fault-isolation limits.","tradeoff":"Partitioning can improve scale while complicating cross-partition operations.","constraint":"Cross-partition coordination must be explicitly handled where required.","edge_case":"A partition contains a disproportionately large or active subset of data.","security_implication":"Partition boundaries must not bypass tenant or authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Good partitioning can improve parallelism; poor partitioning can create hotspots.","testing_guidance":"Test both balanced and highly skewed partition distributions.","verification_guidance":"Verify every record has exactly the intended ownership and replica placement.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B07-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B07-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B09-R02","D17-B09-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B07-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_PARTITIONING_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B09-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Partitioning","subtopic":"Partition Strategy","concept":"Partition key","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"What makes a useful partition key?","answer":"A useful partition key distributes workload and storage sufficiently while preserving the access patterns and correctness boundaries required by the application.","explanation":"A key that is technically unique may still produce severe load concentration if many requests target the same value or range.","principle":"Partitioning should be designed around both data distribution and workload distribution.","rule":"Evaluate cardinality, request distribution, growth behavior, locality, and cross-partition access before selecting a partition key.","correct_example":"A workload with many independent tenants partitions by a key that distributes active tenants while preserving tenant-local operations.","invalid_example":"Using a low-cardinality key that places most traffic on one partition.","problem":"Hot partitions become bottlenecks despite having multiple partitions.","failure_mode":"Uneven latency, throughput collapse, or capacity exhaustion.","cause":"Skewed key or workload distribution.","solution":"Choose a better key, introduce controlled subpartitioning, or use a documented load-distribution strategy.","alternative":"Consistent hashing or other distribution schemes may reduce skew for suitable workloads.","tradeoff":"Better distribution can increase cross-partition query complexity.","constraint":"Partitioning cannot eliminate fundamentally skewed workloads without additional design mechanisms.","edge_case":"One tenant becomes orders of magnitude larger or more active than others.","security_implication":"Partition-key design must not accidentally allow cross-tenant data access.","performance_implication":"Hotspot avoidance is critical to predictable throughput and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Test with realistic skew rather than only uniform synthetic data.","verification_guidance":"Measure partition load distribution against the expected workload.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B09-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B09-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B09-R03","D17-B06-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B09-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_PARTITIONING_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B09-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Partitioning","subtopic":"Cross-Partition Operations","concept":"Cross-partition operation","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why are cross-partition operations more complex?","answer":"A cross-partition operation must coordinate state or collect results from multiple independently managed partitions, increasing communication, failure, and consistency considerations.","explanation":"An operation that is local to one partition can often avoid distributed coordination required by a transaction or query spanning multiple ownership domains.","principle":"Partition boundaries become coordination boundaries when an operation crosses them.","rule":"Prefer partition-local operations where business invariants permit, and explicitly design cross-partition operations where they are required.","correct_example":"A system performs a tenant-local update within one partition and uses an explicit distributed workflow for cross-tenant aggregation.","invalid_example":"Treating a multi-partition update as if one local commit automatically makes all partitions atomic.","problem":"Partial completion occurs across partitions.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent cross-partition state.","cause":"No distributed transaction or compensating workflow semantics.","solution":"Use an appropriate distributed transaction protocol, workflow, saga-like compensation, or redesign the invariant around partition-local ownership.","alternative":"Denormalized or asynchronously aggregated data can avoid synchronous cross-partition coordination for suitable workloads.","tradeoff":"Reducing cross-partition coordination can improve scalability while increasing asynchronous complexity or temporary inconsistency.","constraint":"The selected approach must preserve the required business invariant.","edge_case":"One partition succeeds while another becomes unreachable.","security_implication":"Authorization checks must remain correct across every participating partition.","performance_implication":"Cross-partition fan-out increases latency and resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Fail individual partitions during multi-partition operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify invariants after every possible partial completion and recovery path.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B09-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B09-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B06-R01","D17-B09-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B09-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_PARTITIONING_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B10-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consensus","subtopic":"Agreement","concept":"Distributed consensus","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is distributed consensus?","answer":"Distributed consensus is a protocol problem in which distributed participants must reach an agreement on a value or decision subject to specified failure and communication assumptions.","explanation":"Consensus protocols define safety and liveness properties and operate under explicit assumptions about failures, timing, membership, and communication.","principle":"Agreement requires a protocol; independent replicas do not automatically converge on the same decision.","rule":"Specify the consensus safety and liveness properties separately and state the assumed failure model.","correct_example":"A replicated state machine uses a consensus protocol to agree on the ordered commands that form its replicated log.","invalid_example":"Calling simple majority voting without a defined protocol, membership, message, and failure model a complete consensus solution.","problem":"Consensus is treated as a generic vote instead of a formal protocol.","failure_mode":"Conflicting decisions or inability to make progress.","cause":"Missing protocol semantics and failure assumptions.","solution":"Use a defined consensus algorithm with explicit membership, quorum, timing, persistence, and recovery rules.","alternative":"Centralized leadership can avoid general distributed agreement in architectures where a trusted coordinator is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Consensus improves agreement guarantees but introduces communication rounds, quorum requirements, and failure-handling complexity.","constraint":"Consensus guarantees depend on the specific algorithm and system model.","edge_case":"The leader becomes unreachable while multiple followers remain operational.","security_implication":"Membership and message authenticity can be critical when adversarial participants are in scope.","performance_implication":"Consensus introduces coordination latency and persistent log or state-management overhead in common designs.","testing_guidance":"Test leader loss, message delay, duplication, reordering, and participant restart.","verification_guidance":"Verify safety under all allowed failures before evaluating liveness.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B05-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B05-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B10-R02","D17-B10-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B02-R02","D17-B05-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSENSUS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B10-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consensus","subtopic":"Safety and Liveness","concept":"Consensus safety versus liveness","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is the difference between safety and liveness in distributed consensus?","answer":"Safety means that the protocol does not produce an invalid or conflicting decision under the stated model, while liveness means that the protocol can eventually make required progress under its liveness assumptions.","explanation":"A system can preserve safety by refusing to decide during a failure, while a liveness property requires conditions under which progress resumes.","principle":"Safety and liveness are distinct properties and must not be conflated.","rule":"Verify safety independently from progress assumptions.","correct_example":"A consensus system refuses to commit conflicting values during a partition but can resume progress when sufficient communication is restored.","invalid_example":"Calling a system safe merely because it eventually returns a value, without checking whether conflicting values could have been committed.","problem":"Progress is mistaken for correctness.","failure_mode":"A protocol makes progress by violating agreement or ordering.","cause":"No separate safety argument.","solution":"Define invariants for safety and explicit environmental assumptions for liveness.","alternative":"A system may intentionally prioritize safety over availability during uncertainty.","tradeoff":"Stronger safety restrictions can delay progress when coordination is unavailable.","constraint":"Liveness claims require explicit assumptions about communication, participants, or timing.","edge_case":"Permanent network partition prevents the liveness assumptions from becoming true.","security_implication":"Safety violations can create conflicting authorization, ownership, or security state.","performance_implication":"Conservative safety mechanisms may increase coordination and recovery latency.","testing_guidance":"Test both safety under arbitrary permitted faults and progress under restored conditions.","verification_guidance":"Use invariant-based reasoning for safety and eventual-condition reasoning for liveness.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B10-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B10-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B05-R02","D17-B06-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B10-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSENSUS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B10-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Consensus","subtopic":"Quorum","concept":"Quorum","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why are quorums important in distributed coordination?","answer":"A quorum is a subset of participants sufficient for a protocol-defined decision, commonly selected so required quorums intersect in a way that supports the protocol's safety argument.","explanation":"The exact quorum size and intersection requirements depend on the algorithm, membership model, and tolerated failures.","principle":"A quorum is meaningful only relative to a specific protocol and its intersection and failure assumptions.","rule":"Never infer correctness from a numeric majority alone without understanding the protocol's quorum semantics.","correct_example":"A consensus protocol commits only after receiving the required quorum according to its defined membership and safety rules.","invalid_example":"Assuming any 51 percent vote is automatically a safe distributed consensus mechanism.","problem":"Quorum arithmetic is detached from protocol semantics.","failure_mode":"Conflicting decisions or unsafe failover.","cause":"Incorrect quorum definition or membership assumptions.","solution":"Derive quorum requirements from the protocol's safety and failure model.","alternative":"Centralized coordination can remove quorum management but introduces a coordinator dependency.","tradeoff":"Larger quorums can improve fault tolerance or safety margins while increasing communication and availability requirements.","constraint":"Quorum rules must account for membership changes and failure assumptions.","edge_case":"Membership changes while an old quorum is still active.","security_implication":"Compromised participants can affect quorum decisions; adversarial models require protocol-specific protections.","performance_implication":"Quorum communication adds network round trips and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test quorum formation under node failures, partitions, restarts, and membership changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify quorum intersection and tolerated-failure properties against the exact protocol specification.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B10-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B10-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B10-R02","D17-B07-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B10-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONSENSUS_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B11-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Ordering","subtopic":"Event Ordering","concept":"Total order versus partial order","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between total and partial ordering of distributed events?","answer":"A total order places every pair of events in one global sequence, while a partial order orders only events for which a causal or protocol relationship exists.","explanation":"Independent events may have no inherent causal ordering. Imposing a total order can require additional coordination and may introduce unnecessary serialization.","principle":"Only impose ordering stronger than causality when the application requires it.","rule":"Distinguish causal relationships from arbitrary global ordering requirements.","correct_example":"Two causally unrelated events remain unordered in a causal model while dependent events preserve their required order.","invalid_example":"Assuming that events generated independently on different nodes naturally have one globally correct timestamp order.","problem":"Artificial ordering creates unnecessary coordination.","failure_mode":"Reduced concurrency or incorrect interpretation of unrelated events.","cause":"Confusing total ordering with causal ordering.","solution":"Use the weakest ordering model satisfying the application invariant.","alternative":"A total-order broadcast or ordered log can provide a global sequence when required.","tradeoff":"Stronger ordering simplifies some state-machine reasoning but increases coordination and serialization.","constraint":"A total order must be established by a protocol; it cannot be inferred from local clocks alone.","edge_case":"Concurrent events receive identical or closely spaced physical timestamps.","security_implication":"Security event ordering may require causal evidence rather than arbitrary timestamp sorting.","performance_implication":"Global ordering can become a throughput bottleneck.","testing_guidance":"Generate concurrent events and verify that only required ordering constraints are enforced.","verification_guidance":"Check whether every claimed ordering relationship has protocol evidence.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R02","D17-B12-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_ORDERING_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B11-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Ordering","subtopic":"Causality","concept":"Happened-before relation","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is the happened-before relation?","answer":"The happened-before relation is a logical ordering relation that captures causal precedence between distributed events.","explanation":"An event can precede another because they occur in the same process in order, because one message send precedes its receive, or through transitive causal relationships.","principle":"Causality can be reasoned about without requiring perfectly synchronized physical clocks.","rule":"Use causal relationships when determining whether one distributed event could have influenced another.","correct_example":"A message receive is causally after the corresponding send event.","invalid_example":"Concluding causality solely because one machine's wall-clock timestamp is smaller than another's.","problem":"Physical time is mistaken for causal evidence.","failure_mode":"Incorrect event ordering or causal attribution.","cause":"Clock skew and independent clock behavior.","solution":"Use logical clocks, explicit causal metadata, or protocol-level ordering where required.","alternative":"Physical timestamps can remain useful for approximate temporal analysis when exact causality is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Causal metadata improves reasoning but consumes additional state and bandwidth.","constraint":"Absence of a happened-before relationship does not prove events were simultaneous in physical time.","edge_case":"Two events occur concurrently on different nodes with no causal communication path.","security_implication":"Incident reconstruction benefits from distinguishing causal evidence from timestamp proximity.","performance_implication":"Tracking causal metadata adds metadata-processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Create concurrent and causally linked event sequences and compare inferred relationships.","verification_guidance":"Verify each causal edge against an actual communication or execution dependency.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B11-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B11-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R03","D17-B12-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B11-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CAUSALITY_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B11-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Ordering","subtopic":"Concurrency","concept":"Concurrent events","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What does it mean for two distributed events to be concurrent?","answer":"Two events are concurrent when neither is causally ordered before the other under the relevant causal ordering relation.","explanation":"Concurrent does not mean they happened at exactly the same physical instant. It means the system has no established causal precedence between them.","principle":"Causal independence and physical simultaneity are different concepts.","rule":"Do not impose causal precedence merely because one event was observed first by a particular node.","correct_example":"Two independent updates created without communication between their origin nodes are treated as concurrent.","invalid_example":"Declaring an update causally earlier solely because its local timestamp is numerically smaller.","problem":"Concurrent updates are incorrectly serialized.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary conflicts or incorrect conflict resolution.","cause":"Assumed ordering without causal evidence.","solution":"Represent concurrency explicitly and apply a documented conflict policy when necessary.","alternative":"A central sequencer can impose total ordering when the application requires it.","tradeoff":"Explicit concurrency preserves parallelism but requires conflict-handling logic.","constraint":"The definition of concurrency depends on the causal information available to the system.","edge_case":"Events become causally related later through a reconciliation message.","security_implication":"Concurrent security events should not be merged into a single causal chain without evidence.","performance_implication":"Concurrent processing can improve throughput when conflicts are safely handled.","testing_guidance":"Generate independent updates and verify that the system recognizes their concurrency.","verification_guidance":"Check conflict resolution against causal metadata rather than timestamp ordering alone.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B11-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B11-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B11-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CAUSALITY_FOUNDATIONS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B12-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Clocks","subtopic":"Logical Time","concept":"Lamport logical clock","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What problem does a Lamport logical clock address?","answer":"A Lamport logical clock provides a logical timestamping mechanism that preserves the happened-before ordering relation across distributed events.","explanation":"Logical clocks do not measure physical elapsed time. Their primary purpose is reasoning about event ordering under distributed execution.","principle":"If event A causally precedes event B, the logical timestamp assigned to A must be ordered before that of B under the clock rules.","rule":"Do not interpret a Lamport timestamp as a measurement of real-world time or proof of concurrency.","correct_example":"Logical timestamps are advanced on local events and updated on message receipt so causal precedence is reflected.","invalid_example":"Using Lamport timestamp magnitude as a direct estimate of how many milliseconds separate two events.","problem":"Logical time is mistaken for physical time.","failure_mode":"Incorrect latency or temporal conclusions.","cause":"Semantic misuse of logical timestamps.","solution":"Use logical clocks for ordering and separate physical-time measurement for elapsed-time analysis.","alternative":"Vector clocks can provide richer causal information.","tradeoff":"Lamport clocks are simpler and smaller than richer causal metadata but cannot by themselves identify all concurrent events.","constraint":"Equal or ordered logical timestamps do not necessarily establish physical-time ordering or causality in both directions.","edge_case":"Two concurrent events receive different Lamport timestamps because of local execution order.","security_implication":"Logical ordering can strengthen event reconstruction but should not replace authenticated evidence.","performance_implication":"Logical-clock metadata has low but nonzero processing and message overhead.","testing_guidance":"Verify causal timestamp monotonicity across message exchanges.","verification_guidance":"Test the clock against the happened-before relation rather than physical timestamps.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B11-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B11-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R02","D17-B12-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B11-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_LOGICAL_CLOCKS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B12-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Clocks","subtopic":"Causal Metadata","concept":"Vector clock","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What additional information can vector clocks provide compared with Lamport clocks?","answer":"Vector clocks can represent causal relationships in a way that can distinguish causally ordered events from events that are concurrent within the tracked participant set.","explanation":"Each participant maintains a vector of logical counters representing observed progress from participants. Comparing vectors can establish certain causal relationships or concurrency.","principle":"Richer causal knowledge requires richer metadata.","rule":"Use vector-clock semantics only within the participant and membership assumptions for which the vector is defined.","correct_example":"Two version vectors are compared to determine whether one state causally dominates another or whether the versions conflict concurrently.","invalid_example":"Treating vector-clock comparison as a universal physical-time ordering mechanism.","problem":"Causal metadata is interpreted outside its defined scope.","failure_mode":"Incorrect conflict classification.","cause":"Misunderstanding vector comparison semantics or participant membership.","solution":"Document participant identity, vector maintenance, comparison rules, and membership changes.","alternative":"Lamport clocks are smaller when full concurrency detection is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Vector clocks provide richer causal information at greater metadata size and maintenance cost.","constraint":"Vector size and semantics depend on the tracked participant set.","edge_case":"Dynamic membership causes vector dimensions or identity management to change.","security_implication":"Causal metadata should be integrity-protected when used for security-sensitive conflict decisions.","performance_implication":"Metadata size can grow with the tracked participant population.","testing_guidance":"Generate causally ordered and concurrent versions and verify comparisons.","verification_guidance":"Test that vector comparison correctly distinguishes dominance, equality, and concurrency according to the defined representation.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B12-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B12-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R03","D17-B12-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B12-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CAUSAL_METADATA"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B12-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Clocks","subtopic":"Physical Time","concept":"Clock skew","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is clock skew dangerous in distributed systems?","answer":"Clock skew means different machines' physical clocks disagree, so comparing their timestamps without accounting for clock uncertainty can produce incorrect temporal conclusions.","explanation":"Clock synchronization mechanisms reduce skew but do not automatically make distributed timestamps equivalent to a perfect global clock.","principle":"Physical timestamps have uncertainty and should be interpreted within their synchronization guarantees.","rule":"Never use cross-node wall-clock comparison as stronger evidence than the clock synchronization model supports.","correct_example":"A distributed lease mechanism accounts for clock assumptions and uses documented bounds.","invalid_example":"Assuming timestamps from two unsynchronized hosts provide exact event ordering.","problem":"Cross-node timestamps are treated as exact global time.","failure_mode":"Expired leases, incorrect ordering, or invalid timeout decisions.","cause":"Clock drift and synchronization uncertainty.","solution":"Use monotonic clocks for local elapsed-time measurement and explicit clock-synchronization assumptions where wall-clock coordination is necessary.","alternative":"Logical clocks avoid dependence on physical clock synchronization for causal ordering.","tradeoff":"Physical time is useful for real-world timestamps but introduces synchronization uncertainty.","constraint":"Clock-based correctness requires explicit bounds and failure assumptions.","edge_case":"A machine's clock jumps due to synchronization or administrative changes.","security_implication":"Timestamp-dependent authentication or replay protection can fail if clock assumptions are violated.","performance_implication":"Clock synchronization and monitoring add infrastructure overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject clock offsets and time adjustments in controlled environments.","verification_guidance":"Verify every clock-dependent algorithm against documented clock-error bounds.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B12-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B12-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R02","D17-B13-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B12-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_TIMEKEEPING_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B13-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Leader Election","concept":"Leader election","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is leader election?","answer":"Leader election is a coordination mechanism for selecting one participant to act as the protocol-defined leader or coordinator among eligible participants.","explanation":"Leadership simplifies some coordination problems by assigning a primary decision-making role, but the system must also define leader failure, replacement, and stale-leader handling.","principle":"A leader is a protocol role, not merely the node that currently believes it is leader.","rule":"Leadership claims must be backed by protocol state and bounded authority.","correct_example":"A consensus protocol establishes one current leader for a term and rejects stale leadership messages.","invalid_example":"Allowing any node that locally believes it is leader to perform exclusive writes.","problem":"Multiple nodes can act as leader simultaneously.","failure_mode":"Split-brain and conflicting state transitions.","cause":"Leadership state is not uniquely established or fenced.","solution":"Use epochs, terms, leases, fencing tokens, quorum agreement, or protocol-specific authority mechanisms.","alternative":"Leaderless protocols can distribute coordination differently but introduce other consistency mechanisms.","tradeoff":"Leadership can simplify coordination but creates leader-change and potential bottleneck concerns.","constraint":"Leader authority must have a precise scope and validity period or term.","edge_case":"An old leader remains connected to some clients after losing leadership.","security_implication":"Stale leaders must not retain authority to execute privileged operations.","performance_implication":"A single leader can become a throughput or latency bottleneck.","testing_guidance":"Pause, isolate, and recover leaders during active operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that at most the protocol-permitted leadership authority can commit conflicting operations.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B10-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B10-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B14-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B10-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_COORDINATION_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B13-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Leadership Safety","concept":"Split-brain leadership","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is split-brain behavior?","answer":"Split-brain behavior occurs when multiple components independently believe they hold exclusive authority over the same logical resource or role.","explanation":"It commonly arises after partitions, stale leadership, incorrect failure detection, or insufficient fencing.","principle":"Exclusive authority requires an explicit mechanism preventing stale or competing owners from acting concurrently.","rule":"Never rely solely on local belief to establish exclusive ownership.","correct_example":"A new leader receives a fencing token that prevents operations from an older leadership epoch from being accepted.","invalid_example":"Promoting a replica immediately after losing contact with the old leader without a mechanism preventing the old leader from continuing writes.","problem":"Two owners can issue conflicting operations.","failure_mode":"State divergence or corruption.","cause":"Missing fencing or insufficient quorum authority.","solution":"Use epoch/term numbers, fencing tokens, quorum confirmation, or another protocol-specific exclusivity mechanism.","alternative":"A single centralized owner reduces split-brain risk but can create a single failure dependency.","tradeoff":"Strong fencing adds coordination but protects exclusive ownership invariants.","constraint":"Fencing must be enforced by the resource being protected, not merely by clients' local beliefs.","edge_case":"The old leader reconnects after a new leader has been established.","security_implication":"Stale authority is a direct authorization risk.","performance_implication":"Fencing checks add coordination or metadata processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Create network partitions and reconnect old leaders after leadership transfer.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale leadership cannot perform protected operations.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B13-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B04-R02","D17-B14-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B13-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_LEADERSHIP_FAILURES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B13-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Authority Epochs","concept":"Term or epoch fencing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are terms or epochs useful in distributed leadership?","answer":"A monotonically advancing term or epoch identifier allows participants and protected resources to distinguish newer leadership authority from stale authority.","explanation":"An operation carrying an older term can be rejected after a newer term has become authoritative, preventing stale participants from acting as current leaders.","principle":"Authority should be versioned when stale actors may remain operational.","rule":"Compare authority epochs at the point where protected state is changed.","correct_example":"A storage layer rejects a write carrying a fencing epoch older than the current accepted epoch.","invalid_example":"Checking the epoch only at the client and allowing the storage layer to accept stale writes.","problem":"Stale authority bypasses the intended fencing mechanism.","failure_mode":"Old leaders continue modifying shared state.","cause":"Fencing is not enforced at the resource boundary.","solution":"Enforce monotonically increasing authority versions where the protected resource accepts operations.","alternative":"Lease-based ownership can provide time-bounded authority under suitable clock assumptions.","tradeoff":"Epoch fencing adds metadata and validation but can provide stronger stale-owner protection.","constraint":"The resource must reliably persist or otherwise enforce the current epoch.","edge_case":"The old actor reconnects after a long partition with cached credentials.","security_implication":"Epoch enforcement can limit replay of stale privileged authority.","performance_implication":"Each protected operation may require a lightweight version comparison.","testing_guidance":"Replay operations from old epochs after leadership changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected write path checks the current authority epoch.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B13-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B13-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FENCING_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B14-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Membership","subtopic":"Cluster Membership","concept":"Membership change","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why must membership changes be treated as a protocol operation?","answer":"Changing the set of participants can alter quorum calculations, leadership eligibility, replication responsibility, and safety assumptions.","explanation":"Adding or removing nodes without coordinated membership semantics can create incompatible views of the cluster and unsafe quorum decisions.","principle":"The participant set is part of distributed protocol state.","rule":"Define how membership transitions become authoritative and how old and new configurations interact.","correct_example":"A replicated protocol commits a configuration change according to its documented membership-transition rules.","invalid_example":"Editing a node list independently on each server while the cluster is active.","problem":"Participants use inconsistent membership views.","failure_mode":"Conflicting quorums or simultaneous leadership.","cause":"Uncoordinated configuration change.","solution":"Use explicit configuration epochs and protocol-defined membership transitions.","alternative":"Static membership simplifies reasoning but reduces operational flexibility.","tradeoff":"Dynamic membership improves elasticity but increases coordination complexity.","constraint":"Membership changes must preserve the protocol's safety requirements.","edge_case":"A node removed from the cluster remains network-reachable with stale configuration.","security_implication":"Membership changes are security-sensitive because they change who can participate in trusted coordination.","performance_implication":"Configuration transitions can temporarily reduce available capacity or require data movement.","testing_guidance":"Add, remove, isolate, and recover members during active operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify quorum and authority behavior before, during, and after every membership transition.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B10-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B10-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R02","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B10-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_MEMBERSHIP_PROTOCOLS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B14-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Membership","subtopic":"Configuration Epochs","concept":"Configuration version","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why should cluster configurations be versioned?","answer":"Versioning configurations allows nodes to distinguish current membership and protocol parameters from stale configurations.","explanation":"A configuration identifier or epoch can prevent a participant from applying operations under an obsolete membership view.","principle":"Configuration state should be identifiable and comparable across participants.","rule":"Reject or safely handle operations based on obsolete configuration versions.","correct_example":"A node refuses a leadership or quorum operation associated with an obsolete configuration epoch.","invalid_example":"Accepting configuration-sensitive messages without checking which membership version produced them.","problem":"Stale configuration messages remain actionable.","failure_mode":"Unsafe quorum decisions or invalid replication behavior.","cause":"No configuration version validation.","solution":"Attach configuration versions to protocol state and enforce transition rules.","alternative":"Static configuration avoids dynamic-version handling but limits flexibility.","tradeoff":"Versioned configurations improve safety at the cost of additional metadata and transition logic.","constraint":"Version monotonicity and transition semantics must be defined.","edge_case":"Two configuration versions are simultaneously active during a transition.","security_implication":"Unauthorized or stale configuration messages must not modify membership.","performance_implication":"Configuration validation is usually lightweight but becomes critical on high-frequency control paths.","testing_guidance":"Replay messages from old configurations after a membership change.","verification_guidance":"Verify that obsolete configuration state cannot regain authority.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B14-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B14-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B14-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONFIGURATION_MANAGEMENT"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B14-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Membership","subtopic":"Failure Detection","concept":"Accrual failure detector","knowledge_type":"CONCEPT","question":"What is the purpose of a failure detector in distributed membership management?","answer":"A failure detector supplies information about which participants are suspected to be unavailable so that protocols can adapt membership, leadership, or recovery behavior.","explanation":"Failure detectors are characterized by their assumptions and the possibility of false suspicions. They are inputs to protocols rather than universal proof of physical failure.","principle":"Failure detection and failure truth are distinct concepts.","rule":"Use failure-detector output according to its documented completeness and accuracy properties.","correct_example":"A cluster uses failure suspicion to trigger controlled leader reelection while preserving safeguards against false positives.","invalid_example":"Permanently deleting a participant's state solely because a detector suspects failure.","problem":"Suspicion is treated as certainty.","failure_mode":"Data loss or unsafe membership change.","cause":"Failure detector semantics misunderstood.","solution":"Combine detector output with protocol-level quorum, fencing, or recovery mechanisms.","alternative":"Manual operator confirmation can be used for high-impact membership changes when automation cannot establish sufficient safety.","tradeoff":"More conservative detection reduces false positives but can slow recovery.","constraint":"Detector guarantees depend on the communication and timing model.","edge_case":"A healthy node becomes temporarily unreachable from only one observer.","security_implication":"An attacker may induce false suspicions by disrupting communication.","performance_implication":"Frequent health probes consume network and compute resources.","testing_guidance":"Inject asymmetric packet loss where different observers have different views.","verification_guidance":"Verify that detector mistakes cannot directly violate data or leadership safety.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B04-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B04-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B13-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B04-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_DETECTORS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B15-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replicated State","subtopic":"State Machine Replication","concept":"State machine replication","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is state machine replication?","answer":"State machine replication maintains multiple replicas that apply the same deterministic sequence of commands to equivalent initial state so they produce equivalent state.","explanation":"The key requirement is agreement on command ordering plus deterministic application of those commands under the protocol's assumptions.","principle":"Replicas can converge through identical deterministic execution over an agreed operation sequence.","rule":"Ensure replicated commands have deterministic semantics or explicitly model nondeterminism.","correct_example":"Replicas apply the same committed log entries in the same order using deterministic state-transition logic.","invalid_example":"Replicas execute commands with independent random or wall-clock behavior while assuming identical state will always result.","problem":"Nondeterministic execution causes replica divergence.","failure_mode":"Replicas reach different states despite identical command logs.","cause":"Uncontrolled nondeterminism or environmental dependency.","solution":"Externalize nondeterministic inputs into the replicated command stream or otherwise coordinate them.","alternative":"State synchronization or anti-entropy can reconcile replicas without identical deterministic replay.","tradeoff":"Deterministic replication simplifies convergence reasoning but can constrain implementation patterns.","constraint":"All state-affecting nondeterminism must be accounted for by the replication design.","edge_case":"A command reads local time, random values, or environment-specific data.","security_implication":"Replicas must validate replicated commands consistently to avoid divergent authorization outcomes.","performance_implication":"Every replica repeats command processing, increasing compute cost.","testing_guidance":"Replay identical logs across independent replicas and compare state hashes.","verification_guidance":"Verify deterministic state transitions and log-order consistency.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B10-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B10-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B15-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B10-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"REPLICATED_STATE_MACHINE_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B15-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replicated State","subtopic":"Determinism","concept":"Nondeterminism in replicated execution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can nondeterminism break state machine replication?","answer":"If replicas apply the same command but independently observe different nondeterministic inputs, they can produce different state transitions and diverge.","explanation":"Sources of nondeterminism include local time, random number generation, process scheduling effects, external services, and environment-specific state.","principle":"Replicated execution requires deterministic inputs and transition semantics unless nondeterminism is explicitly coordinated.","rule":"Make nondeterministic inputs part of the agreed replicated operation or otherwise ensure equivalent observation.","correct_example":"A generated identifier is assigned before the replicated command is committed and becomes part of the command data.","invalid_example":"Each replica independently generates a random identifier while applying the same command.","problem":"Identical commands produce different states.","failure_mode":"Replica divergence.","cause":"Uncontrolled nondeterministic input.","solution":"Externalize or deterministically derive nondeterministic values.","alternative":"Use a replicated source of nondeterministic values with protocol-defined agreement.","tradeoff":"Coordinating nondeterminism adds protocol complexity and possibly latency.","constraint":"Every state-affecting external dependency must be accounted for.","edge_case":"A command depends on a local filesystem or environment variable.","security_implication":"Replica divergence can produce inconsistent security decisions.","performance_implication":"Externalizing values can add preprocessing or coordination overhead.","testing_guidance":"Run the same command sequence under different scheduling and environmental conditions.","verification_guidance":"Identify every nondeterministic operation in replicated state transitions.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B15-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B15-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B15-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"REPLICATED_STATE_MACHINE_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B15-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Replicated State","subtopic":"Command Log","concept":"Replicated command log","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is an ordered command log useful for replicated state?","answer":"An ordered command log provides a durable or protocol-defined sequence that replicas can use as the common input to state transitions.","explanation":"A log separates agreement on operation order from application of the operations themselves.","principle":"Agreement on an ordered history can serve as the foundation for deterministic replica convergence.","rule":"Define which log entries are committed, how gaps are handled, and how recovery reconstructs state.","correct_example":"A replica applies only commands that are known to be committed according to the replication protocol.","invalid_example":"Applying uncommitted speculative commands as permanent state without a rollback or reconciliation mechanism.","problem":"Uncommitted state becomes indistinguishable from committed state.","failure_mode":"Incorrect state after leader failure or conflicting histories.","cause":"Missing commit boundary semantics.","solution":"Separate speculative, accepted, committed, and applied states as required by the protocol.","alternative":"Snapshot plus incremental synchronization can reduce replay cost for long histories.","tradeoff":"Logs provide strong history and replayability but consume storage and require compaction or snapshot management.","constraint":"Recovery semantics must preserve the protocol's committed-history guarantees.","edge_case":"A replica has a suffix of entries that was never committed.","security_implication":"Auditability improves when committed history is distinguishable from speculative operations.","performance_implication":"Log replication adds write and storage overhead; snapshots can reduce recovery time.","testing_guidance":"Crash replicas at different log positions and recover them against authoritative history.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only valid committed history determines durable replicated state.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B15-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B15-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B16-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B15-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"REPLICATED_LOG_DESIGN"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B16-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Snapshots","concept":"State snapshot","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are snapshots useful in replicated systems?","answer":"A snapshot captures a representation of state at a defined logical point so recovery does not require replaying the entire historical operation sequence.","explanation":"Snapshots reduce recovery work when combined with a valid protocol-defined point in the history.","principle":"Recovery artifacts must correspond to a well-defined consistent state boundary.","rule":"Associate snapshots with the exact logical history or version they represent.","correct_example":"A replica restores a snapshot at log position N and replays only committed entries after N.","invalid_example":"Restoring an arbitrary filesystem copy and assuming it represents a consistent distributed state.","problem":"Recovery starts from an inconsistent state.","failure_mode":"Corrupted or divergent reconstructed state.","cause":"Snapshot boundary not tied to protocol state.","solution":"Create and validate snapshots at explicit consistent points.","alternative":"Full log replay provides stronger historical reconstruction but may be slower.","tradeoff":"Snapshots improve recovery speed but require storage and snapshot-generation coordination.","constraint":"Snapshot consistency semantics must be documented.","edge_case":"Snapshot creation overlaps with active writes or membership changes.","security_implication":"Snapshots can contain complete sensitive state and require equivalent protection to primary data.","performance_implication":"Snapshot creation can consume substantial I/O and CPU resources.","testing_guidance":"Restore snapshots under active and failed-node scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Compare restored state against the expected state at the declared snapshot boundary.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B15-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B15-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R02","D17-B17-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B15-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RECOVERY_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B16-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Recovery State","concept":"Checkpoint versus snapshot","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction between a checkpoint and a state snapshot?","answer":"A checkpoint is a protocol-defined progress marker indicating that some computation or history has reached a recoverable point, while a snapshot is a captured representation of state at a defined point; implementations may use the terms differently.","explanation":"The exact terminology is system-specific, so the dataset must preserve the operational meaning rather than assume universal naming.","principle":"Recovery metadata and captured state should be connected through explicit version or history boundaries.","rule":"Define checkpoint semantics precisely in the protocol documentation.","correct_example":"A system records a checkpoint corresponding to a known committed log position and maintains state sufficient to resume from it.","invalid_example":"Treating an arbitrary periodic backup as proof of a protocol checkpoint.","problem":"Recovery terminology hides inconsistent semantics.","failure_mode":"Invalid recovery or replay assumptions.","cause":"Undefined checkpoint meaning.","solution":"Specify what operations, state, and history a checkpoint covers.","alternative":"Full snapshots can be simpler when the state size and recovery requirements permit them.","tradeoff":"Fine-grained checkpoints can reduce recovery work but add metadata and coordination.","constraint":"Checkpoint correctness depends on the state and history relationship it claims to represent.","edge_case":"A checkpoint is durable on one node but not replicated.","security_implication":"Checkpoint metadata may expose state versions or operational information.","performance_implication":"Frequent checkpoints consume storage and I/O.","testing_guidance":"Recover from checkpoints created immediately before and after failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery resumes from exactly the checkpoint's declared logical boundary.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B16-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B16-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B16-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RECOVERY_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.92,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B16-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Replay","concept":"Deterministic replay","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is deterministic replay in distributed systems?","answer":"Deterministic replay reconstructs state by applying the same authoritative history under the same defined transition semantics and obtaining an equivalent result.","explanation":"Replay is useful for recovery, debugging, verification, and state reconstruction when the history and state transitions are sufficiently deterministic.","principle":"Replay correctness depends on preserving all state-affecting inputs.","rule":"Record or deterministically reconstruct every input that can affect replayed state.","correct_example":"A replica rebuilds state from a committed command history whose nondeterministic inputs were captured in the commands.","invalid_example":"Replaying a log while allowing each node to use its current wall clock and local environment values.","problem":"Replay produces a different state than the original execution.","failure_mode":"Recovery divergence or irreproducible debugging.","cause":"Missing state-affecting inputs.","solution":"Capture deterministic inputs and isolate environment-dependent behavior.","alternative":"State snapshots can avoid replaying all historical operations.","tradeoff":"Detailed history improves replayability but increases storage and processing requirements.","constraint":"Replay equivalence depends on deterministic application semantics.","edge_case":"A historical command depends on an external service that no longer exists.","security_implication":"Replay logs may contain sensitive inputs and require access controls.","performance_implication":"Long histories increase replay time unless snapshots or checkpoints are used.","testing_guidance":"Replay identical histories multiple times and compare resulting state.","verification_guidance":"Hash or otherwise compare reconstructed state against an independently validated reference state.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B16-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B16-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B16-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_REPLAY_PRINCIPLES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B17-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Anti-Entropy","concept":"Anti-entropy synchronization","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is anti-entropy synchronization?","answer":"Anti-entropy is a synchronization technique in which distributed replicas periodically compare or exchange state so differences can be detected and reconciled.","explanation":"Anti-entropy can help replicas recover from missed updates or temporary disconnection without requiring every update to be synchronously delivered to every replica.","principle":"Periodic state comparison can provide eventual convergence when update propagation is not guaranteed to be immediate.","rule":"Define conflict resolution and authoritative state semantics before using anti-entropy to reconcile divergent replicas.","correct_example":"Replicas exchange version metadata and synchronize missing state after a temporary communication outage.","invalid_example":"Blindly overwriting one replica with another without determining which state is authoritative or whether concurrent updates exist.","problem":"Reconciliation destroys valid concurrent updates.","failure_mode":"Silent data loss during repair.","cause":"No conflict-detection or merge policy.","solution":"Use version vectors, sequence numbers, conflict records, or protocol-specific reconciliation semantics.","alternative":"Synchronous replication can reduce divergence but requires stronger coordination.","tradeoff":"Anti-entropy improves eventual convergence and recovery flexibility but can consume bandwidth and require conflict handling.","constraint":"Convergence is not guaranteed unless the reconciliation process and conflict policy are correct.","edge_case":"Both replicas contain valid concurrent updates.","security_implication":"Reconciliation must authenticate peers and protect against unauthorized state injection.","performance_implication":"Periodic comparison and repair consume network, CPU, and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Disconnect replicas, apply divergent updates, reconnect, and verify reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Verify no valid update is silently discarded by reconciliation.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B08-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B08-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R02","D17-B17-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B07-R03","D17-B08-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RECONCILIATION_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B17-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Conflict Resolution","concept":"Conflict-free versus conflict-requiring replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why do some replicated data types require conflict resolution while others can merge concurrent updates naturally?","answer":"Some state representations are designed so concurrent operations can be combined without ambiguity under defined algebraic rules, while ordinary replicated records may contain concurrent updates that require an explicit conflict policy.","explanation":"Conflict-free designs restrict or structure operations so independently produced updates can converge through deterministic merge semantics.","principle":"Automatic convergence requires merge operations with well-defined correctness properties.","rule":"Do not claim conflict-free convergence unless the data type and merge operation actually provide the required properties.","correct_example":"A counter representation defines a deterministic merge operation for independently generated increments.","invalid_example":"Merging arbitrary conflicting account balances by taking the larger value and calling the result conflict-free.","problem":"Arbitrary merge heuristics are mistaken for mathematically safe convergence.","failure_mode":"Incorrect business state after reconciliation.","cause":"Merge function does not preserve the required invariant.","solution":"Design state and merge operations around explicit invariants and convergence properties.","alternative":"Use coordinated writes when conflicts cannot be safely merged.","tradeoff":"Conflict-free designs can improve availability but may constrain the data model.","constraint":"Business invariants may be stronger than the merge algebra can guarantee.","edge_case":"Concurrent operations individually valid become jointly invalid after merge.","security_implication":"Automatic merges must not bypass authorization or fraud-prevention invariants.","performance_implication":"Merge metadata and reconciliation add storage and CPU costs.","testing_guidance":"Generate concurrent updates and verify convergence plus business invariants.","verification_guidance":"Verify merge properties and application invariants separately.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B17-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B17-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B17-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONFLICT_RESOLUTION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.96,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B17-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Recovery","subtopic":"Convergence","concept":"Convergence invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should be verified when claiming distributed replicas converge?","answer":"The system should establish that, under its stated assumptions and after permitted divergence stops, replicas reach equivalent state according to the protocol's defined equivalence relation.","explanation":"Convergence must account for concurrent updates, dropped messages, retries, recovery, and reconciliation rules rather than only testing normal replication.","principle":"Convergence is a property of the complete propagation and reconciliation protocol.","rule":"Test convergence after realistic partitions and recovery, not merely after successful message delivery.","correct_example":"After partition healing, replicas reconcile and reach equivalent state while preserving defined invariants.","invalid_example":"Testing convergence only in a failure-free network and extrapolating the result to partition recovery.","problem":"Normal-path testing hides recovery divergence.","failure_mode":"Permanent or silent replica divergence.","cause":"Incomplete convergence testing.","solution":"Exercise loss, duplication, reordering, concurrent updates, recovery, and repeated reconciliation.","alternative":"Strong synchronous coordination can reduce the divergence surface but does not eliminate the need for recovery validation.","tradeoff":"More aggressive convergence testing increases test complexity but exposes correctness failures earlier.","constraint":"Convergence claims require explicit assumptions about continued communication and failure recovery.","edge_case":"A replica rejoins after missing multiple generations of state.","security_implication":"A stale replica must not regain authority before its state is safe and current enough for the protocol.","performance_implication":"Repeated reconciliation can be expensive for large state sets.","testing_guidance":"Use fault injection to create divergent replicas and measure convergence after recovery.","verification_guidance":"Compare independently reconstructed states and validate business invariants after convergence.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B17-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B17-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B17-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CONVERGENCE_VERIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B18-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Idempotency","concept":"Idempotent distributed operation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is idempotency important in distributed systems?","answer":"Idempotency allows an operation to be retried without changing the intended final effect beyond the first successful application.","explanation":"Retries are common because clients may not know whether a remote operation completed when a response is delayed or lost.","principle":"Uncertain communication outcomes require operations whose repeated application is safe when possible.","rule":"Use stable operation identifiers and idempotency semantics for retriable side effects.","correct_example":"A payment-like command uses a unique operation identifier so repeated submissions resolve to the same logical operation outcome.","invalid_example":"Retrying a non-idempotent state-changing request without deduplication.","problem":"Communication uncertainty produces duplicate side effects.","failure_mode":"Double creation, duplicate charge, repeated provisioning, or repeated state transition.","cause":"No idempotency mechanism.","solution":"Use idempotency keys, durable deduplication records, or a protocol that guarantees equivalent retry semantics.","alternative":"Exactly-once effect protocols can be used when their stronger requirements are justified.","tradeoff":"Idempotency storage and lookup add state and processing overhead but greatly simplify safe retry behavior.","constraint":"The deduplication record must survive the relevant retry window.","edge_case":"The original operation succeeds but the acknowledgement is lost.","security_implication":"Replayable privileged operations require strong authorization and replay protection.","performance_implication":"Idempotency checks add storage and lookup work.","testing_guidance":"Retry operations after intentionally losing responses at different execution stages.","verification_guidance":"Verify that repeated operation identifiers produce the documented effect exactly once logically.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R02","D17-B18-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_IDEMPOTENCY_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B18-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Exactly-Once Semantics","concept":"Exactly-once effect versus exactly-once delivery","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"Why should exactly-once delivery be distinguished from exactly-once effect?","answer":"Exactly-once delivery concerns how many times a message is delivered, while exactly-once effect concerns whether the intended application-side outcome occurs once despite retries, duplicates, or uncertain delivery.","explanation":"A system may deliver a message more than once while still producing one logical application effect through deduplication or idempotency.","principle":"Application effect semantics are distinct from transport delivery semantics.","rule":"Specify exactly-once claims at the layer where correctness actually matters.","correct_example":"A message is delivered multiple times but the consumer uses a durable operation identifier to apply one logical state transition.","invalid_example":"Claiming exactly-once business behavior solely because a network transport reports reliable delivery.","problem":"Transport guarantees are mistaken for application guarantees.","failure_mode":"Duplicate business effects.","cause":"Layer confusion.","solution":"Define and verify application-level idempotency or transactional coupling explicitly.","alternative":"At-least-once delivery plus idempotent consumers is often simpler than stronger transport assumptions.","tradeoff":"Application-level deduplication requires persistent state but provides clear effect semantics.","constraint":"Exactly-once effect claims require precise scope and failure assumptions.","edge_case":"The consumer commits the effect but crashes before recording the deduplication marker.","security_implication":"Duplicate privileged operations can become security incidents if replay is not controlled.","performance_implication":"Durable deduplication adds storage operations.","testing_guidance":"Crash consumers between side-effect and acknowledgement/deduplication stages.","verification_guidance":"Verify final business state rather than merely counting message deliveries.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B18-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B18-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R02","D17-B18-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B18-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_DELIVERY_SEMANTICS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B18-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Deduplication","concept":"Deduplication window","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must the lifetime of an idempotency or deduplication record be designed explicitly?","answer":"A deduplication record must remain available for at least the period during which duplicate delivery or retry can legitimately occur under the system's failure and retry model.","explanation":"Deleting deduplication state too early can make an old retry appear to be a new operation.","principle":"Retry safety depends on the lifetime of the identity information used to suppress duplicates.","rule":"Derive deduplication retention from maximum retry, replay, recovery, and message-retention assumptions.","correct_example":"A consumer retains operation identifiers longer than the maximum permitted retry and replay period.","invalid_example":"Deleting idempotency keys after a short fixed interval without considering delayed messages or client retries.","problem":"Old duplicates are accepted as new operations.","failure_mode":"Repeated side effects after delayed delivery.","cause":"Insufficient deduplication retention.","solution":"Set retention from protocol-level retry and message lifetime guarantees.","alternative":"Use a durable operation ledger with explicit lifecycle semantics.","tradeoff":"Longer retention increases storage usage but reduces duplicate-effect risk.","constraint":"Retention must align with all upstream retry and replay mechanisms.","edge_case":"A delayed message arrives after a temporary outage lasting longer than normal.","security_implication":"Long-lived replay protection may be necessary for sensitive operations.","performance_implication":"Large deduplication stores require indexing, expiration, and compaction strategies.","testing_guidance":"Deliver messages after simulated delays near and beyond the configured deduplication window.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the documented retry horizon is covered by retained deduplication state.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B18-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B18-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B03-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B18-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_DEDUPLICATION_DESIGN"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B19-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Backpressure and Retry","concept":"Retry amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is retry amplification?","answer":"Retry amplification occurs when failures cause clients or services to retry work, increasing load on an already degraded system and potentially causing further failures.","explanation":"Retries can multiply across dependency layers, turning one failed request into many downstream attempts.","principle":"Retries are additional load and must be treated as part of capacity planning.","rule":"Bound retry count, retry concurrency, and retry timing.","correct_example":"A service uses bounded retries with backoff and stops retrying when the operation is known to be non-retriable.","invalid_example":"Every service layer independently retries a failed downstream request several times without coordination.","problem":"Failure increases workload instead of reducing it.","failure_mode":"Retry storm and cascading overload.","cause":"Unbounded or synchronized retries.","solution":"Use exponential backoff, jitter, budgets, circuit breaking, and explicit retry classification.","alternative":"Fail-fast behavior may be safer when downstream recovery is unlikely within the request lifetime.","tradeoff":"Retries can recover transient failures but consume capacity and increase latency.","constraint":"Only operations safe to retry should be automatically retried.","edge_case":"Thousands of clients retry simultaneously after a common outage.","security_implication":"Attackers can intentionally trigger retry amplification as a denial-of-service vector.","performance_implication":"Retry traffic can dominate normal workload during incidents.","testing_guidance":"Simulate dependency failure with many concurrent clients.","verification_guidance":"Measure total downstream attempts per original request under failure.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B03-R01","D17-B18-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B03-R01","D17-B18-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R02","D17-B06-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B03-R01","D17-B18-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RETRY_FAILURES"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B19-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Retry Strategy","concept":"Exponential backoff with jitter","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are exponential backoff and jitter used for distributed retries?","answer":"Exponential backoff increases the delay between repeated attempts while jitter randomizes retry timing so many clients are less likely to retry simultaneously.","explanation":"This reduces synchronized retry bursts and gives a recovering dependency time to regain capacity.","principle":"Retry timing should reduce correlated load during recovery.","rule":"Bound backoff and jitter according to user latency and recovery requirements.","correct_example":"Clients progressively increase retry delay and randomize the delay within a documented range.","invalid_example":"All clients retry at exactly the same fixed interval after a shared failure.","problem":"Synchronized retries create load spikes.","failure_mode":"Repeated overload during dependency recovery.","cause":"Deterministic retry timing.","solution":"Use bounded exponential backoff with appropriate jitter and retry budgets.","alternative":"Fail-fast or queue-based asynchronous processing may be preferable for long-lived operations.","tradeoff":"Longer backoff reduces pressure but increases recovery latency for individual requests.","constraint":"Backoff must remain bounded and compatible with operation deadlines.","edge_case":"A retry deadline expires while the dependency is recovering.","security_implication":"Retry controls should prevent attackers from multiplying workload through repeated requests.","performance_implication":"Proper backoff can materially reduce incident-time load amplification.","testing_guidance":"Simulate large populations of clients failing at the same time.","verification_guidance":"Measure retry synchronization and total retry load under controlled failure.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B19-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B19-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B19-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RETRY_PATTERNS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B19-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Coordination","subtopic":"Retry Safety","concept":"Retry budget","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"What is a retry budget?","answer":"A retry budget limits the additional work a component is allowed to generate through retries so failures cannot create unbounded amplification.","explanation":"The budget can be expressed using counts, rates, concurrency, resource consumption, or a combination appropriate to the service.","principle":"Failure recovery mechanisms require resource limits just like normal workloads.","rule":"Define retry budgets independently from ordinary request capacity.","correct_example":"A service permits only a bounded proportion of additional retry traffic and stops retrying when the budget is exhausted.","invalid_example":"Allowing each request to retry indefinitely because each individual retry appears inexpensive.","problem":"Retries consume unlimited capacity during prolonged failure.","failure_mode":"Cascading failure and service collapse.","cause":"No retry resource limit.","solution":"Use retry budgets, deadlines, circuit breakers, and explicit error classification.","alternative":"Queueing work for later execution can move retries outside the interactive request path.","tradeoff":"A strict budget may abandon some transiently recoverable operations but protects overall service stability.","constraint":"Budget policy must account for operation criticality and dependency behavior.","edge_case":"A high-priority operation competes with low-priority retries for the same budget.","security_implication":"Retry budgets can limit abuse-induced amplification.","performance_implication":"Budgets protect capacity and stabilize tail latency during dependency failure.","testing_guidance":"Exhaust the retry budget under sustained downstream failure and verify bounded load.","verification_guidance":"Measure maximum retry-generated work against the configured policy.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B19-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B19-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R02","D17-B06-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B19-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_RETRY_CONTROL"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.97,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B20-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Reasoning","subtopic":"Invariant Design","concept":"Distributed invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is a distributed invariant?","answer":"A distributed invariant is a property that must remain true across the combined state and transitions of multiple independently executing components.","explanation":"Distributed invariants must account for concurrency, partial failure, delayed communication, retries, and recovery rather than only normal execution.","principle":"Correctness is defined by invariants that survive permitted interleavings and failures.","rule":"State each critical invariant independently of implementation details and identify every operation that can affect it.","correct_example":"A resource has at most one authoritative owner according to the system's fencing and leadership protocol.","invalid_example":"Assuming exclusive ownership because each node locally records itself as owner.","problem":"Local state does not establish a global invariant.","failure_mode":"Conflicting owners or divergent state.","cause":"Invariant is not enforced across distributed transitions.","solution":"Identify authoritative coordination and enforcement points.","alternative":"Partition-local invariants can reduce coordination requirements where business rules permit.","tradeoff":"Global invariants require stronger coordination than local invariants.","constraint":"Every failure and recovery path must preserve or safely re-establish the invariant.","edge_case":"A network partition creates incompatible local observations.","security_implication":"Security invariants such as exclusive authority must be enforced at trusted resource boundaries.","performance_implication":"Global invariant enforcement can introduce coordination latency.","testing_guidance":"Use model-based and fault-injection tests against every state transition affecting the invariant.","verification_guidance":"Check invariants after normal operations, concurrent operations, partial failures, retries, recovery, and membership changes.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B18-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B18-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R02","D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B18-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_CORRECTNESS_REASONING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B20-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Reasoning","subtopic":"Model Checking","concept":"Interleaving analysis","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should distributed protocols be analyzed across multiple event interleavings?","answer":"Independent components can execute and communicate in many permitted orders, and correctness must hold across relevant interleavings rather than only one observed execution.","explanation":"Concurrency creates schedules involving delayed messages, concurrent operations, failures, and recovery that can expose races not visible in sequential testing.","principle":"One successful execution is insufficient evidence of distributed correctness.","rule":"Explore adversarial but protocol-permitted event orderings.","correct_example":"A test model explores leader loss before and after acknowledgement and verifies the same safety invariant.","invalid_example":"Testing only the nominal message sequence and declaring the protocol correct.","problem":"Race-dependent failures remain undiscovered.","failure_mode":"Rare state corruption under specific timing.","cause":"Insufficient schedule exploration.","solution":"Use model checking, deterministic simulation, systematic concurrency testing, or fault injection where appropriate.","alternative":"Randomized testing can discover unexpected schedules but does not provide exhaustive coverage.","tradeoff":"More schedule exploration increases confidence but can become computationally expensive.","constraint":"The state space must be bounded or abstracted appropriately for practical exhaustive analysis.","edge_case":"A failure occurs between two protocol messages that are usually nearly simultaneous.","security_implication":"Race conditions can create authorization or ownership bypasses.","performance_implication":"Systematic schedule testing can be computationally expensive.","testing_guidance":"Inject controlled message delays and participant pauses at protocol boundaries.","verification_guidance":"Record explored schedules and identify untested state-space regions rather than claiming universal proof from finite tests.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B20-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B20-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B20-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_VERIFICATION_METHODS"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.98,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B20-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems Reasoning","subtopic":"Failure-Oriented Verification","concept":"Fault-injection verification","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"What should fault-injection testing verify in a distributed system?","answer":"Fault-injection testing should verify that declared safety, recovery, consistency, and degradation properties remain valid when specific components, links, messages, or resources fail.","explanation":"The objective is not merely to prove that the system eventually restarts; it is to verify documented behavior at the exact failure boundaries that can affect correctness.","principle":"Failure testing must target protocol assumptions and invariants, not only infrastructure uptime.","rule":"Inject one or more controlled faults while measuring the properties the protocol promises to preserve.","correct_example":"A test isolates a leader, delays acknowledgements, and verifies that no conflicting committed state is produced.","invalid_example":"Restarting one server and checking only whether the health endpoint becomes available again.","problem":"Operational recovery is mistaken for correctness verification.","failure_mode":"Hidden safety or consistency violations survive infrastructure-level tests.","cause":"Tests measure availability but not distributed invariants.","solution":"Pair fault injection with explicit invariant and outcome assertions.","alternative":"Formal reasoning can cover classes of failures that are expensive to reproduce physically.","tradeoff":"Fault injection provides realistic evidence but has finite coverage.","constraint":"Fault experiments must be isolated and controlled to avoid unintended production impact.","edge_case":"Multiple correlated failures occur simultaneously.","security_implication":"Failure tests should include security-control dependencies and fail-closed requirements where appropriate.","performance_implication":"Fault injection can reveal retry amplification and degraded-mode resource behavior.","testing_guidance":"Inject delay, loss, duplication, partition, crash, stale authority, and recovery faults in controlled environments.","verification_guidance":"Record fault, expected invariant, observed outcome, limitations, and whether the scenario was actually executed.","language":"DATABASE_AGNOSTIC","framework":null,"version":"CONCEPTUAL","version_range":"GENERAL","dependencies":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B20-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B20-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01","D17-B17-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B20-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_class":"DISTRIBUTED_FAILURE_VERIFICATION"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":0.99,"validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B21-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Quorum Systems","concept":"Quorum intersection","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must quorum systems provide an intersection property?","answer":"A quorum system must ensure that relevant quorums intersect when correctness depends on a shared decision or piece of replicated state. Intersection allows information accepted by one quorum to overlap with information accepted by another quorum, preventing incompatible decisions under the assumed failure model.","explanation":"Quorum intersection is a structural safety mechanism. The exact intersection requirement depends on the quorum model and fault assumptions.","principle":"Safety-critical quorum designs must establish the required intersection property mathematically rather than assuming that a majority is automatically sufficient.","rule":"Define quorum membership, fault assumptions, and required intersection before selecting quorum sizes.","correct_example":"For a crash-fault majority protocol with five members, a quorum of three intersects every other quorum of three by at least one member.","invalid_example":"Selecting two disjoint groups of replicas as independent decision quorums while assuming their decisions are automatically compatible.","problem":"Independent quorums can authorize conflicting values when no required intersection exists.","failure_mode":"Conflicting decisions","cause":"Quorum sets lack the intersection property required by the protocol.","solution":"Derive quorum requirements from the protocol's safety proof and fault model.","alternative":"Use weighted or asymmetric quorum systems when equal-size majority quorums are unsuitable.","tradeoff":"Larger or more restrictive quorums improve overlap properties but may reduce availability under failures.","constraint":"The quorum definition must match the protocol's failure model and decision semantics.","edge_case":"Membership changes can invalidate an intersection assumption if old and new configurations are treated as unrelated.","security_implication":"A compromised replica may exploit weak quorum assumptions to influence conflicting decisions.","performance_implication":"Larger quorum sizes can increase communication and latency.","testing_guidance":"Test all relevant quorum combinations for the intended cluster size and failure model.","verification_guidance":"Prove or mechanically check the required intersection invariant for every supported configuration.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed replication","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consensus_Literature","Quorum_System_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B21-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Quorum Systems","concept":"Read and write quorum intersection","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can read and write quorums be selected for replicated data?","answer":"A replicated data system can define separate read and write quorum sizes. A common safety requirement is that every valid read quorum intersects every valid write quorum so a read can observe information from at least one replica participating in the relevant write.","explanation":"Read/write quorum design is protocol-specific. Intersection alone does not automatically provide a particular consistency guarantee; version selection and replica state also matter.","principle":"Quorum sizes must be derived together with the read and write protocol.","rule":"Do not infer consistency solely from numeric quorum sizes without analyzing replica versions and selection behavior.","correct_example":"A system with five replicas may require write quorums of three and read quorums of three, producing overlap under a simple equal-weight model.","invalid_example":"Using a read quorum of one and assuming it always contains the latest value merely because writes use a majority.","problem":"A read may contact a stale replica even when writes require a majority.","failure_mode":"Stale read","cause":"Read selection does not enforce the consistency property required by the application.","solution":"Define read quorum, version comparison, repair, or leader-read semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Route reads through a leader or strongly consistent replica set when appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger read guarantees can increase latency and reduce availability.","constraint":"Replica failure and membership changes must be included in quorum calculations.","edge_case":"A quorum may technically intersect a write quorum while the intersecting replica has not durably stored the required state.","security_implication":"Incorrect quorum authorization can permit stale or unauthorized state to be treated as authoritative.","performance_implication":"Increasing read quorum size increases network fan-out and aggregation latency.","testing_guidance":"Test reads against every relevant stale/fresh replica combination.","verification_guidance":"Verify the quorum intersection property and the protocol's freshness/version-selection rule separately.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Quorum intersection","Replication"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Quorum_System_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B21-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Quorum Systems","concept":"Quorum availability trade-off","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How does quorum size affect distributed-system availability?","answer":"More restrictive quorum requirements can preserve stronger safety properties but may make progress impossible when too many members are unavailable or unreachable. Quorum design therefore balances safety, availability, latency, and fault tolerance under the specific protocol model.","explanation":"A quorum protocol must distinguish safety from liveness. A system can remain safe while being unable to complete operations.","principle":"Never treat quorum safety and quorum availability as the same property.","rule":"Evaluate quorum availability against the maximum tolerated failure and partition scenarios.","correct_example":"A majority protocol can continue after losing fewer than half of its voting members, assuming the remaining members can communicate sufficiently.","invalid_example":"Claiming that any quorum protocol remains available during an arbitrary network partition.","problem":"Partitioned quorum members may be unable to form a valid decision quorum.","failure_mode":"Availability loss","cause":"Insufficient reachable members to satisfy quorum requirements.","solution":"Choose quorum topology and failure assumptions appropriate to the required availability target.","alternative":"Use geographically or failure-domain-aware quorum placement.","tradeoff":"Higher redundancy can improve availability but increases replication and coordination cost.","constraint":"The quorum rule must remain valid after membership changes.","edge_case":"A partition may leave multiple groups each believing they have enough local replicas if the quorum definition is incorrectly scoped.","security_implication":"Weak quorum isolation can create split-brain authorization.","performance_implication":"Larger quorums generally increase communication work and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Inject node and network failures and verify both safety and progress behavior.","verification_guidance":"Check safety under all modeled failures and explicitly state the liveness assumptions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Quorum systems","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consensus_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B22-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus","concept":"Consensus problem","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What problem does distributed consensus solve?","answer":"Distributed consensus is the problem of having multiple participating processes agree on a value or decision while satisfying defined safety properties despite specified failures and communication uncertainty.","explanation":"Consensus is an agreement problem, not merely leader election or message delivery. The exact guarantees depend on the system model and failure assumptions.","principle":"A consensus specification must define agreement, validity, termination or liveness conditions, and the failure model.","rule":"Do not claim consensus guarantees without stating the assumed failure and communication model.","correct_example":"A replicated service uses a consensus protocol to choose one command for a log position despite crash failures.","invalid_example":"Calling a best-effort broadcast mechanism consensus without an agreement guarantee.","problem":"Ambiguous consensus definitions lead to incorrect safety and liveness claims.","failure_mode":"Specification mismatch","cause":"Agreement requirements or failure assumptions are unspecified.","solution":"Define the consensus properties and system model before implementing the protocol.","alternative":"Use a simpler coordination mechanism when consensus is not actually required.","tradeoff":"Consensus provides strong coordination but introduces communication and availability costs.","constraint":"Some consensus models cannot guarantee progress under unrestricted asynchronous failures.","edge_case":"A protocol can preserve safety while temporarily making no progress.","security_implication":"If malicious participants are possible, crash-fault consensus assumptions may be insufficient.","performance_implication":"Consensus requires coordination and can add network round trips.","testing_guidance":"Test conflicting proposals, delayed messages, node crashes, leader changes, and message reordering.","verification_guidance":"Check agreement and validity invariants separately from liveness assumptions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed failures","Replication"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R01","D17-B20-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Lamport_Paxos_Made_Simple_2001","Distributed_Consensus_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B22-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus","concept":"Consensus safety versus liveness","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between consensus safety and liveness?","answer":"Safety means that the protocol does not violate its specified correctness properties, such as deciding incompatible values. Liveness means that the protocol eventually makes required progress under its stated assumptions.","explanation":"A distributed system can preserve safety while making no progress during failures. Therefore safety and liveness must be specified and evaluated independently.","principle":"Correctness claims must identify whether they concern safety or liveness.","rule":"Never infer liveness from safety or safety from observed progress.","correct_example":"A consensus cluster refuses to decide while it lacks a valid quorum, preserving safety at the cost of progress.","invalid_example":"Declaring a protocol safe merely because it eventually produced a value in one test run.","problem":"Operational success in one execution does not establish distributed safety.","failure_mode":"False correctness conclusion","cause":"Safety and liveness are conflated.","solution":"Define explicit safety invariants and separate liveness assumptions and tests.","alternative":"Use formal specification/model checking for safety-critical protocol properties.","tradeoff":"Stronger safety constraints can limit progress under adverse conditions.","constraint":"Liveness claims require assumptions about timing, failures, or eventual communication.","edge_case":"A system may alternate leaders indefinitely and remain safe while never committing new entries.","security_implication":"Safety violations can cause conflicting authorization or state decisions.","performance_implication":"Aggressive liveness mechanisms may increase unnecessary elections or coordination traffic.","testing_guidance":"Use fault injection to distinguish safe refusal from incorrect progress.","verification_guidance":"Prove safety independently and document assumptions needed for liveness.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consensus problem"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Lamport_Paxos_Made_Simple_2001","Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B22-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus","concept":"Consensus under asynchronous communication","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must consensus implementations state communication assumptions?","answer":"In asynchronous distributed systems, message delay and process execution speed may be unbounded within the model, making it impossible to distinguish some slow participants from failed participants using timing alone. Consensus liveness therefore requires explicit assumptions or mechanisms beyond an unrestricted asynchronous model.","explanation":"Consensus algorithms can maintain safety under asynchronous conditions while requiring additional timing or failure-detector assumptions for progress.","principle":"Communication-model assumptions are part of the correctness specification.","rule":"Document message-delay, processing-speed, failure, and timing assumptions for consensus implementations.","correct_example":"A consensus implementation uses timeouts for leader election but does not treat a timeout itself as proof that a node has permanently failed.","invalid_example":"Treating every delayed response as definitive evidence of node failure.","problem":"Network delay can resemble process failure.","failure_mode":"False failure detection","cause":"Timing uncertainty is interpreted as certainty.","solution":"Use protocol-defined epochs, terms, ballots, or failure-detector assumptions rather than raw timeout conclusions.","alternative":"Design safety to tolerate false suspicions while using eventual timing assumptions for liveness.","tradeoff":"More aggressive timeouts may improve failover speed but increase false suspicions and coordination overhead.","constraint":"Timeout values are environment-dependent and do not by themselves prove correctness.","edge_case":"A healthy but overloaded node can repeatedly exceed election or RPC timeouts.","security_implication":"An attacker able to delay traffic may trigger unnecessary leadership changes.","performance_implication":"Too-short timeouts can create election churn and increased network traffic.","testing_guidance":"Inject variable latency, packet loss, pauses, and temporary partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify safety independent of timeout accuracy; verify liveness only under stated timing assumptions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure detectors","Consensus safety and liveness"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R03","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consensus_Literature","Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B23-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Raft Consensus","concept":"Raft leader term","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What role does the term concept play in Raft?","answer":"A Raft term identifies a logical period of leadership and election activity. Servers use terms to reject stale leadership messages and establish which leadership information is newer.","explanation":"Terms provide a monotonic logical mechanism for distinguishing stale and current consensus activity.","principle":"Consensus messages must carry enough logical context to prevent obsolete leaders from acting as current leaders.","rule":"Persist and update term information according to the protocol specification.","correct_example":"A server receiving a message with a higher term updates its current term and transitions away from an obsolete leadership state as specified by Raft.","invalid_example":"Accepting a command from an old leader solely because the old leader previously held leadership.","problem":"Stale leaders may continue sending commands after a newer term exists.","failure_mode":"Stale-leader operation","cause":"Current-term validation is missing or incorrect.","solution":"Enforce term checks on all protocol messages and state transitions.","alternative":"Use another monotonically increasing epoch mechanism in a different consensus protocol.","tradeoff":"Term bookkeeping adds persistent protocol state but prevents stale coordination.","constraint":"Term persistence and transition rules must follow the consensus implementation's durability requirements.","edge_case":"A node may be partitioned for a long period and return with an obsolete term.","security_implication":"Forged or replayed stale protocol messages must not regain authority.","performance_implication":"Term checks are inexpensive but occur on protocol message paths.","testing_guidance":"Replay messages from old terms after leadership changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no stale-term participant can cause a conflicting committed decision.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Raft"],"prerequisites":["Consensus","Logical epochs"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B23-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Raft Consensus","concept":"Raft log matching property","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does the Raft log matching property establish?","answer":"If two logs contain entries with the same index and term, the entries at that index and all preceding entries are identical under the Raft log model.","explanation":"The property is central to preventing divergent histories from being treated as equivalent committed state.","principle":"Replicated logs require explicit rules for identifying and resolving divergent prefixes.","rule":"Log replication must enforce the protocol's matching and conflict-resolution rules rather than blindly appending entries.","correct_example":"A follower rejects an AppendEntries request when the required previous-log entry does not match the leader's supplied term/index information.","invalid_example":"Appending a leader entry without checking the required previous-log consistency condition.","problem":"Unvalidated append operations can create incompatible log histories.","failure_mode":"Log divergence","cause":"Previous-entry consistency checks are missing or incorrect.","solution":"Implement the protocol's log matching and conflict-resolution rules exactly.","alternative":"Use another replicated-log protocol with its own formally defined matching invariant.","tradeoff":"Consistency checks add protocol messages and processing but protect replicated history.","constraint":"The property applies within the protocol's defined log model and term/index semantics.","edge_case":"A follower may contain extra uncommitted entries that must be replaced according to the protocol.","security_implication":"Log integrity is critical because malicious divergence could influence replicated decisions.","performance_implication":"Conflict resolution may require multiple replication attempts or optimized backtracking.","testing_guidance":"Create followers with shorter, longer, and conflicting logs and verify reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Model-check or formally test the log matching invariant across representative states.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Raft"],"prerequisites":["Replicated logs","State machine replication"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R03","D17-B16-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B23-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Raft Consensus","concept":"Raft commit index","knowledge_type":"FACT","question":"What is the purpose of the Raft commit index?","answer":"The commit index identifies the highest log entry known to be committed according to Raft's protocol rules. Servers use committed entries as the basis for applying replicated commands to their state machines.","explanation":"A log entry being present on a server is not equivalent to that entry being committed.","principle":"Replication state and commitment state must be distinguished.","rule":"Apply commands to the replicated state machine only when the protocol determines the corresponding log entries are committed.","correct_example":"A follower receives a leader commit index and applies newly committed entries in order.","invalid_example":"Applying every locally appended entry immediately as an externally visible state-machine command.","problem":"Uncommitted entries may later be overwritten.","failure_mode":"Premature state-machine application","cause":"Presence in the local log is incorrectly treated as commitment.","solution":"Track commit state separately from log storage.","alternative":"Use another consensus protocol's explicit commit/learned-state mechanism.","tradeoff":"Delayed application may increase perceived latency but protects consistency.","constraint":"The exact commitment rule is protocol-specific.","edge_case":"A node can contain entries that are validly replicated but not yet committed.","security_implication":"External actions must not be triggered by uncommitted consensus state.","performance_implication":"Commit propagation and ordered application affect end-to-end latency.","testing_guidance":"Crash leaders before commitment and verify that uncommitted commands are not externally applied.","verification_guidance":"Check that every externally applied command corresponds to a committed log entry.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Raft"],"prerequisites":["Replicated log","State machine replication"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R01","D17-B20-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B24-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Raft Consensus","concept":"Leader completeness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does leader completeness mean in Raft?","answer":"Leader completeness means that if a log entry is committed in a given term, that entry will be present in the logs of leaders for later terms, subject to the Raft protocol's assumptions and rules.","explanation":"The property prevents a later valid leader from losing previously committed history.","principle":"Leadership changes must preserve committed state.","rule":"Election and log-up-to-date rules must prevent a leader from being elected with a history that excludes committed entries.","correct_example":"A candidate whose log is not sufficiently up to date according to Raft's election rule cannot obtain the votes required for leadership.","invalid_example":"Allowing any reachable server to become leader regardless of its replicated log state.","problem":"A leader lacking committed history could overwrite or omit prior decisions.","failure_mode":"Committed-history loss","cause":"Election safety or log freshness rules are violated.","solution":"Enforce the protocol's voting and log comparison rules.","alternative":"Use a consensus algorithm with equivalent leader/history preservation properties.","tradeoff":"Restrictive elections may delay leadership changes when candidates have stale logs.","constraint":"The property depends on correct implementation of voting and log replication rules.","edge_case":"A partitioned node may have an old term and stale log when it reconnects.","security_implication":"Authorization decisions encoded in committed state must not disappear during failover.","performance_implication":"Log freshness checks can influence election latency.","testing_guidance":"Create candidates with different log lengths and terms and test election outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Check that every committed entry remains represented after simulated leadership changes.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Raft"],"prerequisites":["Raft log matching","Raft election"],"related_records":["D17-B23-R02","D17-B13-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B24-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus Failure Modes","concept":"Stale leader after partition","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is a partitioned old leader dangerous in a consensus cluster?","answer":"A leader isolated from the quorum may continue operating on outdated information unless protocol rules prevent it from committing or externally authorizing new state. When connectivity changes, its messages may conflict with the current leadership epoch.","explanation":"The primary defense is not merely detecting that a node is stale but preventing stale authority from producing valid committed effects.","principle":"Leadership authority must be bounded by protocol state and quorum-based commitment.","rule":"Do not treat local leadership state as sufficient authority after communication uncertainty.","correct_example":"A former leader discovers a higher term and steps down before accepting further leader-authoritative operations.","invalid_example":"An isolated leader continues treating client commands as committed because it still believes it is leader.","problem":"Stale authority can create conflicting effects or client-visible inconsistency.","failure_mode":"Stale authority","cause":"Missing epoch validation, quorum commitment, or fencing.","solution":"Use terms/epochs, quorum rules, and external fencing where necessary.","alternative":"Use lease-based leadership only when its timing assumptions are explicitly satisfied.","tradeoff":"Additional fencing and quorum checks increase coordination overhead but reduce stale-authority risk.","constraint":"External side effects may require stronger fencing than replicated-log safety alone.","edge_case":"A node can be network-partitioned from the consensus quorum but still reach external clients.","security_implication":"Stale authority can become a privilege-escalation-like control path if authorization depends on leadership.","performance_implication":"Frequent fencing or quorum checks can add latency.","testing_guidance":"Partition the leader from the quorum while leaving it reachable by clients.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no stale leader can create a conflicting committed decision or unauthorized external effect.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consensus","Leader fencing"],"prerequisites":["Raft terms","Quorum"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014","Distributed_Consensus_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B24-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus Membership","concept":"Overlapping consensus configurations","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why must consensus membership changes preserve overlap between configurations?","answer":"Changing the voting membership of a consensus cluster requires a transition mechanism that prevents incompatible majorities from independently making conflicting decisions. Overlapping configurations allow the safety argument to connect decisions made under the old and new memberships.","explanation":"Raft uses joint consensus as one concrete approach to membership changes, requiring overlapping majorities during the transition.","principle":"Dynamic membership is part of the consensus protocol, not merely an administrative database update.","rule":"Treat membership changes as protocol state transitions with explicit safety conditions.","correct_example":"A cluster uses a documented joint-configuration phase before switching fully to the new membership.","invalid_example":"Instantly replacing the voting set while allowing old and new groups to make independent decisions.","problem":"Non-overlapping configurations can break quorum intersection across the transition.","failure_mode":"Configuration split-brain","cause":"Membership changed without a safe transition protocol.","solution":"Use overlapping configurations or another formally justified reconfiguration mechanism.","alternative":"Perform controlled maintenance with a protocol that explicitly prevents concurrent incompatible configurations.","tradeoff":"Safe reconfiguration adds transition complexity and may temporarily increase coordination cost.","constraint":"The transition mechanism must account for failures during reconfiguration.","edge_case":"A member can be removed while unreachable, creating subtle recovery scenarios.","security_implication":"Unauthorized membership changes can alter the set of nodes trusted to make decisions.","performance_implication":"Reconfiguration may temporarily require additional replication or coordination.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures during every stage of membership transition.","verification_guidance":"Verify quorum intersection across old, transition, and new configurations.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consensus","Quorum systems"],"prerequisites":["Quorum intersection","Membership changes"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B14-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B25-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Transactions","concept":"Atomic commit","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does atomic commit require in a distributed transaction?","answer":"Atomic commit requires that participating transactional components reach a consistent commit-or-abort outcome according to the transaction protocol, rather than independently exposing incompatible final outcomes.","explanation":"Atomicity across multiple participants requires coordination because failures can occur between local transaction steps.","principle":"Distributed transaction outcome must be treated as a coordinated decision rather than a collection of unrelated local commits.","rule":"Define the transaction coordinator, participant states, durable decision state, and recovery behavior explicitly.","correct_example":"All participants record and follow the transaction's final commit decision according to a defined commit protocol.","invalid_example":"Each participant independently commits when its local work succeeds.","problem":"Independent commits can leave the system partially committed.","failure_mode":"Partial commit","cause":"No shared atomic decision protocol.","solution":"Use a distributed transaction protocol or redesign the operation around compensating/idempotent workflows.","alternative":"Sagas, transactional outbox, or application-level coordination may be appropriate when strict atomicity is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Strong atomic commit can increase latency, coordination, and failure complexity.","constraint":"Atomic commit does not automatically provide global serializability or arbitrary business invariants.","edge_case":"The coordinator can fail after participants have prepared but before receiving the final decision.","security_implication":"Partial authorization or financial effects can occur if commit boundaries are incorrectly implemented.","performance_implication":"Multiple participants introduce network round trips and coordination latency.","testing_guidance":"Crash coordinator and participants at every protocol transition.","verification_guidance":"Check that no allowed failure schedule produces incompatible committed outcomes.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Transactions","Failure recovery"],"related_records":["D16-B31-R01","D16-B31-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Transaction_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B25-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Two-Phase Commit","concept":"Two-phase commit prepare and commit phases","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"How does two-phase commit coordinate a distributed transaction?","answer":"Two-phase commit first asks participants to prepare and report whether they can commit, then uses the coordinator's decision to commit or abort participants. Participants persist protocol state so recovery can continue according to the protocol.","explanation":"The protocol separates readiness from the final decision. The prepare phase does not mean the transaction is already committed.","principle":"Participant readiness and final commit decision are distinct states.","rule":"Persist protocol state required for recovery before acknowledging durable transitions.","correct_example":"A participant records prepared state durably before acknowledging that it is prepared.","invalid_example":"A participant reports prepared before ensuring it can recover its prepared state after a crash.","problem":"Lost prepared state can make recovery inconsistent with the coordinator's decision.","failure_mode":"Recovery inconsistency","cause":"Protocol state was not durably recorded as required.","solution":"Persist transaction state at the required protocol boundaries and implement recovery rules.","alternative":"Use a different transaction protocol when blocking behavior is unacceptable.","tradeoff":"Durability and coordination provide atomicity but increase latency and resource retention.","constraint":"Participants may need to retain locks/resources while awaiting the final decision.","edge_case":"The coordinator fails after participants prepare but before they learn the final decision.","security_implication":"Prepared but undecided resources can hold security-sensitive state or authorization locks.","performance_implication":"Prepared transactions can retain locks and consume connection/storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Crash participants and coordinator independently between every protocol message.","verification_guidance":"Verify atomic outcome and recovery behavior for every modeled crash point.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Atomic commit","Durable transaction state"],"related_records":["D17-B25-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Transaction_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B25-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Two-Phase Commit","concept":"Two-phase commit blocking","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can two-phase commit block?","answer":"A participant that has prepared may be unable to unilaterally decide the final outcome when the coordinator or required decision information is unavailable. The participant may therefore retain resources while waiting for recovery or a valid decision.","explanation":"This blocking behavior is a consequence of coordinating a shared commit decision under failures.","principle":"Atomic commit guarantees and availability behavior must be evaluated together.","rule":"Do not assume two-phase commit remains fully available during coordinator failure.","correct_example":"A prepared participant enters a recovery state and waits according to the transaction protocol rather than guessing commit or abort.","invalid_example":"A participant independently commits after coordinator failure without protocol authorization.","problem":"Independent guessing can violate atomicity.","failure_mode":"Prepared-state blocking","cause":"Final decision is unavailable and unilateral decision would be unsafe.","solution":"Implement durable recovery and coordinator-recovery procedures; consider alternative transaction architectures when required.","alternative":"Use consensus-backed commit or compensating workflow designs when their semantics fit the workload.","tradeoff":"Reducing blocking generally requires additional coordination, protocol complexity, or weaker transaction semantics.","constraint":"No alternative automatically provides the same guarantees under all failure models.","edge_case":"Long network partitions can extend resource retention significantly.","security_implication":"Held locks may cause denial of service or prolonged authorization-state retention.","performance_implication":"Blocked transactions consume connections, locks, memory, and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Partition the coordinator after prepare and measure resource retention and recovery behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery never produces commit/abort disagreement.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Two-phase commit"],"prerequisites":["Atomic commit"],"related_records":["D17-B25-R02","D17-B19-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Transaction_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B26-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Three-Phase Commit","concept":"Three-phase commit pre-commit phase","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What additional coordination does three-phase commit introduce compared with two-phase commit?","answer":"Three-phase commit introduces an intermediate pre-commit stage intended to reduce certain blocking scenarios by separating the decision-to-commit transition from the final commit state.","explanation":"The additional phase does not make distributed coordination free of failure assumptions. Its guarantees depend on the system model and timing assumptions.","principle":"Adding a protocol phase changes failure-state structure but does not eliminate the need for explicit assumptions.","rule":"Analyze every additional protocol state and its recovery transitions rather than treating 3PC as universally non-blocking.","correct_example":"The protocol defines distinct can-commit, pre-commit, and commit states with explicit recovery rules.","invalid_example":"Assuming that adding a third message round automatically guarantees progress during arbitrary network partitions.","problem":"Incorrect assumptions about failure and timing can invalidate the intended availability behavior.","failure_mode":"Incorrect recovery","cause":"Protocol states are implemented without their required system assumptions.","solution":"Implement the exact protocol model and document timing/failure assumptions.","alternative":"Use consensus-based coordination or application-level compensation where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Additional phases increase message and state complexity.","constraint":"The protocol's claimed non-blocking behavior depends on its failure model.","edge_case":"Network partitions and delayed messages can produce ambiguous recovery situations.","security_implication":"Incorrect phase transitions can produce unauthorized or inconsistent finalization.","performance_implication":"Extra coordination increases normal-case message and latency overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures at every phase boundary and delay protocol messages.","verification_guidance":"Verify all recovery transitions against the protocol's formal safety and liveness assumptions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Two-phase commit","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B25-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Three_Phase_Commit_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B26-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Transactions","concept":"Coordinator failure recovery","knowledge_type":"RECOVERY_PATTERN","question":"What must distributed transaction recovery preserve after coordinator failure?","answer":"Recovery must preserve the transaction protocol's atomic outcome rules and must use durable protocol state or an authoritative decision source rather than guessing the final outcome.","explanation":"Coordinator failure is a normal distributed-systems failure case, not an exceptional condition that can be ignored.","principle":"Recovery logic is part of the transaction protocol itself.","rule":"Define durable recovery records and decision-reconstruction rules before production deployment.","correct_example":"A recovering participant consults durable transaction state or the protocol's authoritative recovery mechanism before finalizing the transaction.","invalid_example":"A participant commits because the transaction appeared successful before the coordinator crashed.","problem":"Local observations may not represent the globally agreed transaction outcome.","failure_mode":"Incorrect recovery decision","cause":"Recovery relies on incomplete local evidence.","solution":"Use durable decision state, transaction logs, or a formally specified recovery protocol.","alternative":"Redesign the workflow so the operation can be retried or compensated safely.","tradeoff":"Stronger recovery coordination increases implementation and operational complexity.","constraint":"Recovery semantics must cover crashes, partitions, duplicate messages, and delayed messages.","edge_case":"Recovery may receive stale protocol messages from a previous transaction incarnation.","security_implication":"Recovery must authenticate transaction identities and decision records to prevent forged commits.","performance_implication":"Recovery scans and coordination can increase restart latency.","testing_guidance":"Simulate crashes at every state transition and replay delayed messages after recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify idempotent recovery and invariant preservation across repeated recovery attempts.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed transactions","Durability"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R03","D17-B25-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Transaction_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B26-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Transaction Architecture","concept":"Atomic transaction versus compensating workflow","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When can a compensating workflow replace a distributed atomic transaction?","answer":"A compensating workflow can replace strict distributed atomicity when the business operation can tolerate intermediate states and has well-defined corrective actions for failures.","explanation":"Compensation is semantic rather than a rollback of every physical side effect. Some effects cannot be perfectly undone.","principle":"Transaction architecture should follow business invariants and side-effect semantics rather than forcing database-style atomicity onto every workflow.","rule":"Use compensation only when each failure state has a defined and acceptable corrective path.","correct_example":"An order workflow reserves inventory, charges payment, and uses explicit compensation for a failed downstream fulfillment step.","invalid_example":"Assuming that sending a compensating command always restores the exact previous state.","problem":"Some side effects are irreversible or externally observable.","failure_mode":"Incomplete compensation","cause":"The workflow assumes reversibility that the real-world side effect does not provide.","solution":"Model irreversible effects explicitly and design business-level reconciliation.","alternative":"Use a distributed transaction where strict atomicity is genuinely required and supported.","tradeoff":"Compensation can improve availability and decouple services but introduces complex intermediate states and reconciliation.","constraint":"Compensation must be idempotent and safe under retries.","edge_case":"A compensation action can itself fail or be duplicated.","security_implication":"Compensation must enforce authorization independently rather than assuming the original authorization remains valid.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous compensation may reduce synchronous latency but increase reconciliation workload.","testing_guidance":"Test partial success, duplicate compensation, compensation failure, and delayed events.","verification_guidance":"Verify business invariants rather than assuming database rollback semantics.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed transactions","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Transaction_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B27-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Concurrency Control","concept":"Global serializability","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is global serializability in a distributed database system?","answer":"Global serializability means that the combined effect of concurrent transactions across distributed participants is equivalent to some serial execution consistent with the system's transaction model.","explanation":"Local serializability at each participant does not automatically guarantee serializability of the global transaction history.","principle":"Distributed concurrency control must reason about cross-participant dependencies.","rule":"Do not infer global serializability solely from independent local transaction guarantees.","correct_example":"A distributed transaction coordinator enforces a global ordering or dependency rule that prevents cycles across participants.","invalid_example":"Declaring the global schedule serializable because each database shard independently reports serializable local execution.","problem":"Cross-shard dependencies can form global serialization cycles.","failure_mode":"Global anomaly","cause":"Local concurrency control ignores distributed transaction dependencies.","solution":"Use a globally coordinated concurrency-control or transaction protocol.","alternative":"Redesign operations to avoid cross-partition transactional dependencies.","tradeoff":"Global coordination increases latency and reduces independent scalability.","constraint":"The serialization model must be defined consistently across participants.","edge_case":"Retries and partial failures can create additional dependency edges.","security_implication":"Global invariants can be violated if transactions observe inconsistent cross-domain state.","performance_implication":"Global coordination increases synchronization and conflict-management overhead.","testing_guidance":"Generate cross-participant transaction schedules and search for serialization cycles.","verification_guidance":"Construct dependency graphs and verify absence of prohibited cycles under the chosen isolation model.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Transactions","Concurrency control"],"related_records":["D16-B33-R03","D17-B25-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B27-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Concurrency Control","concept":"Distributed deadlock","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can deadlocks arise across distributed participants?","answer":"A distributed deadlock can arise when transactions or processes on different nodes hold resources while waiting for resources held by others, forming a cycle that spans multiple participants.","explanation":"No single node necessarily sees the entire wait cycle.","principle":"Distributed resource ownership requires cross-node deadlock detection or prevention when blocking dependencies can span nodes.","rule":"Model distributed wait dependencies explicitly when designing cross-node locking.","correct_example":"A distributed lock manager detects a cycle involving resources owned on multiple nodes and aborts or otherwise resolves one participant.","invalid_example":"Checking only local wait graphs and assuming a global cycle cannot exist.","problem":"Local deadlock detection can miss cross-node cycles.","failure_mode":"Undetected distributed deadlock","cause":"Incomplete dependency visibility.","solution":"Use distributed detection, ordering, timeouts, leases, or application-level lock ordering as appropriate.","alternative":"Avoid distributed locking through partitioned ownership or optimistic concurrency.","tradeoff":"Detection and coordination add overhead; prevention can constrain concurrency.","constraint":"Timeouts detect suspected blocking but do not necessarily prove a deadlock.","edge_case":"Network partitions can make a live dependency appear dead.","security_implication":"Attackers can intentionally create resource contention or lock retention to cause denial of service.","performance_implication":"Deadlock detection traffic and lock metadata increase overhead.","testing_guidance":"Construct wait cycles across multiple nodes and inject message delays.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every reachable deadlock cycle has a defined resolution path.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed locking","Transactions"],"related_records":["D16-B34-R01","D16-B32-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B27-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Concurrency Control","concept":"Timestamp ordering","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can timestamps be used for distributed concurrency control?","answer":"Timestamp-ordering schemes assign transactions or operations logical ordering metadata and use that metadata to determine whether conflicting operations are permitted or must be rejected/retried.","explanation":"Distributed timestamps require careful handling of clock semantics. Physical wall-clock time is not automatically a safe global transaction order.","principle":"Concurrency-control ordering requires a well-defined logical ordering mechanism.","rule":"Do not use unsynchronized wall-clock timestamps as a correctness proof for global transaction ordering.","correct_example":"A protocol uses logical transaction timestamps with explicit conflict rules and durable metadata.","invalid_example":"Assuming two physical timestamps from different hosts always represent a globally ordered sequence of events.","problem":"Clock skew can invalidate assumed ordering.","failure_mode":"Incorrect transaction ordering","cause":"Physical time is treated as globally exact.","solution":"Use logical timestamps or a protocol with explicit clock uncertainty handling.","alternative":"Use locking, validation-based concurrency control, or consensus-backed ordering.","tradeoff":"Logical ordering can simplify coordination but may increase abort/retry rates.","constraint":"Timestamp uniqueness and ordering rules must be defined for the transaction model.","edge_case":"Clock corrections can move wall-clock time backward or forward.","security_implication":"Manipulated time sources can affect protocols that incorrectly trust wall-clock ordering.","performance_implication":"Timestamp metadata is cheap, but retries caused by conflicts can reduce throughput.","testing_guidance":"Inject clock skew and concurrent conflicting transactions.","verification_guidance":"Verify ordering invariants independently of physical clock accuracy.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Logical clocks","Concurrency control"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R01","D17-B12-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B28-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Linearizability","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does linearizability require from a distributed object?","answer":"Linearizability requires that each operation appear to take effect atomically at some point between its invocation and response, while respecting real-time ordering between non-overlapping operations.","explanation":"Linearizability provides a strong per-object consistency model but does not automatically define transaction semantics across multiple objects.","principle":"Strong consistency claims must specify the object and operation boundaries being ordered.","rule":"Do not claim system-wide linearizability from isolated strongly consistent components without analyzing cross-component operations.","correct_example":"A concurrent register implementation returns results consistent with a single sequential history that respects non-overlapping operation order.","invalid_example":"Calling a system linearizable because most reads usually observe recent writes.","problem":"Informal freshness does not establish linearizability.","failure_mode":"Consistency-model misclassification","cause":"Observed freshness is confused with formal operation ordering.","solution":"Specify and test the linearizability history property.","alternative":"Use weaker consistency when the application does not require real-time ordering.","tradeoff":"Linearizability can require stronger coordination and can reduce availability during partitions.","constraint":"The exact operation interface and failure model must be defined.","edge_case":"Concurrent operations may be ordered either way if neither establishes the required real-time precedence.","security_implication":"Authorization state may require strong consistency to avoid accepting stale revocations.","performance_implication":"Strong coordination can increase latency and cross-node communication.","testing_guidance":"Generate concurrent operation histories and check them for a valid linearization.","verification_guidance":"Use history-based linearizability checking rather than simple response-value sampling.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Concurrent histories"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R01","D16-B76-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B28-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Sequential consistency","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is sequential consistency?","answer":"Sequential consistency requires that the result of concurrent operations be equivalent to some sequential execution that preserves the program order of operations from each individual process.","explanation":"Unlike linearizability, sequential consistency does not require preservation of real-time ordering between non-overlapping operations from different processes.","principle":"Consistency models differ in the ordering constraints they impose.","rule":"Do not use sequential consistency as a synonym for linearizability.","correct_example":"A history is accepted when there exists a single sequential ordering consistent with each process's local operation order.","invalid_example":"Rejecting a sequentially consistent history solely because two operations from different processes were observed in an unexpected real-time order.","problem":"Stronger real-time requirements are incorrectly imposed on a weaker model.","failure_mode":"Model mismatch","cause":"Consistency definitions are conflated.","solution":"Represent consistency guarantees formally before designing or evaluating an implementation.","alternative":"Linearizability when real-time ordering is required.","tradeoff":"Sequential consistency can permit more implementation freedom than linearizability but provides weaker real-time guarantees.","constraint":"Program-order preservation remains mandatory.","edge_case":"Concurrent operations from separate clients can be reordered if their individual program orders remain valid.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive state may require stronger guarantees than sequential consistency.","performance_implication":"Weaker ordering can reduce coordination requirements.","testing_guidance":"Construct histories where cross-client real-time order differs from the allowed sequential order.","verification_guidance":"Check whether a valid global sequential history exists while preserving per-process order.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Concurrent histories"],"related_records":["D17-B28-R01","D17-B11-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B28-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Causal consistency","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does causal consistency preserve?","answer":"Causal consistency requires causally related operations to be observed in an order consistent with their causal relationship, while operations that are concurrent may be observed in different orders depending on the system's model.","explanation":"Causality is weaker than a single total order and can therefore permit more distributed concurrency.","principle":"The system must preserve required causal dependencies without necessarily globally ordering unrelated concurrent operations.","rule":"Track or derive causal dependencies explicitly when application correctness depends on them.","correct_example":"If operation B is causally dependent on operation A, replicas do not expose B as preceding A.","invalid_example":"Allowing a replica to expose a causally dependent update before the update it depends on.","problem":"Causal dependency violations can produce impossible application histories.","failure_mode":"Causal inversion","cause":"Dependency metadata is missing or ignored.","solution":"Use causal metadata or a protocol that preserves dependency order.","alternative":"Use stronger consistency when the application requires total or real-time ordering.","tradeoff":"Causal consistency can provide useful ordering with less coordination than strong total ordering.","constraint":"The system must define what creates causal relationships.","edge_case":"Causality can span asynchronous messages and multiple services.","security_implication":"Security policy changes and revocations may require causal propagation guarantees.","performance_implication":"Causal metadata increases message and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Generate dependency chains across replicas and delay individual messages.","verification_guidance":"Check that every observed history preserves all modeled causal edges.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Happened-before","Consistency models"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R02","D17-B12-R02","D16-B76-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B29-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistency and Replication","concept":"Session guarantees","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What are session guarantees in a distributed system?","answer":"Session guarantees define consistency properties from the perspective of an individual client session, such as requiring later reads to observe a value at least as fresh as a value already observed in that session.","explanation":"Session guarantees provide application-useful consistency without necessarily imposing one global ordering across all clients.","principle":"Consistency can be specified relative to client-observable histories rather than only as a global system property.","rule":"Define session identity and state propagation explicitly when implementing session guarantees.","correct_example":"After a client observes version N, subsequent reads in the same session do not return a version older than N.","invalid_example":"Guaranteeing session monotonicity while routing requests to replicas with no mechanism for tracking observed versions.","problem":"The next request may reach a stale replica.","failure_mode":"Session regression","cause":"Session consistency metadata is not propagated or enforced.","solution":"Carry version/session metadata or route reads to sufficiently fresh replicas.","alternative":"Use stronger global consistency when session guarantees are insufficient.","tradeoff":"Session guarantees can improve user-visible consistency without requiring all operations to be globally serialized.","constraint":"Session identity must remain stable across retries and service boundaries when required.","edge_case":"A session can migrate between regions or service instances.","security_implication":"Session metadata must not allow one client to access another client's consistency state.","performance_implication":"Additional metadata and routing can affect request size and latency.","testing_guidance":"Read from alternating fresh and stale replicas within the same session.","verification_guidance":"Check monotonicity properties over complete session histories.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Replication"],"related_records":["D16-B76-R02","D16-B76-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B29-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication Consistency","concept":"Read-your-writes consistency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What does read-your-writes consistency require?","answer":"Read-your-writes consistency requires that after a client successfully performs a write, subsequent reads in the relevant session observe that write or a state that includes it, subject to the system's defined semantics.","explanation":"The guarantee is client-observable and does not necessarily require every replica to be immediately synchronized.","principle":"A successful write should not disappear from the writer's subsequent view when the application promises read-your-writes behavior.","rule":"Define the scope of the session and the version/freshness mechanism used to enforce the guarantee.","correct_example":"After a client updates profile version 12, subsequent session reads return version 12 or newer.","invalid_example":"Routing the immediate post-write read to an arbitrary stale replica with no freshness constraint.","problem":"Users can observe their own confirmed update as missing.","failure_mode":"Read-after-write regression","cause":"Write and subsequent read have no consistency coordination.","solution":"Use session metadata, leader reads, sticky routing, or an equivalent freshness mechanism.","alternative":"Use a stronger read model if the application requires global immediate visibility.","tradeoff":"Freshness routing may increase latency or reduce replica-selection flexibility.","constraint":"The guarantee applies only within its defined session and operation scope.","edge_case":"Retries can make it ambiguous whether the write completed unless request identity is idempotent.","security_implication":"Stale authorization updates can expose data after a policy change.","performance_implication":"Routing to sufficiently fresh replicas may reduce load-balancing options.","testing_guidance":"Perform writes followed by reads through different replicas and network paths.","verification_guidance":"Check session histories for any read older than the confirmed write.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Replication","Session guarantees"],"related_records":["D16-B76-R02","D17-B18-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B29-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistency Models","concept":"Consistency selection by invariant","knowledge_type":"DECISION_RULE","question":"How should an engineer select a distributed consistency model?","answer":"Select the weakest consistency model that still preserves the application's explicit correctness invariants, rather than selecting a model solely by popularity or perceived strength.","explanation":"Different application operations can require different guarantees. The correct choice depends on what anomalies are acceptable and what invariants must never be violated.","principle":"Consistency requirements should be derived from application invariants.","rule":"Document each critical operation's required visibility, ordering, and conflict semantics before selecting replication behavior.","correct_example":"A security-revocation path requires stronger freshness while analytics aggregation permits delayed eventual convergence.","invalid_example":"Using eventual consistency everywhere without checking whether authorization or uniqueness invariants tolerate stale state.","problem":"Weak consistency can violate business or security invariants.","failure_mode":"Invariant violation","cause":"Consistency model selected without analyzing required semantics.","solution":"Map application invariants to explicit consistency requirements.","alternative":"Partition the workload so different data paths use different consistency levels.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency increases coordination cost; weaker consistency increases application-level conflict/reconciliation requirements.","constraint":"Consistency is not a substitute for correct authorization or transactional design.","edge_case":"A single entity may participate in operations with different consistency requirements.","security_implication":"Security-critical state generally requires explicit freshness and revocation semantics.","performance_implication":"Weaker guarantees can reduce coordination latency and improve availability.","testing_guidance":"Inject stale reads and reordered updates and evaluate invariant preservation.","verification_guidance":"Test the actual application invariants under the chosen consistency model, not merely replica convergence.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Distributed invariants"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B28-R01","D17-B28-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B30-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"CAP Theorem","concept":"CAP theorem scope","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does the CAP theorem actually constrain?","answer":"CAP concerns the simultaneous guarantees of consistency, availability, and partition tolerance in a distributed system under network partition conditions. The common simplified statement that a system simply chooses any two of three properties is incomplete because partition tolerance describes behavior when partitions occur and consistency/availability trade-offs arise under that condition.","explanation":"CAP should be analyzed using precise definitions of the consistency and availability properties being claimed and the partition model being considered.","principle":"CAP is about behavior during partitions, not a general rule that every system permanently chooses two properties.","rule":"State the exact consistency and availability definitions before applying CAP reasoning.","correct_example":"An engineer analyzes whether a service rejects some operations during a partition to preserve its specified consistency guarantee.","invalid_example":"Claiming that CAP requires a system to permanently choose exactly two of C, A, and P under all conditions.","problem":"The simplified slogan obscures the actual design trade-off.","failure_mode":"CAP misapplication","cause":"Informal interpretation is treated as the theorem itself.","solution":"Specify consistency, availability, and partition assumptions precisely.","alternative":"Use application-specific consistency and failure analysis rather than relying on CAP labels alone.","tradeoff":"Preserving stronger consistency during a partition can require refusing or delaying some operations.","constraint":"CAP does not quantify all latency, performance, or durability trade-offs.","edge_case":"A system may provide different guarantees for different operations or data types.","security_implication":"Availability during partitions must not override security invariants.","performance_implication":"Consistency coordination can increase latency, especially across regions.","testing_guidance":"Partition network paths and observe each operation's documented behavior.","verification_guidance":"Map observed behavior to explicit consistency and availability specifications.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Network partitions"],"related_records":["D17-B28-R01","D17-B21-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Gilbert_Lynch_CAP_2002"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B30-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"CAP and Network Partitions","concept":"Partition-triggered consistency versus availability decision","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What design decision appears when a network partition prevents coordination?","answer":"When a partition prevents the coordination required for a requested consistency guarantee, the system must follow its defined policy: it may reject or delay some operations to preserve the guarantee, or permit operations with weaker consistency semantics if the application allows them.","explanation":"The correct behavior depends on the application's invariants and documented consistency model.","principle":"Partition behavior must be designed explicitly rather than emerging accidentally from timeout handling.","rule":"Define operation behavior under partition before production deployment.","correct_example":"A strongly consistent write rejects when the required quorum is unavailable rather than silently accepting an uncommittable state.","invalid_example":"Silently accepting conflicting writes during a partition while still advertising strong consistency.","problem":"Undocumented partition behavior can violate application invariants.","failure_mode":"Partition-induced invariant violation","cause":"Fallback behavior weakens consistency without being modeled.","solution":"Define explicit degraded-mode semantics and communicate them to callers.","alternative":"Allow eventual updates only for data whose invariants tolerate reconciliation.","tradeoff":"Rejecting operations preserves stronger guarantees but reduces availability during partitions.","constraint":"Fallback behavior must not contradict externally documented semantics.","edge_case":"A temporary partition may later heal and require conflict resolution if writes were accepted independently.","security_implication":"Fail-open behavior can be dangerous for authorization and policy state.","performance_implication":"Fail-closed consistency can increase retry traffic and perceived latency.","testing_guidance":"Run partition tests for every critical operation and verify documented outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Check invariants before, during, and after partition recovery.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["CAP","Consistency models","Quorum"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R03","D17-B30-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Gilbert_Lynch_CAP_2002","Distributed_Consistency_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B30-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"PACELC","concept":"Latency-consistency-availability trade-off beyond partitions","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What additional trade-off does PACELC emphasize beyond CAP?","answer":"PACELC is a design framework that emphasizes an additional trade-off between latency and consistency when the system is operating normally without a partition, while also discussing consistency versus availability when a partition occurs.","explanation":"PACELC extends the discussion beyond partition behavior by highlighting normal-operation latency costs associated with stronger coordination.","principle":"Distributed-system design must evaluate both failure-mode behavior and normal-operation coordination cost.","rule":"Measure or model normal-path latency separately from partition-path availability and consistency behavior.","correct_example":"A globally replicated service evaluates the latency cost of synchronous cross-region coordination even when all regions are healthy.","invalid_example":"Claiming that CAP alone fully describes the system's latency/consistency trade-offs during normal operation.","problem":"Normal-path coordination cost can dominate user-visible latency even without failures.","failure_mode":"Normal-path latency surprise","cause":"Only partition behavior was analyzed.","solution":"Model consistency coordination latency under healthy and degraded network conditions.","alternative":"Use asynchronous replication or weaker consistency for latency-sensitive operations where invariants allow it.","tradeoff":"Lower normal-path latency may require weaker consistency or asynchronous propagation.","constraint":"PACELC is a conceptual trade-off framework, not a substitute for a precise consistency specification.","edge_case":"Different operations may choose different consistency/latency policies.","security_implication":"Latency optimization must not weaken security-critical consistency guarantees without explicit authorization.","performance_implication":"Cross-region coordination can significantly affect tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark local and cross-region paths under both healthy and degraded network conditions.","verification_guidance":"Validate that measured behavior matches the documented consistency and latency model.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["CAP","Consistency models","Distributed latency"],"related_records":["D17-B30-R01","D17-B28-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["PACELC_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B31-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Paxos","concept":"Paxos proposal numbers and ballots","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why does Paxos require ordered proposal or ballot numbers?","answer":"Paxos uses ordered proposal or ballot identifiers so participants can distinguish newer protocol attempts from older ones and enforce rules that prevent an obsolete proposal from overriding a newer accepted state.","explanation":"The ballot number is protocol metadata used to establish ordering between competing proposal attempts. It is distinct from the log or command slot being decided.","principle":"Distributed coordination requires a monotonic logical mechanism for distinguishing competing protocol rounds.","rule":"Proposal identifiers must satisfy the uniqueness and ordering requirements of the selected Paxos variant.","correct_example":"A proposer with a higher ballot supersedes an older ballot according to the protocol's acceptance rules.","invalid_example":"Using arbitrary non-ordered request identifiers as if they established Paxos proposal precedence.","problem":"Participants cannot consistently determine which proposal attempt is newer.","failure_mode":"Proposal-order ambiguity","cause":"Ballot identity or ordering is not correctly maintained.","solution":"Use unique, totally ordered ballot identifiers as required by the protocol.","alternative":"Use another consensus protocol with its own epoch or term mechanism.","tradeoff":"Ballot bookkeeping adds protocol state but provides a basis for resolving competing proposals.","constraint":"Ballot ordering alone does not establish consensus; the complete protocol invariants are required.","edge_case":"Multiple proposers may generate competing ballots concurrently.","security_implication":"Predictable or forgeable protocol identifiers can facilitate replay or stale-message attacks if authentication is absent.","performance_implication":"Higher contention can cause additional proposal rounds and message traffic.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent proposers with overlapping and increasing ballot numbers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that lower-ballot proposals cannot violate the protocol's acceptance invariants after a higher ballot becomes active.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Paxos"],"prerequisites":["Consensus","Logical ordering"],"related_records":["D17-B23-R01","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex","Paxos_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B31-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Paxos","concept":"Paxos acceptor promise","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What safety purpose does an acceptor promise serve in Paxos?","answer":"An acceptor promise prevents it from accepting proposals below a specified ballot threshold after it has promised to consider a newer ballot, subject to the exact Paxos variant and protocol rules.","explanation":"The promise constrains future acceptance behavior and is part of the mechanism that preserves agreement across competing proposal rounds.","principle":"Safety depends on monotonic participant state that prevents obsolete protocol rounds from being accepted after newer rounds establish constraints.","rule":"Persist or otherwise reliably maintain promise state according to the protocol's durability requirements.","correct_example":"An acceptor rejects a proposal whose ballot is below its recorded promise threshold.","invalid_example":"Accepting an older proposal after recording a promise for a higher ballot.","problem":"A participant can violate the assumptions used by the consensus safety proof.","failure_mode":"Acceptor invariant violation","cause":"Promise state is lost, ignored, or compared incorrectly.","solution":"Implement monotonic ballot checks and durable state transitions as required by the protocol.","alternative":"Use a different consensus implementation with independently specified invariants.","tradeoff":"Persistent protocol state adds storage operations but protects safety across crashes.","constraint":"The exact state required depends on the Paxos variant.","edge_case":"A crash followed by recovery must not cause an acceptor to forget a safety-critical promise.","security_implication":"Rollback or tampering with persisted ballot state can undermine consensus safety.","performance_implication":"Durable writes on protocol state can contribute to consensus latency.","testing_guidance":"Crash an acceptor immediately after promise-state changes and restart it with stale local memory.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovered acceptors never accept proposals prohibited by their durable protocol state.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Paxos"],"prerequisites":["Paxos ballots","Durable state"],"related_records":["D17-B31-R01","D17-B16-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B31-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Paxos","concept":"Paxos quorum intersection for safety","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why does Paxos depend on intersecting quorums?","answer":"Paxos relies on quorum intersection so that sufficiently large sets of acceptors share members across relevant proposal rounds. This overlap carries information about previously accepted values into later rounds and supports the protocol's agreement proof.","explanation":"The exact quorum construction can vary across Paxos variants, but the safety argument depends on preserving the required overlap property.","principle":"Consensus safety requires quorum structures that preserve information across competing rounds.","rule":"Verify the quorum intersection assumption whenever cluster size, weights, or configuration changes.","correct_example":"A majority quorum in a fixed crash-fault configuration intersects every other majority quorum.","invalid_example":"Changing quorum membership so that two valid decision quorums can be disjoint without updating the protocol proof.","problem":"A later proposal may lack information about a previously accepted value.","failure_mode":"Consensus safety violation","cause":"Required quorum intersection was broken.","solution":"Maintain the protocol's quorum intersection property during operation and reconfiguration.","alternative":"Use a formally specified generalized quorum system.","tradeoff":"Larger or more constrained quorums can improve safety relationships while reducing availability.","constraint":"Quorum requirements depend on the supported failure model.","edge_case":"Reconfiguration can temporarily involve multiple quorum definitions.","security_implication":"A malicious configuration change that destroys quorum intersection can undermine consensus.","performance_implication":"Larger quorums increase communication and aggregation overhead.","testing_guidance":"Enumerate quorum combinations for small cluster configurations and test reconfiguration transitions.","verification_guidance":"Formally or exhaustively verify quorum intersection for every supported configuration.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Paxos","Quorum systems"],"prerequisites":["Quorum intersection","Consensus"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R01","D17-B24-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B32-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Multi-Paxos","concept":"Stable leader optimization","knowledge_type":"OPTIMIZATION","question":"Why can a stable leader reduce the cost of repeated Paxos decisions?","answer":"When one leader remains stable, a Multi-Paxos implementation can avoid repeating some coordination work needed to establish leadership for every individual log slot, allowing subsequent decisions to use a more efficient steady-state path.","explanation":"This optimization preserves the underlying safety protocol while reducing repeated setup work. The exact optimization depends on the implementation and variant.","principle":"Stable leadership can amortize coordination costs across multiple decisions.","rule":"Optimize repeated consensus only after preserving the base protocol's safety invariants.","correct_example":"A stable leader processes a sequence of log slots without unnecessarily restarting the full leadership-establishment procedure for every slot.","invalid_example":"Skipping required quorum or acceptance rules merely because the same leader handled previous slots.","problem":"Over-aggressive optimization can silently remove safety checks.","failure_mode":"Unsafe consensus optimization","cause":"Steady-state assumptions are treated as permanent guarantees.","solution":"Separate stable-leader optimizations from mandatory safety transitions.","alternative":"Use Raft or another consensus implementation with an explicit stable-leader log-replication path.","tradeoff":"Stable leadership improves throughput but leadership changes still require full recovery logic.","constraint":"The optimization must handle leader failure, stale messages, and concurrent leadership attempts.","edge_case":"A leader can become unreachable while clients still send requests to it.","security_implication":"Stale leader optimization must not bypass epoch or authority validation.","performance_implication":"Reducing repeated setup rounds can materially reduce per-command coordination overhead.","testing_guidance":"Compare steady-state operation with frequent leader failures and verify identical safety properties.","verification_guidance":"Check safety invariants both before and after optimization is enabled.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Multi-Paxos"],"prerequisites":["Paxos","Replicated logs"],"related_records":["D17-B23-R03","D17-B24-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B32-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus Implementation","concept":"Consensus state persistence","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must safety-critical consensus state survive process crashes?","answer":"Consensus state that participates in safety decisions must survive crashes when the protocol requires it; otherwise a recovered participant may behave as though an earlier promise, term, ballot, or log state never existed.","explanation":"Crash recovery can invalidate safety assumptions if volatile protocol state is treated as authoritative after restart.","principle":"Durability requirements are part of the consensus protocol specification, not merely a storage optimization.","rule":"Identify every state variable referenced by the safety proof and define its crash-recovery semantics.","correct_example":"A recovered participant restores the protocol state required to reject obsolete proposals or messages.","invalid_example":"Restarting with an empty consensus state while retaining old messages and assuming safety remains unchanged.","problem":"Old messages can be accepted against forgotten protocol state.","failure_mode":"Crash-recovery safety violation","cause":"Safety-critical state was not durably recovered.","solution":"Persist and recover required consensus metadata before accepting new protocol messages.","alternative":"Use a consensus implementation whose documented storage/recovery mechanism is already established.","tradeoff":"Durability increases write cost and recovery complexity.","constraint":"Not every runtime variable needs persistence; only protocol-required state should be classified as safety-critical.","edge_case":"Storage corruption can be different from a clean process crash and may require additional integrity checks.","security_implication":"Tampering with consensus state can alter authority or replicated history.","performance_implication":"Synchronous persistence can affect consensus latency.","testing_guidance":"Crash and restart nodes at every durable-state transition.","verification_guidance":"Verify recovery against the protocol's safety invariants rather than merely checking process availability.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consensus","Durability"],"prerequisites":["Paxos or Raft","Failure recovery"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R03","D17-B31-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex","Raft_2014"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B32-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consensus","concept":"Consensus implementation versus consensus specification","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a consensus implementation be evaluated against a protocol specification rather than example executions?","answer":"A consensus protocol is defined by invariants and guarantees over many possible executions, including failures and message reorderings. A few successful runs cannot establish those properties.","explanation":"Distributed executions have a large state space. Correctness requires reasoning over the protocol model, not only nominal-path demonstrations.","principle":"Execution examples demonstrate behavior; they do not by themselves prove distributed safety.","rule":"Separate implementation tests from protocol-level verification.","correct_example":"A test suite combines deterministic unit tests, failure injection, invariant checking, and protocol-level reasoning.","invalid_example":"Declaring consensus verified because a three-node cluster successfully committed several commands in a healthy network.","problem":"Rare failure schedules remain untested.","failure_mode":"False verification","cause":"Nominal execution is mistaken for proof of distributed correctness.","solution":"Use invariants, model-based testing, fault injection, and formal methods where justified.","alternative":"Use a mature consensus implementation with established verification evidence, while still validating integration behavior.","tradeoff":"Deeper verification requires additional tooling and engineering effort.","constraint":"Testing cannot exhaustively cover arbitrary distributed state spaces.","edge_case":"A bug may only appear during simultaneous failure, recovery, and membership changes.","security_implication":"Incorrect consensus can propagate corrupted or unauthorized state to every replica.","performance_implication":"Verification workloads may be expensive but are separate from production execution cost.","testing_guidance":"Include partitions, crashes, message duplication, reordering, delayed delivery, and restart scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Track explicit invariants and evidence for each safety and liveness claim.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consensus"],"prerequisites":["Distributed testing","Formal reasoning"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R02","D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Raft_2014","Paxos_Made_Moderately_Complex"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B33-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"At-most-once delivery","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does at-most-once message delivery mean?","answer":"At-most-once delivery means a message is delivered zero or one time according to the messaging system's defined semantics; a message may be lost, but the system does not intentionally redeliver it after an unsuccessful processing attempt.","explanation":"At-most-once prioritizes avoiding duplicate delivery over guaranteeing successful delivery.","principle":"Delivery guarantees must be evaluated together with failure behavior and acknowledgment timing.","rule":"Do not use at-most-once semantics for operations that require guaranteed processing unless loss is explicitly acceptable.","correct_example":"A consumer advances its position before processing a message, so a consumer crash can lose the message without causing a later redelivery.","invalid_example":"Calling a system at-most-once while it automatically retries failed delivery until acknowledgment.","problem":"The claimed delivery guarantee does not match the actual acknowledgment protocol.","failure_mode":"Message loss","cause":"Acknowledgment or offset advancement occurs before successful processing.","solution":"Use at-least-once or transactional processing when message loss is unacceptable.","alternative":"Use application-level durability and reconciliation for lossy delivery paths.","tradeoff":"Avoiding redelivery can reduce duplicate-processing risk but increases loss risk.","constraint":"Exactly what constitutes delivery must be defined by the messaging protocol.","edge_case":"A producer can lose a message before the broker durably accepts it.","security_implication":"Security events must not use lossy semantics when omission would bypass required detection or policy actions.","performance_implication":"At-most-once can reduce retry traffic and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Crash consumers immediately before and after acknowledgment/offset advancement.","verification_guidance":"Verify the documented point at which ownership of the message transfers from broker to consumer.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Messaging","Acknowledgment semantics"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Apache_Kafka_Design_Documentation"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B33-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"At-least-once delivery","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What does at-least-once message delivery mean?","answer":"At-least-once delivery means the system attempts to ensure that a message is not lost after the defined durability or acknowledgment point, but the same message may be delivered or processed more than once.","explanation":"Redelivery can occur after consumer failure, acknowledgment loss, or retry. Consumers therefore commonly need idempotent processing or deduplication.","principle":"At-least-once delivery transfers some duplicate-handling responsibility to the consumer or downstream system.","rule":"Design consumers so repeated delivery does not create unintended repeated effects when duplicates are possible.","correct_example":"A consumer processes a message using an idempotency key and safely handles redelivery after a crash.","invalid_example":"Assuming an acknowledgment failure means the broker knows the message was already processed.","problem":"The broker may redeliver a message after uncertain consumer completion.","failure_mode":"Duplicate processing","cause":"Processing and acknowledgment are not one atomic operation.","solution":"Use idempotent operations, deduplication, transactions, or coordinated offset/output storage.","alternative":"Use an exactly-once mechanism where its complete scope matches the application requirement.","tradeoff":"At-least-once improves delivery durability but requires duplicate handling.","constraint":"The guarantee depends on the broker's durability and acknowledgment model.","edge_case":"A consumer can successfully perform an external side effect and crash before acknowledging the message.","security_implication":"Repeated security actions must be safe or deduplicated.","performance_implication":"Retries and duplicate processing increase resource usage.","testing_guidance":"Crash consumers after side effects but before acknowledgment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that duplicate delivery does not violate application invariants.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Messaging","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R01","D17-B33-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Apache_Kafka_Design_Documentation","AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B33-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Delivery guarantee scope","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must delivery guarantees be scoped to the complete processing path?","answer":"A messaging system's delivery guarantee does not automatically extend to arbitrary external side effects. A message can be delivered once while an external database write, API call, or file operation still executes more than once or fails independently.","explanation":"End-to-end semantics depend on coordination between message position, processing state, and destination side effects.","principle":"Delivery semantics must be specified across the entire state-transition boundary that matters to the application.","rule":"State exactly which components are covered by any at-most-once, at-least-once, or exactly-once claim.","correct_example":"A streaming system coordinates input offsets, state updates, and output writes within a defined transactional boundary.","invalid_example":"Claiming exactly-once business effects merely because the broker delivers each message once.","problem":"Broker-level semantics do not automatically make external side effects atomic.","failure_mode":"False exactly-once assumption","cause":"Transport semantics are confused with end-to-end effect semantics.","solution":"Define the transaction boundary and use idempotency, transactions, or reconciliation at external destinations.","alternative":"Design external effects to be idempotent and separately auditable.","tradeoff":"End-to-end coordination increases complexity but narrows correctness ambiguity.","constraint":"The destination must support the required transaction or idempotency mechanism for a strong end-to-end guarantee.","edge_case":"The external destination may succeed while the consumer loses the response.","security_implication":"Security actions must be traceable even when transport and effect state diverge.","performance_implication":"Cross-system coordination can increase latency and reduce throughput.","testing_guidance":"Crash the consumer between every boundary: receive, process, external effect, acknowledgment.","verification_guidance":"Verify the complete input-to-effect invariant rather than only broker delivery counts.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Messaging","Distributed transactions"],"prerequisites":["Delivery semantics","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R02","D17-B25-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Apache_Kafka_Design_Documentation"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B34-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Message ordering scope","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must message ordering guarantees specify their scope?","answer":"Ordering may be guaranteed per queue, partition, key, producer, consumer, or other scope rather than globally. An application must therefore identify the exact ordering domain before relying on message sequence.","explanation":"Distributed messaging systems often trade global ordering for partitioned scalability.","principle":"An ordering guarantee is meaningful only when its domain and ordering relation are explicitly defined.","rule":"Never infer global ordering from a local partition or stream ordering guarantee.","correct_example":"An application requiring per-account event order routes all events for an account to the same ordering domain.","invalid_example":"Sending related events to unrelated partitions and assuming the broker preserves their global order.","problem":"Events can be observed in different orders across independent streams.","failure_mode":"Ordering violation","cause":"The application requires a stronger ordering scope than the transport provides.","solution":"Choose an appropriate partitioning or coordination strategy.","alternative":"Use explicit sequence numbers and application-level reordering when bounded reordering is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Stronger ordering reduces parallelism and may increase coordination cost.","constraint":"The ordering mechanism must remain valid during consumer failover and partition reassignment.","edge_case":"Retries can create duplicate messages that complicate sequence interpretation.","security_implication":"Authorization or audit events may require strict ordering to reconstruct policy changes correctly.","performance_implication":"Global ordering can become a scalability bottleneck.","testing_guidance":"Generate concurrent producers and verify ordering separately within and across ordering domains.","verification_guidance":"Check the observed event history against the documented ordering scope.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Messaging","Partitioning"],"related_records":["D17-B11-R01","D17-B33-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B34-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Partition key and ordering relationship","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"How does partition-key selection affect distributed message ordering?","answer":"When a messaging system provides ordering within a partition, placing related events under the same partition key can preserve their relative order while allowing unrelated keys to execute independently.","explanation":"Partitioning determines both distribution and the scope of local ordering. A poor key can create hotspots or split dependent events.","principle":"Partition-key design is simultaneously a correctness and scalability decision.","rule":"Select partition keys from the application's ordering and load-distribution requirements.","correct_example":"All state-changing events for one aggregate are routed consistently to the same partition when per-aggregate order is required.","invalid_example":"Hashing each event independently without preserving the relationship between events that require order.","problem":"Related events can arrive through independent ordering domains.","failure_mode":"Per-entity ordering loss","cause":"Partition key does not correspond to the required ordering identity.","solution":"Use a stable ordering key and validate its distribution characteristics.","alternative":"Use application sequence numbers with buffering when a single partition would create unacceptable hotspots.","tradeoff":"Stronger local ordering can create uneven partition load.","constraint":"The chosen key must remain stable and sufficiently distributed for expected traffic.","edge_case":"One extremely high-volume key can dominate a partition.","security_implication":"Sensitive tenants or identities must not be co-located in ways that violate isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Hot partitions reduce parallelism and increase tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Load-test both uniform keys and pathological hot keys.","verification_guidance":"Verify both per-key ordering and expected partition-load distribution.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Partitioning","Message ordering"],"related_records":["D17-B34-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B34-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Poison message","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a poison message in distributed processing?","answer":"A poison message is a message that repeatedly causes processing failure, potentially preventing progress when the consumer continually retries it instead of isolating or resolving the failure.","explanation":"A permanently invalid payload, incompatible schema, deterministic application bug, or unsupported state can create poison-message behavior.","principle":"Retry policies must distinguish transient failures from failures that repeated retries cannot repair.","rule":"Bound retries and provide an explicit path for messages that cannot currently be processed.","correct_example":"A consumer retries transient failures with backoff and eventually routes an unprocessable message to a controlled dead-letter or quarantine path.","invalid_example":"Retrying the same invalid message indefinitely at full speed.","problem":"One bad message can consume processing capacity and block unrelated work.","failure_mode":"Consumer starvation","cause":"No bounded retry or quarantine mechanism.","solution":"Use retry limits, backoff, dead-letter/quarantine handling, and operator-visible diagnostics.","alternative":"Use per-message failure isolation when ordering requirements permit it.","tradeoff":"Quarantining a message preserves throughput but requires later reconciliation.","constraint":"Dead-letter handling must preserve enough metadata to safely diagnose and replay the message.","edge_case":"A message may become processable after a dependency or schema is repaired.","security_implication":"Quarantined messages may contain sensitive data and require access controls.","performance_implication":"Unbounded retries can multiply load and increase queue backlog.","testing_guidance":"Inject permanently invalid and temporarily invalid messages and observe retry behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that one poison message cannot indefinitely prevent required progress.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Messaging","Retry policy"],"prerequisites":["At-least-once delivery","Backoff"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01","D17-B33-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature","AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B35-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consumer Coordination","concept":"Consumer group ownership","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why do distributed consumers coordinate ownership of partitions or work units?","answer":"A consumer group coordinates which consumer instance owns which work units so that work can be processed in parallel without unintentionally having multiple consumers independently process the same assigned unit under the normal ownership model.","explanation":"Ownership is a coordination problem involving membership, assignment, failure detection, and reassignment.","principle":"Distributed work sharing requires an explicit ownership model and recovery mechanism.","rule":"Define assignment, ownership transfer, and duplicate-processing semantics before scaling consumers horizontally.","correct_example":"A group coordinator reassigns work from a failed consumer to another consumer after ownership changes are safely recognized.","invalid_example":"Allowing every consumer to process every partition without deduplication or an intentional broadcast model.","problem":"Uncoordinated consumers cause duplicate processing and inconsistent ownership assumptions.","failure_mode":"Duplicate work ownership","cause":"No authoritative assignment mechanism.","solution":"Use a coordinator, partition ownership protocol, or explicit work-queue semantics.","alternative":"Broadcast processing when every consumer intentionally requires every message.","tradeoff":"Coordination adds rebalance overhead but enables horizontal work distribution.","constraint":"Ownership transfer must account for in-flight work and processing state.","edge_case":"A slow consumer may appear failed and cause overlapping ownership if fencing is absent.","security_implication":"Unauthorized consumers must not be able to claim protected work units.","performance_implication":"Frequent rebalancing can interrupt processing and increase latency.","testing_guidance":"Join, leave, crash, pause, and restart consumers during active processing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that ownership transitions do not violate the system's duplicate or ordering guarantees.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed membership","Messaging"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B35-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consumer Coordination","concept":"Rebalance disruption","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can consumer rebalancing affect distributed message processing?","answer":"A rebalance changes work ownership among consumers. During the transition, processing may pause, in-flight work may be retried, and ownership assumptions can temporarily change.","explanation":"Rebalancing is necessary for failure recovery and scaling but can itself become a source of latency and duplicate processing.","principle":"Work redistribution must be designed as a state transition, not treated as a transparent event.","rule":"Define what happens to in-flight messages when ownership changes.","correct_example":"A consumer stops accepting new work, records required processing state, and releases ownership according to the protocol before another consumer takes over.","invalid_example":"Changing ownership while the old consumer continues unrestricted side effects.","problem":"Two consumers can concurrently act on the same logical work unit.","failure_mode":"Overlapping ownership","cause":"Ownership transfer lacks fencing or a safe handoff boundary.","solution":"Use generation IDs, leases, fencing, transactional offsets, or equivalent coordination where required.","alternative":"Use work queues with explicit task leases and idempotent processing.","tradeoff":"Stronger handoff guarantees add coordination and state-management overhead.","constraint":"The correct mechanism depends on whether duplicate processing is acceptable.","edge_case":"A network partition can leave an old consumer believing it still owns work.","security_implication":"Stale ownership can permit unauthorized or duplicate actions.","performance_implication":"Frequent rebalances increase latency and reduce effective throughput.","testing_guidance":"Partition an active consumer from the coordinator while it continues receiving work.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale owners cannot perform effects after ownership has been transferred.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consumer groups","Fencing"],"prerequisites":["Distributed ownership","Failure detection"],"related_records":["D17-B24-R02","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B35-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consumer Coordination","concept":"Generation or epoch fencing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a distributed system prevent a previous worker owner from continuing after reassignment?","answer":"The coordinator can assign a monotonically increasing generation, epoch, or fencing token to each ownership configuration. Downstream components can reject operations carrying an obsolete token.","explanation":"Fencing converts uncertain ownership into an enforceable authority check at the resource boundary.","principle":"When stale workers can still reach a resource, ownership state must be enforced where side effects occur.","rule":"Validate the current fencing token before accepting side effects that depend on exclusive ownership.","correct_example":"A storage service rejects a write from a worker whose ownership epoch is lower than the current epoch.","invalid_example":"Relying only on the old worker eventually noticing that ownership was revoked.","problem":"A stale worker may continue operating after a network partition.","failure_mode":"Stale-worker side effect","cause":"Ownership revocation is advisory rather than enforced.","solution":"Use fencing tokens or an equivalent resource-side authority check.","alternative":"Terminate or isolate the old worker when reliable process isolation is available.","tradeoff":"Resource-side validation adds metadata and coordination but provides strong protection against stale owners.","constraint":"The downstream resource must participate in token validation.","edge_case":"A worker may issue a request immediately before receiving the newer epoch.","security_implication":"Fencing is directly relevant to preventing stale or unauthorized actors from performing privileged operations.","performance_implication":"Token validation adds a small cost to each protected operation.","testing_guidance":"Delay revocation notifications and allow stale workers to continue issuing operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected side effect rejects obsolete ownership epochs.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Distributed ownership","Epochs"],"prerequisites":["Leader fencing","Membership"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03","D17-B24-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Systems_Fencing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B36-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Queues","concept":"Queue backlog growth","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What causes an unbounded distributed queue backlog?","answer":"A queue backlog grows when incoming work persistently exceeds effective processing capacity, or when downstream failures prevent consumers from making sufficient progress.","explanation":"Backlog is a state variable that can indicate either legitimate load exceeding capacity or a failure in processing, dependency availability, or consumer coordination.","principle":"Queue depth must be treated as a system-health signal rather than only as a storage metric.","rule":"Define backlog thresholds, processing capacity, and recovery behavior before relying on queues for resilience.","correct_example":"The system monitors arrival rate, processing rate, age of oldest work, and consumer health to detect sustained backlog growth.","invalid_example":"Allowing the queue to grow indefinitely while assuming consumers will eventually catch up.","problem":"Unbounded backlog can exhaust storage and increase end-to-end latency.","failure_mode":"Queue exhaustion","cause":"Arrival rate exceeds sustainable processing capacity or consumers are impaired.","solution":"Control intake, scale consumers, shed noncritical work, prioritize workloads, or repair the limiting dependency.","alternative":"Use admission control or bounded queues when overload must be contained.","tradeoff":"Dropping or delaying work protects the system but may reduce completeness of processing.","constraint":"Scaling consumers may not help if the bottleneck is a shared downstream dependency.","edge_case":"A temporary spike may be harmless if the system has sufficient recovery capacity and backlog drains predictably.","security_implication":"Attackers can intentionally create queue floods to exhaust storage or processing capacity.","performance_implication":"Older backlog increases latency and can create cascading resource pressure.","testing_guidance":"Load the system above sustainable throughput and measure backlog growth and recovery time.","verification_guidance":"Verify that backlog remains bounded under defined normal and failure workloads.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Messaging","Throughput"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Builders_Library_Queue_Backlogs"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B36-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Queues","concept":"Backpressure","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is backpressure in a distributed processing pipeline?","answer":"Backpressure is a mechanism by which downstream capacity or congestion influences upstream production so that work does not accumulate faster than the system can safely process it.","explanation":"Backpressure can be implemented through bounded queues, flow-control signals, rate limits, credits, blocking, or adaptive production rates.","principle":"A stable distributed pipeline must account for capacity differences between producers and consumers.","rule":"Provide an explicit overload response rather than allowing uncontrolled buffering.","correct_example":"A producer reduces its sending rate when the downstream queue exceeds a defined capacity threshold.","invalid_example":"Continuing to produce indefinitely because the queue currently accepts writes.","problem":"Unbounded buffering converts temporary overload into persistent resource exhaustion.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion","cause":"No mechanism limits producer pressure.","solution":"Use bounded buffers, flow control, rate limiting, admission control, or workload shedding.","alternative":"Scale processing capacity when the bottleneck is horizontally scalable.","tradeoff":"Backpressure can increase producer latency or reject work but protects system stability.","constraint":"The chosen mechanism must avoid deadlocks between dependent pipeline stages.","edge_case":"Cyclic pipelines can deadlock if every stage waits for downstream capacity.","security_implication":"Backpressure limits can reduce denial-of-service amplification.","performance_implication":"Proper flow control can improve tail latency and prevent queue explosion.","testing_guidance":"Saturate downstream capacity and verify upstream behavior under sustained overload.","verification_guidance":"Verify bounded resource usage and defined overload behavior.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Queue capacity","Flow control"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R01","D17-B19-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Queueing_Literature","AWS_Builders_Library_Queue_Backlogs"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B36-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Queues","concept":"Bounded queue","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why can a bounded queue be safer than an unbounded queue?","answer":"A bounded queue places an explicit upper limit on buffered work, preventing queue growth from consuming unlimited memory or storage. Once capacity is reached, the system must apply a defined overload policy.","explanation":"A bounded queue does not solve overload automatically; it forces the system to decide whether to block, reject, drop, prioritize, or otherwise manage excess work.","principle":"Resource bounds are part of distributed-system correctness under overload.","rule":"Every bounded queue must define its full-capacity behavior.","correct_example":"A queue rejects low-priority work when full while preserving capacity for critical tasks.","invalid_example":"Setting an arbitrary queue size without defining what happens when the limit is reached.","problem":"Full queues can cause hidden blocking or cascading failures if behavior is unspecified.","failure_mode":"Overload cascade","cause":"Capacity limit exists without a coordinated overload policy.","solution":"Define admission, priority, timeout, and retry behavior for queue saturation.","alternative":"Use controlled persistent buffering when workload durability requires it.","tradeoff":"Bounded queues protect resources but can reject legitimate work during overload.","constraint":"The bound must account for expected workload bursts and recovery capacity.","edge_case":"Retries can refill a bounded queue faster than successful processing drains it.","security_implication":"Queue capacity can be abused for resource exhaustion and should be protected by admission controls.","performance_implication":"Bounded queues improve memory predictability but can increase producer blocking/rejection.","testing_guidance":"Fill the queue completely and test every saturation policy.","verification_guidance":"Verify that queue capacity and overflow behavior remain bounded under adversarial load.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Backpressure","Queue backlog"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R01","D17-B36-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Queueing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B37-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Dead-letter queue semantics","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of a dead-letter queue?","answer":"A dead-letter queue or quarantine stream isolates messages that cannot currently be processed successfully so that they do not indefinitely block normal processing and can be diagnosed or replayed under controlled conditions.","explanation":"Dead-lettering is an operational pattern rather than a guarantee that the message is permanently invalid.","principle":"Failed work should be isolated without losing the information required for diagnosis and recovery.","rule":"Preserve original message identity, failure context, retry history, and relevant metadata when dead-lettering.","correct_example":"A consumer moves a repeatedly failing message to a controlled quarantine stream after a bounded retry policy.","invalid_example":"Discarding the message after retry exhaustion without recording why processing failed.","problem":"Operators cannot distinguish permanent data errors from temporary infrastructure failures.","failure_mode":"Unrecoverable failure state","cause":"Insufficient failure metadata or uncontrolled message disposal.","solution":"Persist failure context and define a replay/reconciliation process.","alternative":"Pause a partition or use per-message isolation when strict ordering requires it.","tradeoff":"Quarantine improves throughput and observability but creates an additional recovery workflow.","constraint":"Replay must avoid recreating the same failure loop indefinitely.","edge_case":"A previously invalid message may become valid after a software or dependency update.","security_implication":"Dead-letter data can contain sensitive payloads and must follow data-access and retention controls.","performance_implication":"Large quarantine volumes require storage and operational capacity.","testing_guidance":"Test repeated failure, quarantine, operator inspection, repair, and replay.","verification_guidance":"Verify that quarantine preserves message identity and does not silently lose required data.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Messaging","Failure handling"],"prerequisites":["Poison messages","Retry policies"],"related_records":["D17-B34-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B37-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Replay safety","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must message replay be safe in distributed systems?","answer":"Replay can intentionally or accidentally execute a previously processed message again. A replay-safe system therefore requires idempotent effects, deduplication, transactional boundaries, or another mechanism that prevents repeated processing from violating invariants.","explanation":"Replay is useful for recovery and debugging but becomes dangerous when processing has irreversible external effects.","principle":"Recoverability requires that historical inputs can be reintroduced without corrupting current state.","rule":"Define whether replay is allowed, what state boundary is replayed, and how external effects are prevented from duplicating.","correct_example":"A consumer replays historical events into a deterministic state store while external side effects are disabled or separately deduplicated.","invalid_example":"Replaying production payment commands directly against an external payment provider without idempotency protection.","problem":"Replay can duplicate irreversible side effects.","failure_mode":"Replay-induced duplicate effect","cause":"Historical processing is treated as if it were a new command without effect protection.","solution":"Separate state reconstruction from external side effects and use idempotency keys where effects must occur.","alternative":"Use event-sourced state reconstruction with explicit side-effect boundaries.","tradeoff":"Replay-safe architecture adds metadata and separation but greatly improves recovery and debugging.","constraint":"Not every historical event is safe to replay after schema or business-rule changes.","edge_case":"A replay under newer application logic may produce a different result from the original execution.","security_implication":"Replay mechanisms must prevent unauthorized historical commands from becoming live effects.","performance_implication":"Large replays can consume substantial compute and I/O capacity.","testing_guidance":"Replay duplicates, old versions, partial histories, and events around external side effects.","verification_guidance":"Verify that replay preserves defined state invariants and does not duplicate protected side effects.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Idempotency","Event processing"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R03","D17-B18-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Event_Processing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B37-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging","concept":"Message schema evolution","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is message schema evolution a distributed-systems problem?","answer":"Producers and consumers may run different software versions simultaneously, so a message schema change must account for mixed-version compatibility, rolling deployment, replay, and delayed messages.","explanation":"A message can remain in transit or storage after the producer that created it has been upgraded or removed.","principle":"Distributed messages outlive individual process versions and therefore require explicit compatibility rules.","rule":"Define forward and backward compatibility requirements before changing an event or message schema.","correct_example":"A producer adds an optional field while older consumers continue to parse the message according to a documented compatibility contract.","invalid_example":"Removing or changing the meaning of a required field while old consumers remain active.","problem":"Mixed-version consumers can fail or interpret data incorrectly.","failure_mode":"Schema compatibility failure","cause":"Producer and consumer versions evolve independently.","solution":"Use versioned schemas, compatibility checks, defaults, or staged migrations.","alternative":"Introduce a new message type when semantics are fundamentally incompatible.","tradeoff":"Compatibility constraints slow some schema changes but enable rolling deployment and replay.","constraint":"Compatibility must cover stored historical messages, not only live traffic.","edge_case":"A newly added field may have semantics that old consumers cannot safely ignore.","security_implication":"Schema changes can accidentally expose sensitive fields to consumers that were never intended to receive them.","performance_implication":"Additional compatibility metadata and translation can increase processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Run old consumers against new producers and new consumers against historical messages.","verification_guidance":"Validate compatibility against the full supported version matrix.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed deployment","Messaging"],"related_records":["D17-B37-R02","D16-B37-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Messaging_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B38-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Distributed work stealing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of work stealing in distributed task processing?","answer":"Work stealing allows an underutilized worker to obtain available tasks from another worker or shared task pool, improving utilization when workloads are unevenly distributed.","explanation":"Distributed work stealing requires coordination over task ownership and must account for races, duplicate acquisition, and worker failure.","principle":"Dynamic work redistribution can improve utilization when static partitioning produces imbalance.","rule":"Task acquisition must be atomic or otherwise protected against multiple workers claiming the same task.","correct_example":"A worker atomically claims an unowned task before executing it.","invalid_example":"Workers read an unclaimed task and then independently mark it owned without conflict control.","problem":"Concurrent claims can produce duplicate execution.","failure_mode":"Duplicate task execution","cause":"Task ownership transition is not atomic or fenced.","solution":"Use atomic claim operations, leases, fencing, or idempotent task execution.","alternative":"Use static partitioning when workload distribution is predictable and coordination cost dominates.","tradeoff":"Dynamic balancing improves utilization but adds coordination and ownership overhead.","constraint":"The task source must support safe concurrent acquisition.","edge_case":"A worker can crash after claiming a task but before completion.","security_implication":"Task claims must be authorized so one tenant or worker cannot steal another's work.","performance_implication":"Work stealing can reduce idle time but may increase coordination traffic and task migration cost.","testing_guidance":"Create highly skewed workloads and inject worker failures during task claims.","verification_guidance":"Verify exactly-once ownership or explicitly safe duplicate execution semantics.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed task ownership","Failure recovery"],"related_records":["D17-B35-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Scheduling_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B38-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Task lease","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are leases useful for distributed task ownership?","answer":"A task lease grants a worker temporary ownership that expires unless renewed, allowing another worker to recover work when the original worker fails or becomes unreachable.","explanation":"Leases turn indefinite ownership into time-bounded ownership, but their correctness depends on how expiration and clock uncertainty are handled.","principle":"Temporary ownership requires explicit expiration semantics and protection against stale owners.","rule":"Never assume lease expiration alone prevents stale side effects; use fencing where the resource requires strong exclusion.","correct_example":"A worker holds a lease and the protected resource validates a current fencing token before accepting the worker's side effect.","invalid_example":"Allowing a worker with an expired lease to continue modifying shared state because it has not yet received a lease-expiration notification.","problem":"Network delay can leave an expired worker unaware of ownership loss.","failure_mode":"Expired-owner side effect","cause":"Lease state is enforced only locally.","solution":"Combine lease ownership with resource-side fencing or equivalent authority validation.","alternative":"Use durable task claims with explicit completion and retry semantics.","tradeoff":"Leases simplify failure recovery but introduce timing and renewal complexity.","constraint":"Lease duration must account for expected pauses, network delays, and clock assumptions.","edge_case":"A long garbage-collection pause can cause a healthy worker to lose its lease.","security_implication":"Expired credentials or leases must not continue authorizing privileged actions.","performance_implication":"Lease renewal generates periodic coordination traffic.","testing_guidance":"Pause workers, delay network messages, and allow leases to expire during active processing.","verification_guidance":"Verify that expired workers cannot perform protected effects after ownership transfer.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Leases","Fencing"],"prerequisites":["Distributed ownership","Failure detection"],"related_records":["D17-B35-R03","D17-B24-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Leasing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B38-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Work stealing versus centralized scheduling","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the trade-off between centralized scheduling and distributed work stealing?","answer":"Centralized scheduling can provide a simple authoritative assignment point but may become a bottleneck or single coordination dependency. Distributed work stealing can improve scalability and utilization but requires more complex ownership and race handling.","explanation":"Neither model is universally superior; the correct choice depends on workload size, task granularity, failure requirements, and coordination cost.","principle":"Scheduling architecture should be chosen from workload and failure characteristics rather than scalability slogans.","rule":"Evaluate scheduler throughput, recovery behavior, contention, and task ownership semantics.","correct_example":"A small workload uses a simple scheduler while a highly parallel workload uses decentralized task distribution with safe claims.","invalid_example":"Replacing a central scheduler with peer stealing without adding ownership conflict controls.","problem":"Distributed scheduling can introduce duplicate claims and harder recovery.","failure_mode":"Coordination complexity","cause":"Scalability architecture is adopted without a matching correctness model.","solution":"Define task ownership and failure semantics before distributing scheduling decisions.","alternative":"Use hierarchical scheduling to combine local autonomy with bounded coordination.","tradeoff":"Decentralization can reduce bottlenecks while increasing protocol complexity.","constraint":"Task granularity must justify coordination overhead.","edge_case":"Very small tasks may cost more to coordinate than to execute.","security_implication":"Distributed task acquisition increases the number of components that can request work.","performance_implication":"Scheduling overhead can dominate for fine-grained workloads.","testing_guidance":"Benchmark task sizes, worker counts, skew, and failure rates.","verification_guidance":"Verify task ownership and completion invariants under concurrent claims.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Distributed scheduling"],"prerequisites":["Task ownership","Work stealing"],"related_records":["D17-B38-R01","D17-B35-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Scheduling_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B39-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Failure Handling","concept":"Timeout budget propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should distributed request timeouts be propagated across service boundaries?","answer":"A request with a finite end-to-end deadline should communicate the remaining time budget to downstream operations so that downstream work does not continue long after the caller can no longer use the result.","explanation":"Independent per-hop timeouts can cause wasted work and resource retention when downstream calls outlive the original request deadline.","principle":"Timeouts should represent an end-to-end resource budget when the application has an end-to-end deadline.","rule":"Propagate remaining deadline information and prevent downstream operations from exceeding the caller's useful time budget.","correct_example":"A service receives a request deadline and passes the remaining budget to its database and downstream service calls.","invalid_example":"Giving every downstream call a fresh long timeout regardless of the original request deadline.","problem":"Downstream work continues after the caller has already timed out.","failure_mode":"Wasted downstream work","cause":"Timeout budgets are reset independently at each service hop.","solution":"Propagate absolute deadlines or remaining budgets and enforce them at each layer.","alternative":"Use bounded per-hop timeouts when no end-to-end deadline exists.","tradeoff":"Deadline propagation improves resource efficiency but requires consistent timeout semantics across services.","constraint":"Clock and protocol differences must be handled correctly when representing deadlines.","edge_case":"Retries can consume the entire remaining budget if each retry receives the original timeout.","security_implication":"Timeout enforcement can limit resource-exhaustion attacks through expensive requests.","performance_implication":"Proper deadlines reduce wasted work and tail-latency amplification.","testing_guidance":"Create multi-hop requests with short deadlines and inject slow downstream dependencies.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no downstream operation continues beyond its permitted request budget without an explicit independent reason.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Timeouts","Distributed RPC"],"prerequisites":["Failure handling","Request lifecycle"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R02","D17-B40-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B39-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Failure Handling","concept":"Retry budget across service layers","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why should retry budgets be coordinated across service layers?","answer":"Independent retries at multiple layers can multiply traffic dramatically when a downstream service is already failing. A coordinated retry budget limits the total amplification caused by nested retry loops.","explanation":"A single client request can trigger retries at gateways, application services, SDKs, and database clients. Multiplication across layers can overload an already degraded dependency.","principle":"Retries are shared resource consumption and should be bounded across the call graph.","rule":"Define which layer owns retry responsibility and limit retries at other layers.","correct_example":"The application layer performs bounded retries while lower-level layers avoid independently multiplying the same retry storm.","invalid_example":"A five-layer call chain where every layer retries a failed request several times independently.","problem":"Nested retries can generate far more traffic than the original workload.","failure_mode":"Retry storm","cause":"Uncoordinated retry policies across layers.","solution":"Centralize or coordinate retry responsibility, cap attempts, use backoff, and propagate deadlines.","alternative":"Allow only the lowest layer with sufficient knowledge to retry a narrowly defined transient failure.","tradeoff":"Fewer retries can reduce recovery attempts while preventing overload amplification.","constraint":"Retries must remain safe for the operation semantics.","edge_case":"A retryable failure at one layer may represent a non-retryable failure at another.","security_implication":"Retry storms can become an application-layer denial-of-service vector.","performance_implication":"Uncontrolled retries increase latency, connection usage, CPU, and downstream load.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures through multi-layer call chains and measure request amplification.","verification_guidance":"Calculate and verify a bounded upper limit on retry amplification.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Retries","Timeouts"],"prerequisites":["Retry budget","Distributed calls"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01","D17-B19-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B39-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Failure Handling","concept":"Fail-fast behavior","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can fail-fast behavior improve distributed-system stability?","answer":"Fail-fast behavior stops work that cannot make useful progress within the available resource or time budget, preventing slow or unavailable dependencies from consuming unbounded threads, connections, memory, or queue capacity.","explanation":"Fail-fast is useful only when the caller has a defined fallback, retry, rejection, or error path.","principle":"A distributed component should not hold scarce resources indefinitely waiting for a dependency that cannot satisfy the request in time.","rule":"Bound waiting and define explicit failure behavior for unavailable dependencies.","correct_example":"A service rejects a request after its deadline rather than holding a worker indefinitely on a failed downstream dependency.","invalid_example":"Waiting indefinitely for a dependency because eventual recovery is possible.","problem":"Resource exhaustion can occur before the dependency recovers.","failure_mode":"Cascading resource exhaustion","cause":"Unbounded waiting.","solution":"Use finite deadlines, bounded queues, circuit-breaking or admission control where appropriate.","alternative":"Asynchronously queue work when immediate completion is not required.","tradeoff":"Fail-fast improves stability but can increase visible errors or rejected work.","constraint":"The application must define what happens after fail-fast rejection.","edge_case":"Temporary latency spikes can cause legitimate requests to fail if deadlines are too aggressive.","security_implication":"Resource bounds reduce susceptibility to slow-request exhaustion attacks.","performance_implication":"Fail-fast reduces resource occupancy and can protect tail latency under overload.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency latency and complete failure while measuring resource usage.","verification_guidance":"Verify resource consumption remains bounded as dependency latency approaches the configured timeout.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Timeouts","Resource limits"],"prerequisites":["Distributed failure handling"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R02","D17-B39-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B40-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging and Recovery","concept":"Transactional offset and output coordination","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a consumer coordinate message progress with its output state?","answer":"A consumer can coordinate its processed-position metadata with its output state so that recovery does not create an ambiguity where the output was committed but the position was not, or the position was committed while the output was not.","explanation":"The required mechanism may be a transaction, atomic storage boundary, or another protocol that gives the application the necessary recovery semantics.","principle":"Processing progress and externally visible output form one logical correctness boundary when duplicate or lost effects are unacceptable.","rule":"Define the atomicity boundary between consumed position and produced state before claiming exactly-once processing.","correct_example":"A stream processor atomically commits its state update and input-position advancement within a supported transactional boundary.","invalid_example":"Writing output successfully and committing the input offset in a separate unreliable step without idempotency or recovery handling.","problem":"A crash between the two operations can cause either duplicate or lost processing.","failure_mode":"Offset-output inconsistency","cause":"Processing progress and output are not coordinated.","solution":"Use transactions, atomic co-location, idempotent output, or explicit reconciliation.","alternative":"Use at-least-once processing with idempotent destination updates.","tradeoff":"Transactional coordination improves correctness but increases implementation and runtime overhead.","constraint":"The destination system must support the required atomicity or idempotency semantics.","edge_case":"External systems outside the transaction boundary can still experience duplicate effects.","security_implication":"Security actions should not be marked processed before the protected effect is durably recorded.","performance_implication":"Transactional coordination can increase latency and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Crash the consumer after output but before offset commit, and after offset commit but before output.","verification_guidance":"Verify recovery produces the intended end-to-end processing invariant under every crash boundary.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Messaging","Transactions"],"prerequisites":["Delivery semantics","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B33-R03","D17-B25-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Apache_Kafka_Design_Documentation"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B40-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging and Recovery","concept":"Exactly-once processing boundary","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why is exactly-once processing not automatically an end-to-end property?","answer":"Exactly-once processing can be guaranteed only within the components and state transitions covered by the protocol's atomicity or idempotency mechanism. An external side effect outside that boundary can still occur multiple times unless separately protected.","explanation":"For example, a stream processor may atomically coordinate input position and internal output while an external API call remains outside that transaction.","principle":"Exactly-once claims must name the exact input, processing, state, and output boundary they cover.","rule":"Never use an unqualified exactly-once claim for a system with external side effects.","correct_example":"A system documents exactly-once semantics for its internal stream state and separately documents idempotency requirements for external APIs.","invalid_example":"Claiming a payment occurs exactly once solely because the input message is processed exactly once by the stream engine.","problem":"The external payment effect has an independent failure boundary.","failure_mode":"End-to-end semantic overclaim","cause":"Internal processing guarantee is generalized to external side effects.","solution":"Use external idempotency keys, transactions, or reconciliation and document the complete guarantee precisely.","alternative":"Bring the effect into a supported transactional boundary where technically and semantically appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger end-to-end guarantees require tighter system coupling or more complex idempotency/reconciliation.","constraint":"External systems may not support the required transaction protocol.","edge_case":"A successful external effect may return no response before the consumer crashes.","security_implication":"Duplicate privileged actions must be prevented independently of internal message semantics.","performance_implication":"Cross-system idempotency or transaction coordination adds latency and storage work.","testing_guidance":"Crash and disconnect the consumer at every boundary around the external effect.","verification_guidance":"Trace the complete effect path and verify the exact-once claim at each boundary.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Messaging","External side effects"],"prerequisites":["Exactly-once semantics","Distributed transactions"],"related_records":["D17-B33-R03","D17-B40-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Apache_Kafka_Design_Documentation"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B40-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Messaging and Reliability","concept":"Retry safety requires idempotency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should retryable distributed operations be idempotent or otherwise duplicate-safe?","answer":"A caller may retry after a timeout even when the original operation actually succeeded. Without idempotency or duplicate detection, the retry can apply the same logical operation more than once.","explanation":"The caller often cannot distinguish a failed operation from a successful operation whose response was lost. Retry safety therefore depends on the operation semantics, not only the transport.","principle":"Uncertain completion requires operations whose repeated invocation has controlled effects.","rule":"Before enabling retries for mutating operations, establish idempotency, deduplication, transactional semantics, or an equivalent duplicate-safe mechanism.","correct_example":"A create request carries a stable idempotency key so repeated attempts return the same logical result instead of creating multiple resources.","invalid_example":"Automatically retrying a non-idempotent create operation after a timeout without a request identity or deduplication mechanism.","problem":"A timeout can produce duplicate side effects.","failure_mode":"Duplicate mutation","cause":"Retry is performed without duplicate-effect protection.","solution":"Introduce idempotency keys, deduplication records, or a transaction that binds request identity to the effect.","alternative":"Avoid automatic retries for operations whose duplicate semantics cannot be controlled.","tradeoff":"Idempotency storage and lookup add state and processing overhead but make retries safer.","constraint":"The idempotency key must have appropriate uniqueness, retention, and scope semantics.","edge_case":"A retry can arrive after the idempotency record has expired.","security_implication":"Idempotency keys must not become reusable authorization tokens or expose another request's result.","performance_implication":"Deduplication storage increases read/write overhead and retention requirements.","testing_guidance":"Force response loss after successful mutation and retry with the same logical request.","verification_guidance":"Verify that repeated requests produce the documented single logical effect across crash and timeout scenarios.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Retries","Idempotency"],"prerequisites":["Distributed failures","Request identity"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R01","D17-B39-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Idempotent_APIs_Guidance","AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B41-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Service discovery","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is service discovery in a distributed system?","answer":"Service discovery is the mechanism by which a client or intermediary determines which network endpoints currently represent an available instance of a logical service.","explanation":"Distributed services can scale, restart, relocate, or fail, so clients cannot safely depend on permanently fixed instance addresses.","principle":"Logical service identity should be separated from the transient network locations of service instances.","rule":"Resolve service identity through an authoritative or appropriately consistent discovery mechanism before making calls.","correct_example":"A client resolves a logical service name to currently registered endpoints and selects an eligible endpoint.","invalid_example":"Hard-coding one instance IP address for a horizontally scaled service.","problem":"Instance locations change independently of clients.","failure_mode":"Stale endpoint routing","cause":"Client endpoint information is not updated after membership changes.","solution":"Use service discovery with controlled registration, health state, and expiration semantics.","alternative":"Use a platform-provided service abstraction that hides instance addressing.","tradeoff":"Dynamic discovery improves elasticity but introduces another distributed dependency.","constraint":"Discovery data can itself become stale or unavailable.","edge_case":"A discovered endpoint can fail immediately after discovery succeeds.","security_implication":"Discovery must prevent unauthorized services from registering as trusted service identities.","performance_implication":"Frequent discovery queries can create control-plane load; caching may reduce it but increases staleness.","testing_guidance":"Add and remove instances while clients continuously make requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify that clients do not indefinitely route to endpoints that are no longer valid.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed membership","Networking"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Service_Discovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B41-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Client-side versus server-side discovery","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between client-side and server-side service discovery?","answer":"In client-side discovery, the client obtains or maintains endpoint information and chooses an instance. In server-side discovery, a routing component resolves the service identity and selects an instance on behalf of the client.","explanation":"The distinction changes where discovery logic, load balancing, failure handling, and policy enforcement reside.","principle":"Discovery architecture determines which component owns endpoint selection responsibility.","rule":"Choose a discovery model based on client complexity, infrastructure capabilities, observability, and policy requirements.","correct_example":"A platform load balancer resolves a logical service name and routes requests to healthy instances in a server-side model.","invalid_example":"Assuming a server-side load balancer automatically eliminates all endpoint-staleness or routing failures.","problem":"Discovery responsibility is unclear or duplicated across layers.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent routing behavior","cause":"Multiple layers independently apply different discovery and selection rules.","solution":"Define one authoritative discovery and routing contract for each request path.","alternative":"Use client-side discovery when clients need direct control over endpoint selection.","tradeoff":"Server-side discovery simplifies clients but adds an intermediary; client-side discovery reduces intermediary dependency but increases client complexity.","constraint":"The selected model must support failure, scaling, and security requirements.","edge_case":"A client-side cache can continue using an endpoint after the server-side registry has removed it.","security_implication":"Routing components become security-sensitive trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Server-side routing can add a network hop; client-side routing can reduce hops but requires local discovery state.","testing_guidance":"Compare behavior during instance churn, registry failure, and routing-component failure.","verification_guidance":"Verify that endpoint selection follows the documented ownership model.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Service discovery"],"prerequisites":["Networking","Load balancing"],"related_records":["D17-B41-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Service_Discovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B41-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Stale discovery registration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when a failed service instance remains registered?","answer":"Clients may continue routing requests to an unavailable instance until its registration is removed, expires, or is otherwise marked unusable.","explanation":"Registration state and actual process health are separate distributed observations and can temporarily diverge.","principle":"Discovery state must have an explicit freshness and failure-detection model.","rule":"Do not treat registration as permanent proof of endpoint availability.","correct_example":"Registrations expire unless renewed and endpoint health is independently evaluated before routing.","invalid_example":"Keeping an instance permanently registered because it successfully registered once.","problem":"Clients receive endpoints that cannot process requests.","failure_mode":"Stale discovery","cause":"No effective expiration, health validation, or membership reconciliation.","solution":"Use leases, TTLs, health checks, or authoritative membership reconciliation.","alternative":"Use a platform that continuously manages service endpoint lifecycle.","tradeoff":"More aggressive expiration reduces staleness but can remove healthy instances during transient control-plane failures.","constraint":"Health detection is itself subject to false positives and network partitions.","edge_case":"A healthy service may be temporarily unreachable from one observer but reachable from others.","security_implication":"Stale registration can redirect sensitive requests to an unintended endpoint.","performance_implication":"Frequent health checks increase control-plane traffic.","testing_guidance":"Partition the registry from a healthy instance and separately partition clients from the instance.","verification_guidance":"Verify endpoint routing behavior when registration and actual reachability disagree.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Service discovery","Failure detection"],"prerequisites":["Membership","Health checking"],"related_records":["D17-B41-R01","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Service_Discovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B42-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health Checking","concept":"Liveness versus readiness","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should liveness and readiness be treated as different health concepts?","answer":"Liveness generally concerns whether a process should be considered alive and potentially restarted, while readiness concerns whether it is currently capable of receiving a particular class of work.","explanation":"A running process can be alive while still initializing, recovering state, waiting for dependencies, or intentionally refusing new traffic.","principle":"Process existence and request-serving eligibility are different states.","rule":"Do not use a single health signal to represent every operational decision.","correct_example":"A service remains running during initialization but is excluded from traffic until its readiness conditions are satisfied.","invalid_example":"Restarting every process that is temporarily unable to serve traffic.","problem":"Health signals trigger inappropriate routing or restarts.","failure_mode":"Restart or routing loop","cause":"Liveness and readiness semantics are conflated.","solution":"Define separate health conditions and consumers for each decision.","alternative":"Use a richer state model when binary health signals are insufficient.","tradeoff":"Separate health signals increase conceptual complexity but reduce incorrect automation.","constraint":"Health checks must not themselves overwhelm the service.","edge_case":"A dependency failure may make a service unready without making the process unhealthy.","security_implication":"Health endpoints must not expose sensitive diagnostic information.","performance_implication":"Expensive health checks can consume production resources.","testing_guidance":"Simulate initialization, dependency failure, overload, and recovery states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each health signal triggers only the actions it is designed to authorize.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Failure detection","Service discovery"],"related_records":["D17-B41-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Service_Health_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B42-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health Checking","concept":"False-positive failure detection","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can health checks incorrectly declare a healthy distributed component failed?","answer":"A health check can fail because of network partitions, transient latency, overloaded dependencies, resource contention, or an observer-specific connectivity problem even when the target process itself remains operational.","explanation":"Failure detection is an inference based on observations rather than direct knowledge of remote state.","principle":"A failure detector should be interpreted according to its accuracy and failure model rather than as perfect truth.","rule":"Design remediation to tolerate false-positive failure detection.","correct_example":"A routing system requires appropriate consecutive failures or independent evidence before removing an instance.","invalid_example":"Removing an instance permanently after one transient probe timeout.","problem":"Healthy capacity can be unnecessarily removed.","failure_mode":"False-positive eviction","cause":"Failure thresholds are too aggressive or network conditions are ignored.","solution":"Use calibrated thresholds, multiple signals, grace periods, and recovery mechanisms.","alternative":"Use lease-based membership with carefully chosen expiration semantics.","tradeoff":"More tolerance reduces false positives but delays detection of real failures.","constraint":"Thresholds must match latency and availability characteristics of the environment.","edge_case":"A regional network partition can make every instance appear unhealthy from one observer.","security_implication":"Attackers may intentionally induce probe failures to remove healthy capacity.","performance_implication":"Frequent probes consume network and compute resources.","testing_guidance":"Inject latency, packet loss, asymmetric connectivity, and dependency failures.","verification_guidance":"Measure false-positive and false-negative behavior under representative failure conditions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Failure detectors","Health checks"],"prerequisites":["Distributed failure model"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R03","D17-B42-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Failure_Detection_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B42-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health Checking","concept":"Health-check dependency recursion","knowledge_type":"ANTI_PATTERN","question":"Why can a health check become unreliable when it recursively depends on every downstream dependency?","answer":"A health endpoint that requires every downstream dependency to succeed can report the entire service as unhealthy whenever any optional dependency fails, potentially causing unnecessary eviction or restart cascades.","explanation":"Health checks should represent the decision they control, not indiscriminately mirror every dependency state.","principle":"Health evidence should be scoped to the operational action that consumes it.","rule":"Include only dependencies whose failure actually makes the service unable to perform the health-check decision's intended function.","correct_example":"A readiness check includes a mandatory database dependency when database access is required for all requests, while a liveness check does not require the database.","invalid_example":"A liveness check fails because an optional analytics service is unavailable.","problem":"A localized dependency failure causes broad service eviction.","failure_mode":"Health cascade","cause":"Health semantics are coupled to nonessential dependencies.","solution":"Separate mandatory and optional dependency checks and define health semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Expose multiple specialized health signals.","tradeoff":"More precise checks require clearer service contracts.","constraint":"The correct dependency set depends on the actual service contract.","edge_case":"A dependency can transition from optional to mandatory for a particular request class.","security_implication":"Health checks must avoid exposing dependency credentials or internal topology.","performance_implication":"Deep dependency checks increase probe latency and load.","testing_guidance":"Disable individual optional and mandatory dependencies and observe health outcomes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each health signal changes only when its documented service condition changes.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Health checking","Service dependencies"],"prerequisites":["Liveness","Readiness"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Service_Health_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B43-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Gossip Protocols","concept":"Gossip dissemination","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is gossip-based dissemination?","answer":"Gossip dissemination spreads information through repeated peer-to-peer exchanges, allowing knowledge such as membership or metadata to propagate without requiring every node to contact a central authority.","explanation":"Each exchange spreads some subset of known information, and repeated exchanges can produce broad convergence.","principle":"Decentralized information dissemination can achieve scalable propagation without centralized communication.","rule":"Define convergence expectations, staleness bounds, message selection, and failure behavior for gossip state.","correct_example":"Each node periodically exchanges membership information with selected peers and merges received state.","invalid_example":"Assuming a single gossip exchange immediately produces globally current membership.","problem":"Nodes temporarily hold different views of system state.","failure_mode":"Stale distributed view","cause":"Propagation is asynchronous and incomplete at any instant.","solution":"Use repeated dissemination, version metadata, expiration, and conflict-resolution rules.","alternative":"Use centralized coordination when strong immediate consistency is required.","tradeoff":"Gossip improves scalability and decentralization but provides eventual dissemination rather than instantaneous global knowledge.","constraint":"The convergence properties depend on peer selection, connectivity, update frequency, and failure conditions.","edge_case":"Network partitions can create independently evolving gossip populations.","security_implication":"Unauthenticated gossip can permit forged membership or metadata propagation.","performance_implication":"Gossip traffic grows with dissemination frequency and cluster size.","testing_guidance":"Measure propagation under node churn, packet loss, and partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence properties and stale-state behavior under the supported failure model.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Membership","Eventual consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B14-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Gossip_Protocol_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B43-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Gossip Protocols","concept":"Gossip anti-entropy","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does anti-entropy gossip differ from simple event dissemination?","answer":"Anti-entropy periodically compares state between peers and exchanges missing or newer information, allowing replicas to repair divergence rather than relying only on forwarding newly observed events.","explanation":"Anti-entropy is particularly useful when messages can be lost because later reconciliation can discover and repair missing state.","principle":"Periodic state reconciliation provides recovery from communication loss.","rule":"Design anti-entropy exchanges so peers can identify and repair state differences efficiently.","correct_example":"Two replicas compare version summaries and exchange only state known to be missing.","invalid_example":"Assuming missed gossip messages are permanently lost because no later reconciliation exists.","problem":"Replica state remains divergent after transient communication failures.","failure_mode":"Persistent replica divergence","cause":"No repair mechanism exists for lost dissemination messages.","solution":"Add periodic anti-entropy synchronization and deterministic conflict handling.","alternative":"Use durable replicated logs when replay-based repair is more appropriate.","tradeoff":"Anti-entropy consumes periodic bandwidth but improves convergence after message loss.","constraint":"The state-summary mechanism must correctly represent missing or newer state.","edge_case":"Concurrent updates may require conflict resolution rather than simple copying.","security_implication":"State synchronization must authenticate peers and protect against malicious state injection.","performance_implication":"Full-state comparison can be expensive; summaries can reduce transfer costs.","testing_guidance":"Drop synchronization messages and verify eventual repair after communication recovers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that divergent replicas converge to the documented state under repeated synchronization.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Gossip","Anti-entropy"],"prerequisites":["Replication","Version metadata"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B17-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Gossip_Protocol_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B43-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Gossip Protocols","concept":"Gossip convergence under partition","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What happens to gossip convergence during a network partition?","answer":"Nodes in separated partitions may continue exchanging information within their local partition while remaining unable to exchange updates across the partition. Global convergence can occur only after communication is restored and reconciliation is possible.","explanation":"Temporary divergence during partition is inherent to asynchronous decentralized dissemination unless stronger coordination is introduced.","principle":"Convergence claims must state the communication assumptions under which they hold.","rule":"Do not claim global convergence during a permanent partition unless the system has another communication path or coordination mechanism.","correct_example":"Two partitions maintain local state and reconcile after connectivity is restored.","invalid_example":"Claiming that gossip guarantees immediate global consistency while the network remains partitioned.","problem":"Operators interpret local convergence as global convergence.","failure_mode":"Incorrect global-state assumption","cause":"The communication model is omitted from the correctness claim.","solution":"Document partition behavior and reconciliation semantics explicitly.","alternative":"Use quorum-based coordination for state requiring stronger consistency.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency reduces independence during partitions.","constraint":"The system must define conflict resolution after partition healing.","edge_case":"Both partitions may independently modify the same object.","security_implication":"Conflicting security-policy updates require deterministic authority and reconciliation rules.","performance_implication":"Partition healing can create a large synchronization burst.","testing_guidance":"Partition the cluster, generate conflicting updates, restore connectivity, and measure convergence.","verification_guidance":"Verify conflict resolution and final convergence after partition healing.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Gossip","Network partitions"],"prerequisites":["Eventual consistency","Conflict resolution"],"related_records":["D17-B43-R01","D17-B28-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Gossip_Protocol_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B44-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Consistent hashing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does consistent hashing address?","answer":"Consistent hashing maps keys and nodes into a shared logical space so that adding or removing a node can change ownership for a relatively limited portion of keys compared with naive modulo-based partitioning.","explanation":"The key benefit is reduced remapping during membership changes, although the exact distribution depends on the algorithm and configuration.","principle":"Partitioning should minimize unnecessary data movement when the set of owners changes.","rule":"Use a consistent hashing design only after defining ownership, replication, rebalancing, and load-distribution requirements.","correct_example":"Removing one node causes primarily its previously assigned key range to move rather than remapping nearly all keys.","invalid_example":"Claiming that consistent hashing eliminates all key movement during node changes.","problem":"Node membership changes cause expensive redistribution.","failure_mode":"Mass key remapping","cause":"Ownership is calculated directly from the current node count.","solution":"Use a consistent hashing scheme or another incremental partitioning mechanism.","alternative":"Use explicit partition maps when operational control is more important than algorithmic simplicity.","tradeoff":"Consistent hashing reduces remapping but adds ring or partition-management complexity.","constraint":"Hash distribution must be evaluated against actual key and traffic distributions.","edge_case":"A small number of nodes can produce uneven ownership without appropriate virtual nodes or balancing mechanisms.","security_implication":"Predictable placement may expose concentration or tenant-locality risks.","performance_implication":"Reduced remapping can significantly reduce migration traffic during membership changes.","testing_guidance":"Add and remove nodes under realistic key distributions and measure movement and load balance.","verification_guidance":"Verify ownership movement and distribution against the documented algorithm.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Partitioning","Hashing"],"prerequisites":["Distributed storage","Membership"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B44-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Consistent_Hashing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B44-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Virtual nodes","knowledge_type":"OPTIMIZATION","question":"Why can virtual nodes improve consistent-hashing distribution?","answer":"Virtual nodes assign multiple logical positions to each physical node, providing more opportunities for keys to distribute across physical nodes and reducing imbalance caused by sparse or uneven ring placement.","explanation":"Virtual nodes improve statistical distribution but do not guarantee perfect balance.","principle":"More independent ownership positions can reduce placement variance.","rule":"Choose the number and placement of virtual nodes based on measured load and operational cost.","correct_example":"Each physical node owns multiple ring positions and receives a more balanced share of keys under representative workloads.","invalid_example":"Assuming adding unlimited virtual nodes always improves the system without cost.","problem":"Some physical nodes receive disproportionately large ownership ranges.","failure_mode":"Load imbalance","cause":"Insufficient or poorly distributed ownership positions.","solution":"Use virtual nodes or explicit balancing mechanisms and validate with production-like key distributions.","alternative":"Use partition-aware balancing with explicit ownership maps.","tradeoff":"More virtual nodes improve distribution but increase metadata and lookup complexity.","constraint":"The hash function and virtual-node placement must remain deterministic and compatible across members.","edge_case":"A skewed workload can remain imbalanced even when key counts are statistically balanced.","security_implication":"Tenant-sensitive placement should be reviewed separately from pure hash balance.","performance_implication":"More virtual nodes increase routing metadata and may increase lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Measure key count, request rate, and storage size distribution across physical nodes.","verification_guidance":"Verify both ownership balance and actual workload balance.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consistent hashing"],"prerequisites":["Hashing","Partitioning"],"related_records":["D17-B44-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Consistent_Hashing_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B44-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Rebalancing cost","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can reduced key remapping still leave significant rebalancing cost?","answer":"Even if only a subset of keys changes owners, those keys may represent large amounts of data, hot traffic, indexes, or dependent state that must still be transferred and rebuilt.","explanation":"The amount of logical key movement and the physical cost of migration are not equivalent metrics.","principle":"Rebalancing must be measured in terms of actual resource movement, not only ownership percentage.","rule":"Measure data volume, request load, migration bandwidth, recovery time, and service impact during rebalancing.","correct_example":"A system evaluates both percentage of keys moved and bytes/requests associated with those keys.","invalid_example":"Declaring rebalancing cheap because only 10 percent of key identities changed.","problem":"A small set of keys can contain most of the workload.","failure_mode":"Hot-range migration overload","cause":"Rebalancing metrics ignore data and traffic skew.","solution":"Use load-aware balancing and migration throttling.","alternative":"Move ownership in smaller controlled batches.","tradeoff":"Slower rebalancing reduces disruption but prolongs the period of imbalance.","constraint":"Migration must preserve correctness while ownership transitions occur.","edge_case":"A single hot key may dominate traffic regardless of balanced storage distribution.","security_implication":"Migration paths must preserve tenant and data-isolation controls.","performance_implication":"Uncontrolled migration can saturate network, disk, and CPU resources.","testing_guidance":"Simulate skewed hot keys and large-value records during node changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify service latency and resource limits throughout rebalancing.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Consistent hashing","Data migration"],"prerequisites":["Partitioning","Load balancing"],"related_records":["D17-B44-R01","D17-B44-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Data_Partitioning_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B45-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Sharding","concept":"Shard-key locality","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why does shard-key selection affect distributed query cost?","answer":"A shard key determines where records are stored and therefore whether a query can be answered by one shard or must contact multiple shards.","explanation":"A key that aligns with dominant access patterns can preserve locality, while an unsuitable key can turn common queries into distributed fan-out operations.","principle":"Data placement should reflect important query and transaction boundaries.","rule":"Select shard keys from actual access patterns, cardinality, distribution, and transaction requirements.","correct_example":"Queries that commonly operate on one tenant are routed to a shard using tenant identity when tenant-locality is a primary requirement.","invalid_example":"Selecting a monotonically increasing timestamp as the shard key without evaluating hotspot behavior.","problem":"Normal queries require broad fan-out or create hot shards.","failure_mode":"Poor shard locality","cause":"Shard key was selected without workload analysis.","solution":"Model query patterns and workload distribution before finalizing the shard key.","alternative":"Use compound keys or application-level routing when one field cannot satisfy all requirements.","tradeoff":"Improved locality can reduce flexibility for other query patterns.","constraint":"Shard keys must balance locality, cardinality, distribution, and future growth.","edge_case":"A formerly balanced key can become skewed as workload composition changes.","security_implication":"Shard placement must not bypass tenant isolation or authorization checks.","performance_implication":"Poor locality increases network calls, coordination, and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Replay representative query distributions against candidate shard keys.","verification_guidance":"Verify expected query fan-out and shard-load distribution under realistic workloads.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Sharding","Query routing"],"prerequisites":["Partitioning","Workload analysis"],"related_records":["D17-B44-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Design_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B45-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Sharding","concept":"Scatter-gather query","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a scatter-gather query?","answer":"A scatter-gather query sends a request to multiple shards or replicas and then combines their responses into a single result.","explanation":"Scatter-gather enables queries that cannot be localized to one shard but introduces coordination and aggregation costs.","principle":"Distributed query fan-out trades query flexibility for network and coordination overhead.","rule":"Control fan-out and define partial-failure semantics before allowing broad distributed queries.","correct_example":"A query coordinator sends requests only to shards that may contain relevant data and merges the results.","invalid_example":"Broadcasting every query to every shard regardless of the query predicate.","problem":"Fan-out grows with cluster size and increases latency and failure probability.","failure_mode":"Distributed query amplification","cause":"Poor data locality or unrestricted query routing.","solution":"Improve shard-key locality, maintain routing metadata, or constrain query patterns.","alternative":"Use a dedicated query index or replicated read model for cross-shard access.","tradeoff":"Additional indexes or read models consume storage and synchronization resources.","constraint":"Partial shard failures require explicit result semantics.","edge_case":"One slow shard can dominate the total query latency.","security_implication":"Every shard must enforce authorization; the coordinator must not become an authorization bypass.","performance_implication":"Tail latency often increases with the number of parallel shard calls.","testing_guidance":"Measure query latency as shard count and failure rate increase.","verification_guidance":"Verify that fan-out remains within defined limits and partial failures are handled correctly.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Sharding","Distributed queries"],"prerequisites":["Shard routing","Aggregation"],"related_records":["D17-B45-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Design_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B45-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Sharding","concept":"Hot shard","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a hot shard?","answer":"A hot shard is a shard that receives disproportionately high traffic, storage growth, or computational work compared with other shards, causing it to become a system bottleneck.","explanation":"A shard distribution can appear balanced by key count while being highly imbalanced by request frequency or record size.","principle":"Distributed load must be evaluated using workload intensity rather than object count alone.","rule":"Monitor per-shard traffic, resource utilization, latency, and data growth.","correct_example":"The system detects one tenant generating most requests on a tenant-keyed shard and applies a workload-aware mitigation.","invalid_example":"Assuming equal record counts imply equal shard load.","problem":"One shard becomes saturated while others remain underutilized.","failure_mode":"Shard hotspot","cause":"Skewed key distribution or workload concentration.","solution":"Use compound keys, sub-sharding, workload-aware routing, or isolate exceptionally large tenants/workloads.","alternative":"Replicate read-heavy data or introduce caching where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Splitting hotspots can complicate routing and consistency.","constraint":"Any mitigation must preserve correctness and authorization boundaries.","edge_case":"A single logical key may be inherently hot and difficult to split without application redesign.","security_implication":"Tenant-specific hotspots can create noisy-neighbor or denial-of-service risks.","performance_implication":"Hot shards increase tail latency and can constrain cluster-wide throughput.","testing_guidance":"Generate Zipfian and single-key-heavy workloads against candidate shard schemes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no supported workload class creates an unbounded single-shard bottleneck.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Sharding","Load distribution"],"prerequisites":["Shard-key selection"],"related_records":["D17-B45-R01","D17-B44-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Database_Design_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B46-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache-aside pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the cache-aside pattern?","answer":"In cache-aside, the application checks the cache first, reads from the authoritative data source on a miss, and then populates the cache with the retrieved result.","explanation":"The application explicitly controls cache population and invalidation rather than requiring the cache to automatically mirror the source.","principle":"A cache should be treated as an optimization unless its correctness contract explicitly makes it authoritative.","rule":"Define cache-miss, population, expiration, invalidation, and failure behavior.","correct_example":"A read miss fetches the current value from the database and stores it in the cache with a defined expiration policy.","invalid_example":"Assuming a cache-aside entry is always current because it originated from the database.","problem":"Cached values can become stale after the source changes.","failure_mode":"Stale cache read","cause":"No invalidation or bounded freshness policy.","solution":"Use TTLs, explicit invalidation, versioning, or a stronger consistency mechanism as required.","alternative":"Use write-through or another cache strategy when its semantics fit the workload.","tradeoff":"Cache-aside is flexible but leaves consistency responsibility with application logic.","constraint":"Cache failure must not violate the authoritative data model unless the cache is intentionally part of correctness.","edge_case":"A cache can fail between database read and cache population without invalidating the source result.","security_implication":"Cached data requires the same or stronger access controls as the underlying data.","performance_implication":"Cache hits reduce source load and latency; misses create additional source traffic.","testing_guidance":"Test cache misses, stale entries, cache outages, source outages, and concurrent updates.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cache behavior matches the documented freshness and correctness contract.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Caching","Authoritative storage"],"prerequisites":["Distributed storage","Consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B28-R01","D17-B46-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B46-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache stampede","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a cache stampede?","answer":"A cache stampede occurs when many clients simultaneously miss or invalidate the same cached item and generate a burst of requests to the underlying source.","explanation":"The cache can unintentionally synchronize many clients around the same expiration event, overwhelming the source precisely when caching was intended to protect it.","principle":"Cache expiration can create correlated load rather than merely reducing average load.","rule":"Design high-contention cache entries with controlled refresh and expiration behavior.","correct_example":"A system uses request coalescing, randomized expiration, or controlled refresh for highly popular entries.","invalid_example":"Giving every client the exact same expiration time for a heavily requested key and allowing all to refresh independently.","problem":"A large number of misses reach the source simultaneously.","failure_mode":"Cache-induced overload","cause":"Synchronized expiration or refresh without coordination.","solution":"Use jittered TTLs, single-flight refresh, prewarming, stale-while-revalidate, or bounded refresh concurrency.","alternative":"Replicate or cache the data at additional layers when appropriate.","tradeoff":"More coordination reduces stampedes but adds cache-management complexity.","constraint":"Serving stale data may be unacceptable for some correctness-sensitive values.","edge_case":"A source outage can cause repeated refresh attempts to amplify the incident.","security_implication":"Attackers may target popular cache keys to trigger source exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Stampedes produce sudden spikes in backend latency and resource use.","testing_guidance":"Expire a highly popular key under peak concurrency and measure backend amplification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that concurrent cache misses remain within a defined backend load bound.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Caching","Concurrency"],"prerequisites":["Cache-aside","Load control"],"related_records":["D17-B46-R01","D17-B36-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B46-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Negative caching","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is negative caching?","answer":"Negative caching stores the result that a requested item does not exist or cannot currently be resolved, so repeated requests do not repeatedly query the underlying source.","explanation":"Negative entries can protect a source from repeated requests for nonexistent keys but can become incorrect when the item is created later.","principle":"Absence is also cacheable state, but it requires its own freshness semantics.","rule":"Apply bounded expiration and invalidation rules to negative cache entries.","correct_example":"A short-lived negative entry prevents repeated nonexistent-key lookups while allowing newly created data to become visible after expiration or invalidation.","invalid_example":"Caching a nonexistent object indefinitely.","problem":"A later-created resource remains invisible because its prior absence is cached.","failure_mode":"Stale negative result","cause":"Negative entry has excessive lifetime or missing invalidation.","solution":"Use short TTLs or explicit invalidation on creation.","alternative":"Avoid negative caching when creation latency and freshness requirements make it unsafe.","tradeoff":"Negative caching reduces repeated misses but can delay visibility of newly created data.","constraint":"The acceptable negative-cache lifetime depends on application semantics.","edge_case":"An authorization failure must not be confused with object nonexistence if information disclosure matters.","security_implication":"Negative caching can unintentionally reveal whether resources exist across authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"It can substantially reduce repeated source queries for high-volume nonexistent keys.","testing_guidance":"Request missing keys repeatedly, then create them and measure visibility delay.","verification_guidance":"Verify that negative entries expire or invalidate within the documented freshness bound.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Caching"],"prerequisites":["Cache freshness","Data lifecycle"],"related_records":["D17-B46-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"MEDIUM","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B47-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cache Consistency","concept":"Write-through caching","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is write-through caching?","answer":"Write-through caching updates the cache as part of the write path so that successful writes update both the cache and the underlying authoritative storage according to the defined coordination semantics.","explanation":"The exact atomicity between cache and source depends on the implementation; write-through does not automatically mean the two stores are transactionally atomic.","principle":"A cache write strategy must explicitly define the relationship between cache state and authoritative state.","rule":"Do not claim atomic cache-source consistency unless the implementation actually provides it.","correct_example":"A write path updates the authoritative store and cache according to a documented success and failure protocol.","invalid_example":"Assuming the cache and database can never diverge merely because both are updated during writes.","problem":"One side can succeed while the other fails.","failure_mode":"Cache-source divergence","cause":"No shared transaction or reconciliation mechanism.","solution":"Use transactional coordination where supported, invalidation, versioning, or reconciliation.","alternative":"Use cache-aside with explicit invalidation when write-through complexity is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Write-through can reduce read misses after writes but adds write-path complexity.","constraint":"Failure semantics must be defined for partial updates.","edge_case":"Cache unavailability may occur after the database commit.","security_implication":"Sensitive data must not become available in cache before authoritative authorization rules permit it.","performance_implication":"Every write may incur cache update overhead.","testing_guidance":"Fail the cache and source independently at each stage of a write.","verification_guidance":"Verify the documented consistency state after every partial-failure scenario.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Caching","Storage"],"prerequisites":["Cache consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B46-R01","D17-B40-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B47-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cache Consistency","concept":"Cache invalidation race","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can concurrent reads and writes create a cache invalidation race?","answer":"A stale read can arrive after a newer write has occurred and repopulate the cache with an older value, depending on timing and the invalidation/update protocol.","explanation":"The cache may receive operations in an order different from the logical order of source changes and reads.","principle":"Cache correctness depends on ordering between source updates, reads, invalidations, and cache writes.","rule":"Use versioning, compare-and-set, ordered invalidation, or another mechanism when stale repopulation would violate requirements.","correct_example":"A cache entry carries a source version and rejects a write carrying an older version.","invalid_example":"Blindly writing every asynchronously fetched value into the cache regardless of version.","problem":"An old read overwrites a newer cached value.","failure_mode":"Stale cache resurrection","cause":"No ordering or version check on cache population.","solution":"Use monotonic versions or serialized update mechanisms.","alternative":"Invalidate after source writes and tolerate bounded stale reads when acceptable.","tradeoff":"Version checks add metadata and comparison work but improve correctness.","constraint":"The authoritative source must expose a usable ordering or version signal.","edge_case":"Concurrent updates from different regions may require causal or stronger ordering metadata.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or policy data can produce incorrect access decisions.","performance_implication":"Version validation adds small overhead but can prevent expensive cache-induced inconsistencies.","testing_guidance":"Race delayed reads against newer writes and invalidations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that an older version can never overwrite a newer version when monotonic freshness is required.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Caching","Versioning"],"prerequisites":["Cache-aside","Consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B47-R01","D17-B12-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B47-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cache Consistency","concept":"Stale-while-revalidate","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is stale-while-revalidate caching?","answer":"Stale-while-revalidate allows an existing cached value to serve while a background process refreshes it, subject to a defined freshness and staleness policy.","explanation":"The pattern trades some freshness for lower request latency and reduced synchronous refresh load.","principle":"Freshness can be treated as a bounded resource when serving slightly stale data is acceptable.","rule":"Define maximum staleness separately from normal cache lifetime.","correct_example":"A request receives a value within an allowed stale window while one controlled refresh updates the cache.","invalid_example":"Serving arbitrarily old data indefinitely because a refresh is occurring.","problem":"Background refresh fails repeatedly while stale values continue being served.","failure_mode":"Unbounded staleness","cause":"No maximum stale-age enforcement.","solution":"Set a hard stale limit and define behavior when refresh repeatedly fails.","alternative":"Require synchronous refresh after the stale limit expires.","tradeoff":"Lower latency and lower source load are obtained at the cost of bounded freshness.","constraint":"The pattern is unsuitable for values requiring immediate consistency.","edge_case":"The source may remain unavailable throughout the entire stale window.","security_implication":"Authorization, credentials, and security-policy data often require stricter freshness guarantees.","performance_implication":"Background refresh can smooth load but requires controlled concurrency.","testing_guidance":"Keep the source unavailable through the refresh period and verify stale-age enforcement.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no value exceeds the documented maximum staleness.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Caching","Refresh"],"prerequisites":["Cache freshness"],"related_records":["D17-B46-R02","D17-B47-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Caching_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B48-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Distributed rate limiting","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is distributed rate limiting harder than local rate limiting?","answer":"Distributed rate limiting requires coordinating request allowances across multiple instances or regions so that the aggregate limit is respected despite concurrent requests and independent local state.","explanation":"Independent local counters can each permit traffic up to the local limit, causing aggregate traffic to exceed the intended global limit.","principle":"A global limit requires a shared or coordinated accounting model.","rule":"Define whether the limit is local, per instance, per tenant, regional, or global before choosing the limiter architecture.","correct_example":"A shared token or quota state coordinates allowances for a tenant across multiple service instances.","invalid_example":"Multiplying a global intended limit by the number of instances without accounting for scaling changes.","problem":"Aggregate traffic exceeds the intended quota.","failure_mode":"Distributed quota overshoot","cause":"Independent local rate-limit state.","solution":"Use centralized, partitioned, or carefully bounded distributed quota accounting.","alternative":"Use local limits when approximate protection is sufficient.","tradeoff":"Stronger global limits require coordination and can become a dependency or bottleneck.","constraint":"Clock, synchronization, and failure semantics must be specified.","edge_case":"A regional partition can prevent global quota coordination.","security_implication":"Rate limits are important controls against abuse and resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Shared rate-limit state adds network and coordination latency.","testing_guidance":"Generate concurrent requests across many instances and measure aggregate enforcement.","verification_guidance":"Verify quota behavior under normal operation, scaling, and limiter-state failure.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Rate limiting","Distributed state"],"prerequisites":["Concurrency","Quotas"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R03","D17-B36-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Rate_Limiting_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B48-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Token bucket","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem does a token-bucket rate limiter solve?","answer":"A token-bucket limiter models an allowed average rate while permitting a bounded burst determined by the bucket capacity, provided sufficient tokens have accumulated.","explanation":"Tokens are replenished according to the configured rate and consumed by requests according to their cost.","principle":"Rate and burst capacity should be modeled separately when workloads legitimately contain short bursts.","rule":"Define token generation rate, bucket capacity, request cost, and behavior when insufficient tokens exist.","correct_example":"A service permits short bursts up to bucket capacity while enforcing the configured long-term token replenishment rate.","invalid_example":"Treating bucket capacity and refill rate as the same parameter.","problem":"Burst allowance does not match the intended traffic policy.","failure_mode":"Incorrect throttling behavior","cause":"Rate and capacity semantics are configured or implemented incorrectly.","solution":"Model refill rate and maximum burst independently and test both.","alternative":"Use a leaky-bucket or fixed-window mechanism when its semantics better fit the requirement.","tradeoff":"Token buckets allow controlled bursts but require accurate state management.","constraint":"Distributed implementations require a defined consistency model for token state.","edge_case":"Large request costs can consume many tokens at once.","security_implication":"Attackers may exploit burst capacity to concentrate expensive requests.","performance_implication":"Limiter state operations add per-request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test steady traffic, burst traffic, sustained overload, and concurrent requests.","verification_guidance":"Verify both long-term rate and maximum burst against the specification.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Rate limiting"],"prerequisites":["Quota accounting"],"related_records":["D17-B48-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Rate_Limiting_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B48-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate-limit fairness","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"Why does fairness matter in distributed rate limiting?","answer":"A rate limiter can protect aggregate capacity while still allowing one tenant, identity, or request class to consume a disproportionate share unless quotas are partitioned or weighted appropriately.","explanation":"Fairness is a policy choice that may involve per-tenant, per-user, priority-based, or weighted limits.","principle":"Aggregate capacity protection and fair resource allocation are related but distinct goals.","rule":"Specify the fairness unit and priority policy rather than assuming a single global rate limit is sufficient.","correct_example":"Each tenant receives an independent quota while system-wide limits provide an additional safety boundary.","invalid_example":"Using one global bucket for all customers and claiming that it guarantees tenant fairness.","problem":"One workload monopolizes shared capacity.","failure_mode":"Noisy-neighbor overload","cause":"No per-principal allocation policy.","solution":"Use hierarchical or partitioned quotas.","alternative":"Use weighted fair scheduling for workloads with different priorities.","tradeoff":"More granular fairness requires more accounting state and policy complexity.","constraint":"Identity classification must be reliable and resistant to spoofing.","edge_case":"A single tenant may legitimately generate more traffic than others.","security_implication":"Rate-limit identity must be tied to trusted authentication or authorization context.","performance_implication":"Fine-grained quota accounting increases state and lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Mix tenants with highly unequal request rates and verify resource allocation.","verification_guidance":"Measure actual allocation against the documented fairness policy.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Rate limiting","Multi-tenancy"],"prerequisites":["Authentication","Quota policy"],"related_records":["D17-B48-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Rate_Limiting_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B49-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Circuit Breakers","concept":"Circuit breaker state machine","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a circuit breaker in a distributed system?","answer":"A circuit breaker is a control mechanism that changes behavior when a dependency exhibits repeated failures or unacceptable latency, commonly transitioning between closed, open, and recovery or half-open states.","explanation":"The mechanism prevents every request from continuing to consume resources against a dependency that is currently failing.","principle":"Failure isolation can require temporarily stopping calls rather than continuously retrying them.","rule":"Define failure thresholds, open duration, recovery probes, and state-transition semantics explicitly.","correct_example":"After a configured failure threshold, the caller stops normal requests and periodically permits limited recovery probes.","invalid_example":"Opening the circuit permanently after one transient error.","problem":"Continued requests amplify dependency failure.","failure_mode":"Dependency failure amplification","cause":"No controlled failure isolation.","solution":"Use a circuit breaker with calibrated thresholds and recovery behavior.","alternative":"Use admission control, load shedding, or fail-fast behavior when a circuit breaker is not appropriate.","tradeoff":"Circuit breaking protects resources but can temporarily reject requests even while the dependency is recovering.","constraint":"The circuit-breaker decision is based on observations and can be wrong.","edge_case":"A partition can make a healthy dependency appear failed to one caller group.","security_implication":"Circuit state must not become an attacker-controlled mechanism for suppressing critical operations.","performance_implication":"Open circuits reduce unnecessary downstream traffic and can protect system capacity.","testing_guidance":"Inject sustained failure, intermittent failure, recovery, and false-positive latency spikes.","verification_guidance":"Verify every state transition and ensure recovery probes are bounded.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Failure isolation","Timeouts"],"prerequisites":["Distributed failures","Health signals"],"related_records":["D17-B39-R03","D17-B42-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Reliability_Guidance","Circuit_Breaker_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B49-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Bulkheads","concept":"Bulkhead isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is bulkhead isolation in distributed systems?","answer":"Bulkhead isolation limits the resources or failure domain available to one workload or dependency so that its failure cannot consume all capacity needed by unrelated workloads.","explanation":"Isolation can be implemented with separate pools, queues, connections, processes, cells, or other resource boundaries.","principle":"Shared capacity creates correlated failure risk; bounded isolation reduces blast radius.","rule":"Partition critical resources when one dependency or workload must not be able to exhaust capacity for others.","correct_example":"Critical traffic uses a dedicated connection or worker pool separate from noncritical traffic.","invalid_example":"Allowing every workload to consume an unlimited shared worker pool.","problem":"One dependency consumes all workers and prevents unrelated operations from progressing.","failure_mode":"Cascading resource exhaustion","cause":"No resource isolation.","solution":"Use bounded pools, quotas, cells, queues, or separate failure domains.","alternative":"Use priority scheduling when full physical separation is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Isolation improves resilience but can leave reserved capacity unused.","constraint":"Isolation boundaries must be chosen according to actual failure domains and workload priorities.","edge_case":"An isolated pool can fail independently while the rest of the system remains healthy.","security_implication":"Isolation can reduce cross-tenant impact and limit lateral resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Dedicated pools can improve tail latency during contention but may reduce aggregate utilization.","testing_guidance":"Saturate one workload's resources and verify independent workloads continue operating.","verification_guidance":"Measure whether defined resource boundaries remain enforceable during overload.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Resource isolation"],"prerequisites":["Failure domains","Capacity management"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B49-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Failure Isolation","concept":"Cell-based failure containment","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of cell-based architecture in distributed systems?","answer":"Cell-based architecture partitions a service into relatively independent cells so that a failure or overload in one cell has a bounded impact on other cells.","explanation":"A cell may contain the resources required to serve a subset of workload, with explicit boundaries between cells.","principle":"Reducing the size of a failure domain can limit blast radius.","rule":"Define routing, isolation, capacity, and recovery boundaries for each cell.","correct_example":"A tenant subset is served by an independent cell so a cell-specific failure does not consume resources allocated to other cells.","invalid_example":"Calling a set of logically named instances separate cells while they still share all critical dependencies.","problem":"Nominal isolation does not correspond to actual failure isolation.","failure_mode":"Hidden shared dependency","cause":"Cells share an unbounded common bottleneck.","solution":"Identify and isolate critical shared dependencies or explicitly account for them.","alternative":"Use bulkheads or regional partitions when full cell architecture is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Smaller failure domains can improve resilience but increase deployment and routing complexity.","constraint":"Cross-cell operations require their own consistency and coordination model.","edge_case":"A common control plane can become a shared failure domain across all cells.","security_implication":"Cell boundaries can support tenant isolation but must not replace authorization.","performance_implication":"Cells may improve tail behavior by reducing contention while creating capacity fragmentation.","testing_guidance":"Fail one complete cell and separately fail shared control-plane components.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cell failure remains within the documented blast-radius boundary.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Bulkheads","Failure domains"],"prerequisites":["Resource isolation","Service routing"],"related_records":["D17-B49-R02","D17-B41-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Well_Architected_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B50-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Graceful degradation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is graceful degradation in a distributed system?","answer":"Graceful degradation is the deliberate reduction of functionality or quality when dependencies, capacity, or resources are constrained, allowing essential functionality to continue instead of allowing the entire system to fail.","explanation":"Degradation must distinguish essential functions from optional features and define safe fallback behavior.","principle":"Partial functionality can preserve system utility when full functionality is unavailable.","rule":"Define degradation states explicitly and ensure that degraded behavior remains correct and safe.","correct_example":"A recommendation service becomes unavailable while core account operations continue without recommendations.","invalid_example":"Returning stale or unauthorized data as a fallback simply to keep the request successful.","problem":"A fallback preserves availability by violating correctness or security.","failure_mode":"Unsafe degradation","cause":"Fallback behavior was optimized for availability without defining invariants.","solution":"Define non-negotiable correctness and security properties that degradation may never violate.","alternative":"Fail closed for operations where incorrect results are more harmful than unavailability.","tradeoff":"Degradation can improve availability but may reduce functionality or data freshness.","constraint":"Critical operations may have no acceptable degraded mode.","edge_case":"The dependency failure may persist longer than the designed fallback window.","security_implication":"Security controls, authorization, auditability, and policy enforcement must not be bypassed by fallback paths.","performance_implication":"Fallbacks can reduce dependency load but may shift work to another subsystem.","testing_guidance":"Disable optional and mandatory dependencies independently and verify each defined degradation mode.","verification_guidance":"Verify that degraded states preserve all explicitly protected invariants.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Failure isolation","Fallback design"],"prerequisites":["Dependency classification","Failure handling"],"related_records":["D17-B49-R01","D17-B49-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Well_Architected_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B50-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Fail-open versus fail-closed","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How should fail-open and fail-closed behavior be selected for distributed failures?","answer":"Fail-open preserves access or functionality when a dependency fails, while fail-closed denies or stops the operation. The correct behavior depends on the protected invariant, risk of false denial, and consequences of incorrect acceptance.","explanation":"Neither behavior is universally appropriate. Security authorization commonly requires careful fail-closed reasoning, while some availability-oriented features may safely fail open.","principle":"Failure behavior should be derived from the consequence of an incorrect decision rather than from availability alone.","rule":"Classify the operation's safety and security consequences before selecting fail-open or fail-closed behavior.","correct_example":"An optional analytics feature fails open by disabling analytics, while a protected authorization decision does not silently grant access when its authoritative policy cannot be evaluated.","invalid_example":"Granting privileged access because the authorization service is unavailable.","problem":"A dependency failure changes the security decision in an unsafe direction.","failure_mode":"Unsafe failure policy","cause":"Availability was prioritized without classifying the protected invariant.","solution":"Define explicit fail-open/fail-closed policies per operation and verify them under dependency failure.","alternative":"Use cached or cryptographically validated policy state when bounded offline decisions are acceptable.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed can reduce availability; fail-open can increase safety or security risk depending on the operation.","constraint":"Policy semantics must be explicit and auditable.","edge_case":"A cached decision can itself become stale during an outage.","security_implication":"Failure behavior can directly determine whether unauthorized actions are possible.","performance_implication":"Fail-closed paths can increase retries or user-visible failures; fail-open paths can reduce immediate latency but may require reconciliation.","testing_guidance":"Disconnect authorization, configuration, identity, and policy dependencies independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify the actual outcome for each failure mode against the documented security and availability policy.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Graceful degradation","Authorization"],"prerequisites":["Failure policy","Security invariants"],"related_records":["D17-B50-R01","D17-B42-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Security_Literature","AWS_Well_Architected_Reliability_Guidance"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B50-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Graceful Degradation","concept":"Fallback amplification","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a fallback mechanism amplify rather than reduce a distributed-system failure?","answer":"A fallback can amplify failure when it redirects large amounts of traffic to an alternative dependency or computation path that lacks sufficient capacity, causing the original failure to propagate into the fallback path.","explanation":"Fallbacks are themselves workloads and must be capacity-tested under the traffic generated by the failure they are intended to handle.","principle":"A fallback is another production dependency and must be analyzed as part of the failure domain.","rule":"Capacity-test fallback paths under simultaneous primary failure and peak traffic.","correct_example":"When a primary recommendation service fails, a bounded local default path handles requests without overwhelming another shared dependency.","invalid_example":"Routing all failed traffic to a single secondary service that has only enough capacity for normal background use.","problem":"Primary failure creates overload in the fallback.","failure_mode":"Fallback cascade","cause":"Fallback capacity and dependency relationships were not modeled.","solution":"Bound fallback traffic, isolate fallback resources, cache safe defaults, or reject nonessential work.","alternative":"Use graceful feature removal rather than a resource-intensive fallback.","tradeoff":"A simpler fallback may preserve fewer features but have a smaller failure footprint.","constraint":"Fallback behavior must preserve security and correctness invariants.","edge_case":"Multiple dependencies may fail simultaneously, activating several fallback paths at once.","security_implication":"Fallbacks must not bypass authorization, validation, auditing, or tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Fallback load can create a secondary latency spike and resource exhaustion event.","testing_guidance":"Fail the primary dependency under peak load and measure the complete fallback dependency graph.","verification_guidance":"Verify that fallback resource consumption remains bounded and does not create a secondary outage.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Graceful degradation","Failure isolation"],"prerequisites":["Fallback design","Capacity planning"],"related_records":["D17-B49-R03","D17-B36-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["AWS_Well_Architected_Reliability_Guidance","Distributed_Failure_Handling_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B51-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication","concept":"Primary-backup replication","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is primary-backup replication?","answer":"Primary-backup replication maintains one designated primary copy that normally accepts writes and one or more backup copies that receive replicated state.","explanation":"The primary provides an ordered write path while backups maintain additional copies for durability or failover.","principle":"A replication topology should define which replica is authoritative for writes and how authority changes after failure.","rule":"Define write ordering, backup acknowledgement, promotion, and stale-replica handling explicitly.","correct_example":"Writes are accepted by the primary and replicated to backups according to a documented acknowledgement policy.","invalid_example":"Promoting an arbitrarily selected stale backup without determining whether it contains all required committed state.","problem":"The primary fails while replicas may contain different amounts of state.","failure_mode":"Stale replica promotion","cause":"Replica freshness is not part of failover selection.","solution":"Track replication progress and select a replica according to the system's recovery guarantee.","alternative":"Use quorum-based or consensus-backed replication when stronger coordination is required.","tradeoff":"Primary-backup simplifies write ordering but creates primary dependency and failover complexity.","constraint":"The replication acknowledgement policy determines durability guarantees.","edge_case":"The primary can fail after accepting a write but before every backup receives it.","security_implication":"Replica promotion must preserve authorization and tenant-isolation state.","performance_implication":"Synchronous replication adds write latency while asynchronous replication reduces latency but increases potential lag.","testing_guidance":"Fail the primary at different points during write replication.","verification_guidance":"Verify recovery behavior for acknowledged and unacknowledged writes separately.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Replication","Failover"],"prerequisites":["Failure models","Distributed storage"],"related_records":["D17-B23-R03","D17-B77-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B51-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B51-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication","concept":"Synchronous versus asynchronous replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between synchronous and asynchronous replication?","answer":"Synchronous replication requires defined replica acknowledgement before the write is considered complete, whereas asynchronous replication can acknowledge the write before all replicas have received it.","explanation":"The distinction directly affects write latency, durability exposure, and behavior during replica or network failures.","principle":"Replication acknowledgement semantics define part of the durability contract.","rule":"Document exactly which replica acknowledgements are required before reporting write success.","correct_example":"A system explicitly states that a write is durable after acknowledgement from a quorum of replicas.","invalid_example":"Calling replication synchronous merely because replicas eventually receive every write.","problem":"Clients misunderstand the durability implied by successful writes.","failure_mode":"Durability contract mismatch","cause":"Replication timing and acknowledgement semantics are undocumented.","solution":"Specify acknowledgement boundaries and failure behavior.","alternative":"Use asynchronous replication when lower write latency is more important than immediate replica convergence.","tradeoff":"Synchronous replication can increase latency or reduce availability during communication failures.","constraint":"The selected policy must match business durability requirements.","edge_case":"A replica acknowledgement can be received immediately before that replica fails.","security_implication":"Security-critical state may require stronger durability guarantees than ordinary cache data.","performance_implication":"Synchronous remote acknowledgements can materially increase write latency.","testing_guidance":"Measure write outcomes during replica delay, loss, and partition scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that reported success matches the documented replication acknowledgement condition.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Replication"],"prerequisites":["Quorums","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R02","D17-B51-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B51-R01"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B51-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication","concept":"Replica lag","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is replica lag?","answer":"Replica lag is the delay between a state change becoming available at one replica and that change becoming available at another replica.","explanation":"Lag can result from network delay, processing backlog, resource contention, throttling, or replication failures.","principle":"Replica freshness is a measurable distributed-system property rather than an assumed condition.","rule":"Monitor and define acceptable lag according to the consistency requirements of each workload.","correct_example":"A read path routes freshness-sensitive requests only to replicas whose replication position satisfies the required bound.","invalid_example":"Assuming all replicas contain the latest state because replication is configured.","problem":"Clients read stale data beyond the allowed freshness window.","failure_mode":"Excessive replica staleness","cause":"Replication backlog or unavailable replication path.","solution":"Monitor lag, control traffic, repair replicas, and route reads according to freshness requirements.","alternative":"Use a primary or quorum read for operations requiring stronger freshness.","tradeoff":"Avoiding stale reads can increase latency or reduce read availability.","constraint":"Freshness guarantees require a defined measurement and observation point.","edge_case":"A replica may report low replication lag while serving requests slowly due to local resource contention.","security_implication":"Stale security-policy or authorization data can produce unsafe decisions.","performance_implication":"Replica lag can increase read latency when fresher replicas are selectively required.","testing_guidance":"Inject replication delay and observe read-routing behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that freshness-sensitive operations never silently exceed their documented lag bound.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Replication","Monitoring"],"prerequisites":["Replication position","Consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B77-R02","D17-B28-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B52-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Quorum Replication","concept":"Quorum read and write semantics","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"How do read and write quorums affect replicated data access?","answer":"A quorum replication system defines how many replicas must participate in reads and writes and relies on overlap properties to obtain the required consistency behavior.","explanation":"The exact guarantees depend on quorum sizes, replica failure assumptions, conflict handling, and the protocol used to select or reconcile values.","principle":"Replica overlap is a mechanism for obtaining information about distributed state without requiring every replica.","rule":"Define quorum sizes and intersection requirements together with failure and conflict semantics.","correct_example":"A system requires a write quorum and read quorum whose documented intersection is sufficient for its freshness guarantee.","invalid_example":"Assuming any majority read automatically provides linearizability.","problem":"Quorum parameters are selected without matching them to the desired consistency model.","failure_mode":"Incorrect quorum guarantee","cause":"Quorum intersection is treated as sufficient without considering protocol semantics.","solution":"Specify the complete read/write protocol and prove or verify its stated guarantee.","alternative":"Use consensus-backed linearizable storage when the required semantics demand it.","tradeoff":"Larger quorums can strengthen availability or freshness properties depending on the design but may increase latency.","constraint":"The replica failure model and membership must remain within the protocol assumptions.","edge_case":"Concurrent writes can require version comparison or conflict resolution.","security_implication":"Every participating replica must enforce authorization and data-integrity requirements.","performance_implication":"Larger quorum operations increase network coordination and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Test quorum operations during replica loss, delay, and concurrent writes.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual quorum intersection and conflict semantics against the specified consistency claim.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Quorum systems","Replication"],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R01","D17-B21-R02","D17-B28-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Quorum_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B52-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication Repair","concept":"Read repair","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is read repair?","answer":"Read repair is a replication-repair technique in which a read operation detects divergent replica state and initiates correction of replicas that are behind or inconsistent.","explanation":"The read path can therefore contribute to convergence rather than merely retrieving data.","principle":"Normal reads can be used as opportunities to repair replica divergence when the consistency protocol permits it.","rule":"Repair only after correctly identifying the authoritative or preferred version according to the replication protocol.","correct_example":"A quorum read detects an older replica version and schedules an update using the protocol's version ordering.","invalid_example":"Overwriting divergent data based only on which response arrived first.","problem":"Replica divergence persists or is incorrectly repaired.","failure_mode":"Incorrect read repair","cause":"No reliable version or conflict-resolution mechanism.","solution":"Use explicit version metadata and deterministic conflict resolution.","alternative":"Use background anti-entropy repair independent of reads.","tradeoff":"Read repair can accelerate convergence but adds work to read operations.","constraint":"Repair must not violate concurrent-update semantics.","edge_case":"A concurrent write may occur between read comparison and repair.","security_implication":"Repair operations must retain normal authorization and integrity checks.","performance_implication":"Repair traffic can increase read latency and background write load.","testing_guidance":"Create divergent replicas and perform concurrent reads and writes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that repair never overwrites a newer valid version with an older one.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Replication","Versioning"],"prerequisites":["Quorum reads","Conflict resolution"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B47-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B52-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replication Repair","concept":"Background repair","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why is background replica repair useful?","answer":"Background repair continuously or periodically identifies and corrects replica divergence without requiring a client read to discover the inconsistency.","explanation":"This allows replicas that are rarely read to converge and can reduce the duration of silent divergence.","principle":"Repair coverage should not depend exclusively on workload access patterns.","rule":"Provide a repair mechanism for replicas that may not receive ordinary reads.","correct_example":"A storage system periodically compares replica state and repairs missing or divergent data.","invalid_example":"Assuming an unread replica will eventually repair itself merely because other replicas remain healthy.","problem":"Cold replicas remain stale indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Undetected replica divergence","cause":"Repair depends entirely on client traffic.","solution":"Use scheduled anti-entropy or equivalent background repair.","alternative":"Use durable replicated logs with explicit catch-up mechanisms.","tradeoff":"Background repair consumes resources even when no clients are reading affected data.","constraint":"Repair must be throttled to avoid competing with foreground traffic.","edge_case":"A replica can remain offline long enough that incremental repair metadata is no longer sufficient.","security_implication":"Repair channels require authentication and integrity protection.","performance_implication":"Large repairs can consume network, disk, and CPU resources.","testing_guidance":"Keep a replica offline and verify recovery after it returns.","verification_guidance":"Measure convergence time and verify repaired state against the authoritative replication contract.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Anti-entropy","Replication"],"prerequisites":["Replica comparison","Version metadata"],"related_records":["D17-B43-R02","D17-B52-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Repair_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B53-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Version Metadata","concept":"Version vector","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What information can a version vector represent?","answer":"A version vector can represent causally relevant version progress across multiple replicas or writers, allowing a system to determine whether one state is causally newer, older, or concurrent with another under the model in which the vector is defined.","explanation":"Each participant contributes a component to the vector, and comparison can reveal causal relationships without relying on synchronized physical clocks.","principle":"Logical version metadata can encode causality without requiring globally synchronized time.","rule":"Define participant identity, increment rules, comparison semantics, and lifecycle for version-vector metadata.","correct_example":"Replica A's vector dominates replica B's vector, indicating that A includes the causal history represented by B.","invalid_example":"Treating a version vector as a globally ordered timestamp.","problem":"Concurrent versions are incorrectly treated as totally ordered.","failure_mode":"False causal ordering","cause":"Vector comparison semantics are misunderstood.","solution":"Use component-wise comparison and explicitly represent concurrency.","alternative":"Use Lamport timestamps when total logical ordering is sufficient for the application.","tradeoff":"Version vectors represent richer causal information but require more metadata.","constraint":"The vector's participants and membership model must be manageable.","edge_case":"Dynamic membership can cause vector metadata growth or identity-management complexity.","security_implication":"Untrusted clients must not be allowed to forge causal metadata that bypasses conflict rules.","performance_implication":"Vector metadata increases storage and network overhead.","testing_guidance":"Generate causally ordered and concurrent updates and verify vector comparisons.","verification_guidance":"Verify that concurrent states are not incorrectly classified as causally ordered.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Causality","Versioning"],"prerequisites":["Vector clocks"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R02","D17-B28-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B53-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Causal_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B53-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Version Metadata","concept":"Lamport timestamp versus version vector","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"When is a Lamport timestamp different from a version vector?","answer":"A Lamport timestamp provides a logical scalar ordering that respects happened-before, while a version vector can additionally represent whether two states are concurrent under its participant model.","explanation":"A scalar logical timestamp can impose an order even between concurrent events, whereas vector comparison can preserve concurrency information.","principle":"The required ordering information determines the appropriate metadata representation.","rule":"Do not use a scalar logical timestamp when the application must distinguish concurrency from causal ordering.","correct_example":"Conflict resolution uses vector-style metadata when concurrent updates must be detected explicitly.","invalid_example":"Using a Lamport timestamp alone to prove that one concurrent update caused another.","problem":"Concurrency information is lost.","failure_mode":"Incorrect conflict classification","cause":"A total scalar order is mistaken for causal evidence.","solution":"Use causal metadata appropriate to the required conflict semantics.","alternative":"Use hybrid or application-specific version metadata when its documented semantics are sufficient.","tradeoff":"Richer causal metadata increases overhead.","constraint":"The metadata model must scale with the number of relevant participants.","edge_case":"Participant churn can make vector metadata expensive.","security_implication":"Version metadata must be integrity-protected if it influences conflict or authorization decisions.","performance_implication":"Scalar timestamps are generally smaller than vector metadata but provide less causal detail.","testing_guidance":"Construct concurrent and causally related update pairs and compare metadata behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the selected metadata can represent every ordering distinction required by the conflict policy.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Lamport clocks","Version vectors"],"prerequisites":["Causality"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R01","D17-B12-R02","D17-B53-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B53-R01"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Time_and_Causality_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B53-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Conflict Resolution","concept":"Last-write-wins conflict resolution","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is the main risk of last-write-wins conflict resolution in distributed systems?","answer":"Last-write-wins can discard concurrent updates when multiple replicas independently modify the same logical item and the selected timestamp ordering does not represent application-level intent.","explanation":"The policy is simple but may lose valid concurrent information.","principle":"A technically deterministic conflict rule is not necessarily semantically correct for the application.","rule":"Use last-write-wins only when discarded concurrent updates are acceptable or recoverable.","correct_example":"A cache-like value where newer timestamped state intentionally supersedes older state uses a documented last-write-wins policy.","invalid_example":"Using last-write-wins for additive business events where concurrent updates must both be preserved.","problem":"Concurrent valid updates disappear.","failure_mode":"Silent conflict loss","cause":"Conflict resolution ignores application semantics.","solution":"Use mergeable representations, explicit conflict handling, or operation-based reconciliation where required.","alternative":"Use CRDT-style or domain-specific merge semantics.","tradeoff":"Simple resolution reduces coordination but can sacrifice information.","constraint":"Timestamp correctness and conflict policy must be clearly defined.","edge_case":"Clock skew can affect timestamp ordering when physical clocks are used.","security_implication":"Security-policy conflicts should not be silently resolved by a generic timestamp rule.","performance_implication":"Simple timestamp comparison is inexpensive compared with richer merge logic.","testing_guidance":"Create concurrent conflicting updates and verify the retained state.","verification_guidance":"Verify that conflict resolution preserves every application invariant that must survive concurrency.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Conflict resolution","Causality"],"prerequisites":["Replication","Consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R02","D17-B53-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Conflict_Resolution_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B54-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Merkle Trees","concept":"Merkle tree for replica comparison","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a Merkle tree help compare distributed replicas?","answer":"A Merkle tree summarizes data ranges using hashes so that replicas can compare higher-level hashes first and recursively identify regions that differ without transferring every record.","explanation":"Hash hierarchy can reduce the amount of data required to locate divergent regions when the replicas organize comparable data deterministically.","principle":"Hierarchical summaries can turn large-state comparison into progressively narrower comparisons.","rule":"Use deterministic partitioning and hashing semantics so corresponding tree nodes represent comparable state.","correct_example":"Two replicas compare root hashes, descend only through mismatching branches, and exchange the affected ranges.","invalid_example":"Comparing Merkle roots and assuming a mismatch identifies the exact differing record immediately.","problem":"Large replicas require expensive full-state comparison.","failure_mode":"Inefficient divergence detection","cause":"No hierarchical state summary.","solution":"Use a Merkle-style tree or another compact comparison structure where appropriate.","alternative":"Use version indexes or replicated logs when state differences can be derived more directly.","tradeoff":"Merkle trees reduce comparison traffic but require hash computation and tree maintenance.","constraint":"Hash collisions are theoretically possible and the chosen integrity assumptions must be documented.","edge_case":"Large simultaneous updates can invalidate many tree branches.","security_implication":"Cryptographic hash construction and collision resistance matter when hashes are security-sensitive.","performance_implication":"Tree maintenance adds computation but can reduce network transfer during comparison.","testing_guidance":"Create sparse and dense replica divergence and measure comparison cost.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every reported equal region corresponds to the same state under the tree's defined scope.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Hashing","Replica comparison"],"prerequisites":["Replication","Data partitioning"],"related_records":["D17-B43-R02","D17-B52-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Literature","Merkle_Tree_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B54-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Merkle Trees","concept":"Merkle tree scope correctness","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must the scope of a Merkle-tree hash be defined precisely?","answer":"A hash comparison is meaningful only when both sides hash equivalent logical state using compatible serialization, partitioning, ordering, and inclusion rules.","explanation":"Identical logical data can produce different summaries if representation rules differ, while an incorrectly scoped hash can omit relevant state.","principle":"A cryptographic summary is only as meaningful as the state definition it summarizes.","rule":"Document the exact data set, encoding, ordering, normalization, and hash construction represented by every tree node.","correct_example":"Both replicas hash the same canonical representation of the same partition range.","invalid_example":"Comparing roots generated from different serialization formats and treating the mismatch as data divergence.","problem":"False divergence or false equality is reported.","failure_mode":"Incorrect state comparison","cause":"Hash scope or representation differs between replicas.","solution":"Canonicalize representation and validate tree-construction compatibility.","alternative":"Use explicit record-level comparison for small or heterogeneous state.","tradeoff":"Canonicalization adds processing requirements but makes summaries comparable.","constraint":"Changes to hashing format require compatibility or migration planning.","edge_case":"A schema migration can change serialized representations without changing logical values.","security_implication":"Hash construction must not be weakened when integrity depends on it.","performance_implication":"Canonicalization can increase CPU cost during tree construction.","testing_guidance":"Compare logically equivalent data encoded through different serialization paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that equal logical state produces equal summaries and materially different scoped state produces distinguishable summaries under the chosen hash assumptions.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Merkle trees","Canonicalization"],"prerequisites":["Cryptographic hashing","State definition"],"related_records":["D17-B54-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Merkle_Tree_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B54-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Replica Reconciliation","concept":"Merkle-tree repair workflow","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is a safe high-level workflow for Merkle-based replica reconciliation?","answer":"A safe workflow compares compatible summaries, identifies divergent ranges, retrieves the relevant version information, applies the system's conflict rules, repairs state, and then verifies convergence.","explanation":"Hash mismatch identifies a region requiring investigation; it does not by itself determine which value is correct.","principle":"Detection of divergence and resolution of divergence are separate operations.","rule":"Never treat a hash mismatch as sufficient authority to overwrite one replica with another.","correct_example":"A mismatch causes range-level comparison followed by version-aware reconciliation and post-repair verification.","invalid_example":"Copying the entire source replica over the target immediately after a root-hash mismatch.","problem":"Valid concurrent updates can be destroyed during repair.","failure_mode":"Destructive reconciliation","cause":"Detection and authority selection are conflated.","solution":"Separate divergence detection, conflict resolution, repair, and verification.","alternative":"Use a durable ordered log to reconstruct missing state when available.","tradeoff":"Version-aware repair is safer but more complex than blind copying.","constraint":"The repair protocol must preserve concurrent writes.","edge_case":"New writes may arrive while repair is in progress.","security_implication":"Repair authorization must be separate from ordinary read access.","performance_implication":"Repair should be throttled to prevent foreground workload starvation.","testing_guidance":"Run concurrent writes while forcing divergent replicas through reconciliation.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence and preservation of valid concurrent updates after repair.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Merkle trees","Conflict resolution","Replication repair"],"prerequisites":["Version metadata","Replica comparison"],"related_records":["D17-B54-R01","D17-B52-R02","D17-B53-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Repair_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B55-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Data Recovery","concept":"Write-ahead log in distributed recovery","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can a write-ahead log help distributed storage recovery?","answer":"A write-ahead log records state-changing operations before or as part of applying them to durable state, allowing recovery procedures to replay or inspect the log after a failure according to the storage protocol.","explanation":"The exact durability and recovery guarantees depend on when log records become durable and how they relate to data checkpoints.","principle":"Durable operation history can provide a reconstruction path after partial failure.","rule":"Define the durability point, log ordering, replay semantics, truncation rules, and checkpoint relationship.","correct_example":"Recovery replays durable log records after the last valid checkpoint to reconstruct committed state.","invalid_example":"Assuming an operation exists in the log merely because it was generated in memory.","problem":"Recovery cannot distinguish durable from lost operations.","failure_mode":"Incorrect replay boundary","cause":"Durability semantics are not explicit.","solution":"Define and persist the log durability boundary before relying on it for recovery.","alternative":"Use replicated state-machine logs when command ordering and consensus are also required.","tradeoff":"Logging improves recoverability but adds storage and write overhead.","constraint":"Log replay must be deterministic and compatible with the stored state format.","edge_case":"A crash can occur while a log record or checkpoint is partially written.","security_implication":"Logs can contain sensitive data and must have appropriate access and retention controls.","performance_implication":"Durable logging adds I/O but can reduce recovery complexity.","testing_guidance":"Crash the system at log-write and state-apply boundaries and replay the surviving log.","verification_guidance":"Verify that replay produces exactly the state permitted by the durability contract.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Durability","Recovery"],"prerequisites":["Storage","Write ordering"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R03","D17-B16-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B55-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Data Recovery","concept":"Checkpoint and log replay boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a recovery system define the relationship between checkpoints and logs?","answer":"Recovery needs to know which log records are already reflected in the checkpoint and which records must be replayed, otherwise operations may be lost or applied twice.","explanation":"The checkpoint and log together form a recovery state representation whose boundary must be unambiguous.","principle":"Recovery correctness depends on an explicit relationship between persisted snapshots and operation history.","rule":"Record enough metadata to establish the exact replay boundary.","correct_example":"A checkpoint identifies the log position or equivalent state boundary from which replay must continue.","invalid_example":"Replaying an arbitrary suffix of the log because the checkpoint does not record its position.","problem":"Recovery duplicates or omits operations.","failure_mode":"Replay boundary error","cause":"Checkpoint and log state are not atomically or unambiguously related.","solution":"Persist a verifiable checkpoint-to-log relationship.","alternative":"Use self-describing snapshots with durable sequence metadata.","tradeoff":"Additional metadata improves recovery correctness but adds storage complexity.","constraint":"The boundary metadata must survive the same failure scenarios as the checkpoint.","edge_case":"A crash can occur during checkpoint creation.","security_implication":"Recovery metadata must be protected against unauthorized modification.","performance_implication":"Frequent checkpoints reduce replay time but increase checkpoint I/O.","testing_guidance":"Crash during checkpoint creation and test every possible recovery boundary.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery never replays a committed operation twice or omits a required operation.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Checkpointing","Write-ahead logging"],"prerequisites":["Durable state"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R02","D17-B55-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B55-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Data Recovery","concept":"Torn or partial state recovery","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why must distributed storage handle partially persisted state after a crash?","answer":"A process or machine can fail while multiple related pieces of state are being written, leaving a mixture of old and new representations unless the storage protocol provides atomicity or recovery metadata.","explanation":"Recovery must distinguish valid durable state from incomplete writes.","principle":"Crash boundaries can occur between logically related persistence operations.","rule":"Design storage recovery around actual durability and atomicity guarantees rather than assumed transaction boundaries.","correct_example":"Recovery detects an incomplete record or checkpoint and rolls back or reconstructs state according to the storage protocol.","invalid_example":"Treating every partially written file or record as valid state.","problem":"Corrupted or mixed-version state is accepted after recovery.","failure_mode":"Partial-state recovery corruption","cause":"No detection or recovery protocol for interrupted persistence.","solution":"Use atomic writes where available, checksums, journaling, version markers, or transactional storage semantics.","alternative":"Use copy-on-write snapshots with explicit commit markers.","tradeoff":"Stronger crash consistency usually increases metadata or I/O overhead.","constraint":"The mechanism must match the underlying filesystem or storage durability guarantees.","edge_case":"Power loss may expose different persistence behavior from a process crash.","security_implication":"Corrupted policy or authorization state must fail according to its safety contract.","performance_implication":"Checksums and durability barriers can increase write cost.","testing_guidance":"Inject crashes at persistence boundaries and inspect recovered state.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery rejects incomplete state and restores only valid committed state.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Recovery","Durability"],"prerequisites":["Storage atomicity","Checksums"],"related_records":["D17-B55-R01","D17-B55-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B56-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Multi-Region Systems","concept":"Active-active multi-region architecture","knowledge_type":"ARCHITECTURE_PATTERN","question":"What is active-active multi-region architecture?","answer":"An active-active architecture allows multiple geographic regions to concurrently serve production traffic rather than maintaining one region exclusively as a passive standby.","explanation":"Active-active designs can improve geographic availability and latency but require explicit cross-region consistency, conflict, and failure semantics.","principle":"Serving traffic concurrently from multiple regions transfers some failure and latency benefits into greater coordination complexity.","rule":"Define data ownership, replication, routing, failover, and conflict behavior for every region.","correct_example":"Users are routed to multiple regions while replicated data follows a documented conflict-resolution and consistency model.","invalid_example":"Calling two regions active-active while writes are actually permitted in only one region without documenting the distinction.","problem":"Regional independence is assumed without modeling cross-region state.","failure_mode":"Multi-region conflict","cause":"Concurrent writes have no defined reconciliation semantics.","solution":"Define ownership, conflict resolution, or coordination before enabling multi-region writes.","alternative":"Use active-passive architecture when cross-region write coordination is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Active-active can improve locality and regional resilience but increases distributed consistency complexity.","constraint":"Cross-region network partitions must be part of the failure model.","edge_case":"Two regions can remain independently writable during a partition.","security_implication":"Cross-region replication must preserve data residency, authorization, and encryption requirements.","performance_implication":"Local writes can reduce latency while replication traffic increases inter-region bandwidth usage.","testing_guidance":"Partition regions and issue concurrent reads and writes.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing, consistency, and recovery behavior during complete regional isolation.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Multi-region replication","Geo-routing"],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Failure domains"],"related_records":["D17-B28-R03","D17-B56-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B56-R03"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Multi_Region_Distributed_Systems_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B56-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Multi-Region Systems","concept":"Cross-region replication latency","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why does cross-region replication introduce additional consistency and latency considerations?","answer":"Data must traverse geographic network paths that have nonzero and variable latency, so a remote replica may lag behind the writer and cross-region coordination can increase request latency.","explanation":"Physical distance and network conditions constrain how quickly geographically separated state can be synchronized.","principle":"Geographic distribution creates unavoidable communication cost that must be incorporated into consistency design.","rule":"Separate latency-sensitive local operations from operations requiring synchronous cross-region coordination when possible.","correct_example":"A service accepts local writes and documents the resulting cross-region freshness bound.","invalid_example":"Assuming geographically separated replicas can maintain zero-latency synchronization.","problem":"Latency expectations and consistency guarantees conflict.","failure_mode":"Cross-region latency amplification","cause":"Remote coordination is placed on every critical request without accounting for network characteristics.","solution":"Choose appropriate consistency boundaries and minimize unnecessary cross-region synchronous operations.","alternative":"Use regional ownership with asynchronous replication for workloads that tolerate bounded lag.","tradeoff":"Synchronous cross-region coordination can strengthen consistency but increases latency and partition sensitivity.","constraint":"The acceptable bound depends on workload and geographic deployment.","edge_case":"Temporary inter-region packet loss can create replication backlog.","security_implication":"Replication traffic requires authenticated and encrypted transport.","performance_implication":"Network round trips contribute directly to tail latency for synchronous operations.","testing_guidance":"Inject inter-region latency, jitter, and packet loss.","verification_guidance":"Verify measured or inferred latency assumptions against the stated architecture contract.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Multi-region replication","Network latency"],"prerequisites":["Replication","Consistency"],"related_records":["D17-B51-R02","D17-B56-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Multi_Region_Distributed_Systems_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B56-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Multi-Region Systems","concept":"Active-passive multi-region architecture","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is active-passive multi-region architecture?","answer":"In an active-passive architecture, one region normally serves production traffic while another region maintains recovery capacity or replicated state for failover.","explanation":"The passive region reduces normal cross-region serving complexity but requires tested failover and recovery procedures.","principle":"Reducing concurrent write ownership can simplify consistency while retaining geographic recovery capability.","rule":"Define how passive state is synchronized and how traffic authority transfers during failover.","correct_example":"A secondary region receives replicated state and remains outside normal production traffic until a controlled failover.","invalid_example":"Assuming a passive region is recoverable merely because data replication exists.","problem":"The recovery region cannot serve production correctly when needed.","failure_mode":"Untested regional failover","cause":"Recovery path was never exercised or maintained.","solution":"Regularly test failover, data freshness, capacity, configuration, and dependency readiness.","alternative":"Use active-active where continuous multi-region serving is required and justified.","tradeoff":"Active-passive can simplify normal operation but may provide slower recovery or lower normal utilization.","constraint":"The passive region must maintain sufficient recovery capacity.","edge_case":"A regional outage may coincide with stale replication or control-plane failure.","security_implication":"Passive regions require equivalent protection for replicated sensitive data.","performance_implication":"Normal operation may use fewer regions but failover introduces a capacity transition.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled regional failover and restoration exercises.","verification_guidance":"Verify the documented recovery point and recovery time objectives using evidence from tests.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Disaster recovery","Replication"],"prerequisites":["Regional failure model","Recovery objectives"],"related_records":["D17-B56-R01","D17-B56-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B56-R01"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Multi_Region_Disaster_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B57-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Geo-routing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is geo-routing in a distributed system?","answer":"Geo-routing directs clients or requests toward service locations based on geographic, latency, availability, policy, or other routing criteria.","explanation":"Routing policy determines which region handles a request and therefore affects latency, availability, data locality, and compliance.","principle":"Traffic routing and data placement must be considered together.","rule":"Do not route requests to a region that cannot legally or semantically serve the requested data.","correct_example":"A routing layer directs a user to an eligible healthy region while respecting data residency constraints.","invalid_example":"Routing solely by geographic proximity when the required data is unavailable or unauthorized in that region.","problem":"The nearest region cannot correctly serve the request.","failure_mode":"Invalid regional routing","cause":"Routing decisions ignore data and policy constraints.","solution":"Combine health, latency, data locality, authorization, and policy requirements.","alternative":"Use fixed regional affinity when dynamic geo-routing is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Dynamic routing improves flexibility but adds policy and control-plane complexity.","constraint":"Routing state can become stale during regional failures.","edge_case":"A region may appear healthy to the routing system while a subset of its dependencies is unavailable.","security_implication":"Routing must preserve tenant, identity, data residency, and authorization boundaries.","performance_implication":"Latency-aware routing can reduce network distance but may increase load imbalance.","testing_guidance":"Fail individual regions and verify routing decisions under stale health information.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every routing decision satisfies both availability and data-policy constraints.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Geo-distribution","Service discovery"],"prerequisites":["Regional health","Routing policy"],"related_records":["D17-B41-R01","D17-B56-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Geo_Distributed_Systems_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B57-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Regional affinity","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can regional affinity reduce distributed-system complexity?","answer":"Regional affinity keeps a workload associated with a preferred region, reducing cross-region coordination and improving locality at the cost of potentially concentrating traffic or reducing failover flexibility.","explanation":"Affinity establishes a predictable relationship between workload and serving region.","principle":"Stable locality can reduce coordination and improve cache and data locality.","rule":"Define when affinity is overridden by regional failure or policy constraints.","correct_example":"A tenant normally uses one region while failover permits another eligible region when the primary region becomes unavailable.","invalid_example":"Pinning a tenant permanently to one region without a tested failure path.","problem":"Regional failure leaves workload without an operational serving location.","failure_mode":"Affinity-induced outage","cause":"No failover or reassignment policy.","solution":"Define controlled failover and reassignment semantics.","alternative":"Use active-active routing when continuous multi-region serving is required.","tradeoff":"Affinity simplifies normal operation but can concentrate risk.","constraint":"Data movement during reassignment may be expensive or restricted.","edge_case":"A region can partially fail while remaining reachable.","security_implication":"Reassignment must preserve tenant authorization and data residency rules.","performance_implication":"Affinity can improve cache and data locality.","testing_guidance":"Fail the preferred region and measure reassignment time and data availability.","verification_guidance":"Verify that affinity never overrides required availability or policy constraints.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Geo-routing","Regional ownership"],"prerequisites":["Multi-region deployment"],"related_records":["D17-B57-R01","D17-B56-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Geo_Distributed_Systems_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B57-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Regional data residency","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why must data residency constraints be integrated into distributed-system architecture?","answer":"Data residency requirements can restrict where particular data may be stored, replicated, processed, or accessed, which directly constrains replication and routing choices.","explanation":"Geographic distribution is not solely a performance decision when legal, contractual, or organizational policies restrict data movement.","principle":"Data location can be part of the correctness and compliance model.","rule":"Classify data-location requirements before selecting replication and failover topology.","correct_example":"A routing and replication system prevents restricted tenant data from being copied to unauthorized regions.","invalid_example":"Replicating all data globally and attempting to enforce residency only at the user interface.","problem":"Data crosses a prohibited geographic boundary.","failure_mode":"Residency violation","cause":"Storage and replication topology ignored policy constraints.","solution":"Encode residency constraints into placement, replication, routing, and recovery mechanisms.","alternative":"Use regional data domains with controlled cross-domain metadata.","tradeoff":"Strict locality can reduce replication flexibility and recovery options.","constraint":"Residency requirements may differ by tenant, data class, or jurisdiction.","edge_case":"Backups, logs, metrics, and replicas may unintentionally contain the same restricted data.","security_implication":"Data-location controls are part of the security and compliance boundary.","performance_implication":"Regional restrictions can require additional routing or local processing.","testing_guidance":"Trace sensitive data through storage, replication, logging, backup, and recovery paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify actual data placement rather than relying only on configuration intent.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Multi-region storage","Data governance"],"prerequisites":["Data classification","Replication topology"],"related_records":["D17-B56-R01","D17-B57-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Data_Governance_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B58-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Quorum-based availability during replica failure","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"How does replica failure affect quorum-based storage availability?","answer":"A quorum-based system can continue operations while enough replicas remain available to satisfy the configured quorum, but operations may become unavailable once insufficient replicas can participate.","explanation":"The precise availability boundary depends on replication factor, quorum requirements, failure independence, and protocol semantics.","principle":"Replication improves failure tolerance only within the protocol's quorum and failure assumptions.","rule":"Calculate and test the supported failure threshold rather than assuming replication implies indefinite availability.","correct_example":"A system documents how many replica failures can occur before reads or writes become unavailable.","invalid_example":"Claiming that three replicas guarantee availability after any two failures regardless of quorum configuration.","problem":"Failure tolerance is overstated.","failure_mode":"Quorum availability mismatch","cause":"Replication factor is confused with operational quorum requirements.","solution":"Document and test the actual quorum availability conditions.","alternative":"Use asynchronous fallback or degraded read paths when their consistency semantics are acceptable.","tradeoff":"Higher quorum requirements can strengthen consistency but reduce availability during failures.","constraint":"Replica failures may be correlated rather than independent.","edge_case":"A rack, zone, or region failure can remove multiple replicas simultaneously.","security_implication":"Degraded modes must not bypass integrity or authorization requirements.","performance_implication":"Higher quorum requirements can increase latency and reduce successful throughput under partial failure.","testing_guidance":"Fail replicas individually and by failure domain.","verification_guidance":"Verify availability boundaries against the actual deployment topology.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Quorum replication","Failure domains"],"prerequisites":["Replication factor","Quorum semantics"],"related_records":["D17-B21-R03","D17-B52-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Quorum_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B58-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Failure-domain-aware replica placement","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why should replicas be distributed across independent failure domains?","answer":"Placing replicas across independent failure domains reduces the probability that one infrastructure failure removes multiple copies simultaneously.","explanation":"Three replicas on three machines in one failure domain may provide less resilience than replicas distributed across independent domains.","principle":"Replica diversity should correspond to the actual correlated failure model.","rule":"Place replicas across failure domains that are meaningfully independent for the expected threat model.","correct_example":"Replicas are distributed across separate failure domains so a single domain failure does not remove the required quorum.","invalid_example":"Counting three replicas as independent when all share one power, network, or availability domain.","problem":"Nominal replication does not provide expected failure tolerance.","failure_mode":"Correlated replica loss","cause":"Replica placement ignores common failure domains.","solution":"Model and enforce failure-domain-aware placement.","alternative":"Use erasure coding or additional replicas when appropriate.","tradeoff":"Cross-domain placement can increase network and operational cost.","constraint":"True independence is difficult to guarantee and must be based on infrastructure knowledge.","edge_case":"A larger infrastructure failure can span multiple nominal domains.","security_implication":"Placement policies may also affect data-residency and tenant-isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Cross-domain writes can increase latency and bandwidth use.","testing_guidance":"Simulate complete failure of each defined domain.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the required number of replicas remains available after each supported domain failure.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Replication","Failure domains"],"prerequisites":["Quorum systems","Infrastructure topology"],"related_records":["D17-B58-R01","D17-B56-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Architecture_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B58-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Erasure coding versus replication","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the architectural difference between replication and erasure coding for distributed storage?","answer":"Replication stores multiple complete copies, while erasure coding stores encoded fragments that collectively permit reconstruction according to the coding scheme.","explanation":"Erasure coding can reduce storage overhead for durable data but usually introduces more complex reconstruction and update behavior.","principle":"Durability mechanisms trade storage overhead against computation, network traffic, and recovery complexity.","rule":"Select the mechanism according to access pattern, failure tolerance, recovery workload, and latency requirements.","correct_example":"Cold large objects use erasure coding when reconstruction latency and operational complexity are acceptable.","invalid_example":"Replacing low-latency replicated storage with erasure coding without evaluating reconstruction and small-write costs.","problem":"The durability mechanism does not match workload characteristics.","failure_mode":"Recovery-cost mismatch","cause":"Storage efficiency was optimized without modeling access and repair behavior.","solution":"Evaluate normal reads, writes, failure recovery, and repair traffic together.","alternative":"Use replication for latency-sensitive hot data and erasure coding for suitable durable objects.","tradeoff":"Erasure coding can reduce storage overhead but may increase computation and reconstruction complexity.","constraint":"The coding scheme defines how many fragments can be lost while retaining recoverability.","edge_case":"Multiple failures during reconstruction can reduce recovery margin.","security_implication":"Encoded fragments still require equivalent confidentiality and integrity protections.","performance_implication":"Reconstruction can consume CPU, disk, and network resources.","testing_guidance":"Measure normal and degraded reads plus simultaneous fragment failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify recoverability against the documented failure threshold.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Distributed storage","Fault tolerance"],"prerequisites":["Replication","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B58-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B59-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Read quorum versus repair quorum","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why should read participation and repair participation be treated as separate concepts?","answer":"A read quorum determines which replicas participate in satisfying a read operation, while repair participation determines which replicas are updated or reconciled after divergence is detected.","explanation":"The sets of replicas needed for observation and repair can have different correctness and operational requirements.","principle":"Observation of state and modification of replica state are separate distributed operations.","rule":"Define read and repair authority independently.","correct_example":"A read detects divergence from a sufficient replica set and a controlled repair mechanism updates the affected replicas.","invalid_example":"Allowing every reader to overwrite any replica based solely on its observed value.","problem":"Read operations accidentally become uncontrolled write authority.","failure_mode":"Unsafe repair authority","cause":"Observation and mutation semantics are conflated.","solution":"Separate detection, conflict resolution, authorization, repair, and verification.","alternative":"Perform all repair through a dedicated background reconciliation subsystem.","tradeoff":"Dedicated repair improves control but introduces additional operational machinery.","constraint":"Repair must preserve concurrent updates.","edge_case":"Replica membership can change between read and repair.","security_implication":"Repair privileges should be more restricted than ordinary read privileges.","performance_implication":"Separating repair can reduce read-path overhead but adds background work.","testing_guidance":"Change replica membership and state between detection and repair.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only authorized repair operations can modify divergent replicas.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Replication repair","Quorum reads"],"prerequisites":["Authorization","Conflict resolution"],"related_records":["D17-B52-R02","D17-B54-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Repair_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B59-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Replica bootstrap","knowledge_type":"PROCEDURE","question":"What is replica bootstrap?","answer":"Replica bootstrap is the process of initializing a new or previously empty replica with the state required to participate correctly in the distributed storage system.","explanation":"Bootstrap may involve copying a snapshot, replaying a log, transferring partitions, or combining several recovery mechanisms.","principle":"A new replica must become correct before it is treated as an authoritative participant.","rule":"Define bootstrap state, transfer consistency, catch-up, validation, and activation criteria.","correct_example":"A new replica receives a consistent base snapshot, catches up from subsequent changes, validates its state, and is then admitted for normal traffic.","invalid_example":"Adding an empty replica to the active read quorum before it contains valid state.","problem":"A newly added replica returns incomplete or incorrect data.","failure_mode":"Premature replica activation","cause":"Bootstrap completion is not part of membership state.","solution":"Separate bootstrap, catch-up, validation, and active membership states.","alternative":"Use a prebuilt snapshot when available to reduce bootstrap time.","tradeoff":"Longer bootstrap delays full redundancy but prevents serving incomplete state.","constraint":"Bootstrap must coexist safely with concurrent writes.","edge_case":"The source state can change continuously during bootstrap.","security_implication":"Bootstrap transfers may contain the entire dataset and require strong access controls and encryption.","performance_implication":"Bootstrap can generate substantial network and disk load.","testing_guidance":"Add replicas under sustained writes and verify catch-up correctness.","verification_guidance":"Verify that activation occurs only after the replica satisfies the system's membership criteria.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"dependencies":["Replication","Snapshots"],"prerequisites":["Membership","Recovery"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R01","D17-B14-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B59-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Storage","concept":"Replica removal safety","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why can removing a replica be unsafe if membership changes are not coordinated?","answer":"Removing a replica can reduce the available quorum, durability margin, or redundancy below the system's required threshold, especially when another failure occurs during the transition.","explanation":"Membership changes alter the set over which replication and quorum assumptions are defined.","principle":"Replica membership is part of the distributed protocol state.","rule":"Validate quorum, durability, and failure-domain constraints before completing replica removal.","correct_example":"A replica is removed only after the remaining replicas satisfy the required replication and quorum conditions.","invalid_example":"Deleting a replica immediately because its storage is no longer needed without checking active membership state.","problem":"The system temporarily loses required redundancy.","failure_mode":"Unsafe membership reduction","cause":"Physical removal is performed without protocol-aware membership transition.","solution":"Use controlled membership transitions and verify resulting redundancy.","alternative":"Replace the replica first, then remove the old member.","tradeoff":"Controlled transitions consume additional capacity during replacement.","constraint":"Concurrent failures during removal must be considered.","edge_case":"Another replica can fail while the replacement is still catching up.","security_implication":"Removed replicas may retain sensitive data and require secure decommissioning.","performance_implication":"Replacement and re-replication can increase network and storage load.","testing_guidance":"Remove replicas while injecting another failure during reconfiguration.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the supported quorum and durability guarantees remain true throughout the transition.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Membership","Replication"],"prerequisites":["Quorum systems","Failure domains"],"related_records":["D17-B24-R03","D17-B59-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Storage_Replication_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B60-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Recovery","concept":"Recovery point objective versus recovery time objective","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What is the difference between recovery point objective and recovery time objective in distributed systems?","answer":"Recovery point objective describes the amount of data loss or state rollback that is acceptable after a failure, while recovery time objective describes the target time for restoring service or functionality.","explanation":"The two objectives constrain different dimensions of disaster recovery and must be supported by different mechanisms.","principle":"Recovery correctness requires both a defined acceptable state-loss boundary and an acceptable restoration-time boundary.","rule":"Do not claim recovery compliance without evidence for both the state-loss and time-to-recovery dimensions.","correct_example":"A system specifies the maximum acceptable replicated-state lag and the maximum target restoration duration, then tests both.","invalid_example":"Reporting a short restoration time while ignoring that the recovered state can be substantially stale.","problem":"Recovery performance is evaluated on only one dimension.","failure_mode":"Incomplete recovery objective","cause":"RPO and RTO are conflated or one is omitted.","solution":"Define and test them independently.","alternative":"Use workload-specific recovery objectives when different data classes have different requirements.","tradeoff":"Tighter objectives generally require more replication, standby capacity, automation, or operational investment.","constraint":"Objectives are requirements, not evidence that they have been achieved.","edge_case":"A system may recover quickly but with unacceptable state loss.","security_implication":"Recovery must preserve security configuration, identity, authorization, and audit integrity.","performance_implication":"Tighter objectives can require additional storage, replication, and standby resources.","testing_guidance":"Perform controlled recovery tests and measure both recovered-state freshness and restoration time.","verification_guidance":"Verify objectives using measured recovery evidence rather than architecture claims alone.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Disaster recovery","Replication"],"prerequisites":["Recovery planning","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B56-R03","D17-B55-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Disaster_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B60-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Recovery","concept":"Recovery testing","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"Why must distributed recovery procedures be tested rather than only documented?","answer":"Recovery procedures can fail because of stale configuration, missing dependencies, incorrect assumptions, insufficient capacity, or untested state transitions, so documentation alone does not establish operational correctness.","explanation":"A recovery design is a claim about behavior under failure and therefore requires evidence under representative conditions.","principle":"Recovery capability must be demonstrated under controlled failure conditions.","rule":"Exercise recovery paths regularly and record actual results, limitations, and deviations from objectives.","correct_example":"A controlled regional failure test measures restoration time, recovered state, dependency readiness, and operator actions.","invalid_example":"Declaring disaster recovery verified because a runbook exists.","problem":"The documented recovery path differs from real system behavior.","failure_mode":"Untested recovery failure","cause":"Recovery was treated as documentation rather than an executable capability.","solution":"Perform repeatable recovery exercises and regression-test the recovery process.","alternative":"Use automated recovery validation where safe and feasible.","tradeoff":"Recovery testing consumes resources and can introduce controlled risk, but untested recovery carries unknown risk.","constraint":"Tests must not compromise production data or violate safety boundaries.","edge_case":"Recovery may fail only under simultaneous dependency failures.","security_implication":"Recovery environments and procedures must preserve access controls and secrets management.","performance_implication":"Recovery tests can expose capacity bottlenecks invisible during normal operation.","testing_guidance":"Test node, zone, region, storage, identity, network, and dependency failures according to the supported failure model.","verification_guidance":"Record evidence of actual recovery state, duration, data loss, and limitations.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Recovery","Fault injection"],"prerequisites":["RPO","RTO","Failure models"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R03","D17-B60-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Disaster_Recovery_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B60-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Recovery","concept":"Recovery evidence versus recovery claim","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What distinguishes a recovery claim from verified recovery capability?","answer":"A recovery claim describes intended behavior, while verified recovery capability requires evidence from appropriate tests, observations, or formal analysis demonstrating that the system satisfies the defined recovery property within known limitations.","explanation":"Architecture descriptions and runbooks can establish intended design but cannot by themselves prove operational behavior.","principle":"Recovery verification requires evidence tied directly to the recovery requirement.","rule":"Label recovery capabilities according to the strength and scope of available evidence.","correct_example":"A recovery objective is marked tested after a controlled exercise records the measured result and limitations.","invalid_example":"Marking a recovery objective verified because the failover script completed without observing recovered application correctness.","problem":"Successful automation execution is mistaken for successful recovery.","failure_mode":"False recovery verification","cause":"Verification checks only procedure execution rather than system outcome.","solution":"Verify end-state correctness, data integrity, service availability, and objective compliance.","alternative":"Combine automated checks with formal recovery invariants and targeted manual validation.","tradeoff":"Outcome verification requires more effort than checking procedure completion.","constraint":"Evidence must match the scope and failure conditions of the claim.","edge_case":"A recovery test can pass under one failure combination while failing under another.","security_implication":"Recovery verification must include security and authorization invariants.","performance_implication":"Comprehensive recovery validation increases test duration and resource use.","testing_guidance":"Separate procedural success, service restoration, data correctness, and objective satisfaction into distinct checks.","verification_guidance":"Maintain explicit evidence linking each recovery claim to the test or analysis that supports it.","language":null,"framework":null,"version":null,"version_range":null,"dependencies":["Recovery testing","Verification"],"prerequisites":["RPO","RTO","Verification model"],"related_records":["D17-B60-R01","D17-B60-R02","D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":["Distributed_Recovery_Verification_Literature"]},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B61-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache-aside pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the cache-aside pattern in a distributed system?","answer":"The cache-aside pattern keeps the cache outside the primary data store workflow: the application reads from the cache first, loads the source of truth on a miss, and then populates the cache.","explanation":"The application explicitly manages cache reads and population. This separates cache lifecycle from the authoritative data store but requires deliberate handling of stale entries, concurrent misses, and invalidation.","principle":"The cache is an optimization and the authoritative store remains the source of truth.","rule":"A cache-aside implementation must define miss behavior, expiration or invalidation, failure handling, and consistency expectations.","correct_example":"On a cache miss, read the current value from the authoritative store, return it, and populate the cache with an appropriate expiration policy.","invalid_example":"Treating the cache as the only durable copy while the authoritative store is unavailable or inconsistent.","problem":"Cache entries can become stale or many clients can simultaneously miss the same key.","failure_mode":"A cache miss causes excessive load on the authoritative store.","cause":"Concurrent misses, expired entries, or broad invalidation can concentrate requests on the backing system.","solution":"Use bounded expiration, targeted invalidation, request coalescing, or other stampede-control mechanisms appropriate to the workload.","alternative":"Write-through or write-behind caching can move cache population responsibility into a cache layer.","tradeoff":"Cache-aside provides application control but increases application complexity and consistency responsibility.","constraint":"The authoritative store must remain available for cache misses unless another source of truth is explicitly defined.","edge_case":"The backing store changes between the cache read and cache population.","security_implication":"Cached data must enforce the same authorization boundaries as the source data; sensitive entries must not leak across tenants or identities.","performance_implication":"Cache hits can reduce backing-store latency and load, while poorly controlled misses can amplify load.","testing_guidance":"Test hits, misses, stale values, concurrent misses, backing-store failures, expiration, and invalidation behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that cache failure does not silently convert an authoritative-store failure into incorrect data.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed caching","Authoritative data stores"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B51-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B61-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Caching_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B61-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache invalidation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why is cache invalidation a distributed-systems problem?","answer":"Cache invalidation is a distributed-systems problem because multiple cache replicas and the authoritative store can observe updates at different times, creating stale-read windows.","explanation":"Invalidation messages can be delayed, lost, reordered, duplicated, or processed after a newer update. Correctness therefore depends on the consistency requirement and invalidation mechanism.","principle":"An invalidation signal is not automatically equivalent to proof that every cache replica has removed stale state.","rule":"The design must specify how stale entries are detected, invalidated, expired, or versioned.","correct_example":"Associate cached values with versions and reject an older cached value when a newer version is known.","invalid_example":"Assuming that sending one invalidation message guarantees instantaneous global cache consistency.","problem":"Different cache nodes can retain different versions of the same logical object.","failure_mode":"Clients observe stale data after an update.","cause":"Delayed, lost, reordered, or incomplete invalidation propagation.","solution":"Use versioning, bounded TTLs, reliable invalidation mechanisms, read-through validation, or a consistency protocol matching the required guarantee.","alternative":"Avoid caching data whose freshness requirements cannot tolerate the chosen stale-read window.","tradeoff":"Stronger cache consistency usually increases coordination, latency, or infrastructure complexity.","constraint":"The achievable consistency guarantee depends on the cache topology and communication assumptions.","edge_case":"A stale invalidation arrives after a newer value has already been cached.","security_implication":"Invalidation failures must not expose revoked or tenant-restricted data beyond the permitted consistency window.","performance_implication":"Aggressive invalidation can reduce hit rate and increase backing-store traffic.","testing_guidance":"Test delayed, duplicated, reordered, and missing invalidations together with concurrent updates.","verification_guidance":"Measure or otherwise establish the actual stale-data window instead of assuming invalidation is instantaneous.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed caching","Cache-aside pattern"],"related_records":["D17-B61-R01","D17-B53-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B61-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Caching_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B61-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache stampede prevention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a cache stampede and how can it be controlled?","answer":"A cache stampede occurs when many requests simultaneously miss or invalidate the same cache entry and overload the backing system. Controls include request coalescing, jittered expiration, stale-while-revalidate behavior, and bounded refresh concurrency.","explanation":"A cache can transform synchronized expiration into a sudden burst against the authoritative service. Stampede control reduces synchronization and limits concurrent regeneration work.","principle":"Cache expiration should not create an uncontrolled synchronized workload against the source of truth.","rule":"The system should bound concurrent regeneration for hot keys and define behavior when regeneration is slow or fails.","correct_example":"Allow one worker to refresh a hot key while other requests use an acceptable stale value or wait within a bounded limit.","invalid_example":"Allowing every request that encounters an expired hot key to independently query the backing store.","problem":"Synchronized cache expiry creates a sudden request spike.","failure_mode":"Backing-store overload during cache regeneration.","cause":"Identical expiration times and unbounded concurrent misses.","solution":"Use randomized TTL jitter, request coalescing, bounded refresh concurrency, or controlled stale serving.","alternative":"Precompute or proactively refresh particularly hot entries.","tradeoff":"Serving stale data improves availability and load control but weakens freshness.","constraint":"Stale serving must comply with application consistency and security requirements.","edge_case":"The refresh operation fails while the old cached value remains available.","security_implication":"Stale-serving mechanisms must not extend access to data after authorization or confidentiality requirements make the old value invalid.","performance_implication":"Stampede protection can significantly reduce peak backing-store load.","testing_guidance":"Generate synchronized expiration and concurrent requests for the same key and observe backing-store concurrency.","verification_guidance":"Verify that regeneration concurrency remains bounded during cache expiry storms.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed caching","Cache invalidation"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R02","D17-B39-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B61-R01","D17-B61-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Caching_Performance_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B62-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Write-through caching","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is write-through caching?","answer":"Write-through caching updates the cache and authoritative storage as part of the write path so that a successful cache write corresponds to a completed backing-store write according to the defined protocol.","explanation":"The cache layer participates directly in writes rather than relying on later application-managed population.","principle":"A cache write should not be treated as durable unless the configured durability boundary has been reached.","rule":"The system must explicitly define what constitutes a successful write and how partial failures are reconciled.","correct_example":"A write operation updates the backing store and cache according to a defined atomicity or ordering protocol.","invalid_example":"Reporting success after updating only the cache when the cache is not the durable source of truth.","problem":"Cache and backing store can diverge after partial failure.","failure_mode":"Clients observe cached data that was never durably committed.","cause":"Ambiguous write ordering or failure handling.","solution":"Define the write ordering, failure semantics, retry behavior, and reconciliation process.","alternative":"Cache-aside with explicit invalidation or write-through at a dedicated cache layer.","tradeoff":"Write-through can simplify read freshness but can add write latency and coordination complexity.","constraint":"The exact consistency guarantee depends on the cache and storage implementation.","edge_case":"The backing store succeeds but cache update fails.","security_implication":"Authorization and tenant boundaries must apply consistently to both cache and storage writes.","performance_implication":"Writes may become slower because cache and storage operations participate in the write path.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures before and after each write stage and verify resulting state.","verification_guidance":"Verify the documented success boundary against observed durable state.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed caching","Durable storage"],"related_records":["D17-B61-R01","D17-B55-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B62-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Caching_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B62-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Write-behind caching","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is write-behind caching and what risk does it introduce?","answer":"Write-behind caching acknowledges a write at the cache layer and propagates it to the backing store asynchronously. This can reduce write latency but introduces a window in which cached state is newer than durable storage.","explanation":"The design separates client acknowledgment from durable persistence. Correctness depends on the durability, ordering, retry, and recovery guarantees of the asynchronous propagation mechanism.","principle":"Acknowledging a write before durable persistence changes the durability contract.","rule":"A write-behind system must define buffering, ordering, retry, loss prevention, and recovery semantics.","correct_example":"Queue cache mutations durably before acknowledging them when the system's contract requires preservation across process failure.","invalid_example":"Acknowledging write-behind data from volatile memory while claiming immediate durable persistence.","problem":"Pending writes can be lost or reordered during failures.","failure_mode":"The cache contains data that is missing or inconsistent in the backing store.","cause":"Non-durable buffers, propagation failures, or incorrect ordering.","solution":"Use durable queues or logs, idempotent persistence operations, ordering controls, and recovery procedures.","alternative":"Write-through caching when stronger write durability semantics are required.","tradeoff":"Lower write latency can be obtained at the cost of increased durability and recovery complexity.","constraint":"The acceptable loss window must be explicitly defined.","edge_case":"The cache node fails after acknowledgment but before persistence.","security_implication":"Sensitive writes must not be acknowledged under weaker durability than the security or compliance requirement permits.","performance_implication":"Asynchronous persistence can reduce foreground latency and smooth backend load.","testing_guidance":"Kill cache nodes at different points in the write pipeline and verify recovery behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify acknowledged writes against durable storage after induced failures.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed caching","Asynchronous messaging"],"related_records":["D17-B37-R02","D17-B60-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B62-R01"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Caching_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B62-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache consistency boundary","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"How should a distributed system define a cache consistency boundary?","answer":"A cache consistency boundary specifies which operations may observe stale data, the maximum acceptable staleness if bounded, and which operations require confirmation from a stronger source.","explanation":"Caching is not a single consistency model. Different data and operations can have different freshness requirements.","principle":"Consistency requirements should be attached to business invariants and operations rather than to the cache as an undifferentiated component.","rule":"Document the permitted stale-read behavior for each consistency-sensitive data class.","correct_example":"Allow approximate analytics data to be cached with bounded staleness while requiring authoritative reads for authorization decisions.","invalid_example":"Using the same cache policy for authorization state and non-critical display data without considering their different correctness requirements.","problem":"A generic cache policy cannot satisfy every consistency requirement.","failure_mode":"A stale cache value violates a business invariant.","cause":"Missing consistency classification.","solution":"Classify data by freshness and correctness requirements and select cache behavior accordingly.","alternative":"Avoid caching highly consistency-sensitive state.","tradeoff":"Stronger consistency increases coordination and can reduce cache effectiveness.","constraint":"The consistency boundary must account for failures and delayed propagation.","edge_case":"A normally non-critical field becomes security-sensitive due to a state transition.","security_implication":"Security authorization, revocation, and policy state may require stronger consistency than ordinary application data.","performance_implication":"Selective strong reads preserve performance by avoiding globally strong cache consistency.","testing_guidance":"Test operations across the declared consistency boundary under delayed invalidation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each operation's observed behavior matches its documented consistency requirement.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Consistency models","Distributed caching"],"related_records":["D17-B28-R01","D17-B29-R03","D17-B61-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B28-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Consistency_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B63-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Service registry","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a service registry in a distributed system?","answer":"A service registry maintains information used to locate service instances, such as logical service identity, endpoint information, and potentially health or metadata state.","explanation":"Service discovery separates logical service naming from the current set of reachable instances.","principle":"Service identity and instance location are separate concerns in dynamically changing systems.","rule":"Registry data must have explicit lifecycle and freshness semantics.","correct_example":"Clients resolve a logical service name to currently eligible service instances through a discovery mechanism.","invalid_example":"Treating an indefinitely old registry entry as proof that an instance is still available.","problem":"Service instances can disappear while registry information remains temporarily present.","failure_mode":"Clients repeatedly route traffic to unavailable instances.","cause":"Stale registration or insufficient health integration.","solution":"Use registration expiry, health-aware eligibility, leases, or active health checking as appropriate.","alternative":"Use platform-native service discovery or DNS-based mechanisms where their semantics satisfy the system requirements.","tradeoff":"More active registry coordination can improve freshness but increases control-plane traffic and complexity.","constraint":"Discovery information is itself distributed state and can become stale.","edge_case":"A network partition separates an instance from the registry while clients can still reach the instance.","security_implication":"Discovery responses must not expose unauthorized service endpoints or metadata.","performance_implication":"Discovery caching reduces control-plane load but increases staleness.","testing_guidance":"Test registration expiry, instance failure, network partitions, concurrent registration changes, and stale responses.","verification_guidance":"Verify that discovery freshness and availability behavior match the documented contract.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Naming","Distributed membership"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R01","D17-B42-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Service_Discovery_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B63-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Client-side versus server-side discovery","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How do client-side and server-side service discovery differ?","answer":"In client-side discovery, the client resolves service instances and selects a destination. In server-side discovery, the client sends a request to an intermediary that performs discovery and routing.","explanation":"The two approaches place discovery and selection responsibility in different components.","principle":"Discovery responsibility can be centralized in infrastructure or distributed into clients.","rule":"The selected model should account for language diversity, routing policy, failure handling, and operational control.","correct_example":"A polyglot organization may centralize discovery in a platform proxy so clients do not each implement registry protocols.","invalid_example":"Assuming that either approach automatically eliminates stale discovery information or routing failures.","problem":"Discovery logic may become duplicated or centralized into a critical infrastructure dependency.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent routing behavior across services.","cause":"Different clients implementing different discovery and health policies.","solution":"Standardize discovery semantics and health eligibility regardless of where selection occurs.","alternative":"Hybrid discovery with platform-level resolution and client-side load balancing.","tradeoff":"Client-side discovery provides control but increases client complexity; server-side discovery centralizes policy but adds an infrastructure hop or dependency.","constraint":"Both models still depend on correct and sufficiently fresh service-instance information.","edge_case":"The discovery component itself becomes partially unavailable.","security_implication":"The discovery layer must authenticate clients and restrict endpoint information according to trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Client-side selection can avoid an extra proxy hop while server-side discovery can centralize connection management.","testing_guidance":"Test stale endpoints, discovery failure, uneven instance health, and routing changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing behavior under registry and intermediary failures.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Service registry"],"related_records":["D17-B63-R01","D17-B57-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B63-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Service_Discovery_Architecture_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B63-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Stale discovery registration","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What happens when service discovery contains stale registrations?","answer":"Stale registrations can cause requests to be routed to instances that no longer exist, are unhealthy, or should no longer receive traffic.","explanation":"Discovery state has its own lifecycle and can lag behind actual service membership.","principle":"Reachability cannot be inferred solely from historical registration state.","rule":"Registrations must expire, be actively removed, or be filtered by current health and eligibility information.","correct_example":"A service instance loses eligibility after its lease expires or health status becomes invalid.","invalid_example":"Keeping every historical service endpoint permanently eligible for routing.","problem":"Clients continue selecting obsolete instances.","failure_mode":"Connection failures, increased latency, or requests reaching unintended instances.","cause":"Missing expiration, failed deregistration, or delayed control-plane propagation.","solution":"Use leases, TTLs, health checks, and safe stale-entry handling.","alternative":"Platform-managed discovery with integrated endpoint lifecycle.","tradeoff":"Short expiration improves freshness but increases control-plane traffic and sensitivity to transient failures.","constraint":"Expiration policy must tolerate temporary control-plane or network disruptions without causing unnecessary mass removal.","edge_case":"A healthy instance temporarily cannot communicate with the registry.","security_implication":"Stale registrations can expose traffic to endpoints that are no longer authorized to receive it.","performance_implication":"Repeated selection of dead instances increases connection failures and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Partition registry communication and observe endpoint expiration and routing behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale endpoints become ineligible within the documented bound.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Service registry","Health checking"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R01","D17-B63-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B63-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Service_Discovery_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B64-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Hash ring","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a consistent-hashing ring?","answer":"A consistent-hashing ring maps both keys and nodes into a common hash space so that ownership changes can be localized when nodes join or leave.","explanation":"Unlike simple modulo hashing, consistent hashing is designed to reduce the amount of key remapping caused by membership changes.","principle":"Membership changes should minimize unnecessary movement of unrelated keys.","rule":"The ownership algorithm must define hash-space ordering, node placement, and behavior during membership changes.","correct_example":"Map each node and key into a common circular hash space and assign a key according to the defined successor or ownership rule.","invalid_example":"Claiming that consistent hashing guarantees perfectly equal load without considering node placement or workload distribution.","problem":"Node changes can cause ownership movement and uneven load.","failure_mode":"A small number of nodes receive disproportionately large key ranges.","cause":"Poor hash distribution or insufficient virtual nodes.","solution":"Use an appropriate placement strategy and monitor key and request distribution.","alternative":"Rendezvous hashing or other deterministic assignment schemes.","tradeoff":"The ring can reduce movement but may require more metadata and careful balancing.","constraint":"The distribution depends on the hash function and node-placement strategy.","edge_case":"A node failure removes a large ownership interval.","security_implication":"Hash placement must not permit untrusted input to deliberately create pathological concentration when adversarial workloads are possible.","performance_implication":"Reduced key movement can lower rebalancing cost.","testing_guidance":"Simulate node additions and removals and measure key movement and load distribution.","verification_guidance":"Verify ownership mapping deterministically across independent clients.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Hashing","Distributed partitioning"],"related_records":["D17-B44-R01","D17-B45-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Consistent_Hashing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B64-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Virtual nodes","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why are virtual nodes used with consistent hashing?","answer":"Virtual nodes assign multiple positions in the hash space to each physical node, which can improve load distribution and make ownership movement more granular.","explanation":"A single position per physical node can create large ownership ranges. Multiple positions provide more opportunities for statistical balancing.","principle":"Finer-grained ownership can reduce imbalance caused by discrete node placement.","rule":"Virtual-node count should be selected and validated according to node count, workload distribution, and operational overhead.","correct_example":"Assign multiple deterministic ring positions to each physical node and measure ownership skew before production use.","invalid_example":"Assuming that adding virtual nodes automatically solves every hotspot caused by skewed key popularity.","problem":"Uniform key ownership does not guarantee uniform request load.","failure_mode":"A node remains overloaded despite balanced key-space ownership.","cause":"Hot keys or non-uniform request frequency.","solution":"Combine ownership balancing with hotspot detection, replication, request routing, or workload-specific mitigation.","alternative":"Rendezvous hashing or explicit partition assignment.","tradeoff":"More virtual nodes can improve balance but increase ring metadata and lookup or rebalancing overhead.","constraint":"Virtual nodes do not change the popularity distribution of keys.","edge_case":"One extremely hot key dominates traffic regardless of ownership distribution.","security_implication":"Adversarial key selection should be considered where clients can influence keys and workload concentration matters.","performance_implication":"Better balancing can reduce hotspots, while excessive virtual-node counts increase metadata costs.","testing_guidance":"Compare ownership and request distributions under several virtual-node counts and skewed workloads.","verification_guidance":"Verify deterministic node-to-key mapping and quantify distribution skew.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Hash ring"],"related_records":["D17-B64-R01","D17-B44-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B64-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Consistent_Hashing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B64-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Consistent Hashing","concept":"Key movement during membership change","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How does consistent hashing limit key movement when nodes change?","answer":"With an appropriate consistent-hashing scheme, a node addition or removal primarily changes ownership for keys associated with the affected hash-space intervals rather than remapping all keys.","explanation":"The exact amount of movement depends on the assignment scheme, replica policy, and membership transition.","principle":"Membership changes should have localized data-movement effects when the partitioning scheme is designed for that property.","rule":"Rebalancing procedures must account for both logical ownership changes and physical data transfer.","correct_example":"After adding a node, transfer only the partitions whose ownership changes according to the new ring.","invalid_example":"Assuming that changing the ring automatically moves the required data without a separate migration mechanism.","problem":"Logical ownership and physical data placement can temporarily diverge.","failure_mode":"Requests reach an owner before the required data has been safely transferred.","cause":"Incomplete rebalancing coordination.","solution":"Use explicit ownership states, migration tracking, and safe read/write handling during transfer.","alternative":"Centralized partition assignment with explicit shard migration.","tradeoff":"Localized movement reduces rebalancing volume but requires more sophisticated transition management.","constraint":"The hash algorithm alone does not guarantee safe physical migration.","edge_case":"A node fails while receiving a partition during rebalancing.","security_implication":"Data movement during rebalancing must preserve encryption, authorization, and tenant isolation.","performance_implication":"Localized movement can reduce network and storage load during scaling events.","testing_guidance":"Inject node failures during partition movement and verify no acknowledged data is lost or incorrectly routed.","verification_guidance":"Compare expected ownership transitions with actual data placement and routing.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Hash ring","Virtual nodes","Data migration"],"related_records":["D17-B45-R02","D17-B55-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B64-R01","D17-B64-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Consistent_Hashing_Rebalancing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B65-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Distributed lease","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a distributed lease?","answer":"A distributed lease grants temporary ownership or authority to an actor for a bounded period, subject to a renewal or expiration mechanism.","explanation":"Leases can reduce the need for permanent ownership and allow systems to recover authority after failures or inactivity.","principle":"A lease is time-bounded authority, not permanent proof of ownership.","rule":"Lease-based correctness must account for expiration, renewal failure, clock assumptions, and stale holders.","correct_example":"A worker receives a lease for a task and must renew it before expiration to retain ownership.","invalid_example":"Assuming that a lease holder can act indefinitely after losing connectivity to the coordination service.","problem":"A lease holder can become stale while another actor acquires the same logical lease.","failure_mode":"Concurrent actors both perform operations believed to require exclusive ownership.","cause":"Expiration, delayed messages, or incorrect time assumptions.","solution":"Use fencing tokens or another mechanism that makes stale authority ineffective at the protected resource.","alternative":"Consensus-backed ownership or explicit lock protocols.","tradeoff":"Leases simplify failure recovery but require careful treatment of time and stale actors.","constraint":"A lease's correctness depends on the protocol's timing and fencing assumptions.","edge_case":"A client pauses or becomes partitioned after obtaining a lease and resumes later.","security_implication":"Lease authority must not bypass authorization after expiration.","performance_implication":"Leases can reduce coordination traffic compared with continuously held locks.","testing_guidance":"Test pause, partition, delayed renewal, expiration, and concurrent reacquisition.","verification_guidance":"Verify that expired holders cannot perform operations requiring current authority.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed coordination","Fencing"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03","D17-B35-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B65-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Lease_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B65-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Lease renewal failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why is lease renewal failure dangerous?","answer":"Lease renewal failure can cause a previously authorized holder to continue acting after its lease has expired while another actor has acquired the same logical authority.","explanation":"A local process may not immediately know that its remote lease has expired. Network partitions, pauses, overload, and delayed responses can create stale actors.","principle":"Local belief about lease ownership is not sufficient proof of current distributed authority.","rule":"Critical operations must prevent expired lease holders from causing conflicting effects.","correct_example":"Use a fencing token issued with each lease generation and require the protected resource to reject older generations.","invalid_example":"Allowing a process to continue writes merely because its local lease timer has not yet expired.","problem":"Local clocks and remote lease state can diverge.","failure_mode":"Two actors concurrently execute operations under apparently exclusive authority.","cause":"Partition, process pause, delayed renewal response, or clock-related assumptions.","solution":"Use fencing, generation numbers, or a resource-side authority check.","alternative":"Use a coordination protocol with explicit ownership epochs.","tradeoff":"Fencing adds protocol and resource support but greatly reduces stale-holder risk.","constraint":"The protected resource must enforce the fencing mechanism for it to provide protection.","edge_case":"A stale client resumes after several newer lease generations have completed.","security_implication":"Expired authority must not remain usable for privileged operations.","performance_implication":"Resource-side fencing checks add small coordination or metadata overhead.","testing_guidance":"Pause a lease holder beyond expiration and attempt protected operations after another holder acquires authority.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale generations are rejected by the protected resource.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed lease","Fencing tokens"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03","D17-B65-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B65-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Lease_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B65-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Coordination","concept":"Lock ownership versus lease ownership","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"How does lease-based ownership differ from an indefinite distributed lock?","answer":"A lease grants time-bounded ownership that expires unless renewed, while an indefinite lock normally remains held until explicitly released or until a failure-recovery mechanism determines that it can be reclaimed.","explanation":"Leases make expiration part of the ownership model and can provide bounded recovery from abandoned ownership.","principle":"The ownership lifetime is part of the correctness contract.","rule":"Choose a lease when bounded ownership duration is useful and define what happens after expiration.","correct_example":"Use a lease for temporary worker ownership where failed workers must eventually stop blocking progress.","invalid_example":"Using a lease while treating expiration as impossible or irrelevant to correctness.","problem":"A design may require either guaranteed exclusion until release or bounded automatic recovery.","failure_mode":"The chosen ownership mechanism does not match the required failure semantics.","cause":"Confusing mutual exclusion with bounded authority.","solution":"Specify ownership duration, recovery behavior, fencing, and release semantics before implementation.","alternative":"Consensus-backed lock with explicit recovery semantics.","tradeoff":"Leases provide automatic expiry but introduce timing and renewal concerns.","constraint":"Neither mechanism alone guarantees correctness without a well-defined protected-resource protocol.","edge_case":"The owner is alive but temporarily unable to renew.","security_implication":"Expired authorization must not remain effective merely because the holder process is still running.","performance_implication":"Lease renewal introduces periodic coordination traffic.","testing_guidance":"Compare failure, recovery, and contention behavior under both ownership models.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the mechanism's actual failure semantics match the system invariant.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed locks","Distributed leases"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R03","D17-B34-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B65-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Coordination_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B66-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lock acquisition safety","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What must a distributed lock guarantee to provide mutual exclusion?","answer":"A distributed lock must provide a defined ownership protocol that prevents conflicting owners from simultaneously being accepted by the protected resource under the stated failure and timing model.","explanation":"A lock API returning success is insufficient if stale holders can still modify the protected resource.","principle":"Mutual exclusion must be enforced at the effect boundary, not merely at the lock-service response boundary.","rule":"Critical lock designs should specify owner identity, acquisition generation, release behavior, failure recovery, and stale-owner prevention.","correct_example":"Associate lock acquisition with a fencing generation that the protected resource validates on each critical operation.","invalid_example":"Using a local boolean flag or an unverified remote lock response as the sole distributed exclusion mechanism.","problem":"Lock-service state and protected-resource state can diverge.","failure_mode":"Multiple clients perform conflicting critical operations.","cause":"Network partition, stale ownership, lock-service failure, or missing fencing.","solution":"Use a coordination protocol with explicit ownership generations and resource-side validation where stale actions are dangerous.","alternative":"Single-writer architecture or partition ownership enforced by a consensus-backed state machine.","tradeoff":"Stronger lock safety requires coordination and resource integration.","constraint":"The exact guarantee depends on the failure model and protocol assumptions.","edge_case":"The lock holder becomes partitioned but continues operating locally.","security_implication":"Privileged operations protected by distributed locks must reject stale authority.","performance_implication":"Lock acquisition can become a latency and availability bottleneck under contention.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent acquisition, holder failure, network partition, delayed release, and stale-holder operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify mutual exclusion at the protected side effect, not only through lock-service logs.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed coordination","Fencing"],"related_records":["D17-B65-R02","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B65-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Lock_Verification_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B66-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lock release after failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What should happen when a distributed lock holder fails?","answer":"The system should have a defined recovery mechanism that eventually makes ownership reclaimable without allowing an obsolete holder to continue causing conflicting effects.","explanation":"Automatic recovery can use lease expiration, session loss, epochs, or other coordination mechanisms. Recovery must not sacrifice safety for availability.","principle":"Reclaiming ownership and preventing stale-holder effects are separate requirements.","rule":"A lock-recovery mechanism must define both when ownership becomes reclaimable and how old owners are prevented from acting.","correct_example":"Expire a failed owner's lease and assign a newer fencing generation to the replacement owner.","invalid_example":"Immediately assigning ownership to another client without preventing the old client from continuing writes.","problem":"Failure detection is imperfect and ownership may be uncertain during recovery.","failure_mode":"Two clients believe they own the same resource.","cause":"Premature reclamation or missing stale-owner protection.","solution":"Combine failure detection with epochs, fencing, or a resource-side generation check.","alternative":"Require explicit release when availability requirements permit it.","tradeoff":"Faster recovery can increase split-ownership risk unless stale actions are fenced.","constraint":"Failure detectors may be inaccurate or delayed.","edge_case":"The failed process recovers after ownership has been reassigned.","security_implication":"Recovered stale processes must not retain privileged authority automatically.","performance_implication":"Long recovery intervals reduce availability; short intervals increase false-reclamation risk.","testing_guidance":"Recover old holders after simulated failure and verify that stale operations are rejected.","verification_guidance":"Verify both reclamation progress and stale-holder exclusion.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed locks","Failure detection","Fencing"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R03","D17-B65-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B66-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Lock_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B66-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lock contention","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"How does distributed lock contention affect a system?","answer":"High distributed lock contention can serialize otherwise independent work, increase queueing and tail latency, amplify coordination traffic, and create a throughput bottleneck.","explanation":"A distributed lock introduces a shared coordination point. Contention can therefore limit scalability even when individual operations are fast.","principle":"Coordination cost grows with the amount of shared ownership and contention.","rule":"Locks should protect the smallest correctness-critical scope practical and should not be used where partitioned or lock-free designs satisfy the invariant.","correct_example":"Partition work so independent keys can be processed concurrently rather than serializing all operations behind one global lock.","invalid_example":"Using a single distributed lock for every request to a high-throughput service.","problem":"Shared lock ownership becomes a bottleneck.","failure_mode":"Throughput collapses and tail latency rises as concurrency increases.","cause":"Large critical sections or excessive lock scope.","solution":"Reduce lock scope, partition ownership, batch work, or redesign around idempotent/commutative operations where possible.","alternative":"Sharded locks, optimistic concurrency, or CRDT-style conflict handling depending on the invariant.","tradeoff":"More concurrency can increase complexity and conflict management.","constraint":"Reducing lock scope must not violate the protected invariant.","edge_case":"One hot key receives a disproportionate amount of traffic.","security_implication":"Lock contention can become an availability attack surface if untrusted clients can force exclusive work.","performance_implication":"Contention directly affects throughput and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Measure throughput and latency as concurrent contenders increase.","verification_guidance":"Verify that lock scope corresponds to the actual invariant rather than incidental implementation structure.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed locks","Concurrency"],"related_records":["D17-B32-R01","D17-B39-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B66-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Concurrency_Performance_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B67-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Membership","concept":"Gossip dissemination","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is gossip-based dissemination in a distributed system?","answer":"Gossip dissemination spreads information by having nodes periodically exchange state with selected peers, allowing information to propagate through the system without requiring every node to contact a central authority.","explanation":"Repeated peer-to-peer exchanges can provide scalable dissemination and resilience to individual node failures.","principle":"Local peer exchanges can produce system-wide eventual dissemination.","rule":"The protocol must define state versioning, peer selection, exchange frequency, duplicate handling, and convergence expectations.","correct_example":"Nodes periodically exchange membership versions and merge newer information according to a deterministic rule.","invalid_example":"Assuming that one gossip exchange guarantees immediate global agreement.","problem":"Gossip is generally asynchronous and can temporarily produce inconsistent views.","failure_mode":"Nodes retain stale membership or metadata information.","cause":"Propagation delay, failed peers, message loss, or insufficient dissemination frequency.","solution":"Use versioned state, repeated exchanges, anti-entropy, and measurable convergence criteria.","alternative":"Centralized coordination or hierarchical dissemination.","tradeoff":"Gossip scales well and avoids a single dissemination bottleneck but provides eventual rather than instantaneous propagation.","constraint":"Convergence depends on network connectivity and protocol parameters.","edge_case":"A subset of nodes becomes isolated for an extended period.","security_implication":"Unauthenticated gossip can allow malicious state injection or poisoning.","performance_implication":"More frequent or broader gossip improves propagation speed but increases network traffic.","testing_guidance":"Measure propagation under packet loss, node churn, partitions, and asymmetric connectivity.","verification_guidance":"Verify eventual convergence under the declared fault assumptions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed membership","Eventual convergence"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R01","D17-B42-R03","D17-B48-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B67-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Gossip_Protocol_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B67-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Membership","concept":"Gossip convergence","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should gossip convergence be evaluated?","answer":"Gossip convergence should be evaluated using an explicit state definition, propagation metric, fault model, and convergence target rather than by assuming that repeated communication is sufficient.","explanation":"Convergence can mean different things, such as all reachable nodes eventually learning a state update or reaching an equivalent membership view.","principle":"A distributed convergence claim requires measurable semantics.","rule":"Define the state, acceptable divergence, convergence deadline if bounded, and fault assumptions.","correct_example":"Measure the time until all non-partitioned nodes observe a particular membership version under specified message-loss conditions.","invalid_example":"Declaring convergence because most nodes received an update once.","problem":"Informal convergence claims are difficult to validate.","failure_mode":"Operational systems underestimate stale-state duration.","cause":"No explicit convergence metric or fault assumptions.","solution":"Define and measure convergence properties under representative failure conditions.","alternative":"Use stronger coordination when bounded global agreement is required.","tradeoff":"Measurable eventual convergence can scale better than strong coordination but permits temporary divergence.","constraint":"Some partitions or permanent failures may prevent global convergence until connectivity is restored.","edge_case":"A node rejoins after missing many state versions.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive membership changes may require stronger guarantees than ordinary gossip convergence.","performance_implication":"Convergence targets influence gossip frequency and bandwidth usage.","testing_guidance":"Measure convergence after controlled node failures, packet loss, and partitions.","verification_guidance":"Compare observed convergence against the explicitly declared bound and fault model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Gossip dissemination","Failure model"],"related_records":["D17-B17-R03","D17-B67-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B67-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Gossip_Convergence_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B67-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Membership","concept":"Gossip state merge","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must gossip state have a deterministic merge rule?","answer":"A deterministic merge rule allows independently received state updates to produce compatible results despite duplication, reordering, and different arrival sequences.","explanation":"Distributed dissemination does not guarantee a common message order. Merge semantics therefore determine whether replicas can converge.","principle":"State convergence requires compatible merge semantics under arbitrary permitted delivery orders.","rule":"The merge operation must explicitly define how concurrent and conflicting updates are resolved.","correct_example":"Merge membership observations using versioned state and a deterministic conflict-resolution rule.","invalid_example":"Letting each node apply conflicting membership updates according to local arrival order with no convergence rule.","problem":"Different delivery orders can produce different final states.","failure_mode":"Nodes permanently disagree despite receiving the same logical updates.","cause":"Non-deterministic or order-sensitive merge logic.","solution":"Define deterministic, version-aware, or mathematically convergent merge semantics.","alternative":"Use ordered consensus when the application requires a single total order.","tradeoff":"Mergeable eventual-consistency protocols can avoid global ordering but may require richer metadata.","constraint":"A merge rule must preserve the application's safety invariants.","edge_case":"Two concurrent updates independently modify the same field.","security_implication":"Conflict resolution must not allow a lower-trust update to overwrite a higher-trust state merely because it arrived later.","performance_implication":"Rich version metadata increases message and storage overhead.","testing_guidance":"Deliver identical updates in many permutations and compare final states.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence across all relevant message-order permutations for the defined state model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Gossip dissemination","Version metadata"],"related_records":["D17-B53-R01","D17-B17-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B67-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B22-R01"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_State_Merge_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B68-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Hashing","concept":"Rendezvous hashing","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is rendezvous hashing?","answer":"Rendezvous hashing, also called highest-random-weight hashing, selects an owner by computing a deterministic score for each candidate node and choosing the highest-ranked eligible node.","explanation":"The method provides deterministic assignment while allowing membership changes with relatively localized ownership changes.","principle":"Ownership can be derived independently by every participant from the same key and membership set.","rule":"All participants must use compatible scoring and membership information to obtain the same owner.","correct_example":"Compute a deterministic score for a key-node pair and choose the highest score among eligible nodes.","invalid_example":"Using different node membership views while assuming all clients will route identically.","problem":"Different membership snapshots can produce different owners.","failure_mode":"Requests for the same key are routed to different nodes.","cause":"Inconsistent membership state or incompatible hashing implementation.","solution":"Synchronize or version membership information and define behavior during transitions.","alternative":"Consistent-hashing ring with virtual nodes.","tradeoff":"Rendezvous hashing can simplify node assignment but may require evaluating multiple candidates unless optimized.","constraint":"Correct routing depends on consistent membership and scoring semantics.","edge_case":"A node is added or removed while requests are in flight.","security_implication":"Untrusted actors must not be able to manipulate membership or node eligibility to redirect sensitive traffic.","performance_implication":"Ownership lookup cost depends on the number of candidate nodes and implementation strategy.","testing_guidance":"Compare deterministic assignments across clients and membership transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify identical key and membership inputs produce identical ownership decisions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Hashing","Distributed membership"],"related_records":["D17-B64-R01","D17-B44-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B68-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Rendezvous_Hashing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B68-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Hashing","concept":"Hashing-based replica selection","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"How can hashing be used to select multiple replicas?","answer":"A deterministic hashing scheme can rank candidate nodes for a key and select a configured number of distinct eligible nodes as replicas.","explanation":"Replica selection can be derived from key and membership state without requiring a central assignment table, provided all participants share compatible membership information.","principle":"Replica placement should be deterministic and failure-domain aware when availability requires independent failure domains.","rule":"Replica selection must exclude duplicate nodes and should account for placement constraints such as zones or regions where required.","correct_example":"Select the top eligible nodes for a key while rejecting placements that violate configured failure-domain diversity.","invalid_example":"Selecting the first N nodes without checking whether they all reside in the same failure domain.","problem":"Purely deterministic ranking may produce correlated failure exposure.","failure_mode":"Multiple replicas become unavailable during one infrastructure failure.","cause":"Replica placement ignores failure domains.","solution":"Apply placement constraints after ranking or incorporate failure-domain awareness into the placement algorithm.","alternative":"Explicit partition maps managed by a coordination service.","tradeoff":"Failure-domain awareness improves resilience but makes placement logic more complex.","constraint":"Replica diversity depends on accurate node metadata.","edge_case":"The available nodes cannot satisfy the requested diversity constraints.","security_implication":"Replica placement must respect tenant and data-residency restrictions.","performance_implication":"More replicas increase read availability but consume storage and network bandwidth.","testing_guidance":"Simulate zone and region failures and verify replica distribution.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each key's replica set satisfies configured placement constraints.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed hashing","Replication","Failure domains"],"related_records":["D17-B58-R02","D17-B51-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B68-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Replica_Placement_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B68-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Hashing","concept":"Membership snapshot consistency for routing","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can inconsistent membership snapshots break distributed routing?","answer":"If different clients use different membership snapshots, they can independently choose different owners or replica sets for the same key, causing inconsistent routing and potentially unnecessary retries or conflicting ownership transitions.","explanation":"Deterministic hashing only produces deterministic results when its inputs are equivalent.","principle":"Deterministic algorithms do not eliminate inconsistency when distributed inputs differ.","rule":"Membership versions or epochs should be associated with routing decisions when transitions require coordination.","correct_example":"Route using a known membership generation and handle generation changes explicitly.","invalid_example":"Assuming deterministic hashing guarantees identical routing while each client has an unrelated node list.","problem":"Clients disagree about the active membership set.","failure_mode":"Traffic is split across old and new owners during reconfiguration.","cause":"Asynchronous membership propagation.","solution":"Version membership, define transition states, and coordinate migration or forwarding behavior.","alternative":"Centralized routing service with a strongly managed membership view.","tradeoff":"Stronger membership synchronization improves routing consistency but adds coordination overhead.","constraint":"Membership convergence can be delayed during partitions.","edge_case":"A client reconnects after missing several membership generations.","security_implication":"Routing state must not be manipulated to redirect traffic across unauthorized boundaries.","performance_implication":"Membership synchronization adds control-plane overhead but can reduce misrouted traffic.","testing_guidance":"Run clients with intentionally different membership generations and observe routing behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify generation handling during node addition, removal, and failure.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed membership","Hash-based routing"],"related_records":["D17-B14-R02","D17-B68-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B68-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Routing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B69-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Rate Limiting","concept":"Token bucket in distributed systems","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a token-bucket rate limiter be distributed?","answer":"A distributed token bucket coordinates token state across multiple request-processing nodes so that the aggregate request allowance follows a defined rate and burst policy.","explanation":"The central challenge is shared rate state. Local independent buckets do not automatically enforce a global limit.","principle":"A global rate limit requires coordinated state or a deliberately partitioned quota model.","rule":"Define whether the limit is global, per-node, per-tenant, per-identity, or another scope before selecting the coordination mechanism.","correct_example":"Maintain shared or partitioned quota state so all request handlers enforce the same documented limit semantics.","invalid_example":"Running identical local token buckets on every node while claiming they enforce one global quota.","problem":"Independent nodes can collectively exceed the intended aggregate rate.","failure_mode":"Global traffic exceeds the configured limit.","cause":"Uncoordinated local counters or token state.","solution":"Use shared quota state, deterministic partitioning, centralized rate limiting, or a distributed quota algorithm.","alternative":"Per-node limits with a calculated aggregate budget.","tradeoff":"Global precision requires coordination; local limits improve availability and latency but provide weaker aggregate control.","constraint":"Network partitions can prevent exact global enforcement without sacrificing availability.","edge_case":"A node continues accepting traffic while disconnected from the shared quota service.","security_implication":"Rate-limit bypass can become an abuse or denial-of-service vector.","performance_implication":"Centralized quota state can become a hot coordination path.","testing_guidance":"Generate traffic concurrently across many nodes and compare aggregate behavior with the configured policy.","verification_guidance":"Verify whether the observed limit is global or only local and document the actual guarantee.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Rate limiting","Distributed state"],"related_records":["D17-B49-R01","D17-B21-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Rate_Limiting_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B69-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Rate Limiting","concept":"Distributed quota partitioning","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What is quota partitioning in a distributed rate limiter?","answer":"Quota partitioning assigns portions of a total allowance to independent nodes or partitions, allowing local enforcement without coordinating every request globally.","explanation":"Partitioning can reduce coordination but may cause unused capacity on one node while another node exhausts its allocation.","principle":"Removing per-request coordination generally requires accepting less precise global utilization or adding rebalancing mechanisms.","rule":"Quota allocation must account for traffic distribution and define behavior when one partition exhausts its share.","correct_example":"Assign tenant quotas across nodes with controlled borrowing or redistribution when permitted.","invalid_example":"Assuming static equal quotas remain optimal when traffic is highly skewed.","problem":"Static partitions can become imbalanced.","failure_mode":"One node rejects requests while unused quota exists elsewhere.","cause":"Uneven traffic or static quota allocation.","solution":"Use adaptive allocation, centralized refill, controlled borrowing, or a higher-level quota service.","alternative":"Fully centralized global quota accounting.","tradeoff":"Partitioning improves scalability and availability but may reduce utilization or precision.","constraint":"Borrowing across partitions reintroduces coordination.","edge_case":"A partition becomes isolated while holding unused quota.","security_implication":"Quota borrowing must preserve tenant isolation and prevent one identity from consuming another identity's allowance.","performance_implication":"Local enforcement reduces coordination latency and load.","testing_guidance":"Test skewed traffic, node failures, partitions, and quota redistribution.","verification_guidance":"Verify aggregate usage against the documented quota semantics under normal and failure conditions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed rate limiting","Quota management"],"related_records":["D17-B69-R01","D17-B21-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B69-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Quota_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B69-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Rate Limiting","concept":"Rate-limit consistency under partition","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"What happens to a distributed rate limit during a network partition?","answer":"During a network partition, exact global rate enforcement may conflict with availability because isolated nodes cannot reliably coordinate shared quota state.","explanation":"A design must choose or explicitly document whether isolated nodes reject traffic, use local fallback budgets, or temporarily permit bounded excess.","principle":"Global coordination and continued independent availability can be incompatible during communication failure.","rule":"Partition behavior must be explicit rather than left to accidental timeout and retry behavior.","correct_example":"Use a preallocated emergency budget per node and document the maximum temporary aggregate excess possible during partition.","invalid_example":"Claiming an exact global rate limit while nodes continue accepting requests without communication.","problem":"Nodes cannot observe the complete global consumption state.","failure_mode":"Aggregate traffic exceeds the intended global quota.","cause":"Unavailable coordination state.","solution":"Choose fail-closed, bounded fallback quotas, or another explicitly defined partition policy.","alternative":"Centralized enforcement that becomes unavailable when the coordinator is unreachable.","tradeoff":"Fail-closed protects the quota but reduces availability; fallback quotas preserve availability with bounded enforcement drift.","constraint":"The maximum divergence should be quantifiable where security or abuse controls depend on the limit.","edge_case":"The partition lasts long enough to exhaust all locally allocated emergency capacity.","security_implication":"Partition fallback must not create an unbounded bypass for abuse-prevention controls.","performance_implication":"Fail-closed behavior can cause abrupt traffic rejection during coordinator outages.","testing_guidance":"Partition the quota coordinator and measure accepted traffic against the documented bound.","verification_guidance":"Verify the maximum possible enforcement deviation under the declared partition model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed rate limiting","Network partitions"],"related_records":["D17-B30-R02","D17-B69-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B69-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Rate_Limiting_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B70-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Health-aware load balancing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is health-aware load balancing in a distributed system?","answer":"Health-aware load balancing routes traffic using both service availability information and a distribution policy so that unhealthy or ineligible instances receive reduced or no traffic.","explanation":"A load balancer that considers only static membership can continue routing to failed or degraded instances.","principle":"Routing eligibility should reflect current service health and operational state.","rule":"Health signals must have explicit freshness, failure, and eligibility semantics.","correct_example":"Remove an instance from the eligible routing set after a defined health failure threshold while preserving controlled recovery behavior.","invalid_example":"Sending equal traffic to all registered instances regardless of current health state.","problem":"Health state can become stale or noisy.","failure_mode":"Traffic is routed to failed instances or healthy instances are removed unnecessarily.","cause":"Inaccurate health signals, slow propagation, or unsuitable thresholds.","solution":"Define liveness/readiness semantics, health-check intervals, thresholds, and recovery behavior.","alternative":"Client-side adaptive load balancing based on observed request outcomes.","tradeoff":"Aggressive health removal improves failure isolation but can reduce capacity during transient conditions.","constraint":"Health checks must themselves tolerate temporary network or dependency failures.","edge_case":"An instance is healthy for one endpoint but degraded for another.","security_implication":"Health endpoints must not disclose sensitive internal information or permit unauthorized control.","performance_implication":"Health-aware routing can reduce failed requests and tail latency but adds health-check traffic.","testing_guidance":"Inject dependency failures, slow responses, and transient health changes.","verification_guidance":"Verify routing decisions against current health state and documented thresholds.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Service discovery","Health checking","Load balancing"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R01","D17-B63-R01","D17-B39-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B63-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Load_Balancing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B70-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Load balancing feedback loop","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can load balancing create a feedback loop during overload?","answer":"A load balancer can amplify overload when unhealthy or slow instances remain eligible, causing retries and additional traffic that further increases their latency and failure rate.","explanation":"Routing, queueing, retries, and health signals interact dynamically. A local overload decision can therefore become a system-wide feedback loop.","principle":"Traffic distribution must account for downstream capacity and failure amplification.","rule":"Load balancing should coordinate with timeout, retry, backpressure, and overload-protection policies.","correct_example":"Reduce traffic to an overloaded instance and prevent failed requests from generating unbounded retries.","invalid_example":"Retrying every failed request immediately against the same overloaded pool.","problem":"Overload creates additional requests faster than capacity recovers.","failure_mode":"Cascading latency and failure across services.","cause":"Retries, synchronized traffic, insufficient load shedding, or stale health state.","solution":"Combine health-aware routing with bounded retries, backpressure, load shedding, and circuit breaking where appropriate.","alternative":"Admission control or centralized workload scheduling.","tradeoff":"Aggressive load shedding protects system stability but rejects more work.","constraint":"Protection thresholds must reflect actual service capacity and dependency behavior.","edge_case":"All instances become simultaneously degraded.","security_implication":"An attacker may exploit retry or routing amplification to increase resource consumption.","performance_implication":"Feedback loops can dramatically increase tail latency and reduce effective throughput.","testing_guidance":"Inject controlled overload and measure traffic, retries, latency, and rejection across dependent services.","verification_guidance":"Verify that overload protection reduces rather than amplifies downstream work.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Load balancing","Retries","Backpressure"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01","D17-B36-R02","D17-B50-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B70-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Overload_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B70-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Adaptive load balancing","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"What is adaptive load balancing?","answer":"Adaptive load balancing changes routing decisions using observed service conditions such as latency, errors, queue depth, or capacity rather than relying only on static weights.","explanation":"The routing policy becomes a feedback controller over observed system state. Poorly designed feedback can oscillate or amplify instability.","principle":"Routing decisions should use reliable signals and account for feedback delay.","rule":"Adaptive routing must define measurement windows, signal quality, adjustment limits, and stabilization behavior.","correct_example":"Gradually reduce traffic to instances showing sustained latency degradation while preventing rapid oscillation between targets.","invalid_example":"Immediately sending all traffic away from an instance after one transient slow request.","problem":"Noisy measurements can cause unstable routing decisions.","failure_mode":"Traffic oscillates between instances or overloads previously healthy instances.","cause":"Overreactive thresholds, stale measurements, or excessive control gain.","solution":"Use smoothing, bounded adjustments, hysteresis, and controlled evaluation windows.","alternative":"Static weighted routing combined with explicit health checks.","tradeoff":"Adaptive routing can respond to real-time conditions but adds control complexity and observability requirements.","constraint":"The quality of routing depends on the quality and freshness of telemetry.","edge_case":"A shared downstream dependency makes every instance appear unhealthy simultaneously.","security_implication":"Routing telemetry must not be manipulated by untrusted clients to redirect traffic.","performance_implication":"Adaptive routing can improve tail latency when signals are accurate but can add measurement and control overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject latency, error, and capacity changes and observe routing stability over time.","verification_guidance":"Verify that routing changes follow declared thresholds and remain stable under noisy signals.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Load balancing","Observability","Feedback control"],"related_records":["D17-B70-R01","D17-B70-R02","D17-B21-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B70-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Adaptive_Load_Balancing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B71-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"End-to-end backpressure","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What is end-to-end backpressure in a distributed system?","answer":"End-to-end backpressure is a mechanism by which downstream capacity limits propagate upstream so producers reduce or defer work instead of continuously increasing queued work.","explanation":"Without backpressure, queues can grow faster than consumers can drain them, causing memory pressure, latency growth, and cascading failures.","principle":"Work admission should be bounded by sustainable downstream processing capacity.","rule":"A distributed pipeline should define how producers react when downstream capacity is exhausted.","correct_example":"A consumer signals bounded capacity and producers slow submission or temporarily reject additional work.","invalid_example":"Allowing producers to enqueue unlimited work whenever consumers become slower.","problem":"Producer throughput exceeds sustainable consumer throughput.","failure_mode":"Unbounded queue growth and resource exhaustion.","cause":"Missing or ineffective flow-control feedback.","solution":"Apply bounded queues, credits, rate control, admission control, or explicit rejection.","alternative":"Buffer work in durable storage when delayed processing is acceptable.","tradeoff":"Backpressure protects stability but can increase producer latency or cause rejection.","constraint":"The system must define acceptable queueing and rejection behavior.","edge_case":"The downstream service becomes unavailable for an extended period.","security_implication":"Backpressure prevents resource exhaustion that could otherwise be triggered intentionally through workload amplification.","performance_implication":"Bounded flow control limits memory growth and stabilizes latency under overload.","testing_guidance":"Slow consumers while keeping producers active and measure queue depth, latency, memory, and rejection.","verification_guidance":"Verify that producer work does not grow without bound when downstream capacity is constrained.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Backpressure","Distributed messaging"],"related_records":["D17-B36-R02","D17-B70-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B36-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Backpressure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B71-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Credit-based flow control","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does credit-based flow control provide backpressure?","answer":"Credit-based flow control allows a receiver to grant a producer a bounded amount of work or data that may be sent before additional credit is required.","explanation":"Credits represent available receiving capacity and prevent senders from exceeding the receiver's declared bound.","principle":"Transmission authority can be coupled directly to receiver capacity.","rule":"Credits must have explicit ownership, accounting, replenishment, and failure semantics.","correct_example":"A consumer grants a producer credits for a bounded number of messages and replenishes them as capacity becomes available.","invalid_example":"Granting unlimited credits regardless of consumer capacity.","problem":"Credit state can become inconsistent after retries or connection failures.","failure_mode":"The producer either overwhelms the consumer or stops unnecessarily.","cause":"Incorrect credit accounting or lost state transitions.","solution":"Use idempotent credit updates, bounded credit state, and explicit connection recovery semantics.","alternative":"Token-based rate control or pull-based consumption.","tradeoff":"Credit flow control provides precise bounds but increases protocol state and coordination.","constraint":"Both sides must agree on credit accounting semantics.","edge_case":"A producer reconnects after the consumer has already changed its credit state.","security_implication":"Credit grants must not be forgeable by unauthorized participants.","performance_implication":"Credits can provide efficient flow control without per-message global coordination.","testing_guidance":"Inject message loss, duplicate credit updates, disconnects, and reconnects.","verification_guidance":"Verify that sent work never exceeds the receiver's valid credit under the protocol model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Backpressure","Flow control"],"related_records":["D17-B71-R01","D17-B33-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B71-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B71-R03"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Flow_Control_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B71-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Backpressure","concept":"Pull-based consumption","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How does pull-based consumption provide flow control?","answer":"Pull-based consumption lets consumers request or fetch work when they have capacity instead of allowing producers to push an unbounded amount of work.","explanation":"The consumer controls admission to its own processing pipeline, naturally coupling demand to available capacity.","principle":"Consumers can regulate workload by controlling how much work they accept.","rule":"The system must still define fairness, batching, polling behavior, and behavior when work is unavailable.","correct_example":"A consumer requests the next bounded batch only after completing or safely buffering the current batch.","invalid_example":"A nominal pull protocol that immediately prefetches unlimited work into local memory.","problem":"Prefetch can undermine the intended flow-control bound.","failure_mode":"Consumer-side buffers grow despite pull-based semantics.","cause":"Unbounded or excessive prefetch.","solution":"Bound prefetch according to processing and memory capacity.","alternative":"Push-based credit flow control.","tradeoff":"Pull simplifies consumer-driven control but may add polling or scheduling overhead.","constraint":"The pull bound must reflect actual downstream capacity.","edge_case":"Processing time varies dramatically between individual messages.","security_implication":"Pull limits can reduce exposure to unbounded workload injection.","performance_implication":"Batch pulling can improve throughput while maintaining bounded buffering.","testing_guidance":"Vary processing time and verify that local buffers remain within configured bounds.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the number of outstanding unprocessed items is bounded by policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Backpressure","Consumer coordination"],"related_records":["D17-B35-R01","D17-B71-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B71-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B71-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Flow_Control_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B72-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Overload Control","concept":"Load shedding","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is load shedding in a distributed system?","answer":"Load shedding deliberately rejects, drops, or defers selected work when demand exceeds sustainable capacity so that the system can continue serving a controlled subset of traffic.","explanation":"Load shedding converts uncontrolled overload into explicit admission control.","principle":"Preserving system stability can require refusing work before resources are exhausted.","rule":"The system must define which work may be shed and which work has priority or must be preserved.","correct_example":"Reject low-priority requests with an explicit overload response while preserving critical control operations.","invalid_example":"Randomly dropping requests without considering priority or business invariants.","problem":"Demand temporarily exceeds capacity.","failure_mode":"Resource exhaustion causes broad service failure.","cause":"No admission control or insufficient capacity protection.","solution":"Implement priority-aware shedding, bounded queues, and overload signaling.","alternative":"Autoscaling where workload elasticity and scaling latency make it effective.","tradeoff":"Load shedding sacrifices some work to preserve overall availability and stability.","constraint":"Dropped work must be safe to retry or explicitly reported as rejected.","edge_case":"All incoming work is classified as critical.","security_implication":"Attackers should not be able to manipulate priority classification to bypass shedding controls.","performance_implication":"Shedding can stabilize latency and prevent memory exhaustion under overload.","testing_guidance":"Generate sustained overload and verify priority ordering and resource bounds.","verification_guidance":"Verify that shedding activates before critical resource exhaustion.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Overload control","Backpressure"],"related_records":["D17-B70-R02","D17-B71-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B71-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Overload_Control_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B72-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Overload Control","concept":"Priority-aware admission control","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why should load shedding use explicit priority classes?","answer":"Explicit priority classes allow a system to preserve critical operations while rejecting less important work during capacity shortages.","explanation":"Without explicit priority semantics, overload behavior can accidentally reject essential work or preserve expensive low-value work.","principle":"Admission decisions should reflect system invariants and declared service priorities.","rule":"Priority classification must be deterministic, authorized, and resistant to arbitrary client manipulation.","correct_example":"Reserve capacity for health, recovery, or control-plane operations while limiting best-effort workloads.","invalid_example":"Trusting a client-provided priority field without authorization or validation.","problem":"All traffic competes equally during overload.","failure_mode":"Critical operations are rejected along with non-critical work.","cause":"No priority-aware admission policy.","solution":"Define protected classes and enforce priority at a trusted boundary.","alternative":"Separate physical or logical capacity pools for critical workloads.","tradeoff":"Priority protection improves resilience for critical work but may increase rejection of lower-priority work.","constraint":"Priority policy must not violate fairness or contractual service requirements.","edge_case":"A low-priority workload becomes necessary for recovery.","security_implication":"Priority escalation can become an abuse path if clients can self-classify.","performance_implication":"Protected capacity can reduce effective utilization under normal load but improves overload resilience.","testing_guidance":"Exhaust capacity with mixed-priority workloads and verify deterministic admission behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that only authorized priority classifications influence admission.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Load shedding","Authorization"],"related_records":["D17-B72-R01","D17-B50-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B72-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Admission_Control_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B72-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Overload Control","concept":"Overload signal propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should overload information propagate upstream?","answer":"Upstream propagation allows producers and callers to reduce work before overloaded downstream services become saturated and fail more broadly.","explanation":"Local overload without upstream signaling can cause queues and retries to continue generating work after capacity is already exhausted.","principle":"Overload control is more effective when demand reduction occurs close to the source of excess work.","rule":"Overload signals should have defined semantics and should not automatically trigger unlimited retries.","correct_example":"A service returns an explicit overload response and callers reduce concurrency or delay retry according to policy.","invalid_example":"Treating every overload response as an immediate retry instruction.","problem":"Downstream saturation causes upstream traffic amplification.","failure_mode":"Cascading overload across service dependencies.","cause":"Missing or misinterpreted overload signals.","solution":"Propagate bounded admission signals and coordinate them with retry and backpressure policies.","alternative":"Centralized workload scheduling.","tradeoff":"Upstream control improves stability but requires callers to correctly interpret and honor signals.","constraint":"Signals may be delayed or lost during network failures.","edge_case":"Several layers simultaneously become overloaded and emit conflicting signals.","security_implication":"Overload signals must not be forgeable if they control access to protected capacity.","performance_implication":"Early demand reduction reduces queueing and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Inject downstream overload and trace how concurrency changes across upstream services.","verification_guidance":"Verify that overload causes demand reduction rather than retry amplification.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Load shedding","Backpressure","Retries"],"related_records":["D17-B19-R01","D17-B70-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B72-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Overload_Control_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B73-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Circuit Breakers","concept":"Circuit breaker state machine","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the state-machine model of a distributed circuit breaker?","answer":"A circuit breaker commonly models closed operation, an open state that rejects or bypasses calls, and a recovery-testing state that permits controlled probes before normal traffic resumes.","explanation":"The breaker turns repeated downstream failures into explicit admission control rather than allowing every caller to continue stressing the failing dependency.","principle":"Repeated failure should change request behavior instead of being treated as independent transient events forever.","rule":"State transitions must define failure thresholds, recovery probes, reset conditions, and concurrency behavior.","correct_example":"After a defined failure condition, reject new calls temporarily and allow a limited probe set to test recovery.","invalid_example":"Opening the breaker permanently after one transient failure.","problem":"A dependency can repeatedly fail while callers continue sending traffic.","failure_mode":"Cascading failures and wasted resources.","cause":"No failure-state transition or uncontrolled retries.","solution":"Use bounded failure thresholds and controlled recovery probing.","alternative":"Explicit load shedding or dependency-specific admission control.","tradeoff":"Circuit breakers protect the caller and dependency but can temporarily reject requests that might have succeeded.","constraint":"Failure thresholds must account for request volume and dependency semantics.","edge_case":"A low-volume dependency experiences one serious but isolated failure.","security_implication":"Breaker state should not be externally manipulable unless explicitly authorized.","performance_implication":"Open-state rejection avoids expensive calls to known-failing dependencies.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures and recovery transitions and verify every state transition.","verification_guidance":"Verify state transitions against the declared failure and recovery policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Circuit breakers","Failure detection"],"related_records":["D17-B50-R01","D17-B70-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Circuit_Breaker_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B73-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Circuit Breakers","concept":"Circuit breaker false opening","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How can a circuit breaker open incorrectly?","answer":"A circuit breaker can open incorrectly when transient failures, low sample volume, local network faults, or overly aggressive thresholds are interpreted as evidence that the dependency is broadly unavailable.","explanation":"Breaker decisions are local observations and may not represent the dependency's global state.","principle":"Failure signals must be interpreted within their sampling and scope context.","rule":"Thresholds should account for request volume, failure type, observation window, and whether failures originate locally or remotely.","correct_example":"Use a minimum sample requirement and distinguish dependency failures from caller-side timeouts or local resource exhaustion where possible.","invalid_example":"Opening after a single timeout regardless of traffic volume or failure origin.","problem":"The breaker confuses local or transient failures with sustained dependency failure.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary request rejection.","cause":"Poor threshold selection or ambiguous failure classification.","solution":"Classify failures and tune thresholds using representative workload data.","alternative":"Adaptive failure detection with explicit confidence thresholds.","tradeoff":"Sensitive breakers react faster but risk false positives; conservative breakers protect less aggressively.","constraint":"Thresholds must be validated against actual workload characteristics.","edge_case":"A network path fails only for one client while the dependency remains healthy for others.","security_implication":"False or malicious breaker signals can be used to deny access to a service.","performance_implication":"False opening can reduce throughput even while capacity remains available.","testing_guidance":"Inject isolated, transient, and sustained failures and measure breaker decisions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that breaker activation corresponds to the intended failure scope.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Circuit breaker state machine","Failure classification"],"related_records":["D17-B73-R01","D17-B14-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B73-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Circuit_Breaker_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B73-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Circuit Breakers","concept":"Circuit breaker recovery probing","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why should circuit-breaker recovery use limited probes?","answer":"Limited recovery probes test whether a dependency has recovered without immediately restoring the full traffic load that may have caused or prolonged the failure.","explanation":"A recovering dependency may be able to serve a small amount of traffic but fail again under normal load.","principle":"Recovery should be gradual when dependency capacity is uncertain.","rule":"The number, frequency, and success criteria of recovery probes must be bounded.","correct_example":"Allow a small controlled number of test requests and close the breaker only after the configured recovery condition succeeds.","invalid_example":"Immediately reopening all traffic after a single successful probe.","problem":"Recovery traffic can overwhelm a partially recovered dependency.","failure_mode":"Repeated breaker oscillation between open and closed states.","cause":"Uncontrolled recovery load or insufficient stabilization criteria.","solution":"Use limited probes, hysteresis, and explicit recovery thresholds.","alternative":"Gradual traffic ramp-up with adaptive load balancing.","tradeoff":"Conservative recovery improves stability but can delay restoration of full capacity.","constraint":"Probe semantics must match the dependency's operation safety.","edge_case":"Probe requests have side effects and cannot safely be duplicated.","security_implication":"Recovery probes must use authorized and safe operations.","performance_implication":"Controlled probing reduces recovery-induced latency spikes.","testing_guidance":"Simulate partial dependency recovery and verify gradual traffic restoration.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the breaker cannot restore unrestricted traffic after insufficient recovery evidence.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Circuit breaker","Health checking"],"related_records":["D17-B73-R01","D17-B70-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B73-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Circuit_Breaker_Recovery_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B74-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Bulkheads","concept":"Bulkhead isolation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is bulkhead isolation in distributed systems?","answer":"Bulkhead isolation separates resources or concurrency limits so that failure or overload in one workload does not consume capacity needed by unrelated workloads.","explanation":"The pattern limits failure propagation by preventing one dependency, tenant, or request class from exhausting all shared resources.","principle":"Shared capacity creates a failure-correlation path unless isolation boundaries are deliberately established.","rule":"Critical workloads should have explicit resource or concurrency boundaries when shared exhaustion would be unsafe.","correct_example":"Allocate separate concurrency limits for independent downstream dependencies so one slow dependency cannot consume all worker capacity.","invalid_example":"Using one unbounded worker pool for every dependency and workload.","problem":"One workload monopolizes shared resources.","failure_mode":"Unrelated requests fail because a single dependency consumes all capacity.","cause":"Missing isolation boundaries.","solution":"Use separate pools, quotas, queues, or concurrency limits.","alternative":"Process-level or service-level isolation.","tradeoff":"Isolation improves resilience but may leave reserved capacity unused when one partition is idle.","constraint":"Isolation boundaries must match actual resource contention paths.","edge_case":"A supposedly independent workload shares a hidden downstream resource.","security_implication":"Tenant and privilege boundaries may require dedicated resource pools to prevent cross-tenant resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"Bulkheads can stabilize tail latency during partial failures.","testing_guidance":"Exhaust one isolated pool and verify unrelated pools remain usable.","verification_guidance":"Verify resource consumption cannot cross the declared isolation boundary.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Resource isolation","Concurrency control"],"related_records":["D17-B50-R02","D17-B72-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Bulkhead_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B74-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Bulkheads","concept":"Concurrency isolation","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why can concurrency limits act as a distributed-system bulkhead?","answer":"Concurrency limits bound the number of simultaneously active operations and can prevent one dependency or workload from consuming all execution capacity.","explanation":"The limit becomes an explicit resource boundary. In distributed systems, the boundary should be applied at the resource actually being protected.","principle":"Concurrency is a consumable resource that should be bounded where contention occurs.","rule":"Concurrency limits must account for operation duration, dependency behavior, and queueing effects.","correct_example":"Limit concurrent calls to a slow dependency independently from calls to unrelated dependencies.","invalid_example":"Applying one global concurrency limit to all downstream operations regardless of their resource requirements.","problem":"Slow operations consume execution slots for too long.","failure_mode":"Global worker starvation.","cause":"Shared concurrency pool without dependency-specific isolation.","solution":"Partition concurrency budgets or use adaptive limits based on observed capacity.","alternative":"Separate worker pools.","tradeoff":"More granular limits improve isolation but increase configuration complexity.","constraint":"A limit that is too low can unnecessarily reduce throughput.","edge_case":"A dependency becomes faster after a configuration change but the static limit remains restrictive.","security_implication":"Per-tenant concurrency limits can prevent one tenant from exhausting shared resources.","performance_implication":"Correct limits reduce queueing and tail latency during downstream degradation.","testing_guidance":"Vary downstream latency and verify that unrelated operations retain capacity.","verification_guidance":"Verify each concurrency boundary using controlled saturation tests.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkhead isolation","Concurrency"],"related_records":["D17-B74-R01","D17-B39-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B74-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Concurrency_Isolation_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B74-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Bulkheads","concept":"Isolation boundary mismatch","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is an isolation-boundary mismatch?","answer":"An isolation-boundary mismatch occurs when a system appears to isolate workloads but they still share an underlying constrained resource that can fail or saturate across the supposed boundary.","explanation":"Separating application queues is ineffective if both queues ultimately consume the same exhausted connection pool, database, CPU, or network path.","principle":"Isolation must follow the actual resource contention path.","rule":"Trace shared dependencies before declaring two workloads independent.","correct_example":"Separate both worker concurrency and the constrained downstream connection pools when those pools are the real bottleneck.","invalid_example":"Creating two application queues while leaving both connected to the same unbounded shared database pool.","problem":"Logical isolation does not prevent physical resource contention.","failure_mode":"One workload still degrades another despite apparent bulkheads.","cause":"Hidden shared resource.","solution":"Identify and isolate the actual bottleneck or explicitly accept the shared dependency.","alternative":"Move workloads to independent services or resource pools.","tradeoff":"True isolation costs additional resources and operational complexity.","constraint":"Some infrastructure resources cannot be completely isolated.","edge_case":"A shared network or storage layer fails across all isolated workloads.","security_implication":"Hidden shared resources can create cross-tenant availability coupling.","performance_implication":"False isolation provides little protection under real saturation.","testing_guidance":"Saturate suspected shared resources rather than only logical application queues.","verification_guidance":"Trace resource utilization across isolation boundaries during failure injection.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"FAILURE_HANDLING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Bulkheads","Resource dependency analysis"],"related_records":["D17-B74-R01","D17-B74-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B74-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Resilience_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B75-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Event time versus processing time","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What is the difference between event time and processing time in distributed stream processing?","answer":"Event time represents when an event occurred according to its event metadata or source, while processing time represents when a processing system handled the event.","explanation":"Distributed networks can delay, reorder, or batch events, so processing time may differ substantially from the time represented by the event itself.","principle":"Time semantics must match the meaning of the computation.","rule":"Analytics that depend on when an event occurred should define whether event time or processing time is authoritative.","correct_example":"Compute a time-windowed metric using event timestamps while explicitly handling late arrivals.","invalid_example":"Using ingestion time as event time without considering transport delay.","problem":"Out-of-order and delayed events distort time-based computation.","failure_mode":"Incorrect window aggregates or temporal decisions.","cause":"Confusing processing time with event time.","solution":"Use explicit event-time semantics and a late-data policy.","alternative":"Processing-time windows for workloads where ingestion timing is the intended semantic.","tradeoff":"Event-time processing improves temporal correctness but requires additional state and late-event handling.","constraint":"Event timestamps must have defined source semantics and acceptable clock quality.","edge_case":"Events arrive after their logical window has already been finalized.","security_implication":"Timestamp manipulation can affect time-sensitive controls and must be considered when event time comes from untrusted sources.","performance_implication":"Event-time state may remain open longer because of late data.","testing_guidance":"Generate delayed and reordered events and compare event-time and processing-time results.","verification_guidance":"Verify window results against the declared timestamp semantics.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed streams","Time semantics"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R03","D17-B75-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B12-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Processing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B75-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Windowing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why are windows used in distributed stream processing?","answer":"Windows divide an unbounded event stream into bounded logical regions so computations such as aggregation can be performed over defined temporal or count-based scopes.","explanation":"A continuously arriving stream has no natural end, so windowing provides a finite state-management boundary.","principle":"Unbounded streams require explicit state and completion semantics for bounded computations.","rule":"A window definition must specify its boundary, triggering behavior, allowed lateness, and state-retention policy.","correct_example":"Compute a five-minute event-time aggregation with an explicit lateness and finalization policy.","invalid_example":"Aggregating an unbounded stream without any state-retention or finalization rule.","problem":"State grows continuously when there is no bounded computation scope.","failure_mode":"Unbounded memory growth or undefined results.","cause":"Missing window or state-retention semantics.","solution":"Define fixed, sliding, session, or another appropriate window type with explicit retention.","alternative":"Continuous aggregation with external compaction or snapshots.","tradeoff":"Smaller windows reduce state but may reduce analytical context.","constraint":"Window semantics must match business meaning.","edge_case":"An event arrives after the window has been finalized.","security_implication":"Windowed security analytics must account for late events that could alter threat conclusions.","performance_implication":"Window size directly affects state size and computation cost.","testing_guidance":"Test boundary timestamps, empty windows, late events, and high-cardinality keys.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each event contributes only to windows permitted by the declared semantics.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Event time","Stream processing"],"related_records":["D17-B75-R01","D17-B75-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B75-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Windowing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B75-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Late event handling","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"How should distributed stream processors handle late events?","answer":"A stream processor should explicitly define whether late events are accepted, update previously produced results, routed to a side path, or discarded after a configured lateness boundary.","explanation":"Network delay and source disorder make late arrival normal in many distributed streams.","principle":"Late-data behavior is part of the computation's correctness semantics.","rule":"Define an allowed lateness policy and the effect of events arriving after finalization.","correct_example":"Accept events within a defined lateness interval and update the affected aggregate according to the system's correction semantics.","invalid_example":"Silently discarding all late events while claiming exact event-time results.","problem":"Late events may arrive after downstream consumers have observed a preliminary result.","failure_mode":"Previously emitted results become inconsistent or incomplete.","cause":"No correction or late-data policy.","solution":"Use allowed-lateness windows, retractions, corrections, or a late-event stream.","alternative":"Drop late events when approximate results are explicitly acceptable.","tradeoff":"Supporting late corrections increases state and downstream complexity.","constraint":"Downstream consumers must understand whether results are provisional or final.","edge_case":"A very late event arrives after historical state has been compacted.","security_implication":"Late security events may contain evidence of earlier activity and should not be silently discarded when they affect investigations.","performance_implication":"Longer lateness windows increase retained state and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Inject events across lateness boundaries and verify correction behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that late-event outcomes match the declared finality policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Event-time processing","Windowing"],"related_records":["D17-B75-R01","D17-B75-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B75-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Late_Data_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B76-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Watermark","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a watermark in distributed stream processing?","answer":"A watermark is progress information used by a stream-processing system to indicate that events earlier than a certain logical time are expected to be sufficiently complete according to the system's assumptions.","explanation":"Watermarks help processors decide when time-based computation can advance despite out-of-order event arrival.","principle":"Progress in event time must be estimated because an unbounded stream cannot provide absolute knowledge that no earlier event will ever arrive.","rule":"Watermark semantics must state what completeness assumption they represent and how late events are handled.","correct_example":"Advance a window when its watermark passes the configured end boundary while retaining the declared late-data handling policy.","invalid_example":"Treating a watermark as proof that no earlier event can ever arrive.","problem":"Processors need a practical finalization signal despite incomplete knowledge.","failure_mode":"Windows finalize too early or remain open indefinitely.","cause":"Incorrect watermark generation or interpretation.","solution":"Define source progress, lateness assumptions, and finalization behavior explicitly.","alternative":"Use processing-time finalization when event-time completeness is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Earlier watermarks reduce latency but increase the probability or cost of late corrections.","constraint":"Watermark quality depends on source progress and distributed observation.","edge_case":"One slow partition prevents global event-time progress.","security_implication":"Security analytics should not interpret a watermark as absolute proof of event absence.","performance_implication":"Watermarks control state retention and result latency.","testing_guidance":"Delay one partition while advancing others and observe global watermark behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify watermark progression against the declared completeness assumptions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Event time","Windowing","Distributed streams"],"related_records":["D17-B75-R01","D17-B75-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B75-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Processing_Watermark_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B76-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Partitioned watermark progress","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can one slow stream partition delay global watermark progress?","answer":"If global progress depends on the minimum or another conservative combination of partition progress, a slow or inactive partition can prevent the system from safely advancing event-time finalization.","explanation":"A global watermark must account for partitions that may still contain earlier events.","principle":"Global temporal completeness is constrained by the least-progressed relevant source partition.","rule":"Systems must define how idle or failed partitions are detected before excluding them from progress calculations.","correct_example":"Use explicit idle-partition detection before allowing other partitions to advance global event-time progress.","invalid_example":"Ignoring a slow partition immediately and finalizing windows as if its events cannot arrive.","problem":"A single partition delays event-time computation.","failure_mode":"Unnecessary latency and retained state.","cause":"Conservative watermark calculation or missing idle-partition handling.","solution":"Use bounded idleness detection and explicit source-health semantics.","alternative":"Partition-local processing when global aggregation is not required.","tradeoff":"Aggressive idle detection lowers latency but increases late-data risk.","constraint":"Idle detection must be compatible with expected source behavior.","edge_case":"A legitimately slow source is mistaken for an idle source.","security_implication":"Excluding a source from temporal progress can hide delayed events if attackers can influence source availability.","performance_implication":"Correct idle detection can significantly reduce unnecessary state retention.","testing_guidance":"Simulate silent, slow, failed, and recovering partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that excluded partitions satisfy the declared idleness condition.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Watermarks","Partitioned streams"],"related_records":["D17-B76-R01","D17-B75-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B76-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Watermark_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B76-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Watermark versus completion proof","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Is a watermark proof that all earlier events have arrived?","answer":"No. A watermark is generally a progress or completeness assumption under defined source and lateness semantics, not an absolute proof that no earlier event can arrive.","explanation":"Distributed systems cannot generally know that an unbounded source has permanently stopped producing an earlier event unless stronger assumptions are available.","principle":"Operational progress signals must not be confused with absolute historical completeness.","rule":"Documentation must state the assumptions and uncertainty represented by a watermark.","correct_example":"Treat a watermark as sufficient for provisional window finalization while retaining a correction mechanism for allowed late data.","invalid_example":"Deleting all historical state immediately because the watermark proves that no earlier event can exist.","problem":"Applications may overinterpret progress metadata.","failure_mode":"Irreversible loss of late information.","cause":"Confusing a temporal heuristic or contract with absolute proof.","solution":"Preserve explicit lateness semantics and correction paths where required.","alternative":"Use a stronger source contract if absolute completeness can actually be established.","tradeoff":"Stronger completeness guarantees usually require stronger source coordination or delayed finalization.","constraint":"The achievable guarantee depends on source behavior and network assumptions.","edge_case":"A source is offline and later emits old events.","security_implication":"Forensic or compliance systems should avoid treating watermarks as absolute evidence of event absence.","performance_implication":"Longer retention protects against late data but increases storage and processing cost.","testing_guidance":"Replay old events after watermark advancement and verify documented handling.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the implementation does not claim stronger completeness than its source contract supports.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Watermarks","Late-event handling"],"related_records":["D17-B76-R01","D17-B76-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B76-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Processing_Semantics_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B77-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Stream State","concept":"Stateful stream operator","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is a stateful stream operator?","answer":"A stateful stream operator maintains information across multiple events so that future processing depends on previously observed data.","explanation":"Examples include keyed aggregation, joins, deduplication, session tracking, and pattern detection.","principle":"State used for stream computation is part of the distributed system's correctness boundary.","rule":"State must have ownership, persistence, recovery, retention, and consistency semantics.","correct_example":"Maintain per-key aggregation state and persist it sufficiently to recover after operator failure.","invalid_example":"Keeping critical stream state only in process memory while claiming fault-tolerant processing.","problem":"Operator failure can erase computation state.","failure_mode":"Duplicate, missing, or inconsistent results after recovery.","cause":"Non-durable or incorrectly checkpointed state.","solution":"Use checkpoints, durable state stores, replay, or another defined recovery mechanism.","alternative":"Recompute state entirely from a durable event log when feasible.","tradeoff":"Persisted state improves recovery but adds I/O and storage overhead.","constraint":"State partitioning must remain compatible with event routing and recovery.","edge_case":"A key moves between processing nodes during rebalancing.","security_implication":"State stores must preserve tenant and authorization boundaries across reassignments.","performance_implication":"State size and access pattern directly affect throughput and memory usage.","testing_guidance":"Kill operators, restore from checkpoints, replay events, and compare resulting state.","verification_guidance":"Verify recovered state against an independently derived expected state.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Stream processing","Checkpointing"],"related_records":["D17-B16-R02","D17-B55-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B16-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stateful_Stream_Processing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B77-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Stream State","concept":"State partitioning by key","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why is stream state commonly partitioned by key?","answer":"Key-based partitioning sends events for the same logical entity to a consistent processing owner so stateful operations can be performed without global coordination for every event.","explanation":"The key defines the state ownership boundary and allows parallel processing across independent keys.","principle":"Partitioning state by independent logical keys converts global coordination into local coordination where the workload permits it.","rule":"The partition key must preserve the locality required by the computation.","correct_example":"Route all events for one account to the same state partition when account-level aggregation is required.","invalid_example":"Partitioning randomly when the operator requires all events for an entity to share state.","problem":"Related events arrive at different state owners.","failure_mode":"Incorrect aggregation or expensive cross-node coordination.","cause":"Incompatible partition key.","solution":"Choose a key aligned with the operator's state and join requirements.","alternative":"Replicated or globally coordinated state when locality cannot be guaranteed.","tradeoff":"Key partitioning improves scalability but can create hotspots for highly popular keys.","constraint":"Cross-key operations may still require distributed coordination.","edge_case":"One key receives substantially more events than others.","security_implication":"Partitioning must not allow events from different tenants to share state accidentally.","performance_implication":"Good partitioning improves parallelism and local state access.","testing_guidance":"Test skewed keys, key reassignment, and cross-key operations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every event reaches the state owner required by the operator's invariant.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Stateful stream processing","Partitioning"],"related_records":["D17-B45-R03","D17-B77-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B77-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Stream_Partitioning_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B77-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Stream State","concept":"Hot-key state skew","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"What is a hot key in distributed stream processing?","answer":"A hot key is a logical key whose event volume or state activity is disproportionately high, causing its assigned processing partition to become a bottleneck.","explanation":"Key-based partitioning balances keys rather than necessarily balancing workload. One popular key can dominate one partition.","principle":"Balanced key counts do not guarantee balanced processing load.","rule":"Systems should detect and explicitly handle hot-key skew when workload distributions are non-uniform.","correct_example":"Detect a high-volume key and use a workload-specific strategy such as subpartitioning when the computation permits it.","invalid_example":"Adding more ordinary partitions while keeping one indivisible hot key assigned to one partition.","problem":"One key overwhelms its state owner.","failure_mode":"One partition reaches high latency while others remain underutilized.","cause":"Highly skewed key popularity.","solution":"Use key salting, hierarchical aggregation, replicated read state, or another algorithm-specific mitigation.","alternative":"Dedicated processing resources for known hot entities.","tradeoff":"Splitting a key improves throughput but may require a second-stage aggregation.","constraint":"Not every stateful computation can safely split one key across workers.","edge_case":"A hot key becomes cold and a previously specialized topology becomes inefficient.","security_implication":"Attackers may intentionally create hot keys to exhaust one partition.","performance_implication":"Hot-key skew can dominate tail latency and limit horizontal scalability.","testing_guidance":"Generate Zipfian and adversarial key distributions and measure partition skew.","verification_guidance":"Verify workload distribution, not only key-count distribution.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"STREAM_PROCESSING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["State partitioning","Stream processing"],"related_records":["D17-B64-R02","D17-B77-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B77-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Stream_Processing_Performance_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B78-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Distributed task lease","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can a distributed scheduler prevent multiple workers from executing the same task concurrently?","answer":"A distributed scheduler can assign a task ownership lease or generation to a worker and require ownership validity before execution or completion.","explanation":"Task scheduling occurs under failures and retries, so ownership can become stale while another worker takes over.","principle":"Task ownership must remain safe under worker failure and delayed messages.","rule":"Task completion must be accepted only from a valid ownership generation or through an idempotent result protocol.","correct_example":"Assign a task lease generation and reject completion from workers holding an obsolete generation.","invalid_example":"Accepting any worker completion after a task has been reassigned.","problem":"A slow worker may continue executing after reassignment.","failure_mode":"Duplicate or conflicting task effects.","cause":"Stale task ownership.","solution":"Use task generations, leases, fencing, or idempotent task effects.","alternative":"Centralized scheduler with explicit task-state transitions.","tradeoff":"Lease-based scheduling improves recovery but requires stale-worker protection.","constraint":"External side effects may require stronger fencing than task-state storage alone.","edge_case":"A worker pauses and resumes after the task has been reassigned.","security_implication":"Stale workers must not retain authorization to perform privileged task effects.","performance_implication":"Task leases enable automatic recovery but add renewal and coordination traffic.","testing_guidance":"Pause workers beyond lease expiry and verify that stale completion cannot corrupt task state.","verification_guidance":"Verify task-state transitions using ownership generations.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed leases","Task scheduling"],"related_records":["D17-B65-R01","D17-B38-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B65-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Scheduling_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B78-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Work stealing","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is distributed work stealing?","answer":"Distributed work stealing allows workers with available capacity to obtain tasks from other workers or shared task pools instead of relying solely on static task assignment.","explanation":"Work stealing can improve utilization when workloads are uneven or task duration varies.","principle":"Idle capacity can be used to rebalance dynamically when task ownership permits migration.","rule":"Stealing must preserve task ownership, prevent duplicate execution where unsafe, and account for locality.","correct_example":"An idle worker claims an unowned task from another worker's queue using an atomic ownership transition.","invalid_example":"Copying tasks to another worker without changing ownership or deduplicating execution.","problem":"Static scheduling leaves some workers idle while others are overloaded.","failure_mode":"Poor utilization and uneven completion time.","cause":"Uneven task duration or assignment.","solution":"Use controlled work stealing with atomic task claiming and locality-aware policies.","alternative":"Centralized dynamic scheduling.","tradeoff":"Work stealing improves utilization but can increase coordination and data-movement costs.","constraint":"Task migration must be safe for the task's state and external side effects.","edge_case":"A task becomes partially executed before another worker attempts to steal it.","security_implication":"Workers must be authorized to claim and execute the tasks they steal.","performance_implication":"Stealing can reduce idle time but may hurt cache or data locality.","testing_guidance":"Use highly skewed task durations and measure utilization, migration, and duplicate execution.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each task's ownership transition is atomic according to the scheduler protocol.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Task ownership"],"related_records":["D17-B38-R01","D17-B78-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B78-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Work_Stealing_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B78-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Scheduling fairness versus locality","knowledge_type":"TRADEOFF","question":"What trade-off exists between distributed scheduling fairness and data locality?","answer":"Moving work toward idle workers can improve fairness and utilization but may require transferring data or state away from the location where it is cheapest to process.","explanation":"A scheduler must balance resource utilization against network transfer, cache locality, storage locality, and task affinity.","principle":"The globally fairest assignment is not always the least expensive assignment.","rule":"Scheduling decisions should include locality when data movement is a significant cost.","correct_example":"Prefer local tasks for a worker when their expected completion cost remains within an acceptable fairness bound.","invalid_example":"Always moving the largest task to the least-loaded worker without considering data-transfer cost.","problem":"Load balancing can conflict with data locality.","failure_mode":"Network and storage traffic increase enough to reduce overall throughput.","cause":"Scheduler optimizes only worker utilization.","solution":"Use locality-aware cost models and bounded imbalance tolerance.","alternative":"Static data-local scheduling with separate capacity scaling.","tradeoff":"Better locality may tolerate some load imbalance; stronger balancing may increase data movement.","constraint":"The cost model must reflect actual infrastructure behavior.","edge_case":"A worker becomes overloaded while holding a very large local dataset.","security_implication":"Data movement must preserve access controls and residency requirements.","performance_implication":"Locality can dominate scheduling efficiency for data-intensive workloads.","testing_guidance":"Compare scheduling policies under varying data-transfer costs and task skew.","verification_guidance":"Measure both utilization and data-movement cost before declaring a scheduling policy effective.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Work stealing","Data locality"],"related_records":["D17-B78-R02","D17-B57-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B78-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Scheduling_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B79-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Duplicate task execution","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why can distributed schedulers execute the same task more than once?","answer":"Duplicate execution can occur when ownership information is delayed, workers retry after uncertain outcomes, or a task is reassigned while its previous owner is still active.","explanation":"Exactly-once task execution is difficult when failures can occur after a side effect but before the scheduler learns that the task completed.","principle":"Task assignment state and task side effects can become inconsistent under failure.","rule":"Tasks with non-idempotent effects require idempotency keys, transactional coordination, fencing, or another explicit duplicate-prevention strategy.","correct_example":"Use a durable task identifier and idempotent effect boundary so repeated execution produces one logical outcome.","invalid_example":"Assuming a single task assignment guarantees a single execution in a failure-prone distributed system.","problem":"The scheduler cannot always know whether an old worker completed a task.","failure_mode":"Duplicate side effects.","cause":"Ambiguous completion after timeout or worker failure.","solution":"Use idempotency, transactional result recording, or fenced ownership.","alternative":"Design tasks as naturally idempotent operations.","tradeoff":"Duplicate protection adds storage or coordination overhead.","constraint":"External systems may not support atomic idempotency without integration.","edge_case":"A worker completes the side effect immediately before losing network connectivity.","security_implication":"Duplicate privileged actions can have amplified security impact.","performance_implication":"Deduplication state consumes storage and lookup resources.","testing_guidance":"Fail workers after side effects but before acknowledgments and verify duplicate handling.","verification_guidance":"Verify logical effect uniqueness independently from scheduler assignment logs.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B18-R01","D17-B78-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B78-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Task_Execution_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B79-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Task result commit boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a distributed scheduler define a task result commit boundary?","answer":"A task result commit boundary identifies when a task's outcome becomes durable and authoritative so retries and recovery can distinguish completed work from uncertain work.","explanation":"Without a clear boundary, scheduler state and external effects can disagree after partial failure.","principle":"Execution completion and durable result commitment are distinct states.","rule":"The system must define the ordering between task execution, external effects, result persistence, and acknowledgment.","correct_example":"Persist an idempotent result record before acknowledging task completion when that record is the authoritative outcome.","invalid_example":"Marking a task complete only in volatile worker memory.","problem":"The scheduler cannot distinguish completed effects from interrupted execution.","failure_mode":"Lost results or duplicate work after recovery.","cause":"Undefined commit boundary.","solution":"Define and durably persist the authoritative result state.","alternative":"Use an external transactional workflow engine where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Durable result recording adds latency and storage overhead but improves recovery correctness.","constraint":"External side effects may still require separate idempotency or transaction coordination.","edge_case":"Result persistence succeeds while the acknowledgment message is lost.","security_implication":"Completion records must be protected against unauthorized alteration.","performance_implication":"Durable result commits add I/O but reduce expensive ambiguous retries.","testing_guidance":"Fail between every step of task execution and acknowledgment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery can determine task status from durable evidence.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Task scheduling","Durable state","Idempotency"],"related_records":["D17-B60-R03","D17-B79-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B79-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Task_Recovery_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B79-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Poison task","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a poison task in distributed scheduling?","answer":"A poison task is a task that repeatedly fails in a way that causes continuous retries without a realistic path to successful completion.","explanation":"Unbounded retries can consume worker capacity and prevent healthy tasks from progressing.","principle":"Failure recovery must distinguish transient failures from tasks requiring isolation or human intervention.","rule":"Repeated task failure should trigger bounded retry behavior and an explicit terminal or quarantine path.","correct_example":"Move a repeatedly failing task to a dead-letter or quarantine state after the configured retry policy is exhausted.","invalid_example":"Retrying the same deterministic failure indefinitely with no state change.","problem":"One invalid or permanently failing task consumes scheduling capacity.","failure_mode":"Worker starvation and queue blockage.","cause":"Unbounded retries or lack of failure classification.","solution":"Use retry limits, failure classification, dead-letter handling, and operator-visible diagnostics.","alternative":"Pause the task queue when preserving strict ordering is more important than throughput.","tradeoff":"Quarantine improves throughput but requires explicit recovery and inspection workflows.","constraint":"Tasks with side effects must remain safe across repeated attempts.","edge_case":"A task fails transiently for a long period before eventually becoming valid.","security_implication":"Poison tasks can be used as resource-exhaustion inputs.","performance_implication":"Quarantine prevents repeated failure from consuming worker capacity.","testing_guidance":"Inject deterministic and transient task failures and verify different retry paths.","verification_guidance":"Verify that retry exhaustion produces a durable and observable terminal state.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Retries","Dead-letter handling"],"related_records":["D17-B37-R01","D17-B34-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B79-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Task_Failure_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B80-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Work-conserving scheduling","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does work-conserving mean in distributed scheduling?","answer":"A work-conserving scheduler attempts not to leave an eligible worker idle while executable work is available for that worker, subject to the scheduler's constraints.","explanation":"The property concerns utilization under the scheduler's eligibility and locality rules; it does not mean every worker must always be busy.","principle":"Available capacity should be used when doing so does not violate scheduling constraints.","rule":"Work-conserving behavior must be defined relative to eligibility, locality, priority, and resource constraints.","correct_example":"Assign an eligible queued task to an idle worker when no higher-priority constraint prevents assignment.","invalid_example":"Calling a scheduler non-work-conserving merely because it intentionally reserves capacity for critical workloads.","problem":"Workers can remain idle while executable work exists elsewhere.","failure_mode":"Reduced throughput and increased completion time.","cause":"Poor scheduling policy, locality restrictions, or excessive reservation.","solution":"Measure idle capacity and distinguish intentional reservation from avoidable underutilization.","alternative":"Aggressive work stealing or centralized dynamic scheduling.","tradeoff":"Strict utilization can conflict with locality, fairness, and reserved capacity.","constraint":"A task may not be executable on every worker.","edge_case":"Only one worker satisfies a task's data, hardware, or authorization constraints.","security_implication":"Scheduling eligibility may depend on authorization and isolation requirements.","performance_implication":"Work-conserving behavior can improve utilization when tasks are portable.","testing_guidance":"Create idle workers with eligible pending tasks and verify assignment behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify work-conservation relative to the scheduler's declared eligibility model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Resource eligibility"],"related_records":["D17-B78-R02","D17-B78-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Scheduling_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B80-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Scheduling starvation","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is starvation in distributed scheduling?","answer":"Scheduling starvation occurs when eligible work remains indefinitely delayed because other work repeatedly receives the available scheduling capacity.","explanation":"Starvation differs from temporary queueing because the affected work may continue to be postponed despite repeated opportunities for execution.","principle":"Fairness requires that eligible work eventually receive service when the scheduling model promises bounded or eventual progress.","rule":"Schedulers should define fairness or aging semantics when starvation is unacceptable.","correct_example":"Increase the priority of long-waiting tasks according to an explicit aging policy.","invalid_example":"Always selecting newly arrived high-priority work with no bound on how long lower-priority work can wait when fairness is required.","problem":"Some tasks receive persistent preference over others.","failure_mode":"Certain workloads never complete despite available capacity.","cause":"Strict priority without aging or insufficient fairness policy.","solution":"Use aging, weighted fairness, quotas, or explicit service-level guarantees.","alternative":"Separate queues with reserved capacity.","tradeoff":"Improved fairness can reduce peak throughput for high-priority workloads.","constraint":"Fairness must not override safety or mandatory priority requirements.","edge_case":"A continuous stream of high-priority work arrives faster than reserved capacity.","security_implication":"Tenant fairness can be required to prevent one tenant from monopolizing shared resources.","performance_implication":"Starvation increases tail completion time and can create hidden backlog.","testing_guidance":"Run sustained mixed-priority workloads and measure maximum waiting time.","verification_guidance":"Verify waiting-time behavior against the scheduler's stated fairness policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Fairness"],"related_records":["D17-B78-R03","D17-B80-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B80-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Scheduling_Fairness_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B80-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Scheduling","concept":"Scheduling deadline awareness","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"How should a distributed scheduler handle task deadlines?","answer":"A deadline-aware scheduler considers task deadlines and expected execution cost when selecting work, while explicitly handling tasks that can no longer meet their deadlines.","explanation":"Deadline information changes scheduling from simple queue ordering to time-sensitive admission and prioritization.","principle":"A task whose deadline is impossible to meet should not silently consume the same resources as a feasible task when deadline-aware behavior is required.","rule":"Define deadline semantics, clock assumptions, priority policy, and behavior for missed deadlines.","correct_example":"Prioritize feasible tasks by deadline while explicitly marking impossible or expired tasks according to policy.","invalid_example":"Treating an expired task as normally schedulable without considering its deadline semantics.","problem":"Scheduling capacity is consumed by work that may already have lost its value.","failure_mode":"Missed deadlines increase while resources remain occupied by infeasible tasks.","cause":"No deadline-aware scheduling policy.","solution":"Use deadline-aware priority, admission control, cancellation, or graceful degradation.","alternative":"FIFO scheduling when deadlines are irrelevant.","tradeoff":"Deadline optimization can reduce fairness for non-deadline workloads.","constraint":"Distributed clocks and task-duration estimates may be imperfect.","edge_case":"Clock skew causes different nodes to interpret the same deadline differently.","security_implication":"Deadline metadata should not be client-manipulable when it controls privileged resource allocation.","performance_implication":"Deadline-aware scheduling can improve deadline attainment but requires additional scheduling metadata.","testing_guidance":"Inject clock skew, variable execution times, expired tasks, and competing deadlines.","verification_guidance":"Verify deadline handling against the declared clock and scheduling model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"DISTRIBUTED_SCHEDULING","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["Distributed scheduling","Clock semantics"],"related_records":["D17-B12-R03","D17-B80-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B12-R03"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING","source_registry":"Distributed_Deadline_Scheduling_Literature"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B81-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache-aside consistency boundary","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What consistency boundary does the cache-aside pattern create?","answer":"Cache-aside separates the cache from the authoritative data store: reads may return cached data until invalidation or expiration, while writes normally update the authoritative store and require a strategy for making cached entries sufficiently fresh.","explanation":"The cache is not automatically part of the authoritative transaction. Correctness therefore depends on explicit cache population, invalidation, expiration, or versioning rules.","principle":"A cache-aside cache must not silently become the authoritative source unless the system explicitly defines it as such.","correct_example":"Write authoritative data first, then invalidate or version the affected cache entry according to the consistency requirement.","invalid_example":"Assume a successful database write automatically makes every previously cached copy current.","problem":"Cached values can remain stale after an authoritative write.","failure_mode":"Stale cache read","cause":"Missing, delayed, or failed invalidation/update.","solution":"Define explicit invalidation, version validation, bounded TTL, or read-through reconciliation appropriate to the required consistency.","alternative":"Write-through caching or versioned cache entries.","tradeoff":"Stronger cache freshness requires additional coordination, latency, or cache-miss cost.","constraint":"Cache correctness must be evaluated against the application's actual consistency requirement.","edge_case":"Invalidation succeeds for some cache nodes but fails for others.","security_implication":"Stale authorization or policy data can create security exposure if security decisions depend on cached state.","performance_implication":"Aggressive invalidation can increase backend load and cache misses.","testing_guidance":"Test writes followed immediately by reads through different cache paths and simulate failed invalidation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the observed behavior matches the documented freshness guarantee under normal and failure conditions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B76-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B51-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B51-R03","D17-B52-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":["D17-B46-R01"],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B81-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B81-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache invalidation ordering","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why does ordering between an authoritative write and cache invalidation matter?","answer":"If invalidation occurs before the authoritative write and a concurrent reader repopulates the cache from the old value, the cache can become stale again after invalidation.","explanation":"A cache invalidation is an event in a concurrent system rather than a permanent guarantee. Correct ordering or version checks are needed when concurrent readers and writers can race.","principle":"Cache invalidation must account for concurrent repopulation races.","correct_example":"Use a versioned cache entry or an ordering protocol that prevents an older value from replacing a newer one.","invalid_example":"Delete the cache entry before updating the database and assume the entry cannot become stale again.","problem":"A stale value can be repopulated after invalidation.","failure_mode":"Stale repopulation race","cause":"Concurrent read and write paths have no ordering or version protection.","solution":"Use write-then-invalidate, version validation, fencing, or another explicitly justified ordering mechanism.","alternative":"Short TTL with acceptable bounded staleness.","tradeoff":"Versioning and coordination increase implementation complexity.","constraint":"The chosen ordering must match the application's concurrency model.","edge_case":"A delayed cache-fill response arrives after a newer write.","security_implication":"Stale security configuration may be incorrectly reintroduced.","performance_implication":"Additional version checks add metadata operations but can prevent repeated invalidation storms.","testing_guidance":"Race delayed cache fills against writes and invalidations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that an older cache-fill response cannot overwrite a newer version.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B81-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B28-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B53-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B81-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B46-R03"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B81-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Caching","concept":"Cache stampede prevention","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is a cache stampede?","answer":"A cache stampede occurs when many requests simultaneously miss or invalidate the same cache entry and overload the backing service with duplicate recomputation or retrieval work.","explanation":"Expiration can synchronize many clients around the same missing key. Distributed systems need mechanisms that limit duplicate work or spread refresh activity.","principle":"Cache freshness mechanisms should avoid converting synchronized cache misses into synchronized backend load.","correct_example":"Use request coalescing, staggered expiration, refresh-ahead, or bounded randomized expiration where appropriate.","invalid_example":"Set the same short expiration time for a heavily requested key and assume the backend will absorb the resulting concurrent misses.","problem":"A popular cache key expires and many clients regenerate it simultaneously.","failure_mode":"Backend overload caused by synchronized cache misses","cause":"Synchronized expiration and absence of duplicate-work suppression.","solution":"Use single-flight/request coalescing, jittered TTLs, refresh-ahead, or controlled stale serving.","alternative":"Serve stale data for a bounded period while one request refreshes the value.","tradeoff":"Stale serving can improve availability at the cost of freshness.","constraint":"Stale serving is unsafe when freshness is part of the correctness invariant.","edge_case":"The refresh operation itself becomes slow or unavailable.","security_implication":"An attacker may intentionally trigger expensive cache misses to amplify backend load.","performance_implication":"Stampedes can produce large tail-latency spikes and backend saturation.","testing_guidance":"Expire a hot key under concurrent load and measure backend request amplification.","verification_guidance":"Verify that concurrent cache misses remain within the intended backend amplification bound.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B71-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B71-R02","D17-B72-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":["D17-B46-R03"],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B82-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Probabilistic Structures","concept":"Bloom filter for distributed storage","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What problem can a Bloom filter solve in a distributed storage system?","answer":"A Bloom filter can compactly represent probable set membership and help avoid unnecessary remote reads when an item is definitely absent from a represented set.","explanation":"Bloom filters can produce false positives but not false negatives under their standard construction, making them useful as a pre-check before more expensive distributed lookups.","principle":"A probabilistic negative filter should only suppress work when its false-negative behavior is compatible with the correctness requirement.","correct_example":"Use a Bloom filter to avoid an expensive lookup when it reports definite absence, then perform the authoritative lookup when it reports possible presence.","invalid_example":"Treat a Bloom-filter positive result as proof that the item exists.","problem":"Probabilistic membership is mistaken for authoritative state.","failure_mode":"Incorrect positive interpretation","cause":"Failure to distinguish probabilistic filtering from authoritative lookup.","solution":"Use the filter only as an optimization and retain an authoritative verification path.","alternative":"Cuckoo filters or another appropriate probabilistic structure.","tradeoff":"The filter consumes memory and has a configurable false-positive rate.","constraint":"Filter contents can become stale when the underlying dataset changes.","edge_case":"Deletion-heavy workloads require explicit handling because basic Bloom filters do not directly support arbitrary deletion.","security_implication":"Filters must not be treated as authorization evidence or secret-state proof.","performance_implication":"A good filter can reduce network and storage I/O at the cost of memory and hash computation.","testing_guidance":"Test absent, present, and stale-filter cases and measure false-positive behavior.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no correctness-critical decision relies solely on a probabilistic positive result.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B54-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B54-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B82-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Hashing","concept":"Distributed hash table lookup","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the core purpose of a distributed hash table?","answer":"A distributed hash table maps keys to responsible nodes through a distributed routing structure, allowing a large keyspace to be managed without requiring every node to maintain a complete mapping.","explanation":"DHT designs distribute ownership and routing state across nodes and provide lookup mechanisms that operate over changing membership.","principle":"Distributed ownership requires routing metadata that remains sufficiently correct as nodes join, leave, or fail.","correct_example":"Route a key through the DHT's routing structure until the node responsible for that key is reached.","invalid_example":"Assume a DHT always knows the current owner without membership maintenance.","problem":"Membership changes invalidate routing information.","failure_mode":"Stale routing","cause":"Delayed or failed membership and routing updates.","solution":"Use stabilization, routing-table repair, and failure detection appropriate to the DHT design.","alternative":"Centralized partition metadata or consistent-hashing-based routing.","tradeoff":"DHTs avoid centralized mapping but introduce distributed routing and maintenance complexity.","constraint":"Correctness depends on the specific DHT algorithm and its failure assumptions.","edge_case":"A lookup begins during simultaneous node failure and membership change.","security_implication":"Malicious nodes can affect routing or ownership in designs without adequate trust controls.","performance_implication":"Routing hop count and metadata maintenance determine lookup overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test lookups during joins, departures, partitions, and stale routing states.","verification_guidance":"Verify lookup correctness under the stated membership and failure model.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B44-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B14-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B44-R02","D17-B45-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B82-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Hashing","concept":"Hot-key mitigation","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can consistent hashing fail to prevent hot keys?","answer":"Consistent hashing distributes key ownership, but a small number of extremely popular keys can still concentrate traffic on their owners.","explanation":"Balanced key placement does not imply balanced request volume. Workload skew must be considered separately from key-space distribution.","principle":"Capacity planning must account for request frequency as well as key distribution.","correct_example":"Detect hot keys and use controlled replication, request coalescing, or workload-aware routing where semantics permit.","invalid_example":"Assume evenly distributed hash ranges guarantee evenly distributed request load.","problem":"A small set of keys receives disproportionate traffic.","failure_mode":"Hotspot saturation","cause":"Access-frequency skew rather than ownership imbalance.","solution":"Use hot-key detection and an appropriate replication or caching strategy.","alternative":"Application-level sharding of high-volume logical entities.","tradeoff":"Replicating hot data increases consistency and invalidation complexity.","constraint":"Hot-key replication must preserve the required write and read semantics.","edge_case":"A key becomes hot suddenly due to an external event.","security_implication":"Hot-key amplification can be intentionally induced as a denial-of-service vector.","performance_implication":"Tail latency can increase sharply at the overloaded owner.","testing_guidance":"Generate skewed key distributions and measure owner utilization and tail latency.","verification_guidance":"Verify that hotspot mitigation preserves correctness while reducing concentration.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B82-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B44-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B72-R01","D17-B49-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B82-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B44-R03"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B83-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lock ownership authority","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"What must a distributed lock system establish before a holder performs a protected operation?","answer":"It must establish that the holder currently possesses valid authority for the protected operation under the lock protocol's failure and expiration model.","explanation":"A lock grant alone may not remain valid after a timeout, partition, or coordinator failure. Protected resources need a mechanism that prevents stale holders from acting as current owners.","principle":"Distributed lock correctness requires current ownership authority, not merely historical acquisition success.","correct_example":"Associate lock ownership with a lease or fencing mechanism and reject operations from stale holders.","invalid_example":"Acquire a lock once and continue using it indefinitely after communication with the lock service is lost.","problem":"A stale lock holder continues operating after its authority should have expired.","failure_mode":"Stale-owner action","cause":"Resource does not validate current ownership or fencing authority.","solution":"Use lease validation, fencing tokens, or another resource-enforced ownership mechanism.","alternative":"Centralized serialized execution where appropriate.","tradeoff":"Stronger ownership enforcement requires additional coordination at the protected resource.","constraint":"The protected resource must participate in the authority model if stale holders must be prevented.","edge_case":"The old holder resumes after a long network partition.","security_implication":"Stale authority can cause unauthorized state modification.","performance_implication":"Per-operation fencing validation adds coordination overhead.","testing_guidance":"Pause a lock holder beyond its lease and attempt operations after another holder acquires the lock.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale holders cannot modify protected state.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B13-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B43-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B43-R02","D17-B59-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B13-R03"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B83-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lease expiration uncertainty","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why is lease expiration difficult to reason about across distributed nodes?","answer":"A node cannot generally determine from its own local clock that another node has observed or enforced lease expiration at the same instant, especially under network delay and clock uncertainty.","explanation":"Lease protocols depend on timing assumptions. Local elapsed time does not automatically prove remote state or current authority.","principle":"Timing-based authority must explicitly account for communication delay, clock behavior, and failure assumptions.","correct_example":"Define conservative lease validity rules and combine timing assumptions with server-side ownership or fencing where required.","invalid_example":"Assume local wall-clock expiration immediately proves that every other participant has rejected the old holder.","problem":"Different participants have inconsistent views of lease validity.","failure_mode":"Concurrent ownership due to timing ambiguity","cause":"Unmodeled network delay, clock uncertainty, or failure detection assumptions.","solution":"Use protocol-defined lease semantics, conservative timing bounds, and fencing at the protected resource when necessary.","alternative":"Logical epochs or monotonic ownership generations.","tradeoff":"Conservative lease margins reduce availability windows but improve safety.","constraint":"Correctness depends on the timing and failure model explicitly assumed by the protocol.","edge_case":"A paused process resumes after its lease expires remotely.","security_implication":"Incorrect lease assumptions can permit stale authority to modify protected resources.","performance_implication":"Longer safety margins can increase failover latency.","testing_guidance":"Inject process pauses and network delays around lease expiration.","verification_guidance":"Verify stale holders are rejected under the documented timing bounds.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B12-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B83-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B13-R02","D17-B13-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B83-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B83-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Distributed Locks","concept":"Lock release after owner failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"How should a distributed lock system handle a lock holder that fails?","answer":"The system should recover ownership according to an explicit lease, expiration, or failure-recovery protocol rather than relying on a failed holder to release the lock.","explanation":"A crashed process cannot perform cleanup. Correct recovery requires lock state to have an independent lifecycle.","principle":"Resource ownership must be recoverable when the current owner disappears.","correct_example":"Use an expiring lease and safely transfer ownership after the protocol's recovery conditions are satisfied.","invalid_example":"Require the failed process to send a release message before any other process can acquire the lock.","problem":"A failed holder leaves lock state permanently occupied.","failure_mode":"Stuck lock","cause":"Ownership depends on successful cleanup by the holder.","solution":"Use leases, epochs, or coordinator-managed recovery.","alternative":"Transactional ownership records with recovery procedures.","tradeoff":"Automatic recovery improves availability but must avoid overlapping ownership.","constraint":"Recovery must preserve mutual exclusion under the defined failure model.","edge_case":"The old holder recovers after the lock has been reassigned.","security_implication":"Incorrect recovery can permit two principals to believe they hold authority.","performance_implication":"Recovery timeout affects failover latency.","testing_guidance":"Kill the owner, wait for recovery, acquire from another node, then restart the old owner.","verification_guidance":"Verify that old and new owners cannot both perform protected operations.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B83-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B55-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B59-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B83-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B84-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Service registry consistency","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What information does a service registry provide in a distributed system?","answer":"A service registry maintains discoverable information about service instances, such as identifiers, endpoints, health or lifecycle metadata, and sometimes capabilities or routing attributes.","explanation":"Clients or routing components use registry information to locate service instances without hard-coding every endpoint.","principle":"Discovery metadata must have an explicitly defined freshness and authority model.","correct_example":"Register an instance, publish its endpoint and lifecycle state, and remove or expire the registration when the instance is no longer eligible.","invalid_example":"Assume a registry entry remains valid indefinitely after the instance disappears.","problem":"Clients receive stale endpoints.","failure_mode":"Stale service discovery","cause":"Delayed deregistration, missed heartbeats, or registry inconsistency.","solution":"Use leases/TTL, health-aware discovery, and explicit stale-entry handling.","alternative":"DNS-based discovery or platform-native service discovery.","tradeoff":"Aggressive expiration improves freshness but increases registry traffic and sensitivity to transient failures.","constraint":"Discovery data is metadata and may itself be subject to distributed consistency limits.","edge_case":"An instance is healthy but temporarily unable to renew its registration.","security_implication":"Stale or unauthorized endpoints can redirect traffic to unintended destinations.","performance_implication":"Frequent health and registry updates increase control-plane load.","testing_guidance":"Simulate instance failure, network isolation, delayed registration, and recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify that ineligible instances are removed or excluded within the documented freshness bound.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B14-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B42-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R02","D17-B84-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":["D17-B41-R01"],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B84-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Client-side versus server-side discovery","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the distinction between client-side and server-side service discovery?","answer":"In client-side discovery, the caller obtains service-instance information and selects an instance itself. In server-side discovery, the caller addresses an intermediary that performs discovery and routing.","explanation":"The distinction changes where discovery logic, health filtering, load balancing, and failure handling reside.","principle":"Discovery responsibility should be placed where its operational and consistency requirements can be managed reliably.","correct_example":"A client-side system can query a registry and select a healthy instance; a server-side system can send requests to a routing layer that performs the selection.","invalid_example":"Treat the two models as identical merely because both ultimately locate service instances.","problem":"Discovery logic becomes duplicated or inconsistent across clients.","failure_mode":"Fragmented routing behavior","cause":"Multiple clients implement different health and selection policies.","solution":"Centralize policy where appropriate or provide a well-defined client library and contract.","alternative":"Service mesh or platform-native discovery.","tradeoff":"Client-side discovery can reduce intermediary hops but increases client complexity.","constraint":"The chosen model must account for registry freshness and routing failures.","edge_case":"The discovery source becomes temporarily unavailable while cached endpoints remain usable.","security_implication":"Discovery must prevent unauthorized endpoint registration or routing.","performance_implication":"Client-side selection can reduce intermediary load but requires local discovery state.","testing_guidance":"Compare behavior during registry failure, instance failure, and topology change.","verification_guidance":"Verify that selected endpoints satisfy the documented health and routing policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B84-R01"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B41-R02","D17-B49-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B84-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B84-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Discovery","concept":"Discovery and readiness separation","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should service discovery eligibility be distinguished from process liveness?","answer":"A process can be alive while temporarily unable to serve traffic correctly. Discovery systems therefore often need to distinguish liveness from readiness or traffic eligibility.","explanation":"Liveness answers whether a process is functioning enough to remain running, while readiness addresses whether it should receive a particular class of work.","principle":"Traffic eligibility should reflect service capability rather than process existence alone.","correct_example":"Keep a running instance out of traffic while dependencies are unavailable or initialization is incomplete, then reintroduce it after readiness criteria recover.","invalid_example":"Route traffic to every process that responds to a basic liveness probe.","problem":"Live but unready instances receive requests.","failure_mode":"Traffic to unhealthy instance","cause":"Discovery uses an insufficient health signal.","solution":"Separate liveness, readiness, and where needed startup or dependency-health states.","alternative":"Health-aware load balancer policy.","tradeoff":"More health states improve routing precision but increase operational complexity.","constraint":"Health signals must be designed to avoid cascading false failures.","edge_case":"A dependency is degraded but the service can still provide partial functionality.","security_implication":"Security-sensitive readiness conditions must not expose internal dependency details.","performance_implication":"Frequent deep readiness checks can add control-plane and dependency load.","testing_guidance":"Test startup, dependency failure, partial degradation, and recovery transitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that traffic eligibility changes according to the declared readiness contract.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B84-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B42-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R02","D17-B73-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B84-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B85-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health and Membership","concept":"Failure suspicion versus failure certainty","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"Why is a distributed failure detector generally a suspicion mechanism rather than absolute proof of failure?","answer":"A node that stops responding may be crashed, partitioned, overloaded, delayed, or temporarily unreachable. Without stronger assumptions, absence of a response does not uniquely identify the cause.","explanation":"Distributed systems must often act on incomplete information. Failure detectors therefore provide classifications or suspicions under stated timing assumptions rather than omniscient knowledge.","principle":"A failure signal should be treated according to its documented accuracy and completeness properties.","correct_example":"Use a failure suspicion to trigger bounded recovery actions while allowing protocol mechanisms to correct false suspicions.","invalid_example":"Treat every missed heartbeat as definitive proof that the remote process has permanently failed.","problem":"Transient communication problems trigger irreversible failure actions.","failure_mode":"False failure detection","cause":"Network delay, overload, or partition interpreted as process failure.","solution":"Use thresholds, suspicion states, epochs, and recovery-safe actions.","alternative":"Application-level health signals combined with infrastructure observations.","tradeoff":"Faster detection can increase false positives.","constraint":"Failure-detection guarantees depend on the timing and communication model.","edge_case":"A node is healthy but isolated from the observer.","security_implication":"Attackers may induce connectivity loss to trigger failover or exclusion.","performance_implication":"Aggressive probes increase network and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Inject packet loss, latency, process pauses, and partitions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that false suspicions do not violate safety invariants.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B14-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B42-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B83-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B85-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health and Membership","concept":"Gossip dissemination fanout","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What role does fanout play in gossip-based dissemination?","answer":"Fanout determines how many peers a node contacts when spreading information during a dissemination round or cycle.","explanation":"Higher fanout can accelerate dissemination but increases communication cost. Lower fanout reduces per-node traffic but may require more rounds for broad propagation.","principle":"Gossip dissemination trades convergence speed against communication overhead.","correct_example":"Choose a bounded fanout and measure convergence under the expected membership and failure conditions.","invalid_example":"Assume maximum fanout is always optimal because faster dissemination has no cost.","problem":"Membership or metadata spreads too slowly or consumes excessive bandwidth.","failure_mode":"Poor dissemination efficiency","cause":"Fanout and dissemination cadence are mismatched to workload and cluster size.","solution":"Tune fanout, cadence, and payload size using measured convergence requirements.","alternative":"Hierarchical or centralized dissemination for suitable environments.","tradeoff":"Higher fanout improves propagation speed but increases network traffic.","constraint":"Gossip behavior depends on topology, membership churn, and failure model.","edge_case":"Rapid membership churn occurs while dissemination is in progress.","security_implication":"Unauthenticated gossip can allow forged membership or metadata propagation.","performance_implication":"Network overhead can grow significantly with fanout and cluster size.","testing_guidance":"Measure convergence time and message volume across different fanout values and churn rates.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence within the required bound without exceeding the communication budget.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B48-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B17-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B42-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B85-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Health and Membership","concept":"Membership epoch","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can an epoch or generation number help with membership changes?","answer":"An epoch identifies a particular configuration or authority generation so participants can distinguish current membership decisions from stale decisions associated with an earlier configuration.","explanation":"Distributed messages can arrive after topology changes. Epochs provide metadata for rejecting or fencing stale membership state.","principle":"Topology-dependent decisions should be associated with an identifiable configuration generation when stale messages are possible.","correct_example":"Attach the current membership epoch to ownership or coordination decisions and reject messages from obsolete epochs where required.","invalid_example":"Accept a membership message solely because it contains a valid node identifier.","problem":"Messages from an obsolete configuration modify current state.","failure_mode":"Stale configuration application","cause":"No generation identity or stale-message rejection.","solution":"Use monotonically advancing epochs or configuration versions with explicit acceptance rules.","alternative":"Versioned configuration objects with quorum-based activation.","tradeoff":"Epoch management adds metadata and requires correct advancement semantics.","constraint":"Epoch uniqueness and monotonicity must be maintained within the relevant authority scope.","edge_case":"Two candidates attempt to establish different epochs after a partition.","security_implication":"Epoch forgery can enable stale or unauthorized control if authentication is weak.","performance_implication":"Epoch checks are inexpensive but may require additional state propagation.","testing_guidance":"Deliver delayed messages from older epochs after a membership change.","verification_guidance":"Verify that obsolete configuration messages cannot override the active configuration.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B14-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B35-R03","D17-B83-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B86-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Distributed token bucket state","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What additional problem appears when implementing a token bucket across multiple nodes?","answer":"The nodes must coordinate or partition rate-limit state so that aggregate request allowance matches the intended global policy.","explanation":"Independent local buckets can collectively allow more traffic than a global limit. Shared state introduces consistency, latency, and availability trade-offs.","principle":"A distributed rate limit is a global invariant only if the enforcement mechanism accounts for all relevant enforcement points.","correct_example":"Partition the limit explicitly by tenant or key, or coordinate shared allowance state according to the required accuracy.","invalid_example":"Configure the same global limit independently on every node and assume the cluster-wide rate equals that limit.","problem":"Aggregate traffic exceeds the intended global allowance.","failure_mode":"Distributed rate-limit overshoot","cause":"Independent local enforcement without global coordination or correct partitioning.","solution":"Use centralized counters, coordinated token allocation, bounded local budgets, or an explicitly approximate design.","alternative":"Per-instance limits when aggregate enforcement is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Stronger global accuracy generally costs coordination and availability.","constraint":"The enforcement model must specify whether limits are global, regional, partition-local, or approximate.","edge_case":"A node becomes disconnected from the shared rate-limit state.","security_implication":"Rate-limit bypass can expose authentication or API endpoints to abuse.","performance_implication":"Central coordination can become a latency or throughput bottleneck.","testing_guidance":"Generate traffic across many enforcement nodes and compare aggregate consumption with the configured policy.","verification_guidance":"Verify the measured allowance against the documented error bound or exactness requirement.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B49-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B21-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B72-R02","D17-B72-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B86-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Load Balancing","concept":"Load-aware routing","knowledge_type":"DESIGN_RULE","question":"Why can request count alone be insufficient for distributed load balancing?","answer":"Different requests can consume substantially different CPU, memory, I/O, or downstream capacity, so equal request counts do not necessarily represent equal load.","explanation":"Load balancing policies should use signals relevant to the resource being protected when workload cost is heterogeneous.","principle":"Routing fairness should be defined against the resource constraint that actually limits capacity.","correct_example":"Use weighted routing, observed utilization, queue depth, or workload classes when request costs differ materially.","invalid_example":"Assume ten lightweight requests and ten expensive requests impose equivalent load.","problem":"A nominally balanced request distribution overloads expensive-work instances.","failure_mode":"Resource-imbalanced routing","cause":"Routing policy uses an inadequate load signal.","solution":"Incorporate workload weights or resource-aware signals.","alternative":"Workload classification followed by separate pools.","tradeoff":"More accurate routing requires more telemetry and control-plane complexity.","constraint":"Load signals can themselves be delayed or noisy.","edge_case":"A load signal changes faster than the routing system can react.","security_implication":"Attackers may exploit expensive request classes to concentrate resource usage.","performance_implication":"Better load estimation can reduce tail latency and overload.","testing_guidance":"Mix requests with different resource costs and compare resource utilization under different policies.","verification_guidance":"Verify the selected load metric correlates with the protected resource and meets routing objectives.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B49-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B72-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B80-R01","D17-B72-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B86-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Rate Limiting","concept":"Hierarchical rate limits","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why might a distributed service use multiple levels of rate limits?","answer":"Different limits can protect different resources, such as per-user, per-tenant, per-service, and global capacity.","explanation":"A single limit may allow one dimension of traffic to exhaust another resource. Hierarchical limits express multiple independent constraints.","principle":"Admission control should model the actual resource boundaries that must remain protected.","correct_example":"Apply a tenant limit and a global service limit so that one tenant cannot consume all capacity while aggregate traffic also remains bounded.","invalid_example":"Use only a global limit when individual tenants require isolation guarantees.","problem":"One identity or workload class monopolizes shared capacity.","failure_mode":"Resource starvation across tenants","cause":"Insufficient granularity in admission control.","solution":"Introduce appropriate hierarchical limits and priority policies.","alternative":"Dedicated capacity pools.","tradeoff":"Multiple limits improve isolation but complicate policy and state management.","constraint":"Limit interactions must be deterministic and documented.","edge_case":"A request consumes multiple independently limited resources.","security_implication":"Per-tenant limits can reduce abuse amplification and cross-tenant resource exhaustion.","performance_implication":"More limit checks increase per-request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Generate competing traffic across tenants and verify each limit independently and collectively.","verification_guidance":"Verify that no permitted traffic pattern violates any configured limit invariant.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B86-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B78-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B49-R01","D17-B72-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B86-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B87-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Object Storage","concept":"Object storage consistency boundary","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What distinguishes object storage from a traditional hierarchical filesystem abstraction?","answer":"Object storage commonly exposes objects identified by keys with associated metadata and does not require the same directory and filesystem semantics as a hierarchical filesystem.","explanation":"Distributed object stores can scale independently across large namespaces while exposing object-level operations and storage semantics.","principle":"Applications must rely only on the object-store consistency and metadata semantics explicitly provided by the system.","correct_example":"Treat object keys and metadata as the storage API and use documented conditional or versioned operations where available.","invalid_example":"Assume object storage automatically provides every filesystem operation and consistency property.","problem":"An application relies on unsupported filesystem semantics.","failure_mode":"Semantic mismatch","cause":"Storage abstraction is treated as interchangeable with a filesystem.","solution":"Model the application against the documented object-storage contract.","alternative":"Distributed filesystem when filesystem semantics are required.","tradeoff":"Object storage can simplify large-scale durable storage but may require application-level directory, transaction, or coordination logic.","constraint":"Exact semantics vary by implementation and version.","edge_case":"Concurrent writes target the same object key.","security_implication":"Object authorization must be enforced independently of object naming assumptions.","performance_implication":"Object operations can have different latency and batching characteristics than local filesystem operations.","testing_guidance":"Test concurrent writes, missing objects, metadata changes, and partial failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify application behavior against the exact storage contract rather than assumed filesystem semantics.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B58-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B56-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"MEDIUM","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B87-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Object Storage","concept":"Object versioning","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can object versioning help with distributed storage recovery?","answer":"Object versioning can retain multiple object generations so applications or operators can identify or recover earlier states after overwrites or accidental deletion, subject to the storage system's semantics.","explanation":"Version identifiers create explicit object generations and can support recovery workflows without requiring the application to reconstruct every prior state.","principle":"Recovery is stronger when prior authoritative states remain identifiable and retrievable.","correct_example":"Store version identifiers and use conditional operations or explicit version selection according to the storage contract.","invalid_example":"Assume versioning alone guarantees application-level transaction rollback across multiple objects.","problem":"A single object is recoverable but a multi-object operation remains partially applied.","failure_mode":"Cross-object recovery inconsistency","cause":"Object versioning is mistaken for distributed transaction semantics.","solution":"Combine versioning with application-level transaction or workflow coordination where multi-object atomicity is required.","alternative":"Immutable objects plus manifest versions.","tradeoff":"Version retention improves recovery but increases storage and metadata cost.","constraint":"Retention and deletion policies must preserve required recovery points.","edge_case":"Versions exist but metadata linking a consistent object set is missing.","security_implication":"Old object versions may contain data that has later been restricted or deleted.","performance_implication":"Version proliferation increases storage and listing or metadata overhead.","testing_guidance":"Test overwrite, delete, recovery, retention, and partial multi-object update scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the documented recovery process can identify the intended object generation.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B87-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B60-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B55-R01","D17-B60-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B87-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B87-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Object Storage","concept":"Immutable object and manifest pattern","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"How can immutable objects and manifests support consistent distributed datasets?","answer":"Data objects can be written immutably while a separately versioned manifest identifies the authoritative set and generation of objects belonging to a logical dataset.","explanation":"Separating immutable content from mutable metadata reduces in-place update races and allows readers to select a coherent manifest generation.","principle":"A small authoritative metadata object can define a consistent view over immutable distributed content.","correct_example":"Write new objects, construct a validated manifest, then publish a new manifest generation atomically according to the metadata store's capabilities.","invalid_example":"Modify many existing objects in place and assume readers observe all modifications as one atomic operation.","problem":"Readers observe a mixture of old and new objects.","failure_mode":"Inconsistent dataset snapshot","cause":"Multiple object mutations lack a shared publication boundary.","solution":"Use immutable objects and versioned manifests or another explicit snapshot mechanism.","alternative":"Transactional metadata database with object references.","tradeoff":"Immutable storage simplifies readers but requires additional storage and garbage-collection mechanisms.","constraint":"Manifest publication must itself have a clearly defined consistency contract.","edge_case":"Some referenced objects are unavailable after manifest publication.","security_implication":"Manifest authorization must cover the complete object set it exposes.","performance_implication":"Immutable objects can improve caching and parallel reads.","testing_guidance":"Publish manifests while concurrent readers operate and inject missing-object failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every published manifest references the intended complete object set.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B87-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B16-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B55-R02","D17-B56-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B87-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B88-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Storage Engines","concept":"Log-structured storage","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the core idea of log-structured storage?","answer":"Log-structured storage writes updates sequentially into an append-oriented log and later reorganizes data through mechanisms such as compaction.","explanation":"Sequential writes can simplify write paths and improve locality, while compaction manages obsolete versions and read amplification.","principle":"Append-oriented updates trade simpler sequential writes for additional read and background-reorganization costs.","correct_example":"Append new records and use compaction to consolidate live records and reclaim obsolete data.","invalid_example":"Assume append-only storage requires no mechanism to reclaim obsolete versions.","problem":"Obsolete records accumulate indefinitely.","failure_mode":"Unbounded storage amplification","cause":"Missing or ineffective compaction and retention policy.","solution":"Use compaction, tombstone handling, and retention rules appropriate to the storage model.","alternative":"In-place update storage engines.","tradeoff":"Compaction consumes I/O and CPU but controls long-term storage growth.","constraint":"Compaction must preserve all records required by the consistency and recovery model.","edge_case":"Compaction races with readers or active writers.","security_implication":"Deleted sensitive data may remain in obsolete storage until physical reclamation semantics complete.","performance_implication":"Poor compaction scheduling can create I/O contention and tail-latency spikes.","testing_guidance":"Generate updates and deletes over time and measure read/write amplification and compaction impact.","verification_guidance":"Verify that compaction preserves live state and correctly handles obsolete versions and tombstones.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B55-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B88-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B88-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Storage Engines","concept":"Compaction strategy","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why does compaction strategy matter in a distributed storage engine?","answer":"Compaction determines when and how fragmented or obsolete data is reorganized, affecting write amplification, read amplification, space amplification, and foreground latency.","explanation":"Different compaction strategies shift costs between foreground writes, background work, and reads.","principle":"Compaction should be selected according to workload, latency objectives, storage constraints, and recovery semantics.","correct_example":"Tune compaction scheduling and thresholds based on measured workload characteristics while preserving correctness.","invalid_example":"Choose compaction solely because it minimizes one isolated metric.","problem":"Background compaction consumes excessive resources or allows read amplification to grow too high.","failure_mode":"Compaction-induced performance degradation","cause":"Compaction policy mismatched to workload or capacity.","solution":"Measure amplification and tune scheduling, thresholds, concurrency, and resource isolation.","alternative":"Different compaction strategy or workload-specific storage layout.","tradeoff":"Reducing one amplification dimension can increase another.","constraint":"Actual behavior depends on the storage engine implementation.","edge_case":"Compaction falls behind during sustained write bursts.","security_implication":"Resource exhaustion through compaction amplification can become an availability concern.","performance_implication":"Compaction directly affects I/O utilization and tail latency.","testing_guidance":"Run sustained writes, deletes, and reads while measuring foreground latency and background resource use.","verification_guidance":"Verify measured amplification and latency against explicit system objectives.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B88-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B21-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B88-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B88-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B88-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Storage Engines","concept":"Tombstone retention","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why must distributed compaction preserve tombstones for an appropriate period?","answer":"A deletion marker may need to remain until all relevant replicas or readers can no longer expose the deleted value; removing it too early can allow stale data to reappear.","explanation":"In replicated systems, a delete is often metadata that must propagate and dominate older versions before it can safely be discarded.","principle":"Deletion metadata can be part of correctness state until the system proves older replicas cannot resurrect the deleted value.","correct_example":"Retain tombstones according to the replication and repair guarantees before reclaiming them.","invalid_example":"Delete the tombstone immediately after the local replica removes the object.","problem":"A repaired or stale replica reintroduces previously deleted data.","failure_mode":"Delete resurrection","cause":"Deletion metadata reclaimed before stale replicas were safely excluded or repaired.","solution":"Use a retention rule derived from the replication and repair protocol.","alternative":"Versioned deletion markers with explicit garbage-collection conditions.","tradeoff":"Longer retention consumes storage but reduces resurrection risk.","constraint":"The correct retention period is system-specific and must be derived from actual repair guarantees.","edge_case":"A replica remains offline longer than the expected repair interval.","security_implication":"Premature tombstone removal can resurrect deleted sensitive information.","performance_implication":"Long retention increases storage and compaction overhead.","testing_guidance":"Take a replica offline, perform deletion, compact active replicas, then bring the stale replica back and test repair.","verification_guidance":"Verify that stale replicas cannot reintroduce deleted state after compaction.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B52-R03","D17-B88-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B53-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B51-R03","D17-B59-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B88-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B89-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Distributed trace context propagation","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why must distributed traces propagate context across service boundaries?","answer":"Context propagation allows spans created by different services to be associated with the same logical request or operation.","explanation":"Without propagated correlation context, individual service traces may remain disconnected and cross-service latency or failure analysis becomes harder.","principle":"A distributed operation needs a stable correlation mechanism across every participating boundary.","correct_example":"Propagate a trace or correlation context through supported RPC, messaging, or asynchronous workflow boundaries according to the tracing system's contract.","invalid_example":"Generate a new unrelated trace identifier at every service boundary.","problem":"A single logical operation appears as multiple unrelated traces.","failure_mode":"Broken distributed trace","cause":"Context is dropped, overwritten, or incorrectly propagated.","solution":"Define propagation requirements for synchronous and asynchronous boundaries and test them explicitly.","alternative":"Application-level correlation identifiers.","tradeoff":"Propagation improves observability but adds metadata and processing overhead.","constraint":"Context propagation must respect trust and data-sensitivity boundaries.","edge_case":"A message is processed asynchronously long after the originating request ends.","security_implication":"Trace context may contain metadata that must not cross unauthorized trust boundaries.","performance_implication":"Trace propagation and collection add request metadata and telemetry overhead.","testing_guidance":"Trace requests across multiple services, retries, queues, and asynchronous consumers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the expected spans can be correlated across every supported boundary.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B89-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B33-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B39-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B89-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Distributed trace versus log correlation","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is the difference between distributed tracing and log correlation?","answer":"Distributed tracing models a logical operation as connected spans across components, while log correlation associates independently generated log records using identifiers or contextual metadata.","explanation":"Tracing provides a structured causal or temporal view of an operation, whereas logs provide detailed component-local events that can be correlated with the operation.","principle":"Observability signals should be designed to complement rather than substitute for one another.","correct_example":"Use trace context to connect service spans and include the same correlation context in relevant logs.","invalid_example":"Assume logs alone automatically reveal the complete cross-service causal path.","problem":"Cross-service diagnosis lacks either structural trace context or detailed local evidence.","failure_mode":"Incomplete incident reconstruction","cause":"Observability signals are disconnected or insufficiently detailed.","solution":"Correlate traces, logs, and metrics using consistent identifiers and timestamps.","alternative":"Event-based audit trails for operations requiring stronger evidence semantics.","tradeoff":"More correlated telemetry improves diagnosis but increases collection and storage costs.","constraint":"Correlation identifiers must remain stable enough for the intended diagnostic scope.","edge_case":"A retry creates multiple attempts for one logical operation.","security_implication":"Telemetry correlation must not expose sensitive identifiers unnecessarily.","performance_implication":"High-cardinality correlation metadata can increase telemetry cost.","testing_guidance":"Generate retries, asynchronous operations, and partial failures and verify cross-signal correlation.","verification_guidance":"Verify that an investigator can reconstruct the operation from the documented telemetry set.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B89-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B39-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B89-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B89-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Observability","concept":"Tail latency amplification","knowledge_type":"PERFORMANCE_CASE","question":"Why can a distributed request have high tail latency even when individual services appear fast on average?","answer":"A request composed of multiple sequential or parallel remote operations is affected by the slow tail of those operations, and retries or queueing can amplify the effect.","explanation":"Averages hide the upper-tail behavior that often dominates user-visible latency in distributed call graphs.","principle":"Distributed latency analysis must examine percentiles and call-graph composition rather than averages alone.","correct_example":"Measure service and dependency latency percentiles and identify the critical path contributing to end-to-end tail latency.","invalid_example":"Conclude that end-to-end latency is healthy because every service has a low mean latency.","problem":"End-to-end tail latency violates the service objective despite acceptable averages.","failure_mode":"Tail-latency amplification","cause":"Sequential dependencies, parallel fan-out, queueing, or retries expose high-percentile behavior.","solution":"Reduce critical-path fan-out, control queueing, bound retries, and optimize the slowest critical dependencies.","alternative":"Caching, asynchronous processing, or degraded responses where semantics permit.","tradeoff":"Reducing tail latency may require additional capacity or weaker synchronous coupling.","constraint":"Optimization must preserve correctness and stated latency objectives.","edge_case":"One slow dependency affects only a subset of requests but dominates high percentiles.","security_implication":"Attackers can exploit expensive dependency paths to create resource pressure.","performance_implication":"Tail behavior often drives user-visible latency and capacity requirements.","testing_guidance":"Measure p50, p95, p99, and higher percentiles under realistic fan-out and failure conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify end-to-end latency against explicit percentile objectives under representative load and failures.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B39-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B72-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B86-R02","D17-B71-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B39-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B90-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integration Semantics","concept":"Distributed operation idempotency across retries","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must idempotency extend across the complete retry boundary?","answer":"If any layer can retry an operation, the operation's externally visible effect must remain safe under duplicate attempts or be protected by a durable deduplication mechanism.","explanation":"Retries can occur at clients, gateways, service libraries, queues, or workflow engines. Designing only one layer for duplicate execution is insufficient when another layer can repeat the request.","principle":"Idempotency must be defined at the semantic effect boundary, not merely at one transport layer.","correct_example":"Use a durable idempotency key or naturally idempotent operation semantics at the boundary where the business effect becomes committed.","invalid_example":"Assume transport-level duplicate suppression guarantees that a business operation cannot execute twice.","problem":"Multiple retry layers create duplicate business effects.","failure_mode":"Cross-layer retry duplication","cause":"Idempotency guarantee is narrower than the actual retry topology.","solution":"Identify every retry boundary and enforce idempotency at the effect boundary.","alternative":"Single controlled retry owner with explicit downstream contracts.","tradeoff":"Durable deduplication consumes storage and requires retention policy.","constraint":"Deduplication records must remain available for the entire duplicate-attempt window.","edge_case":"A response is lost after the effect commits and the client retries much later.","security_implication":"Duplicate sensitive operations can cause unauthorized repeated effects if identity and idempotency are weak.","performance_implication":"Durable deduplication adds lookup and persistence overhead.","testing_guidance":"Drop responses after successful effects and force retries from multiple layers.","verification_guidance":"Verify that one logical operation produces the intended effect count across all retry paths.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B18-R01","D17-B19-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B40-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B79-R01","D17-B79-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B90-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integration Semantics","concept":"Partial success in distributed workflows","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why must distributed workflows explicitly model partial success?","answer":"Multiple independent operations can commit different outcomes before communication failure or process failure prevents the workflow from completing uniformly.","explanation":"A distributed workflow does not automatically become atomic merely because its steps belong to one logical request.","principle":"Every multi-step distributed workflow needs an explicit partial-success and recovery model.","correct_example":"Record completed steps and use compensation, retry, reconciliation, or explicit operator intervention according to the workflow's semantics.","invalid_example":"Assume a timeout means that all previously attempted distributed steps were rolled back.","problem":"The coordinator cannot determine which remote effects committed.","failure_mode":"Unknown partial completion","cause":"Distributed commit state is not fully observable at failure time.","solution":"Use durable workflow state, idempotent operations, reconciliation, and compensating actions where appropriate.","alternative":"Distributed transaction protocol when atomicity is genuinely required and feasible.","tradeoff":"Compensation can provide practical recovery but does not always recreate true atomicity.","constraint":"Some side effects cannot be safely compensated.","edge_case":"A downstream operation commits just before the coordinator records its completion.","security_implication":"Partial authorization or provisioning actions can leave inconsistent access state.","performance_implication":"Reconciliation and compensation consume additional resources after failures.","testing_guidance":"Inject failures between every workflow step and after remote commit but before acknowledgment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every failure point has a defined observable and recoverable outcome.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B25-R01","D17-B26-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B90-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B79-R02","D17-B60-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B25-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B90-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integration Semantics","concept":"Distributed invariant under partial failure","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a distributed invariant be evaluated when some components are unavailable?","answer":"The invariant should be evaluated against all reachable and durable evidence while explicitly accounting for unknown or delayed state rather than treating missing observations as proof of a desired state.","explanation":"Partial failure creates incomplete information. A correct verifier must distinguish confirmed state, absence of evidence, and contradictory evidence.","principle":"Unknown distributed state must not be silently converted into a successful verification result.","correct_example":"Classify the invariant as satisfied, violated, or indeterminate according to the available evidence and verification contract.","invalid_example":"Declare the invariant satisfied because no reachable node currently reports a violation.","problem":"Unavailable components hide possible violations.","failure_mode":"False verification under partial observability","cause":"Missing state is interpreted as negative evidence.","solution":"Use explicit UNKNOWN or INDETERMINATE verification outcomes and require sufficient evidence for a positive claim.","alternative":"Quorum-based verification when the invariant supports quorum reasoning.","tradeoff":"Conservative verification can reduce availability of automated decisions.","constraint":"The required evidence threshold must be defined by the invariant's semantics.","edge_case":"A required evidence source is partitioned while reachable nodes report a healthy state.","security_implication":"False verification can approve unsafe or unauthorized distributed actions.","performance_implication":"Obtaining sufficient evidence may increase verification latency.","testing_guidance":"Partition evidence sources and attempt verification under incomplete observations.","verification_guidance":"Verify that unavailable evidence cannot be silently interpreted as successful proof.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B20-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B90-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B43-R01","D17-B60-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B90-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B20-R01"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

{"record_id":"D17-B91-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Mutual TLS in distributed systems","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"What does mutual TLS provide between distributed services?","answer":"Mutual TLS can provide encrypted transport and mutual cryptographic authentication between communicating endpoints when both sides validate the peer certificate according to the configured trust policy.","explanation":"Unlike one-sided TLS authentication, mutual TLS requires both communicating parties to authenticate themselves to each other. Authorization still remains a separate policy decision.","principle":"Transport authentication is not equivalent to application authorization.","correct_example":"Authenticate service identities using mutual TLS and separately authorize the requested operation according to identity and policy.","invalid_example":"Assume that possession of any certificate automatically grants permission to every service operation.","problem":"Authentication and authorization boundaries are conflated.","failure_mode":"Over-privileged authenticated service","cause":"Certificate identity is treated as unrestricted authorization.","solution":"Map authenticated identities to explicit authorization policies.","alternative":"Application-layer authentication combined with encrypted transport.","tradeoff":"Mutual TLS improves service identity assurance but adds certificate lifecycle and trust-management complexity.","constraint":"Certificate validation, rotation, revocation, and trust roots must be operationally maintained.","edge_case":"A service certificate expires while existing connections remain active.","security_implication":"Incorrect trust configuration can permit impersonation or unauthorized service access.","performance_implication":"Handshake and cryptographic processing consume CPU and can affect connection establishment latency.","testing_guidance":"Test valid, expired, revoked, wrong-identity, and untrusted certificates.","verification_guidance":"Verify both peer authentication and independent authorization behavior.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B84-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B91-R02","D17-B91-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B91-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Distributed trust boundary","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why must trust boundaries be explicit between distributed services?","answer":"Every network boundary can expose a service to untrusted or differently trusted inputs, even when the services belong to the same organization or cluster.","explanation":"Network location does not establish trust. Service identities, authorization policies, validation, and least privilege must define what each boundary permits.","principle":"Internal network placement must not be treated as implicit authorization.","correct_example":"Authenticate the caller, authorize the requested operation, validate inputs, and restrict service privileges at each relevant boundary.","invalid_example":"Trust every request arriving from an internal subnet.","problem":"A compromised internal service can access unrelated capabilities.","failure_mode":"Lateral privilege expansion","cause":"Implicit network-based trust.","solution":"Use explicit service identity, authorization, least privilege, and boundary validation.","alternative":"Zero-trust service-to-service authorization architecture.","tradeoff":"Explicit authorization increases policy and operational complexity.","constraint":"Trust policies must remain synchronized with service ownership and deployment changes.","edge_case":"A temporary debugging service is deployed inside the trusted network.","security_implication":"Internal compromise can become organization-wide lateral movement if boundaries are weak.","performance_implication":"Authentication and authorization checks add small per-request overhead.","testing_guidance":"Attempt unauthorized service-to-service operations from authenticated but insufficiently privileged identities.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every protected operation has an explicit authorization decision.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B91-R01"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B91-R03","D17-B86-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B91-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B91-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Security","concept":"Service identity rotation","knowledge_type":"SECURITY_CASE","question":"Why is identity rotation important in distributed systems?","answer":"Rotating service credentials limits the lifetime of compromised credentials and allows trust relationships to evolve without requiring permanent secrets or certificates.","explanation":"Distributed systems often have many long-lived identities and connections. Rotation must therefore be designed as a normal lifecycle operation rather than an emergency-only procedure.","principle":"Credential rotation must be safe, observable, and compatible with overlapping deployment generations.","correct_example":"Introduce a new credential, distribute trust for it, transition clients, then retire the previous credential after confirming migration.","invalid_example":"Replace every service credential simultaneously without providing a compatibility window.","problem":"Credential rotation causes widespread connection failures.","failure_mode":"Rotation-induced outage","cause":"No overlap or coordinated transition strategy.","solution":"Use staged rotation and explicit old/new credential compatibility windows.","alternative":"Short-lived automatically issued identities.","tradeoff":"Overlapping credentials simplify migration but temporarily increase the number of valid credentials.","constraint":"Retired credentials must eventually become unusable.","edge_case":"A disconnected region continues using an older credential during rotation.","security_implication":"Long-lived credentials increase the impact window of compromise.","performance_implication":"Frequent cryptographic identity operations can increase control-plane activity.","testing_guidance":"Perform rotation during normal traffic and simulate delayed or disconnected clients.","verification_guidance":"Verify both uninterrupted authorized communication and eventual rejection of retired credentials.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B91-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B91-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B85-R03","D17-B56-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B91-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B92-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Byzantine Fault Tolerance","concept":"Byzantine fault model","knowledge_type":"DEFINITION","question":"What is a Byzantine fault in a distributed system?","answer":"A Byzantine fault is a failure in which a participant can behave arbitrarily, including sending inconsistent, incorrect, or malicious information to different participants.","explanation":"The Byzantine model is stronger than crash or omission failure models because faulty participants are not assumed to follow the protocol.","principle":"A protocol's fault tolerance guarantee is meaningful only relative to its stated failure model.","correct_example":"Analyze a consensus protocol under explicit assumptions about the maximum number and placement of Byzantine participants.","invalid_example":"Assume a crash-fault-tolerant protocol automatically tolerates arbitrary malicious behavior.","problem":"A system claims Byzantine resilience without satisfying Byzantine-specific assumptions.","failure_mode":"Failure-model mismatch","cause":"Protocol guarantees are applied outside their stated model.","solution":"Explicitly define the failure model and select a protocol whose guarantees match it.","alternative":"Crash-fault-tolerant consensus when Byzantine behavior is outside scope.","tradeoff":"Byzantine tolerance generally requires stronger protocols, communication, identity, and quorum assumptions.","constraint":"Exact fault tolerance depends on the protocol and system assumptions.","edge_case":"A compromised participant behaves correctly most of the time but equivocates during critical decisions.","security_implication":"Byzantine participants may actively attempt to violate safety or manipulate state.","performance_implication":"Additional validation and communication can increase latency and bandwidth.","testing_guidance":"Use controlled fault injection to model equivocation and contradictory messages.","verification_guidance":"Verify safety claims against the precise Byzantine assumptions of the protocol.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B22-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B92-R02","D17-B92-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":["D17-B92-R02"],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B92-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Byzantine Fault Tolerance","concept":"Crash fault versus Byzantine fault","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"How does Byzantine failure differ from crash failure?","answer":"A crashed participant stops making progress, while a Byzantine participant may continue communicating while violating protocol rules or presenting different information to different peers.","explanation":"Crash-fault protocols can often rely on non-malicious participant behavior. Byzantine protocols must additionally defend against arbitrary behavior.","principle":"Fault assumptions determine which safety and liveness mechanisms are necessary.","correct_example":"Use crash-fault consensus when participants are trusted to follow the protocol and Byzantine consensus when arbitrary participant behavior must be tolerated.","invalid_example":"Treat silence and malicious contradictory responses as equivalent failure conditions.","problem":"The selected fault model does not cover the actual threat model.","failure_mode":"Insufficient fault tolerance","cause":"Incorrect failure-model selection.","solution":"Document the threat and failure model before selecting the coordination protocol.","alternative":"Reduce the trusted computing base or isolate untrusted participants.","tradeoff":"Stronger fault tolerance increases system complexity and resource requirements.","constraint":"The actual deployment threat model must match the protocol assumptions.","edge_case":"A participant alternates between correct and Byzantine behavior.","security_implication":"Byzantine behavior can directly target safety properties.","performance_implication":"Byzantine detection and agreement can require additional communication.","testing_guidance":"Test crash, omission, delay, equivocation, and malicious-message scenarios separately.","verification_guidance":"Verify that the protocol's documented guarantee covers the injected failure type.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B92-R01"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B22-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B92-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B92-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Byzantine Fault Tolerance","concept":"Quorum intersection under Byzantine behavior","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why must Byzantine quorum systems use stronger intersection properties than ordinary crash-fault quorum systems?","answer":"A Byzantine participant can provide conflicting information, so quorum intersection must provide enough honest overlap to prevent conflicting decisions under the assumed fault bound.","explanation":"Ordinary quorum intersection may ensure a shared participant, but that participant may itself be faulty under a Byzantine model.","principle":"Safety requires sufficient honest intersection, not merely numerical intersection.","correct_example":"Use the quorum and fault thresholds specified by the chosen Byzantine consensus protocol.","invalid_example":"Reuse a crash-fault quorum threshold without checking whether it leaves enough honest overlap.","problem":"Conflicting decisions become possible despite quorum overlap.","failure_mode":"Byzantine safety violation","cause":"Insufficient honest quorum intersection.","solution":"Use protocol-specific Byzantine quorum thresholds and authenticated participant identities.","alternative":"Reduce Byzantine exposure through trusted components or smaller fault domains.","tradeoff":"Larger quorums can improve safety margins but reduce availability.","constraint":"Thresholds depend on the protocol's precise assumptions.","edge_case":"Network partitions prevent the required honest quorum from forming.","security_implication":"Weak quorum thresholds can allow conflicting or forged decisions.","performance_implication":"Larger quorums increase communication and coordination cost.","testing_guidance":"Inject equivocation and compare decision outcomes across overlapping quorums.","verification_guidance":"Prove or formally validate the quorum intersection property under the stated fault bound.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B92-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B21-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B31-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B92-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B93-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Formal Verification","concept":"Distributed protocol invariant","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What is an invariant in distributed protocol verification?","answer":"An invariant is a property that must remain true across every reachable protocol state or every state within the defined verification model.","explanation":"Invariants provide a way to reason about safety independently of a particular execution trace.","principle":"A distributed safety property should be expressed as a state property that survives every permitted transition.","correct_example":"Specify that two distinct leaders cannot both be authoritative for the same term and prove the property across allowed transitions.","invalid_example":"Verify only one successful execution and infer that the invariant always holds.","problem":"Safety is inferred from examples rather than all relevant state transitions.","failure_mode":"Insufficient verification coverage","cause":"Testing is mistaken for exhaustive reasoning.","solution":"Define explicit invariants and use model checking, proof, or systematic state exploration where feasible.","alternative":"Property-based and fault-injection testing when exhaustive verification is impractical.","tradeoff":"Formal verification can provide stronger assurance but may require abstraction and model construction.","constraint":"A proof applies only to the modeled system and assumptions.","edge_case":"The implementation contains behavior absent from the formal model.","security_implication":"Unverified invariants can hide safety vulnerabilities.","performance_implication":"Formal state exploration can become computationally expensive.","testing_guidance":"Combine invariant checks with adversarial transition sequences.","verification_guidance":"Record the model scope, assumptions, proof or exploration method, and uncovered implementation behavior.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B90-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B20-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B93-R02","D17-B93-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B20-R01"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B93-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Formal Verification","concept":"State-space explosion","knowledge_type":"LIMITATION","question":"Why is exhaustive model checking difficult for distributed systems?","answer":"The number of possible states and interleavings can grow rapidly with additional processes, messages, failures, and state variables.","explanation":"Concurrency creates many possible execution orders. Formal models therefore often require abstraction, symmetry reduction, bounded exploration, or decomposition.","principle":"Verification scope must be explicit when exhaustive exploration is not computationally feasible.","correct_example":"Use a bounded or abstract model and clearly record which implementation behaviors and state sizes it represents.","invalid_example":"Claim exhaustive verification of an unbounded distributed implementation from a small finite test model.","problem":"The verification result is generalized beyond the model's scope.","failure_mode":"Verification overclaim","cause":"Model limitations are omitted from the evidence claim.","solution":"Record bounds, abstractions, assumptions, and unverified behaviors.","alternative":"Compositional verification or targeted invariant proofs.","tradeoff":"More abstraction improves tractability but can omit implementation-specific defects.","constraint":"A verification result cannot exceed the coverage of its model.","edge_case":"A rare failure requires more nodes or message states than the model permits.","security_implication":"Security claims based on bounded models may miss larger-scale attack states.","performance_implication":"State-space size can make verification itself resource intensive.","testing_guidance":"Compare model predictions with implementation fault-injection scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Report exact model bounds and determine whether omitted states can affect the claimed property.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B93-R01"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B93-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B93-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Formal Verification","concept":"Implementation-model refinement","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why must a formal model be related to the implementation it represents?","answer":"A verified model provides useful evidence for an implementation only when the implementation's relevant behavior is shown to correspond to the modeled states and transitions.","explanation":"A correct proof about an inaccurate abstraction does not automatically prove correctness of the implementation.","principle":"Formal assurance requires an explicit relationship between specification, model, and implementation.","correct_example":"Define a mapping between implementation states and modeled states and verify that relevant implementation transitions preserve the model assumptions.","invalid_example":"Prove a protocol model correct and claim the production implementation is therefore verified without checking correspondence.","problem":"The verified model diverges from implementation behavior.","failure_mode":"Model-to-code gap","cause":"No refinement or correspondence argument.","solution":"Use refinement proofs, conformance tests, generated implementations, or systematic mapping validation.","alternative":"Property-based testing against the formal model.","tradeoff":"Maintaining correspondence adds engineering effort but strengthens assurance.","constraint":"Only behavior covered by the correspondence argument is supported by the verification claim.","edge_case":"Implementation-specific timeout or recovery behavior is absent from the model.","security_implication":"Security-critical implementation deviations can invalidate formal safety claims.","performance_implication":"Refinement validation can require additional testing or formal machinery.","testing_guidance":"Generate model traces and compare implementation responses under equivalent inputs.","verification_guidance":"Verify that implementation transitions satisfy the model's assumptions and properties.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B93-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B93-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B93-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B94-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Container Orchestration","concept":"Control plane versus data plane","knowledge_type":"DISTINCTION","question":"What is the difference between the control plane and data plane in distributed infrastructure?","answer":"The control plane manages desired state, membership, scheduling, configuration, and coordination, while the data plane performs the workload's actual traffic or data processing.","explanation":"Separating management from workload execution helps isolate operational control mechanisms from application traffic.","principle":"Control-plane failure and data-plane failure should be modeled separately because their consequences and recovery paths differ.","correct_example":"Continue serving existing traffic during a temporary control-plane outage when the workload can safely operate from already applied configuration.","invalid_example":"Assume that every control-plane outage immediately means all existing data-plane work must stop.","problem":"Operational dependencies unnecessarily couple workload availability to management availability.","failure_mode":"Control-plane dependency amplification","cause":"Data-plane operation requires continuous control-plane availability without necessity.","solution":"Define which data-plane functions can continue with cached or local state.","alternative":"Tightly coupled control and execution when dynamic coordination is a correctness requirement.","tradeoff":"Reduced control-plane dependency can improve availability but makes local state and reconciliation more complex.","constraint":"Some operations genuinely require current control-plane decisions.","edge_case":"The control plane recovers with state that differs from data-plane reality.","security_implication":"Control-plane compromise can affect large numbers of workloads.","performance_implication":"Control-plane polling or reconciliation generates background load.","testing_guidance":"Partition control-plane connectivity while existing workloads continue operating.","verification_guidance":"Verify documented degraded-mode behavior and eventual reconciliation after control-plane recovery.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B85-R03"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B94-R02","D17-B94-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B94-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Container Orchestration","concept":"Reconciliation loop","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What is the purpose of a reconciliation loop in distributed orchestration?","answer":"A reconciliation loop repeatedly compares observed state with desired state and performs actions intended to reduce the difference between them.","explanation":"Distributed state can change because of failures, manual intervention, or concurrent controllers. Reconciliation makes correction an ongoing process rather than a one-time command.","principle":"Desired-state systems require repeatable convergence logic because distributed state can diverge after any action.","correct_example":"Observe actual workload state, calculate the difference from desired state, apply safe corrective actions, and observe again.","invalid_example":"Assume a successful deployment command proves that desired and actual state will remain equal indefinitely.","problem":"Actual state drifts after the initial operation.","failure_mode":"Configuration drift","cause":"No continuous observation and reconciliation.","solution":"Use idempotent reconciliation with explicit convergence and conflict rules.","alternative":"Imperative orchestration with external drift detection.","tradeoff":"Continuous reconciliation improves resilience but adds control-plane processing and potential action churn.","constraint":"Reconciliation must be safe when actions are repeated or partially completed.","edge_case":"Two controllers attempt to reconcile overlapping portions of the same state.","security_implication":"A compromised controller can continuously reapply unauthorized state.","performance_implication":"Frequent reconciliation increases API, storage, and control-plane load.","testing_guidance":"Inject drift, partial failures, controller restarts, and conflicting updates.","verification_guidance":"Verify eventual convergence without violating safety constraints or creating action loops.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B94-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B18-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B90-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B94-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B94-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Container Orchestration","concept":"Controller action idempotency","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why should distributed controllers make reconciliation actions idempotent where possible?","answer":"Controllers can retry actions after timeouts or restarts because they may not know whether a previous attempt succeeded. Idempotent actions allow repeated reconciliation without multiplying side effects.","explanation":"A lost acknowledgment creates uncertainty about whether an action committed. Idempotent operations turn repeated attempts into safe convergence steps.","principle":"Control-plane retries should converge toward desired state rather than accumulate duplicate effects.","correct_example":"Use create-if-absent, compare-and-update, or state-reconciliation operations whose repeated execution yields the same desired state.","invalid_example":"Trigger a non-idempotent side effect every reconciliation cycle without durable completion tracking.","problem":"Controller retries create duplicate effects.","failure_mode":"Reconciliation amplification","cause":"Action semantics are not safe under retry.","solution":"Make actions idempotent or persist durable operation identity and completion state.","alternative":"Single-shot workflows with explicit transactional state.","tradeoff":"Idempotency may require additional state or conditional operations.","constraint":"Irreversible external side effects may not be naturally idempotent.","edge_case":"The controller crashes after the side effect but before recording completion.","security_implication":"Repeated privileged actions can cause unintended security changes.","performance_implication":"Idempotency checks add metadata or state lookups.","testing_guidance":"Crash the controller between external effect and local acknowledgment.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery does not produce duplicate externally visible effects.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B94-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B90-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B79-R01","D17-B79-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B94-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B95-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Mesh","concept":"Sidecar versus library-based distributed policy","knowledge_type":"COMPARISON","question":"What is a key architectural distinction between sidecar-based and library-based service communication policy?","answer":"A sidecar can move communication concerns outside the application process, while a library embeds those concerns directly into application code or runtime dependencies.","explanation":"The placement changes deployment, upgrade, observability, failure, and resource-isolation characteristics.","principle":"Infrastructure policy placement should be evaluated against operational isolation, application control, and failure behavior.","correct_example":"Use a sidecar when uniform transport and routing policy across heterogeneous applications is more important than minimizing per-workload proxy overhead.","invalid_example":"Assume moving networking policy to a sidecar removes all failure modes associated with the networking layer.","problem":"Operational overhead or proxy failures become hidden from application architecture.","failure_mode":"Infrastructure abstraction mismatch","cause":"Policy location is selected without considering its new failure boundary.","solution":"Model proxy, application, and control-plane failure modes explicitly.","alternative":"Language-specific communication libraries.","tradeoff":"Sidecars can standardize policy but add processes, hops, and resource consumption.","constraint":"The service mesh design must define behavior when control-plane or proxy components are unavailable.","edge_case":"Application remains healthy while its proxy is unavailable.","security_implication":"The proxy becomes a security-critical enforcement point.","performance_implication":"Additional hops can affect latency and CPU utilization.","testing_guidance":"Test application, proxy, and control-plane failures independently.","verification_guidance":"Verify that security and routing guarantees remain valid during mesh component failure.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B94-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B91-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B95-R02","D17-B95-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B95-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Mesh","concept":"Data-plane proxy failure","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What can happen when a service's data-plane proxy fails?","answer":"Requests may become unavailable even when the application process itself remains healthy, depending on how traffic is routed through the proxy.","explanation":"A service mesh introduces additional distributed components into the request path. Their availability becomes part of end-to-end availability.","principle":"Every additional network hop is an additional failure boundary that must be included in reliability analysis.","correct_example":"Define behavior for proxy restart, temporary unavailability, stale configuration, and control-plane disconnection.","invalid_example":"Assume proxy failure is irrelevant because the application process remains running.","problem":"Application health indicators disagree with actual request availability.","failure_mode":"Proxy-induced service outage","cause":"Proxy availability is excluded from the service dependency model.","solution":"Include proxies in health, capacity, and failure testing.","alternative":"Direct application networking where proxy functionality is unnecessary.","tradeoff":"Removing the proxy reduces one failure boundary but may sacrifice centralized communication features.","constraint":"Actual behavior depends on deployment architecture.","edge_case":"The proxy is healthy but has stale routing or authorization configuration.","security_implication":"A failed or compromised proxy can affect traffic authorization and confidentiality.","performance_implication":"Proxy CPU, memory, and queueing affect request latency.","testing_guidance":"Kill, restart, overload, and isolate proxies while measuring application availability.","verification_guidance":"Verify that documented service-level availability assumptions include proxy behavior.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B95-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B73-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B95-R03","D17-B89-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B95-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B95-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Service Mesh","concept":"Control-plane propagation delay","knowledge_type":"EDGE_CASE","question":"Why can a service mesh temporarily enforce stale policy?","answer":"Policy changes must propagate from management components to data-plane components, so there can be an interval during which different proxies use different policy generations.","explanation":"Distributed configuration propagation is subject to communication delay, retries, partitions, and proxy restarts.","principle":"Configuration changes require an explicit propagation and consistency model.","correct_example":"Version policies, expose propagation status, and define behavior while different data-plane instances hold different generations.","invalid_example":"Assume a control-plane update becomes simultaneously active on every proxy.","problem":"Operators expect immediate cluster-wide policy consistency.","failure_mode":"Stale policy enforcement","cause":"Propagation delay or failed configuration delivery.","solution":"Use versioned configuration, convergence monitoring, and safe transition policies.","alternative":"Centralized enforcement for decisions requiring stronger immediate consistency.","tradeoff":"Faster global consistency generally requires more coordination.","constraint":"The actual convergence bound depends on topology and control-plane health.","edge_case":"A disconnected proxy continues enforcing an older authorization policy.","security_implication":"Stale security policy can temporarily permit or deny actions incorrectly.","performance_implication":"Frequent configuration pushes increase control-plane traffic.","testing_guidance":"Partition proxies from the control plane and measure policy convergence after reconnection.","verification_guidance":"Verify policy generation and convergence status before making strong consistency claims.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B95-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B85-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B94-R02","D17-B91-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B95-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B96-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Cross-region failover state","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"What state must be considered during cross-region failover?","answer":"Failover must account for replicated data freshness, in-flight operations, configuration generations, identity state, and traffic-routing state rather than only whether a secondary region is running.","explanation":"A functioning standby does not necessarily contain the latest authoritative state or safe-to-serve configuration.","principle":"Failover correctness depends on state convergence, not merely process availability.","correct_example":"Establish replication freshness, routing readiness, and application recovery criteria before directing traffic to the secondary region.","invalid_example":"Send all traffic to a running secondary region immediately after the primary becomes unreachable.","problem":"Traffic reaches a region with stale or incomplete state.","failure_mode":"Unsafe regional failover","cause":"Failover checks process availability but not state readiness.","solution":"Define explicit failover readiness criteria and verify replicated state.","alternative":"Active-active operation with continuous conflict handling.","tradeoff":"Stricter failover validation can increase recovery time while reducing stale-state risk.","constraint":"Recovery objectives determine acceptable data loss and recovery delay.","edge_case":"The primary returns while the secondary is serving traffic.","security_implication":"Incorrect failover can expose inconsistent authorization or identity state.","performance_implication":"Cross-region state verification can increase failover latency.","testing_guidance":"Simulate region loss, delayed replication, and primary recovery.","verification_guidance":"Verify traffic is routed only after documented data and control-plane readiness conditions are met.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B56-R01","D17-B60-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B51-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B96-R02","D17-B96-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B96-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Split-brain regional failover","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"What is regional split-brain?","answer":"Regional split-brain occurs when multiple regions independently believe they are authoritative or active for the same state after connectivity or coordination failure.","explanation":"Without authoritative ownership or fencing, independent failover decisions can create conflicting writes and routing.","principle":"Multi-region failover requires a clear authority model that prevents simultaneous incompatible ownership.","correct_example":"Use an explicit authority or fencing mechanism before allowing a region to perform operations that require exclusive ownership.","invalid_example":"Allow every isolated region to promote itself independently without conflict protection.","problem":"Two regions accept conflicting authoritative writes.","failure_mode":"Regional split-brain","cause":"Independent promotion without a shared authority decision.","solution":"Use fencing, quorum-based promotion, or application semantics that explicitly tolerate concurrent ownership.","alternative":"Active-active conflict resolution where concurrent authority is intentional.","tradeoff":"Exclusive authority can reduce availability during partitions; active-active designs require conflict handling.","constraint":"The selected model must match the application's consistency requirements.","edge_case":"A previously isolated primary returns after another region has been promoted.","security_implication":"Split-brain can bypass intended authorization or data ownership boundaries.","performance_implication":"Conflict detection and reconciliation can consume substantial resources.","testing_guidance":"Partition regions and attempt concurrent promotion and writes.","verification_guidance":"Verify that at most the permitted authorities can perform authoritative operations under partition.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B96-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B13-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B56-R03","D17-B83-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B96-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B96-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Geo-Distribution","concept":"Data residency boundary","knowledge_type":"CONSTRAINT","question":"Why can data residency requirements affect distributed-system architecture?","answer":"Data residency constraints can limit where particular data may be stored, processed, replicated, cached, logged, or backed up.","explanation":"Replication and observability systems can unintentionally copy data across geographic or legal boundaries unless placement rules are explicit.","principle":"Data location is an architectural constraint when policy or regulatory requirements restrict geographic processing or storage.","correct_example":"Classify data and constrain storage, replication, backup, and telemetry destinations according to the applicable residency policy.","invalid_example":"Replicate all data to every region without checking residency requirements.","problem":"Data is copied into a prohibited region or processing environment.","failure_mode":"Residency violation","cause":"Replication topology ignores data classification and geographic constraints.","solution":"Apply data-aware placement and replication policies.","alternative":"Regional data partitions with controlled cross-region metadata.","tradeoff":"Residency controls can reduce replication flexibility and increase regional operational complexity.","constraint":"Actual requirements depend on applicable policy, contract, and jurisdiction.","edge_case":"Telemetry or backups contain the same restricted data as the primary store.","security_implication":"Unauthorized geographic copies increase exposure surface.","performance_implication":"Regional isolation can increase latency for cross-region operations.","testing_guidance":"Trace sensitive data through primary storage, replicas, caches, logs, backups, and telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Verify every data-bearing path against the declared residency policy.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B57-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B91-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B96-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B97-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Checkpoint consistency","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What makes a distributed stream-processing checkpoint useful for recovery?","answer":"A checkpoint must capture sufficient consistent operator state and progress information so that recovery can resume without incorrectly losing or duplicating data beyond the system's processing guarantees.","explanation":"State and input progress are coupled. Recording one without the other can cause incorrect replay or state divergence.","principle":"A recovery checkpoint must define a consistent boundary between durable state and processed input.","correct_example":"Persist operator state together with the corresponding input-progress information according to the stream engine's checkpoint protocol.","invalid_example":"Save operator memory independently of the input offsets it represents.","problem":"Recovery resumes with mismatched state and input position.","failure_mode":"Checkpoint inconsistency","cause":"State and progress are persisted at incompatible logical points.","solution":"Use coordinated checkpoint semantics and verify state/progress alignment.","alternative":"Deterministic replay from an earlier durable state.","tradeoff":"Frequent checkpoints reduce replay work but increase I/O and coordination overhead.","constraint":"Checkpoint semantics depend on source, sink, state-store, and processing guarantees.","edge_case":"Checkpoint completion succeeds for some operators but not others.","security_implication":"Incomplete recovery state can expose or duplicate sensitive processing results.","performance_implication":"Checkpoint frequency directly affects storage and runtime overhead.","testing_guidance":"Interrupt processing during checkpoint creation and recover from partial checkpoint states.","verification_guidance":"Verify that recovery restores a valid state/progress boundary.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B77-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B16-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B97-R02","D17-B97-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B97-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Replay after checkpoint recovery","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why can stream processing replay records after recovery?","answer":"If the durable input position lags behind the last state update, recovery may intentionally reread records so state can be reconstructed consistently.","explanation":"Replay is often part of fault recovery and is safe only when processing and sink semantics correctly handle repeated input.","principle":"Replay correctness depends on state recovery and side-effect idempotency or transactional coordination.","correct_example":"Restore checkpointed state and replay input from the recorded durable position while preserving the documented processing guarantee.","invalid_example":"Assume a restarted stream processor can continue exactly from its in-memory position after a crash.","problem":"Recovery either loses records or produces unintended duplicate effects.","failure_mode":"Incorrect replay boundary","cause":"Mismatch between durable state and durable input progress.","solution":"Use coordinated checkpoints, transactional output, or idempotent sinks.","alternative":"Externalized state with independently durable offsets.","tradeoff":"Stronger replay guarantees require additional coordination or storage.","constraint":"Exactly-once effects require stronger conditions than merely replaying records once.","edge_case":"The output commit succeeds but the input-progress commit does not.","security_implication":"Replay can repeat externally visible security-sensitive actions if sinks are not idempotent.","performance_implication":"Large replay windows increase recovery time.","testing_guidance":"Crash at every boundary between state update, output commit, and offset persistence.","verification_guidance":"Verify the observed recovery behavior against the declared delivery and processing guarantee.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B97-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B40-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B40-R02","D17-B97-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B97-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B97-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Stream Processing","concept":"Replay determinism","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why is deterministic processing important when state is reconstructed by replay?","answer":"If replaying the same input can produce different state because of uncontrolled nondeterminism, recovery may not reproduce the prior logical state.","explanation":"Sources of nondeterminism can include wall-clock reads, random values, external mutable state, unordered iteration, and race-dependent behavior.","principle":"Replay-based recovery requires deterministic or explicitly controlled sources of nondeterminism.","correct_example":"Record required nondeterministic inputs or derive processing from deterministic event data and controlled time semantics.","invalid_example":"Assume replay produces identical state even when processing reads the current wall clock during each event.","problem":"Recovered state differs from the original state despite identical input records.","failure_mode":"Non-deterministic recovery divergence","cause":"Uncontrolled nondeterministic inputs.","solution":"Capture nondeterministic inputs or redesign state transitions to be deterministic.","alternative":"Snapshot-based recovery that does not require full deterministic replay.","tradeoff":"Capturing additional inputs increases state and storage requirements.","constraint":"Determinism must be defined relative to the recovery guarantee being claimed.","edge_case":"An external dependency changes between original processing and replay.","security_implication":"Replay divergence can repeat or omit security-sensitive decisions.","performance_implication":"Recording deterministic context increases storage and processing overhead.","testing_guidance":"Replay identical inputs multiple times under varied timing and external conditions.","verification_guidance":"Verify that all state-affecting nondeterministic inputs are controlled or persisted.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B97-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B16-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B15-R02","D17-B97-R01"],"depends_on":["D17-B97-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B16-R03"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B98-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery dependency graph","knowledge_type":"PATTERN","question":"Why should disaster recovery plans model dependencies rather than individual services only?","answer":"A service may depend on databases, identity systems, configuration stores, queues, DNS, certificates, external providers, and other infrastructure that must also be recoverable.","explanation":"Recovering application processes without their required dependencies can produce a technically running but unusable system.","principle":"Recovery readiness is a property of the dependency graph, not merely of individual components.","correct_example":"Map critical service dependencies and define recovery ordering, readiness criteria, and fallback behavior.","invalid_example":"Declare a service recovered because its application containers are running.","problem":"Recovered applications cannot reach required dependencies.","failure_mode":"Dependency recovery failure","cause":"Recovery plan ignores dependency ordering or availability.","solution":"Model the dependency graph and test end-to-end recovery.","alternative":"Reduce dependencies through architectural isolation or local fallback.","tradeoff":"Dependency-aware recovery planning increases operational complexity but improves realism.","constraint":"External dependencies may not be fully controllable by the recovery team.","edge_case":"A dependency is technically reachable but has stale state after recovery.","security_implication":"Identity and key-management dependencies must recover safely before protected operations resume.","performance_implication":"Dependency warm-up can dominate recovery time.","testing_guidance":"Perform end-to-end recovery while selectively withholding dependencies.","verification_guidance":"Verify service-level recovery rather than process-level startup only.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","dependencies":[],"prerequisites":["D17-B60-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B96-R01"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B98-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery ordering","knowledge_type":"RULE","question":"Why can recovery ordering matter in distributed systems?","answer":"Some components must become authoritative, consistent, or available before dependent components can safely resume operations.","explanation":"Starting all services simultaneously can cause stale reads, invalid writes, retry storms, or incorrect initialization.","principle":"Recovery sequencing should follow data authority, dependency, and safety requirements.","correct_example":"Recover durable state and required coordination services before enabling dependent write traffic.","invalid_example":"Start every component concurrently and allow clients to discover whatever becomes available first.","problem":"Dependent services operate against incomplete or stale prerequisites.","failure_mode":"Recovery-order inconsistency","cause":"No dependency-aware startup or traffic gating.","solution":"Define recovery phases and readiness gates.","alternative":"Architect the system for independent recovery with explicit degraded modes.","tradeoff":"Strict ordering can increase recovery time but reduce unsafe startup behavior.","constraint":"The correct order depends on application invariants and dependency topology.","edge_case":"A dependency becomes available temporarily and then fails during recovery.","security_implication":"Authorization services may need to recover before privileged operations are enabled.","performance_implication":"Sequential recovery can increase RTO.","testing_guidance":"Start components in incorrect orders and compare behavior with the documented safe sequence.","verification_guidance":"Verify that every recovery phase has explicit entry and exit criteria.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B98-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B94-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B96-R01","D17-B60-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B98-R01"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B98-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Disaster Recovery","concept":"Recovery objective validation","knowledge_type":"TEST_CASE","question":"How should declared recovery objectives be validated?","answer":"Recovery objectives should be tested through controlled recovery exercises that measure actual restoration time and recoverable data state against the declared objectives.","explanation":"A documented RTO or RPO is a requirement, not evidence that the system can achieve it.","principle":"Recovery claims require measured evidence from representative recovery scenarios.","correct_example":"Perform recovery exercises under realistic failure conditions and record achieved recovery time and recoverable data point.","invalid_example":"Declare an RTO or RPO satisfied because backups exist.","problem":"Recovery objectives are documented but untested.","failure_mode":"Unsupported recovery claim","cause":"Existence of mechanisms is mistaken for demonstrated recovery capability.","solution":"Run recovery drills and preserve evidence of results and limitations.","alternative":"Automated continuous recovery validation where feasible.","tradeoff":"Recovery exercises consume operational resources but provide direct evidence.","constraint":"Test results apply only to the tested architecture, workload, and conditions.","edge_case":"The recovery test environment differs materially from production.","security_implication":"Recovery procedures can expose sensitive data or credentials if poorly controlled.","performance_implication":"Recovery tests can temporarily consume capacity and network bandwidth.","testing_guidance":"Measure recovery under representative data volume, dependency failures, and traffic conditions.","verification_guidance":"Compare measured recovery results with the declared objectives and record any gap explicitly.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B60-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B98-R02"],"related_records":["D17-B60-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B98-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B60-R02"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B99-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cross-System Correctness","concept":"Safety versus liveness classification","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"Why should distributed-system failures be classified as safety or liveness violations?","answer":"Safety concerns preventing something bad from happening, while liveness concerns ensuring that something good eventually happens under the stated assumptions.","explanation":"The distinction helps identify whether a failure risks incorrect state or lack of progress and guides protocol analysis and testing.","principle":"Correctness claims should identify whether they concern safety, liveness, or both.","correct_example":"State that a protocol must never commit conflicting values for safety and must eventually commit under stated healthy conditions for liveness.","invalid_example":"Describe a system as correct without identifying which correctness properties are guaranteed.","problem":"A protocol appears operationally healthy while violating a safety property or appears safe while making no progress.","failure_mode":"Incomplete correctness specification","cause":"Safety and liveness are not separately defined.","solution":"Specify explicit safety and liveness properties and their assumptions.","alternative":"Use domain-specific invariants and progress metrics.","tradeoff":"More precise correctness specifications require additional modeling and evidence.","constraint":"Liveness claims depend strongly on timing, failure, and availability assumptions.","edge_case":"A protocol preserves safety by refusing to make progress during a prolonged partition.","security_implication":"Safety violations can create unauthorized or inconsistent state.","performance_implication":"Liveness mechanisms often trade latency and availability against safety constraints.","testing_guidance":"Test both conflicting-decision scenarios and prolonged non-progress scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Verify safety independently from liveness and document the assumptions required for each.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B22-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B93-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B20-R01","D17-B93-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B99-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cross-System Correctness","concept":"Assumption ledger","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"Why should distributed-system correctness claims maintain an explicit assumption ledger?","answer":"Distributed guarantees depend on assumptions about clocks, network behavior, failures, persistence, identity, quorum availability, and implementation behavior. Recording these assumptions prevents guarantees from being applied outside their valid scope.","explanation":"A protocol can be correct under one model and unsafe under another. The assumptions are therefore part of the evidence for the claim.","principle":"A distributed-system guarantee is incomplete without its environmental and implementation assumptions.","correct_example":"Record the communication, timing, failure, storage, and membership assumptions alongside each correctness claim.","invalid_example":"Copy a consensus guarantee into a deployment with materially different failure assumptions without analysis.","problem":"A formally or theoretically correct property is misapplied to a different environment.","failure_mode":"Assumption drift","cause":"Deployment conditions change without revisiting protocol guarantees.","solution":"Maintain explicit assumptions and validate them against deployment reality.","alternative":"Automated environment conformance checks for machine-verifiable assumptions.","tradeoff":"Assumption tracking adds documentation and validation work but improves reasoning reliability.","constraint":"Unknown assumptions must not be silently treated as satisfied.","edge_case":"A dependency changes its failure or persistence behavior after an upgrade.","security_implication":"Security assumptions can become invalid after identity or trust-boundary changes.","performance_implication":"Timing assumptions can become invalid under load or geographic expansion.","testing_guidance":"Continuously test critical assumptions such as latency bounds, persistence, and quorum availability.","verification_guidance":"Verify every critical guarantee against current deployment assumptions and versioned dependencies.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B93-R03"],"prerequisites":[],"related_records":["D17-B99-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B99-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Cross-System Correctness","concept":"Distributed-system claim hierarchy","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"How should a distributed-system claim be separated from its supporting evidence?","answer":"A claim should identify the property being asserted, the system scope, assumptions, evidence method, observed result, and limitations rather than treating design intent as proof.","explanation":"Architecture descriptions, tests, formal proofs, production observations, and benchmarks provide different levels and types of evidence.","principle":"Claim strength must not exceed evidence strength.","correct_example":"State that a protocol was structurally reviewed, tested under defined fault injections, or formally verified only for the exact scope supported by evidence.","invalid_example":"State that a distributed system is verified merely because it contains a verification component.","problem":"Design intent is confused with demonstrated correctness.","failure_mode":"Evidence overclaim","cause":"No distinction between implementation, execution, testing, and verification.","solution":"Maintain explicit evidence levels and validation states.","alternative":"Independent assurance reports or formal proof artifacts.","tradeoff":"Detailed evidence tracking increases metadata and audit work.","constraint":"Unexecuted or unverified claims must remain explicitly limited.","edge_case":"A test passes in a limited environment while production configuration differs materially.","security_implication":"False assurance can cause unsafe deployment of security-critical coordination systems.","performance_implication":"Benchmark claims require workload and environment metadata to remain meaningful.","testing_guidance":"Record exact test environment, workload, faults, expected result, and observed result.","verification_guidance":"Verify that each claim's evidence directly supports the stated scope.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B99-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B60-R03"],"related_records":["D17-B93-R03","D17-B20-R03"],"depends_on":["D17-B99-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B100-R01","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integrated Failure Reasoning","concept":"End-to-end distributed failure chain","knowledge_type":"FAILURE_MODE","question":"Why should distributed-system testing examine chains of failures rather than isolated failures only?","answer":"Real incidents can combine latency, partial failure, retries, stale membership, overload, and recovery actions, producing behavior that no isolated fault reveals.","explanation":"Distributed mechanisms interact. A timeout can trigger retries, retries can cause overload, overload can trigger health failures, and failover can expose stale state.","principle":"System-level reliability requires testing interacting failure mechanisms, not only individual component failures.","correct_example":"Inject a primary failure followed by delayed recovery, retry amplification, stale discovery, and dependency overload while checking system invariants.","invalid_example":"Test each failure independently and assume their composition is safe.","problem":"Failure interactions produce emergent instability.","failure_mode":"Cascading distributed failure","cause":"Independent mechanisms amplify one another under combined faults.","solution":"Use multi-fault scenarios and verify invariants throughout the failure chain.","alternative":"Formal compositional analysis for selected critical interactions.","tradeoff":"Multi-fault testing is more expensive and harder to reproduce.","constraint":"Scenario complexity must remain observable and diagnosable.","edge_case":"Recovery itself triggers another overload event.","security_implication":"Attackers may intentionally combine failure triggers to amplify impact.","performance_implication":"Cascading failures often manifest as extreme tail latency and resource exhaustion.","testing_guidance":"Build controlled multi-stage fault scenarios and capture causal telemetry.","verification_guidance":"Verify safety, bounded overload, recovery, and eventual convergence across the entire scenario.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B20-R03","D17-B99-R01"],"prerequisites":["D17-B39-R02","D17-B72-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B90-R03","D17-B98-R03"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B100-R02","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integrated Failure Reasoning","concept":"Distributed-system convergence","knowledge_type":"PRINCIPLE","question":"What does convergence mean in a distributed system?","answer":"Convergence means that, under the protocol's assumptions and after relevant disturbances stop, distributed state moves toward a stable condition satisfying the system's defined consistency or membership properties.","explanation":"Convergence does not necessarily mean instantaneous equality. The system must define which state should converge, under what assumptions, and within what practical bounds.","principle":"Eventual convergence is meaningful only when the target state, triggering conditions, and convergence assumptions are explicit.","correct_example":"Define that replicas converge to equivalent authoritative state after communication resumes and repair completes, subject to the replication protocol.","invalid_example":"Claim that all distributed state is always identical at every moment.","problem":"Eventual consistency is confused with instantaneous consistency.","failure_mode":"Incorrect convergence expectation","cause":"No explicit convergence contract.","solution":"Define convergence target, trigger conditions, conflict rules, and observability requirements.","alternative":"Strong consistency when the invariant requires immediate agreement.","tradeoff":"Faster or stronger convergence generally requires additional coordination or resource cost.","constraint":"Some partitions or permanent failures prevent convergence until recovery conditions are restored.","edge_case":"A continuously changing workload prevents the system from reaching a stable state.","security_implication":"Security policy replicas must converge within a bound appropriate to the protected operation.","performance_implication":"Repair and reconciliation consume network, CPU, and storage resources.","testing_guidance":"Measure convergence after partitions, delayed messages, and replica failures.","verification_guidance":"Verify convergence against an explicit target state and documented assumptions.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B17-R03"],"prerequisites":["D17-B99-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B52-R03","D17-B94-R02"],"depends_on":[],"extends":[],"refines":[],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}
{"record_id":"D17-B100-R03","dataset_id":"D17","topic":"Distributed Systems","subtopic":"Integrated Failure Reasoning","concept":"Distributed-system completeness boundary","knowledge_type":"VERIFICATION_RULE","question":"What should a final distributed-system architecture audit verify before claiming readiness?","answer":"The audit should verify that communication, failure assumptions, consistency, coordination, recovery, security, observability, performance, testing, and operational governance are explicitly modeled and that every important guarantee has supporting evidence.","explanation":"Distributed correctness is cross-cutting. A system can have correct individual components while remaining unsafe because their assumptions or failure boundaries do not compose.","principle":"System readiness requires cross-layer evidence, not merely component-level implementation completeness.","correct_example":"Trace critical invariants from requirements through architecture, implementation, tests, failure scenarios, recovery procedures, and evidence records.","invalid_example":"Declare the distributed architecture complete because all planned modules exist.","problem":"Implementation completeness is mistaken for system correctness.","failure_mode":"Cross-layer verification gap","cause":"No final traceability and assumption audit.","solution":"Perform requirement-to-evidence tracing and explicitly record uncovered or unknown areas.","alternative":"Independent architecture and reliability review.","tradeoff":"A comprehensive audit requires substantial analysis but exposes hidden integration gaps.","constraint":"Claims must remain bounded by actual verification scope.","edge_case":"A component is individually verified but its integration changes its assumptions.","security_implication":"Cross-layer gaps can undermine otherwise strong security controls.","performance_implication":"Integration behavior can invalidate component-level performance assumptions.","testing_guidance":"Run cross-layer integration, fault-injection, recovery, security, and performance scenarios.","verification_guidance":"Require explicit evidence for each critical invariant and mark unsupported claims as UNKNOWN or PARTIALLY_VERIFIED.","language":"GENERAL","framework":"NONE","version":"N/A","version_range":"N/A","dependencies":["D17-B99-R03","D17-B100-R01","D17-B100-R02"],"prerequisites":["D17-B93-R01"],"related_records":["D17-B60-R03","D17-B90-R03","D17-B99-R02"],"depends_on":["D17-B100-R01","D17-B100-R02"],"extends":[],"refines":["D17-B99-R03"],"contrasts_with":[],"contradicts":[],"supersedes":[],"provenance":{"status":"SOURCE_REGISTRY_PENDING"},"evidence_level":"HIGH","confidence":"HIGH","validation_status":"STRUCTURAL_REVIEWED","created_at":"2026-09-18","updated_at":"2026-09-18"}

